window.KV_TRACKS = {
  goal: 6,
  levels: [{"id":"b","name":"Basic"},{"id":"i","name":"Intermediate"},{"id":"a","name":"Advanced"}],
  tracks: [
    { id: "py", name: "Python", topic: "Python", blurb: "From your first line of code to classes, testing and pandas.", units: [
      { id: "py.b.01", level: "b", n: 1, title: "Print and variables", cards: [
        {"t":"learn","topic":"Python","title":"Variables hold values","body":"A **variable** is a name for a value. `=` stores the value on the right in the name on the left, and `print()` shows values on screen. Names are **case-sensitive**: `name` and `Name` are different.","code":"name = \"Asha\"\nage = 21\nprint(name, age)    # Asha 21","lang":"python","id":"py.b.01.1"},
        {"t":"bug","topic":"Python","q":"This program crashes with a NameError. Which line crashes?","code":["name = \"Ravi\"","greeting = \"Hello \"","print(greeting + Name)"],"bad":2,"why":"Python is **case-sensitive**: `name` and `Name` are different. `Name` was never created: `NameError: name 'Name' is not defined`.","id":"py.b.01.2"},
        {"t":"output","topic":"Python","code":"x = 5\nx = x + 2\nprint(x)","a":"7","w":["5","x + 2","52"],"why":"A variable can change. `x = x + 2` reads the old value (5), adds 2 and stores `7` back in `x`.","id":"py.b.01.3"},
        {"t":"output","topic":"Python","code":"name = \"Asha\"\nprint(\"Hi\", name, \"!\")","a":"Hi Asha !","w":["Hi Asha!","HiAsha!","Hi name !"],"why":"`print()` puts a **space between** each value you separate with commas, even before the `!`. Use an f-string like `f\"Hi {name}!\"` to avoid it.","id":"py.b.01.4"},
        {"t":"choice","topic":"Python","q":"Which is a valid Python variable name?","a":"total_marks","w":["total marks","2total","total-marks"],"why":"Names can use letters, digits and `_`, but cannot start with a digit or contain spaces. `-` would mean minus.","id":"py.b.01.5"},
        {"t":"output","topic":"Python","code":"a = 1\nb = 2\na, b = b, a\nprint(a, b)","a":"2 1","w":["1 2","2 2","Error"],"why":"`a, b = b, a` **swaps** the two values in one line: Python reads the right side first (2, 1), then stores them.","id":"py.b.01.6"},
        {"t":"order","topic":"Python","q":"Put the lines in order so the program prints: Hello, Ravi","items":["name = \"Ravi\"","greeting = \"Hello, \" + name","print(greeting)"],"why":"Python runs from top to bottom, so each variable must be created **before** the line that uses it.","id":"py.b.01.7"},
        {"t":"check","topic":"Python","code":"x = 10\ny = x\nx = 20\nprint(y)","a":"10","w":["20","x","Error"],"why":"`y = x` stores the value 10 in `y` at that moment. Changing `x` later does not change `y`.","id":"py.b.01.8"},
        {"t":"check","topic":"Python","q":"What does `print(\"5 + 3\")` show?","a":"5 + 3","w":["8","\"5 + 3\"","53"],"why":"Inside quotes it is **text**, so Python prints it exactly as written, without the quotes. `print(5 + 3)` would show 8.","id":"py.b.01.9"}
      ] },
      { id: "py.b.02", level: "b", n: 2, title: "Numbers and maths", cards: [
        {"t":"learn","topic":"Python","title":"Python does maths","body":"`+`, `-` and `*` work as usual. `/` always gives a decimal (a float). `//` drops the decimal part, `%` gives the remainder and `**` is power. `round(x, 1)` keeps 1 decimal.","code":"print(7 / 2)     # 3.5\nprint(7 // 2)    # 3\nprint(7 % 2)     # 1\nprint(2 ** 3)    # 8","lang":"python","id":"py.b.02.1"},
        {"t":"output","topic":"Python","code":"x = 10\nx += 5\nprint(x // 4)","a":"3","w":["3.75","4","15"],"why":"`x += 5` makes x 15. `//` is floor division: 15 ÷ 4 = 3.75, and `//` drops the decimal part, so `3`.","id":"py.b.02.2"},
        {"t":"output","topic":"Python","code":"print(10 == 10.0)","a":"True","w":["False","Error","10"],"why":"`==` compares values. 10 and 10.0 are the same number, so `True`, even though one is an int and one is a float.","id":"py.b.02.3"},
        {"t":"output","topic":"Python","code":"x = [1, 2, 3]\nprint(sum(x) / len(x))","a":"2.0","w":["2","6","3"],"why":"`sum(x)` is 6 and `len(x)` is 3. `/` always gives a float, so 6 / 3 is `2.0`, not `2`.","id":"py.b.02.4"},
        {"t":"output","topic":"Python","code":"print(round(7.567, 1))","a":"7.6","w":["7.5","8","7.57"],"why":"`round(number, 1)` keeps 1 digit after the point. 7.567 rounds up to `7.6`.","id":"py.b.02.5"},
        {"t":"choice","topic":"Python","q":"Which operator gives the remainder of a division?","a":"%","w":["//","/","**"],"why":"`%` is the remainder: `10 % 3` is 1. `//` drops the decimal part, `/` divides normally and `**` is power.","id":"py.b.02.6"},
        {"t":"check","topic":"Python","code":"print(2 + 3 * 4)","a":"14","w":["20","24","9"],"why":"Like BODMAS in school maths, `*` happens before `+`: 3 × 4 = 12, then 2 + 12 = `14`. Use brackets to change the order: `(2 + 3) * 4` is 20.","id":"py.b.02.7"},
        {"t":"check","topic":"Python","code":"print(9 // 2, 9 % 2)","a":"4 1","w":["4.5 1","4 0","5 1"],"why":"9 ÷ 2 is 4 with 1 left over: `//` gives `4` and `%` gives `1`.","id":"py.b.02.8"}
      ] },
      { id: "py.b.03", level: "b", n: 3, title: "Strings", cards: [
        {"t":"learn","topic":"Python","title":"Text is a string","body":"Text goes in quotes. Positions start at **0**, and `-1` is the last letter. A slice `s[a:b]` stops **before** `b`. `len()` counts letters, `+` joins text and `*` repeats it.","code":"word = \"Python\"\nprint(word[0], word[-1])   # P n\nprint(word[0:2])           # Py\nprint(\"ab\" * 3)            # ababab","lang":"python","id":"py.b.03.1"},
        {"t":"output","topic":"Python","code":"print(len(\"data\") * 2)","a":"8","w":["datadata","4","16"],"why":"`len(\"data\")` counts the letters: 4. Then `4 * 2` is `8`. Python works from the inside out.","id":"py.b.03.2"},
        {"t":"output","topic":"Python","code":"print(\"Azure\"[1:4])","a":"zur","w":["Azu","zure","zu"],"why":"Positions start at 0: A=0, z=1, u=2, r=3, e=4. `[1:4]` takes 1, 2 and 3 (the end is not included): `zur`.","id":"py.b.03.3"},
        {"t":"output","topic":"Python","code":"words = [\"hi\", \"there\"]\nprint(\" \".join(words).upper())","a":"HI THERE","w":["hi there","HITHERE","['HI', 'THERE']"],"why":"`\" \".join(words)` joins the list with a space: `hi there`. Then `.upper()` makes it capitals.","id":"py.b.03.4"},
        {"t":"output","topic":"Python","code":"print(3 * \"ab\")","a":"ababab","w":["3ab","ab ab ab","TypeError"],"why":"A number times text **repeats** the text: `3 * \"ab\"` is `ababab`, with no spaces.","id":"py.b.03.5"},
        {"t":"output","topic":"Python","code":"city = \"Guntur\"\nprint(len(city), city[-1])","a":"6 r","w":["6 G","5 r","7 r"],"why":"G-u-n-t-u-r has 6 letters, and `[-1]` is the last one: `r`.","id":"py.b.03.6"},
        {"t":"check","topic":"Python","code":"print(\"hello\".upper()[1:3])","a":"EL","w":["el","ELL","HE"],"why":"`.upper()` gives `HELLO`; the slice `[1:3]` takes positions 1 and 2: `EL`.","id":"py.b.03.7"},
        {"t":"check","topic":"Python","code":"name = \"Ravi\"\nprint(f\"Hi {name}, {len(name)} letters\")","a":"Hi Ravi, 4 letters","w":["Hi name, len(name) letters","Hi {name}, {len(name)} letters","Hi Ravi, 5 letters"],"why":"In an **f-string**, the code inside `{ }` is run and its result is put into the text.","id":"py.b.03.8"}
      ] },
      { id: "py.b.04", level: "b", n: 4, title: "Input and type conversion", cards: [
        {"t":"learn","topic":"Python","title":"Text or number?","body":"`\"25\"` in quotes is **text**; `25` is a **number**. Text + number crashes with `TypeError`. Convert with `int()`, `float()` or `str()`. Note: `input()` always gives you text.","code":"age = \"25\"            # text, like input() gives\nprint(int(age) + 1)   # 26","lang":"python","id":"py.b.04.1"},
        {"t":"bug","topic":"Python","q":"This program crashes with a TypeError. Which line crashes?","code":["age = \"25\"","next_year = age + 1","print(next_year)"],"bad":1,"why":"`age` is text (it has quotes). Text + number is not allowed: `TypeError: can only concatenate str (not \"int\") to str`. Fix: `int(age) + 1`.","id":"py.b.04.2"},
        {"t":"output","topic":"Python","code":"print(\"10\" + \"5\")","a":"105","w":["15","10 5","TypeError"],"why":"Both are text (in quotes), so `+` **joins** them: `105`. For maths, convert first: `int(\"10\") + int(\"5\")` is 15.","id":"py.b.04.3"},
        {"t":"bug","topic":"Python","q":"This program crashes with a ValueError. Which line crashes?","code":["text = \"12a\"","num = int(text)","print(num + 1)"],"bad":1,"why":"`int()` can only turn digits into a number. `\"12a\"` has a letter: `ValueError: invalid literal for int() with base 10: '12a'`.","id":"py.b.04.4"},
        {"t":"output","topic":"Python","code":"print(int(\"7\") + int(\"3\"))","a":"10","w":["73","7 3","TypeError"],"why":"`int()` turns each text into a number first, so `+` adds them: 7 + 3 = `10`.","id":"py.b.04.5"},
        {"t":"output","topic":"Python","code":"print(str(5) + \"5\", int(2.9))","a":"55 2","w":["10 3","55 3","10 2"],"why":"`str(5)` is text, so `+` joins: `55`. `int(2.9)` cuts off the decimal part (it does not round): `2`.","id":"py.b.04.6"},
        {"t":"check","topic":"Python","code":"x = input(\"Number: \")    # the user types 4\nprint(x * 2)","a":"44","w":["8","4","TypeError"],"why":"`input()` always gives **text**, so `\"4\" * 2` repeats it: `44`. Use `int(input(...))` to get the number 8.","id":"py.b.04.7"},
        {"t":"check","topic":"Python","q":"Which turns the text `\"3.5\"` into a number you can do maths with?","a":"float(\"3.5\")","w":["int(\"3.5\")","str(\"3.5\")","number(\"3.5\")"],"why":"`float()` handles decimals. `int(\"3.5\")` crashes with `ValueError`, and Python has no `number()` function.","id":"py.b.04.8"}
      ] },
      { id: "py.b.05", level: "b", n: 5, title: "if / elif / else", cards: [
        {"t":"learn","topic":"Python","title":"Making decisions","body":"`if` runs its block only when the condition is True. `elif` checks another condition and `else` catches the rest. Each block is indented 4 spaces. `and` needs both sides True; `or` needs one.","code":"marks = 72\nif marks >= 75:\n    print(\"Distinction\")\nelif marks >= 35:\n    print(\"Pass\")\nelse:\n    print(\"Fail\")","lang":"python","id":"py.b.05.1"},
        {"t":"bug","topic":"Python","q":"Python says IndentationError. Which line is wrong?","code":["score = 80","if score > 50:","print(\"Pass\")"],"bad":2,"why":"The line after `if ...:` must be indented (4 spaces): `    print(\"Pass\")`. Python uses spaces to know what is inside the `if`.","id":"py.b.05.2"},
        {"t":"output","topic":"Python","code":"a = 5\nb = 2\nprint(a > b and b > 3)","a":"False","w":["True","2","Error"],"why":"`and` is True only if **both** sides are True. `5 > 2` is True, but `2 > 3` is False, so the result is `False`.","id":"py.b.05.3"},
        {"t":"indent","topic":"Python","step":4,"q":"Fix the indentation so a temperature above 37 prints Fever.","lines":[[0,"temp = 38"],[0,"if temp > 37:"],[4,"print(\"Fever\")"],[0,"else:"],[4,"print(\"Normal\")"]],"why":"`else:` lines up with its `if` (0 spaces). The line under each one is indented 4 spaces. 38 is above 37, so it prints `Fever`.","id":"py.b.05.4"},
        {"t":"output","topic":"Python","code":"age = 17\nif age >= 18:\n    print(\"Adult\")\nelse:\n    print(\"Minor\")","a":"Minor","w":["Adult","Adult\nMinor","Minor\nAdult"],"why":"17 >= 18 is False, so the `if` block is skipped and only the `else` block runs.","id":"py.b.05.5"},
        {"t":"output","topic":"Python","code":"marks = 80\nif marks >= 35:\n    print(\"Pass\")\nelif marks >= 75:\n    print(\"Distinction\")","a":"Pass","w":["Distinction","Pass\nDistinction","Distinction\nPass"],"why":"Python stops at the **first** True condition. 80 >= 35 is True, so the `elif` is never checked. Put the stricter check (`>= 75`) first.","id":"py.b.05.6"},
        {"t":"check","topic":"Python","code":"temp = 30\nif temp > 35 or temp < 10:\n    print(\"Stay inside\")\nelse:\n    print(\"Go out\")","a":"Go out","w":["Stay inside","Stay inside\nGo out","Error"],"why":"`or` needs at least one side True. 30 > 35 is False and 30 < 10 is False, so the `else` runs.","id":"py.b.05.7"},
        {"t":"check","topic":"Python","q":"Which line correctly checks whether `x` equals 5?","a":"if x == 5:","w":["if x = 5:","if x == 5","if (x equals 5):"],"why":"`==` compares, while `=` stores a value. An `if` line also needs a colon at the end.","id":"py.b.05.8"}
      ] },
      { id: "py.b.06", level: "b", n: 6, title: "Lists", cards: [
        {"t":"learn","topic":"Python","title":"Lists hold many values","body":"A **list** keeps items in order: `[70, 80, 90]`. Positions start at 0 and `-1` is the last. `.append()` adds to the end, `len()` counts items and `sorted()` gives a new sorted list.","code":"marks = [70, 80, 90]\nmarks.append(65)\nprint(marks[0], marks[-1], len(marks))   # 70 65 4","lang":"python","id":"py.b.06.1"},
        {"t":"bug","topic":"Python","q":"This program crashes. Which line crashes?","code":["marks = [70, 80, 90]","total = sum(marks)","print(marks[3])"],"bad":2,"why":"The list has 3 items at positions 0, 1 and 2. There is no position 3: `IndexError: list index out of range`. The last item is `marks[2]` or `marks[-1]`.","id":"py.b.06.2"},
        {"t":"output","topic":"Python","code":"items = [\"a\", \"b\", \"c\"]\nprint(items[-2])","a":"b","w":["c","a","IndexError"],"why":"Negative positions count from the end: `-1` is `c`, `-2` is `b`.","id":"py.b.06.3"},
        {"t":"output","topic":"Python","code":"nums = [3, 1, 2]\nprint(sorted(nums), nums)","a":"[1, 2, 3] [3, 1, 2]","w":["[1, 2, 3] [1, 2, 3]","None [1, 2, 3]","[3, 2, 1] [3, 1, 2]"],"why":"`sorted()` returns a **new** sorted list and leaves the original alone. `nums.sort()` would change `nums` itself.","id":"py.b.06.4"},
        {"t":"output","topic":"Python","code":"print(len([1, [2, 3], 4]))","a":"3","w":["4","2","Error"],"why":"The list has 3 items: `1`, the inner list `[2, 3]`, and `4`. `len()` does not look inside the inner list.","id":"py.b.06.5"},
        {"t":"output","topic":"Python","code":"nums = [4, 9, 2]\nnums[1] = 5\nprint(nums)","a":"[4, 5, 2]","w":["[5, 9, 2]","[4, 9, 2]","[4, 9, 5]"],"why":"Lists can be changed. `nums[1]` is the **second** item (9), and it becomes 5.","id":"py.b.06.6"},
        {"t":"check","topic":"Python","code":"fruits = [\"mango\", \"banana\"]\nfruits.append(\"guava\")\nprint(fruits[1], len(fruits))","a":"banana 3","w":["mango 3","banana 2","guava 3"],"why":"`.append()` adds `guava` at the end, so there are 3 items. Position 1 is the second item: `banana`.","id":"py.b.06.7"},
        {"t":"check","topic":"Python","code":"nums = [10, 20, 30, 40]\nprint(nums[-1] - nums[0])","a":"30","w":["-30","40","10"],"why":"`nums[-1]` is the last item (40) and `nums[0]` the first (10): 40 − 10 = `30`.","id":"py.b.06.8"}
      ] },
      { id: "py.b.07", level: "b", n: 7, title: "for loops and range", cards: [
        {"t":"learn","topic":"Python","title":"Repeat with for","body":"`for x in items:` runs the indented block once for each item. `range(5)` is 0 to 4, `range(1, 5)` is 1 to 4, and `range(2, 8, 2)` jumps by 2. Only the indented lines repeat.","code":"total = 0\nfor n in range(1, 4):\n    total = total + n\nprint(total)    # 6","lang":"python","id":"py.b.07.1"},
        {"t":"bug","topic":"Python","q":"This program will not run. Which line has the mistake?","code":["nums = [3, 5, 7]","for n in nums","    print(n * 2)"],"bad":1,"why":"A `for` line must end with a colon `:`. Python says `SyntaxError: expected ':'`. Fixed: `for n in nums:`","id":"py.b.07.2"},
        {"t":"output","topic":"Python","code":"for i in range(2, 8, 2):\n    print(i)","a":"2\n4\n6","w":["2\n4\n6\n8","2\n3\n4\n5\n6\n7","4\n6\n8"],"why":"`range(start, stop, step)`: start at 2, jump by 2, stop **before** 8. So 2, 4, 6.","id":"py.b.07.3"},
        {"t":"bug","topic":"Python","q":"This program crashes with a NameError. Which line crashes?","code":["total = 0","for n in range(5):","    total = total + m","print(total)"],"bad":2,"why":"A typo: the loop variable is `n`, but the line uses `m`, which was never created: `NameError: name 'm' is not defined`.","id":"py.b.07.4"},
        {"t":"bug","topic":"Python","q":"This program prints 3 numbers and then crashes with IndexError. Which line crashes?","code":["nums = [5, 10, 15]","for i in range(4):","    print(nums[i])"],"bad":2,"why":"The crash happens at `print(nums[i])` when `i` is 3. The real cause is `range(4)`: the list has only 3 items. Better: `for n in nums:`.","id":"py.b.07.5"},
        {"t":"bug","topic":"Python","q":"This program crashes with a NameError. Which line crashes?","code":["scores = [80, 90]","total = 0","for s in scores:","    total += s","print(\"Average:\", total / len(score))"],"bad":4,"why":"A typo: the list is `scores`, but the last line says `score`. `NameError: name 'score' is not defined`.","id":"py.b.07.6"},
        {"t":"indent","topic":"Python","step":4,"q":"Make this loop print 10, 20, 30 and then done.","lines":[[0,"nums = [1, 2, 3]"],[0,"for n in nums:"],[4,"print(n * 10)"],[0,"print(\"done\")"]],"why":"Only `print(n * 10)` is inside the loop (4 spaces), so it runs 3 times. `print(\"done\")` is back at 0 spaces, so it runs once at the end.","id":"py.b.07.7"},
        {"t":"indent","topic":"Python","step":4,"q":"Fix the indentation so it prints only the even numbers from 1 to 5.","lines":[[0,"for n in range(1, 6):"],[4,"if n % 2 == 0:"],[8,"print(n, \"is even\")"]],"why":"The `if` is inside the loop (4 spaces), and the `print` is inside the `if` (8 spaces). It prints `2 is even` and `4 is even`.","id":"py.b.07.8"},
        {"t":"check","topic":"Python","code":"for ch in \"abc\":\n    print(ch.upper())","a":"A\nB\nC","w":["ABC","a\nb\nc","A B C"],"why":"A `for` loop can go through the letters of a string. Each letter is printed in capitals on its own line.","id":"py.b.07.9"},
        {"t":"check","topic":"Python","code":"count = 0\nfor n in [3, 8, 1, 9]:\n    if n > 5:\n        count += 1\nprint(count)","a":"2","w":["4","17","1"],"why":"Only 8 and 9 are greater than 5, so `count` goes up twice: `2`.","id":"py.b.07.10"}
      ] },
      { id: "py.b.08", level: "b", n: 8, title: "while loops", cards: [
        {"t":"learn","topic":"Python","title":"Repeat while True","body":"`while condition:` repeats as long as the condition stays True. Something inside the loop must change, or it never stops. The line ends with a colon, like `if` and `for`.","code":"count = 0\nwhile count < 3:\n    print(count)\n    count = count + 1","lang":"python","id":"py.b.08.1"},
        {"t":"bug","topic":"Python","q":"Python will not run this. Which line has the mistake?","code":["count = 0","while count < 3","    count = count + 1"],"bad":1,"why":"Like `if` and `for`, a `while` line must end with a colon: `while count < 3:`. Python says `SyntaxError: expected ':'`.","id":"py.b.08.2"},
        {"t":"output","topic":"Python","code":"n = 1\nwhile n < 20:\n    n = n * 2\nprint(n)","a":"32","w":["16","20","64"],"why":"`n` doubles: 2, 4, 8, 16, 32. At 16 the condition is still True, so it doubles once more to 32, then 32 < 20 is False and the loop stops.","id":"py.b.08.3"},
        {"t":"output","topic":"Python","code":"count = 3\nwhile count > 0:\n    print(count)\n    count -= 1\nprint(\"Go!\")","a":"3\n2\n1\nGo!","w":["3\n2\n1\n0\nGo!","2\n1\n0\nGo!","3\nGo!"],"why":"It prints 3, 2, 1. When `count` becomes 0, `0 > 0` is False, so the loop ends before printing 0.","id":"py.b.08.4"},
        {"t":"bug","topic":"Python","q":"This loop never ends. Which line is in the wrong place?","code":["count = 0","while count < 3:","    print(count)","count = count + 1"],"bad":3,"why":"`count = count + 1` is not indented, so it is **outside** the loop. `count` stays 0 forever and the loop never stops. Indent it 4 spaces.","id":"py.b.08.5"},
        {"t":"indent","topic":"Python","step":4,"q":"Fix the indentation so it prints 1, 2, 3 and then Done.","lines":[[0,"n = 1"],[0,"while n <= 3:"],[4,"print(n)"],[4,"n = n + 1"],[0,"print(\"Done\")"]],"why":"Both `print(n)` and `n = n + 1` must be inside the loop (4 spaces). `print(\"Done\")` is outside, so it runs once at the end.","id":"py.b.08.6"},
        {"t":"check","topic":"Python","code":"total = 0\ni = 1\nwhile i <= 4:\n    total += i\n    i += 1\nprint(total)","a":"10","w":["4","6","15"],"why":"It adds 1 + 2 + 3 + 4 = `10`. When `i` becomes 5, `5 <= 4` is False and the loop stops.","id":"py.b.08.7"},
        {"t":"check","topic":"Python","q":"Which keyword ends a loop immediately?","a":"break","w":["continue","stop","pass"],"why":"`break` leaves the loop at once. `continue` skips to the next round, `pass` does nothing, and Python has no `stop` keyword.","id":"py.b.08.8"}
      ] },
      { id: "py.b.09", level: "b", n: 9, title: "Functions", cards: [
        {"t":"learn","topic":"Python","title":"Functions: reusable blocks","body":"`def name(params):` creates a function; its body is indented. It runs only when you **call** it, and the call must give every required value. `return` sends a value back.","code":"def area(w, h):\n    return w * h\n\nprint(area(3, 4))    # 12","lang":"python","id":"py.b.09.1"},
        {"t":"bug","topic":"Python","q":"This program crashes with a TypeError. Which line crashes?","code":["def greet(name):","    return \"Hi \" + name","print(greet())"],"bad":2,"why":"`greet` needs one value for `name`, but the call gives none: `TypeError: greet() missing 1 required positional argument: 'name'`. Fix: `greet(\"Asha\")`.","id":"py.b.09.2"},
        {"t":"bug","topic":"Python","q":"Python says IndentationError. Which line is wrong?","code":["def greet(name):","print(\"Hi\", name)","greet(\"Asha\")"],"bad":1,"why":"The body of a function must be indented: `    print(\"Hi\", name)`. Without the spaces Python does not know it belongs to `greet`.","id":"py.b.09.3"},
        {"t":"indent","topic":"Python","step":4,"q":"Fix this function so the program prints 12.","lines":[[0,"def area(w, h):"],[4,"result = w * h"],[4,"return result"],[0,"print(area(3, 4))"]],"why":"The function body (`result = ...` and `return`) is indented 4 spaces under `def`. The call `print(area(3, 4))` is outside, at 0 spaces: 3 × 4 = 12.","id":"py.b.09.4"},
        {"t":"output","topic":"Python","code":"def square(n):\n    return n * n\n\nprint(square(3) + square(4))","a":"25","w":["49","7","34"],"why":"Each call returns a value: 3 × 3 = 9 and 4 × 4 = 16, then 9 + 16 = `25`.","id":"py.b.09.5"},
        {"t":"output","topic":"Python","code":"def show(x):\n    print(x)\n\nresult = show(5)\nprint(result)","a":"5\nNone","w":["5\n5","None\n5","5"],"why":"`show` prints 5 but has no `return`, so it gives back `None`. That is what `result` holds.","id":"py.b.09.6"},
        {"t":"check","topic":"Python","code":"def add(a, b):\n    return a + b\n\nprint(add(\"2\", \"3\"), add(2, 3))","a":"23 5","w":["5 5","23 23","TypeError"],"why":"The same function joins text (`\"2\" + \"3\"` is `23`) and adds numbers (2 + 3 is `5`).","id":"py.b.09.7"},
        {"t":"check","topic":"Python","q":"What does `return` do inside a function?","a":"Sends a value back to the line that called the function","w":["Prints the value on the screen","Starts the function again","Saves the value in a file"],"why":"`return` hands a value back and ends the function. The caller can print it, store it or use it in maths.","id":"py.b.09.8"}
      ] },
      { id: "py.b.10", level: "b", n: 10, title: "Dictionaries", cards: [
        {"t":"learn","topic":"Python","title":"Look up by key","body":"A **dictionary** maps keys to values: `{\"Asha\": 21}`. `d[key]` reads a value but crashes if the key is missing; `d.get(key, 0)` is safe. `d[key] = value` adds or changes. `.items()` gives key and value pairs.","code":"ages = {\"Asha\": 21}\nages[\"Ravi\"] = 25\nprint(ages.get(\"Kiran\", 0))    # 0","lang":"python","id":"py.b.10.1"},
        {"t":"bug","topic":"Python","q":"This program crashes with a KeyError. Which line crashes?","code":["ages = {\"Asha\": 21}","ages[\"Ravi\"] = 25","print(ages[\"Kiran\"])"],"bad":2,"why":"Adding a key with `ages[\"Ravi\"] = 25` is fine. Reading a missing key crashes: `KeyError: 'Kiran'`. Safe way: `ages.get(\"Kiran\", 0)`.","id":"py.b.10.2"},
        {"t":"bug","topic":"Python","q":"This program crashes with an AttributeError. Which line crashes?","code":["marks = {\"maths\": 90}","marks.append(\"science\")","print(marks)"],"bad":1,"why":"`marks` is a dictionary, and dictionaries have no `.append()`. Add a key instead: `marks[\"science\"] = 85`.","id":"py.b.10.3"},
        {"t":"indent","topic":"Python","step":4,"q":"Fix the indentation so it prints only the items that cost more than 15.","lines":[[0,"prices = {\"tea\": 10, \"coffee\": 20}"],[0,"for item, cost in prices.items():"],[4,"if cost > 15:"],[8,"print(item)"]],"why":"`.items()` gives each key and value. The `if` is inside the loop and the `print` inside the `if`, so only `coffee` is printed.","id":"py.b.10.4"},
        {"t":"output","topic":"Python","code":"stock = {\"pen\": 5, \"book\": 2}\nstock[\"pen\"] = stock[\"pen\"] - 1\nprint(stock[\"pen\"], len(stock))","a":"4 2","w":["5 2","4 1","6 2"],"why":"The value for `pen` goes from 5 to `4`. `len()` counts the keys: `pen` and `book`, so `2`.","id":"py.b.10.5"},
        {"t":"output","topic":"Python","code":"ages = {\"Asha\": 21, \"Ravi\": 25}\nfor name in ages:\n    print(name)","a":"Asha\nRavi","w":["21\n25","Asha 21\nRavi 25","('Asha', 21)\n('Ravi', 25)"],"why":"Looping over a dictionary gives its **keys**. Use `.items()` to get keys and values together.","id":"py.b.10.6"},
        {"t":"check","topic":"Python","code":"d = {\"a\": 1}\nd[\"b\"] = 2\nd[\"a\"] = 10\nprint(d)","a":"{'a': 10, 'b': 2}","w":["{'a': 1, 'b': 2}","{'a': 1, 'a': 10, 'b': 2}","{'b': 2, 'a': 10}"],"why":"A key appears only once. `d[\"a\"] = 10` **replaces** the old value, and the keys keep the order they were added in.","id":"py.b.10.7"},
        {"t":"check","topic":"Python","code":"marks = {\"maths\": 90, \"science\": 80}\nprint(\"english\" in marks, marks.get(\"english\", 0))","a":"False 0","w":["True 0","False None","KeyError"],"why":"`in` checks whether a key exists (False). `.get()` with a default returns `0` instead of crashing.","id":"py.b.10.8"}
      ] },
      { id: "py.i.11", level: "i", n: 11, title: "Tuples and sets", cards: [
        {"t":"learn","topic":"Python","title":"Tuples and sets","body":"A **tuple** is like a list, but it cannot be changed after you make it. Write it with `( )`. A **set** keeps only **unique** items, so repeats are dropped, and it has no fixed order.","code":"point = (10, 20)\ntags = {'a', 'b', 'a'}\nprint(len(tags))","lang":"python","id":"py.i.11.1"},
        {"t":"output","topic":"Python","code":"t = (4, 5, 6)\nprint(t[1], len(t))","lang":"python","a":"5 3","w":["4 3","5 2","6 3"],"why":"Counting starts at 0, so `t[1]` is 5. The tuple has 3 items, so `len(t)` is 3.","id":"py.i.11.2"},
        {"t":"bug","topic":"Python","q":"This program fails with `TypeError: 'tuple' object does not support item assignment`. Which line is wrong?","code":["t = (1, 2, 3)","print(t[0])","t[0] = 9","print(t)"],"bad":2,"why":"A tuple cannot be changed. To get a different tuple, you must make a new one.","id":"py.i.11.3"},
        {"t":"output","topic":"Python","code":"marks = [80, 90, 80, 70, 90]\nprint(sorted(set(marks)))","lang":"python","a":"[70, 80, 90]","w":["[80, 90, 80, 70, 90]","[90, 80, 70]","{70, 80, 90}"],"why":"`set(marks)` removes repeats. `sorted(...)` gives a list in order, so it prints with square brackets.","id":"py.i.11.4"},
        {"t":"choice","topic":"Python","q":"Which code makes an empty set?","a":"set()","w":["{}","[]","()"],"why":"`{}` makes an empty dictionary, not a set. Use `set()` for an empty set.","id":"py.i.11.5"},
        {"t":"output","topic":"Python","code":"city, pin = ('Guntur', 522001)\nprint(pin, city)","lang":"python","a":"522001 Guntur","w":["Guntur 522001","('Guntur', 522001)","522001"],"why":"Unpacking puts the first item in `city` and the second in `pin`. The print shows `pin` first.","id":"py.i.11.6"},
        {"t":"output","topic":"Python","code":"a = {1, 2, 3}\nb = {2, 3, 4}\nprint(sorted(a & b))\nprint(sorted(a | b))","lang":"python","a":"[2, 3]\n[1, 2, 3, 4]","w":["[1, 4]\n[1, 2, 3, 4]","[2, 3]\n[2, 3]","[1, 2, 3, 4]\n[2, 3]"],"why":"`&` keeps items found in both sets. `|` joins both sets, without repeats.","id":"py.i.11.7"},
        {"t":"check","topic":"Python","q":"What does this program print?","code":"s = {1, 2}\ns.add(2)\ns.add(3)\nprint(len(s))","lang":"python","a":"3","w":["4","2","5"],"why":"Adding 2 again changes nothing, because a set has no repeats. Only 3 is new, so the set has 1, 2, 3.","id":"py.i.11.8"},
        {"t":"check","topic":"Python","q":"Which code makes a tuple with only ONE item?","a":"(5,)","w":["(5)","tuple(5)","{5}"],"why":"The comma makes the tuple. `(5)` is just the number 5 in brackets, and `{5}` is a set.","id":"py.i.11.9"}
      ] },
      { id: "py.i.12", level: "i", n: 12, title: "String methods and f-strings", cards: [
        {"t":"learn","topic":"Python","title":"String methods","body":"Strings have **methods** such as `strip()`, `split()`, `join()` and `replace()`. A string cannot be changed, so each method gives you a **new** string. Save it in a variable if you want to keep it.","code":"name = '  Asha  '\nprint(name.strip())","lang":"python","id":"py.i.12.1"},
        {"t":"output","topic":"Python","code":"line = 'Ravi,Pune,25'\nparts = line.split(',')\nprint(parts[1])","lang":"python","a":"Pune","w":["Ravi","25","Ravi,Pune,25"],"why":"`split(',')` cuts the text at each comma into a list: Ravi, Pune, 25. Index 1 is Pune.","id":"py.i.12.2"},
        {"t":"output","topic":"Python","code":"words = ['Jai', 'Hind']\nprint('-'.join(words))","lang":"python","a":"Jai-Hind","w":["Jai Hind","JaiHind","['Jai', 'Hind']"],"why":"`join` puts the text before the dot between every item of the list. Here that text is `-`.","id":"py.i.12.3"},
        {"t":"output","topic":"Python","code":"price = 12.5\nprint(f'Rs {price:.2f}')","lang":"python","a":"Rs 12.50","w":["Rs 12.5","Rs 12.50000","Rs {price:.2f}"],"why":"`:.2f` means 2 digits after the decimal point, so 12.5 becomes 12.50.","id":"py.i.12.4"},
        {"t":"output","topic":"Python","code":"x = 42\nprint(f'[{x:>5}]')","lang":"python","a":"[   42]","w":["[42   ]","[00042]","[42]"],"why":"`:>5` makes the value 5 characters wide and pushes it to the right. That adds 3 spaces before 42.","id":"py.i.12.5"},
        {"t":"bug","topic":"Python","q":"This program fails with `TypeError: 'str' object does not support item assignment`. Which line is wrong?","code":["word = 'hello'","print(word.upper())","word[0] = 'H'","print(word)"],"bad":2,"why":"A string cannot be changed letter by letter. Make a new string instead, for example `'H' + word[1:]`.","id":"py.i.12.6"},
        {"t":"output","topic":"Python","code":"s = 'banana'\nprint(s.find('n'))\nprint(s.startswith('ban'))\nprint(s.replace('a', 'o'))","lang":"python","a":"2\nTrue\nbonono","w":["1\nTrue\nbonono","2\nFalse\nbonono","2\nTrue\nbonona"],"why":"`find` gives the first index of 'n', which is 2. The text starts with 'ban', and `replace` changes every 'a' to 'o'.","id":"py.i.12.7"},
        {"t":"check","topic":"Python","q":"What does this program print?","code":"s = '  hi  '\ns.strip()\nprint(len(s))","lang":"python","a":"6","w":["2","4","0"],"why":"`s.strip()` gives a new string, but the result is not saved. So `s` is still 6 characters, with the spaces.","id":"py.i.12.8"},
        {"t":"check","topic":"Python","q":"What does this program print?","code":"name = 'Lakshmi'\nage = 30\nprint(f'{name} is {age + 1}')","lang":"python","a":"Lakshmi is 31","w":["Lakshmi is 30","{name} is {age + 1}","Lakshmi is age + 1"],"why":"Inside `{ }` in an f-string, Python can work out an expression. So `age + 1` becomes 31.","id":"py.i.12.9"}
      ] },
      { id: "py.i.13", level: "i", n: 13, title: "Errors and try/except", cards: [
        {"t":"learn","topic":"Python","title":"Reading error messages","body":"When Python crashes it prints a **traceback**. The last line names the error (`NameError`, `TypeError`...) and the line numbers show where. `try:` with `except:` lets you handle an error instead of crashing.","code":"try:\n    num = int(\"12a\")\nexcept ValueError:\n    print(\"Not a number\")","lang":"python","id":"py.i.13.1"},
        {"t":"order","topic":"Python","q":"Your program crashed. Order the steps to read the error and fix it.","items":["Read the last line: error type and message","Find the line number in the traceback","Look at that line and the lines before it","Fix it, then run again"],"why":"The **last line** of a traceback says what went wrong; the line numbers above it say where. Most bugs are on that line or just before it.","id":"py.i.13.2"},
        {"t":"output","topic":"Python","code":"try:\n    print(10 / 0)\nexcept ZeroDivisionError:\n    print('cannot divide')","lang":"python","a":"cannot divide","w":["ZeroDivisionError","0","Nothing is printed"],"why":"Dividing by zero raises `ZeroDivisionError`. The `except` block catches it, so the program does not crash.","id":"py.i.13.3"},
        {"t":"output","topic":"Python","code":"try:\n    n = int('25')\nexcept ValueError:\n    print('bad')\nelse:\n    print('ok', n)\nfinally:\n    print('done')","lang":"python","a":"ok 25\ndone","w":["bad\ndone","ok 25","done\nok 25"],"why":"No error happened, so `else` runs. `finally` runs last, every time.","id":"py.i.13.4"},
        {"t":"output","topic":"Python","code":"try:\n    print('a' + 1)\nexcept ValueError:\n    print('value')\nexcept TypeError:\n    print('type')","lang":"python","a":"type","w":["value","a1","value\ntype"],"why":"Adding text and a number raises `TypeError`. Python uses the first `except` that matches the error type.","id":"py.i.13.5"},
        {"t":"bug","topic":"Python","q":"This program fails with `SyntaxError: expected 'except' or 'finally' block`. Which line is wrong?","code":["try:","    n = int('5')","print(n)"],"bad":2,"why":"A `try` must be followed by an `except` or `finally` block. Here the next line is plain code instead.","id":"py.i.13.6"},
        {"t":"indent","topic":"Python","step":4,"q":"Put the lines in the right place so the program runs.","lines":[[0,"try:"],[4,"n = int('7')"],[0,"except ValueError:"],[4,"print('bad')"],[0,"else:"],[4,"print(n)"]],"why":"`try`, `except` and `else` line up at the left. The code that belongs to each one is indented by 4 spaces.","id":"py.i.13.7"},
        {"t":"choice","topic":"Python","q":"When does the `finally` block run?","a":"Always, whether or not an error happened","w":["Only when an error happens","Only when no error happens","Only when you use `raise`"],"why":"`finally` is for clean-up work that must always happen, like closing a file.","id":"py.i.13.8"},
        {"t":"check","topic":"Python","q":"What does this program print?","code":"data = {'n': 1}\ntry:\n    print(data['m'])\nexcept KeyError:\n    print('missing')\nprint('end')","lang":"python","a":"missing\nend","w":["missing","end","KeyError\nend"],"why":"The key 'm' is not in the dictionary, so `KeyError` is raised and caught. The program then goes on to print `end`.","id":"py.i.13.9"},
        {"t":"check","topic":"Python","q":"Which line raises an error on purpose?","a":"raise ValueError('bad')","w":["throw ValueError('bad')","error ValueError('bad')","except ValueError('bad')"],"why":"Python uses `raise` to create an error. `except` is used to catch an error, not to make one.","id":"py.i.13.10"}
      ] },
      { id: "py.i.14", level: "i", n: 14, title: "Files (with open)", cards: [
        {"t":"learn","topic":"Python","title":"Files with open","body":"`open()` opens a file. Use it in a `with` block so Python closes the file for you. Mode `\"r\"` reads, `\"w\"` writes (replacing the file) and `\"a\"` adds to the end.","code":"with open(\"notes.txt\", \"w\") as f:\n    f.write(\"hello\")","lang":"python","id":"py.i.14.1"},
        {"t":"choice","topic":"Python","q":"Which line opens a file so that Python closes it automatically?","a":"with open(\"data.txt\") as f:","w":["open(\"data.txt\").close(f)","f = close(\"data.txt\")","import file(\"data.txt\")"],"why":"A `with` block closes the file for you when the block ends, even if an error happens inside.","id":"py.i.14.2"},
        {"t":"output","topic":"Python","code":"with open('f1.txt', 'w') as f:\n    f.write('Hello')\nwith open('f1.txt') as f:\n    print(f.read())","lang":"python","a":"Hello","w":["f1.txt","w","Nothing is printed"],"why":"The first `with` writes 'Hello' into the file. The second one opens it for reading, and `read()` gives back the text.","id":"py.i.14.3"},
        {"t":"output","topic":"Python","code":"with open('f2.txt', 'w') as f:\n    f.write('one\\ntwo\\nthree\\n')\nwith open('f2.txt') as f:\n    lines = f.readlines()\nprint(len(lines))\nprint(repr(lines[0]))","lang":"python","a":"3\n'one\\n'","w":["3\n'one'","1\n'one'","3\n'one\\ntwo'"],"why":"`readlines()` gives one list item per line, and each item still ends with `\\n`. Use `strip()` to remove it.","id":"py.i.14.4"},
        {"t":"output","topic":"Python","code":"with open('f3.txt', 'w') as f:\n    f.write('A\\n')\nwith open('f3.txt', 'a') as f:\n    f.write('B\\n')\nwith open('f3.txt') as f:\n    for line in f:\n        print(line.strip())","lang":"python","a":"A\nB","w":["B","A","A\n\nB"],"why":"Mode `'a'` adds to the end of the file. Looping over the file gives each line, and `strip()` removes the `\\n` at the end.","id":"py.i.14.5"},
        {"t":"bug","topic":"Python","q":"This program fails with `io.UnsupportedOperation: not readable`. Which line is wrong?","code":["with open('f5.txt', 'w') as f:","    f.write('hi')","with open('f5.txt', 'w') as f:","    print(f.read())"],"bad":3,"why":"Mode `'w'` is for writing only. To read a file, open it with `'r'`, which is the default.","id":"py.i.14.6"},
        {"t":"indent","topic":"Python","step":4,"q":"Put the lines in the right place so the program runs.","lines":[[0,"with open('f6.txt', 'w') as f:"],[4,"f.write('Asha\\n')"],[4,"f.write('Ravi\\n')"],[0,"print('saved')"]],"why":"The two `write` lines belong inside the `with` block, so they are indented. `print` is after the block, so it is not indented.","id":"py.i.14.7"},
        {"t":"choice","topic":"Python","q":"Which mode adds new text at the end of a file without erasing it?","a":"'a'","w":["'w'","'r'","'rb'"],"why":"`'a'` means append. `'w'` erases the old content first and starts a new file.","id":"py.i.14.8"},
        {"t":"check","topic":"Python","q":"What does this program print?","code":"with open('f7.txt', 'w') as f:\n    f.write('x\\n')\nwith open('f7.txt', 'w') as f:\n    f.write('a\\n')\nwith open('f7.txt') as f:\n    print(f.read().strip())","lang":"python","a":"a","w":["x","xa","x\na"],"why":"Opening with `'w'` a second time erases the old content. Only 'a' is left in the file.","id":"py.i.14.9"},
        {"t":"check","topic":"Python","q":"Why do we write `with open(...) as f:`?","a":"The file is closed automatically, even if an error happens","w":["It makes the file read faster","It lets two programs write to the file at once","It stops the file from being deleted"],"why":"The `with` block closes the file for you when it ends. You do not need to call `close()`.","id":"py.i.14.10"}
      ] },
      { id: "py.i.15", level: "i", n: 15, title: "Modules and pip", cards: [
        {"t":"learn","topic":"Python","title":"Modules and pip","body":"A **module** is a file of ready-made code. Use `import math` to use it, or `from math import sqrt` to take just one tool. To get a new package from the internet, run `pip install name` in the terminal.","code":"import math\nprint(math.sqrt(16))","lang":"python","id":"py.i.15.1"},
        {"t":"output","topic":"Python","code":"import math\nprint(math.sqrt(16))\nprint(math.floor(3.7))","lang":"python","a":"4.0\n3","w":["4\n3","4.0\n4","4\n3.7"],"why":"`sqrt` always gives a float, so it prints 4.0. `floor` rounds down to the whole number 3.","id":"py.i.15.2"},
        {"t":"output","topic":"Python","code":"from math import pi as p\nprint(round(p, 2))","lang":"python","a":"3.14","w":["3.1","3.142","p"],"why":"`as p` gives the name `pi` a short name `p`. `round(p, 2)` keeps 2 digits after the point.","id":"py.i.15.3"},
        {"t":"bug","topic":"Python","q":"This program fails with `NameError: name 'sqrt' is not defined`. Which line is wrong?","code":["import math","print(math.ceil(2.1))","print(sqrt(9))"],"bad":2,"why":"After `import math`, you must write `math.sqrt`. Plain `sqrt` works only if you wrote `from math import sqrt`.","id":"py.i.15.4"},
        {"t":"output","topic":"Python","code":"import os\nprint(os.path.splitext('report.pdf'))","lang":"python","a":"('report', '.pdf')","w":["('report.pdf', '')","['report', 'pdf']","report"],"why":"`os.path.splitext` splits a file name into the name and the extension. It gives a tuple.","id":"py.i.15.5"},
        {"t":"choice","topic":"Python","q":"Which command installs the package `requests` from the terminal?","a":"pip install requests","w":["import requests","python install requests","pip get requests"],"why":"`pip install` downloads a package. `import` is Python code that you write inside your program, after the package is installed.","id":"py.i.15.6"},
        {"t":"choice","topic":"Python","q":"What does a `requirements.txt` file hold?","a":"A list of packages the project needs, one per line","w":["A copy of all of Python's built-in modules","The password for pip","The source code of your program"],"why":"Anyone can install all of them with `pip install -r requirements.txt`.","id":"py.i.15.7"},
        {"t":"choice","topic":"Python","q":"Which built-in module helps you make random numbers?","a":"random","w":["math","os","sys"],"why":"The `random` module has tools such as `randint` and `choice`. `math` is for maths like `sqrt`.","id":"py.i.15.8"},
        {"t":"check","topic":"Python","q":"What does this program print?","code":"import math as m\nprint(m.sqrt(25) + m.floor(2.9))","lang":"python","a":"7.0","w":["7","5.0","7.9"],"why":"`sqrt(25)` is 5.0 and `floor(2.9)` is 2. Adding a float and an int gives the float 7.0.","id":"py.i.15.9"},
        {"t":"check","topic":"Python","q":"Which line imports only `sqrt`, so you can write `sqrt(4)` directly?","a":"from math import sqrt","w":["import sqrt from math","import math.sqrt","import sqrt"],"why":"The form is `from module import name`. Then you use the name without `math.` in front.","id":"py.i.15.10"}
      ] },
      { id: "py.i.16", level: "i", n: 16, title: "List comprehensions", cards: [
        {"t":"learn","topic":"Python","title":"List comprehensions","body":"A **list comprehension** builds a new list in one line. Write `[value for item in list]`. Add `if` at the end to keep only some items.","code":"squares = [n * n for n in range(1, 5)]\nprint(squares)","lang":"python","id":"py.i.16.1"},
        {"t":"output","topic":"Python","code":"print([n * 2 for n in range(4)])","lang":"python","a":"[0, 2, 4, 6]","w":["[2, 4, 6, 8]","[0, 1, 2, 3]","[0, 2, 4, 6, 8]"],"why":"`range(4)` gives 0, 1, 2, 3. Each number is doubled, so we get 0, 2, 4, 6.","id":"py.i.16.2"},
        {"t":"output","topic":"Python","code":"marks = [35, 72, 48, 90]\npass_marks = [m for m in marks if m >= 50]\nprint(pass_marks)","lang":"python","a":"[72, 90]","w":["[35, 48]","[35, 72, 48, 90]","[True, True]"],"why":"The `if` keeps only marks that are 50 or more: 72 and 90.","id":"py.i.16.3"},
        {"t":"choice","topic":"Python","q":"Which line gives the length of every word in the list `words`?","a":"[len(w) for w in words]","w":["[w.len() for w in words]","[len for w in words]","len(w) for w in words"],"why":"Use `len(w)` as the value, then `for w in words`. Put it all inside square brackets to make a list.","id":"py.i.16.4"},
        {"t":"bug","topic":"Python","q":"This program crashes with a NameError. Which line is wrong?","code":["words = [\"a\", \"bb\"]","lengths = [len(w) for w in word]","print(lengths)"],"bad":1,"why":"The list is called `words`, but line 2 loops over `word`. Python does not know `word`.","id":"py.i.16.5"},
        {"t":"order","topic":"Python","q":"Order the parts inside the brackets of a list comprehension.","items":["the value, like n * n","for n in nums","if n > 0"],"why":"First the value to keep, then the loop, and last the optional `if` filter.","id":"py.i.16.6"},
        {"t":"output","topic":"Python","code":"lengths = {name: len(name) for name in [\"Asha\", \"Ravi\"]}\nprint(lengths)","lang":"python","a":"{'Asha': 4, 'Ravi': 4}","w":["['Asha', 'Ravi']","{'Asha': 'Asha', 'Ravi': 'Ravi'}","{4: 4}"],"why":"A **dict comprehension** uses curly brackets and `key: value`. Each name becomes a key, and its length becomes the value.","id":"py.i.16.7"},
        {"t":"check","topic":"Python","q":"What does this program print?","code":"print(len([n for n in range(10) if n % 3 == 0]))","lang":"python","a":"4","w":["3","10","9"],"why":"The numbers 0, 3, 6 and 9 are divisible by 3. That is 4 items, so `len` gives 4.","id":"py.i.16.8"},
        {"t":"check","topic":"Python","q":"Where does the `if` go when you want to keep only positive numbers?","a":"[n for n in nums if n > 0]","w":["[if n > 0 n for n in nums]","[n for n in nums] if n > 0","[n for n if n > 0 in nums]"],"why":"The filter `if` goes at the end, after the `for` part, inside the brackets.","id":"py.i.16.9"}
      ] },
      { id: "py.i.17", level: "i", n: 17, title: "Default args, *args, **kwargs", cards: [
        {"t":"learn","topic":"Python","title":"Default values","body":"A **default argument** is used when the caller gives no value. Callers can also use **keyword arguments**, like `city=\"Pune\"`, so the order does not matter. Parameters with defaults must come after those without.","code":"def greet(name, city=\"Guntur\"):\n    print(name, \"from\", city)\ngreet(\"Asha\")","lang":"python","id":"py.i.17.1"},
        {"t":"output","topic":"Python","code":"def greet(name, city=\"Guntur\"):\n    print(name, \"from\", city)\ngreet(\"Asha\")\ngreet(\"Ravi\", \"Pune\")","lang":"python","a":"Asha from Guntur\nRavi from Pune","w":["Asha from Pune\nRavi from Pune","Asha from Guntur\nRavi from Guntur","Asha\nRavi from Pune"],"why":"The first call has no city, so the default \"Guntur\" is used. The second call gives \"Pune\", which replaces the default.","id":"py.i.17.2"},
        {"t":"output","topic":"Python","code":"def show(a, b):\n    print(a, \"-\", b)\nshow(b=1, a=2)","lang":"python","a":"2 - 1","w":["1 - 2","b - a","2 - 2"],"why":"With keyword arguments, Python matches by name, not by position. So `a` is 2 and `b` is 1.","id":"py.i.17.3"},
        {"t":"output","topic":"Python","code":"def total(*nums):\n    return sum(nums)\nprint(total(10, 20, 30))","lang":"python","a":"60","w":["10","30","(10, 20, 30)"],"why":"`*nums` collects all extra values into a tuple. `sum` adds 10, 20 and 30 to give 60.","id":"py.i.17.4"},
        {"t":"output","topic":"Python","code":"def info(**kw):\n    for k in sorted(kw):\n        print(k, kw[k])\ninfo(name=\"Asha\", age=20)","lang":"python","a":"age 20\nname Asha","w":["name Asha\nage 20","name age","{'name': 'Asha', 'age': 20}"],"why":"`**kw` collects keyword arguments into a dictionary. We loop over the sorted keys, so \"age\" comes before \"name\".","id":"py.i.17.5"},
        {"t":"output","topic":"Python","code":"def add(x, items=[]):\n    items.append(x)\n    return items\nprint(add(1))\nprint(add(2))","lang":"python","a":"[1]\n[1, 2]","w":["[1]\n[2]","[1, 2]\n[1, 2]","[1]\n[1]"],"why":"The default list is made only once, when the function is defined. Both calls share it. This is the **mutable default trap**.","id":"py.i.17.6"},
        {"t":"bug","topic":"Python","q":"This program fails with a SyntaxError. Which line is wrong?","code":["def f(a, b=1, c):","    return a + b + c","print(f(1, 2, 3))"],"bad":0,"why":"A parameter with a default (`b=1`) cannot come before one without a default (`c`).","id":"py.i.17.7"},
        {"t":"choice","topic":"Python","q":"How do you fix the mutable default trap in `def add(x, items=[])`?","a":"Use `items=None`, then make a new list inside if `items is None`","w":["Use `items=[]` but call the function only once","Write `items=list` as the default","Remove the parameter name `items`"],"why":"`None` is safe. A new empty list is created inside the function on every call, so calls do not share data.","id":"py.i.17.8"},
        {"t":"check","topic":"Python","q":"What does this program print?","code":"def count(*args):\n    return len(args)\nprint(count(1, \"a\", None))","lang":"python","a":"3","w":["1","0","(1, \"a\", None)"],"why":"`*args` collects all three values into one tuple. Its length is 3.","id":"py.i.17.9"},
        {"t":"check","topic":"Python","q":"Inside `def f(**kwargs)`, what type is `kwargs`?","a":"A dictionary","w":["A list","A tuple","A string"],"why":"`**kwargs` gathers the keyword arguments as names and values in a dictionary. `*args` gives a tuple.","id":"py.i.17.10"}
      ] },
      { id: "py.i.18", level: "i", n: 18, title: "Classes and objects", cards: [
        {"t":"learn","topic":"Python","title":"Classes and objects","body":"A **class** is a blueprint. An **object** is made from it, like `Dog(\"Tommy\")`. `__init__` runs when the object is created, and **self** means this object, so `self.name` is its own data.","code":"class Dog:\n    def __init__(self, name):\n        self.name = name\nd = Dog(\"Tommy\")\nprint(d.name)","lang":"python","id":"py.i.18.1"},
        {"t":"output","topic":"Python","code":"class Cart:\n    def __init__(self):\n        self.items = []\nc = Cart()\nc.items.append(\"rice\")\nprint(len(c.items))","lang":"python","a":"1","w":["0","4","rice"],"why":"`__init__` gives the cart an empty list. We add one item, so the length is 1.","id":"py.i.18.2"},
        {"t":"output","topic":"Python","code":"class Account:\n    def __init__(self, balance):\n        self.balance = balance\na, b = Account(100), Account(500)\na.balance += 50\nprint(a.balance, b.balance)","lang":"python","a":"150 500","w":["150 550","100 500","650 500"],"why":"Each object keeps its own data. Changing `a` does not change `b`.","id":"py.i.18.3"},
        {"t":"output","topic":"Python","code":"class Pet:\n    def __init__(self, name):\n        self.name = name\n    def __str__(self):\n        return \"Pet: \" + self.name\nprint(Pet(\"Mani\"))","lang":"python","a":"Pet: Mani","w":["Mani","<__main__.Pet object>","Pet"],"why":"`print` calls `__str__` to get the text to show. Without it, you would see an unhelpful object address.","id":"py.i.18.4"},
        {"t":"output","topic":"Python","code":"class Animal:\n    def eat(self):\n        return \"eats\"\nclass Cow(Animal):\n    pass\nprint(Cow().eat())","lang":"python","a":"eats","w":["Cow","Animal","None"],"why":"`Cow(Animal)` means Cow **inherits** from Animal. So Cow gets the `eat` method for free.","id":"py.i.18.5"},
        {"t":"bug","topic":"Python","q":"This program crashes with a TypeError. Which line is wrong?","code":["class Dog:","    def bark():","        print(\"woof\")","Dog().bark()"],"bad":3,"why":"Python passes the object as the first argument, so `bark` needs `self`. The error appears when the method is called on line 4.","id":"py.i.18.6"},
        {"t":"indent","topic":"Python","step":4,"q":"Indent the lines so the class works.","lines":[[0,"class Dog:"],[4,"def __init__(self, name):"],[8,"self.name = name"],[0,"d = Dog(\"Tommy\")"],[0,"print(d.name)"]],"why":"The method is inside the class (4 spaces). Its body is inside the method (8 spaces). The last two lines are outside the class.","id":"py.i.18.7"},
        {"t":"choice","topic":"Python","q":"In a method, what does `self` mean?","a":"The object that is calling the method","w":["The class name","The name of the file","A special word that must be left out when calling"],"why":"`self` is the object itself. Python fills it in for you when you write `d.bark()`.","id":"py.i.18.8"},
        {"t":"check","topic":"Python","q":"What does this program print?","code":"class Rect:\n    def __init__(self, w, h):\n        self.w, self.h = w, h\n    def area(self):\n        return self.w * self.h\nprint(Rect(3, 4).area())","lang":"python","a":"12","w":["7","34","(3, 4)"],"why":"`area` multiplies width and height: 3 times 4 is 12.","id":"py.i.18.9"},
        {"t":"check","topic":"Python","q":"Which method runs automatically when you create a new object?","a":"__init__","w":["__str__","__start__","__create__"],"why":"`__init__` is called right after the object is made. It sets up the starting data. `__str__` is for printing.","id":"py.i.18.10"}
      ] },
      { id: "py.i.19", level: "i", n: 19, title: "Virtual environments", cards: [
        {"t":"learn","topic":"Python","title":"Virtual environments","body":"A **virtual environment** (venv) is a private folder of packages for one project. Create it with `python -m venv .venv`, then **activate** it. After that, `pip install` puts packages only inside it.","code":"python -m venv .venv\nsource .venv/bin/activate   # Linux\n.venv\\Scripts\\Activate.ps1   # Windows PowerShell\npip install requests","lang":"shell","id":"py.i.19.1"},
        {"t":"choice","topic":"Python","q":"Why do we use a virtual environment for each project?","a":"Each project keeps its own packages and versions, so they do not clash","w":["It makes Python run faster","It lets us run Python without installing it","It saves the program on the internet"],"why":"Different projects may need different versions of the same package. A venv keeps them apart.","id":"py.i.19.2"},
        {"t":"choice","topic":"Python","q":"Which command creates a virtual environment in a folder named `.venv`?","a":"python -m venv .venv","w":["python venv create .venv","pip venv .venv","python -m pip .venv"],"why":"`python -m venv` runs the built-in venv module. The last word is the folder name.","id":"py.i.19.3"},
        {"t":"choice","topic":"Python","q":"On Linux, which command activates the venv in `.venv`?","a":"source .venv/bin/activate","w":[".venv/Scripts/Activate.ps1","python activate .venv","pip activate .venv"],"why":"On Linux and macOS, the activate script is in `.venv/bin/`. `Scripts` is the Windows folder.","id":"py.i.19.4"},
        {"t":"choice","topic":"Python","q":"How can you see that a venv is active?","a":"The venv name, like `(.venv)`, shows at the start of the prompt","w":["The terminal turns green","Python prints \"venv on\" every time","A new window opens"],"why":"Activation adds the environment name in brackets before your prompt.","id":"py.i.19.5"},
        {"t":"choice","topic":"Python","q":"Which command saves the list of installed packages into a file?","a":"pip freeze > requirements.txt","w":["pip save requirements.txt","python -m venv requirements.txt","pip install > requirements.txt"],"why":"`pip freeze` prints the installed packages with versions. `>` sends that text into the file.","id":"py.i.19.6"},
        {"t":"order","topic":"Python","q":"Order the steps to start a new project with a venv.","items":["Create the venv","Activate it","Install packages with pip","Run your program"],"why":"You must create and activate the venv before installing, so the packages go inside it.","id":"py.i.19.7"},
        {"t":"check","topic":"Python","q":"Project A needs version 1 of a package. Project B needs version 2. What solves this?","a":"Use a separate venv for each project","w":["Install both versions in the main Python","Rename the package","Use one venv for both projects"],"why":"Each venv has its own copy of packages, so each project gets the version it needs.","id":"py.i.19.8"},
        {"t":"check","topic":"Python","q":"A venv is active. You run `pip install requests`. Where does it install?","a":"Only inside that venv","w":["For every project on the computer","In the Windows or Linux system folders","Nowhere, until you restart"],"why":"While a venv is active, `pip` belongs to it, so packages stay inside that project.","id":"py.i.19.9"}
      ] },
      { id: "py.i.20", level: "i", n: 20, title: "JSON and CSV", cards: [
        {"t":"learn","topic":"Python","title":"JSON and CSV","body":"**JSON** is text for data. `json.dumps(data)` turns Python data into JSON text, and `json.loads(text)` turns it back. **CSV** is text for tables, with commas between values. Use the `csv` module to read it.","code":"import json\ndata = {\"name\": \"Asha\", \"age\": 20}\ntext = json.dumps(data)\nprint(text)","lang":"python","id":"py.i.20.1"},
        {"t":"output","topic":"Python","code":"import json\nprint(json.dumps({\"a\": 1, \"b\": [1, 2]}))","lang":"python","a":"{\"a\": 1, \"b\": [1, 2]}","w":["{'a': 1, 'b': [1, 2]}","{a: 1, b: [1, 2]}","[\"a\", \"b\"]"],"why":"JSON text always uses double quotes. `dumps` returns a string, and `print` shows it.","id":"py.i.20.2"},
        {"t":"output","topic":"Python","code":"import json\ndata = json.loads('{\"city\": \"Pune\", \"pop\": 7}')\nprint(data[\"city\"])","lang":"python","a":"Pune","w":["\"city\"","{\"city\": \"Pune\"}","7"],"why":"`loads` turns the JSON text into a Python dictionary. Then `data[\"city\"]` gives \"Pune\".","id":"py.i.20.3"},
        {"t":"output","topic":"Python","code":"import json\nprint(json.dumps({\"ok\": True, \"x\": None}))","lang":"python","a":"{\"ok\": true, \"x\": null}","w":["{\"ok\": True, \"x\": None}","{\"ok\": \"True\", \"x\": \"None\"}","{\"ok\": 1, \"x\": 0}"],"why":"JSON writes `true`, `false` and `null` in small letters. Python `True` and `None` become these.","id":"py.i.20.4"},
        {"t":"output","topic":"Python","code":"import json\nwith open(\"d.json\", \"w\") as f:\n    json.dump([1, 2], f)\nwith open(\"d.json\") as f:\n    print(json.load(f))","lang":"python","a":"[1, 2]","w":["\"[1, 2]\"","d.json","[1, 2]\n[1, 2]"],"why":"`dump` and `load` (no s) work with files. We write the list, read it back, and print it.","id":"py.i.20.5"},
        {"t":"output","topic":"Python","code":"import csv\nrows = [\"name,marks\", \"Asha,90\"]\nfor row in csv.reader(rows):\n    print(row)","lang":"python","a":"['name', 'marks']\n['Asha', '90']","w":["['name,marks']\n['Asha,90']","['name', 'marks']\n['Asha', 90]","name marks\nAsha 90"],"why":"`csv.reader` splits each line at the commas into a list. All values are strings, so 90 is `'90'`.","id":"py.i.20.6"},
        {"t":"bug","topic":"Python","q":"This program fails with a JSONDecodeError. Which line is wrong?","code":["import json","text = \"{'a': 1}\"","data = json.loads(text)","print(data)"],"bad":2,"why":"Valid JSON needs double quotes. The text uses single quotes, so `loads` on line 3 fails.","id":"py.i.20.7"},
        {"t":"check","topic":"Python","q":"What does this program print?","code":"import csv\nrows = [\"name,marks\", \"Asha,90\"]\nfor row in csv.DictReader(rows):\n    print(row[\"name\"], int(row[\"marks\"]) + 5)","lang":"python","a":"Asha 95","w":["Asha 90","name 95","Asha 905"],"why":"`DictReader` uses the first line as keys. `int` turns \"90\" into a number, and adding 5 gives 95.","id":"py.i.20.8"},
        {"t":"check","topic":"Python","q":"Which function turns a JSON string into Python data?","a":"json.loads","w":["json.dumps","json.parse","json.text"],"why":"`loads` means \"load from string\". `dumps` goes the other way, from Python data to a string.","id":"py.i.20.9"}
      ] },
      { id: "py.a.21", level: "a", n: 21, title: "Iterators and generators", cards: [
        {"t":"learn","topic":"Python","title":"Iterators and generators","body":"An **iterator** gives one value at a time with `next()`, and raises `StopIteration` when it is finished. A function with **`yield`** is a **generator**: it is lazy, so it makes a value only when asked, and it can be used only once.","code":"def count_up(n):\n    for i in range(1, n + 1):\n        yield i\nfor x in count_up(3):\n    print(x)","lang":"python","id":"py.a.21.1"},
        {"t":"choice","topic":"Python","q":"`count_up` has `yield` in it. Asha writes `g = count_up(3)`. When does the body of `count_up` start to run?","a":"At the first `next(g)` (or the first loop step)","w":["At once, when `count_up(3)` is called","When Python imports the file","After all values are stored in a list"],"why":"Calling a generator function only creates the generator. The body runs step by step, each time a value is asked for.","id":"py.a.21.2"},
        {"t":"output","topic":"Python","q":"What does this program print?","lang":"python","code":"def gen():\n    yield 1\n    yield 2\ng = gen()\nprint(sum(g))\nprint(sum(g))","a":"3\n0","w":["3\n3","3\n[]","6\n0"],"why":"A generator can be used only once. After the first `sum(g)` it is used up, so the second `sum(g)` adds nothing and gives 0.","id":"py.a.21.3"},
        {"t":"bug","topic":"Python","q":"The list has only two items, and the program crashes with `StopIteration`. Which line crashes? (Its fix gives a default value.)","code":["it = iter([1, 2])","print(next(it))","print(next(it))","print(next(it))"],"lang":"python","bad":3,"why":"`next(it, default)` returns the default instead of raising `StopIteration` when the iterator is finished.","fix":"print(next(it, \"done\"))","id":"py.a.21.4"},
        {"t":"order","topic":"Python","q":"Put these lines in the right order to read a big file lazily, one line at a time.","items":["def read_lines(path):","with open(path) as f:","for line in f:","yield line.strip()"],"why":"Define the function, open the file, loop over its lines, and `yield` each cleaned line. Only one line is in memory at a time.","id":"py.a.21.5"},
        {"t":"output","topic":"Python","q":"What does this program print?","lang":"python","code":"def outer():\n    yield 0\n    yield from [1, 2]\n    yield 3\nprint(*outer())","a":"0 1 2 3","w":["0 [1, 2] 3","0 3","0 1 2"],"why":"`yield from` hands out every item of another iterable, one by one. It does not yield the list as one item.","id":"py.a.21.6"},
        {"t":"indent","topic":"Python","step":4,"q":"Fix the indentation: a generator that gives the even numbers below `n`.","lang":"python","lines":[[0,"def evens(n):"],[4,"for i in range(n):"],[8,"if i % 2 == 0:"],[12,"yield i"],[0,"print(list(evens(7)))"]],"why":"Each level inside `def`, `for` and `if` goes 4 spaces deeper. The `print` is back at the left edge.","id":"py.a.21.7"},
        {"t":"check","topic":"Python","q":"What does this program print?","lang":"python","code":"gen = (n * 2 for n in range(3))\nprint(next(gen))\nprint(next(gen))","a":"0\n2","w":["2\n4","0\n1","0\n4"],"why":"A generator expression gives values lazily. The first two values of `n * 2` are 0 and 2.","id":"py.a.21.8"},
        {"t":"check","topic":"Python","q":"Ravi must read a very big log file line by line. Why is a generator a good fit?","a":"It gives one line at a time, so the whole file is never in memory","w":["It makes the disk read faster","It reads the file in many threads","It sorts the lines for you"],"why":"A generator is lazy: it makes the next value only when asked. That is how big files can be handled with little memory.","id":"py.a.21.9"}
      ] },
      { id: "py.a.22", level: "a", n: 22, title: "Decorators", cards: [
        {"t":"learn","topic":"Python","title":"Decorators","body":"In Python a function is an object, so you can pass it to another function. A **decorator** takes a function and returns a new **wrapper** function. Writing `@shout` above `def hi():` means `hi = shout(hi)`.","code":"def shout(fn):\n    def wrapper():\n        return fn().upper()\n    return wrapper","lang":"python","id":"py.a.22.1"},
        {"t":"choice","topic":"Python","q":"Lakshmi writes `@shout` on the line above `def hi():`. What does Python do with it?","a":"It runs `hi = shout(hi)` after the function is defined","w":["It runs `hi = hi(shout)`","It runs `shout` every time the file is saved","It copies the code of `shout` into `hi`"],"why":"A decorator line is a short way to write `hi = shout(hi)`. The name `hi` then points to the wrapper that `shout` returned.","id":"py.a.22.2"},
        {"t":"output","topic":"Python","q":"What does this program print?","lang":"python","code":"def log(fn):\n    def wrapper():\n        return fn()\n    return wrapper\n@log\ndef pay():\n    return 1\nprint(pay.__name__)","a":"wrapper","w":["pay","log","pay.wrapper"],"why":"After decorating, the name `pay` points to `wrapper`, so it shows the wrapper's name. `functools.wraps` fixes this.","id":"py.a.22.3"},
        {"t":"choice","topic":"Python","q":"What does `@functools.wraps(fn)` do when you put it on the wrapper function?","a":"It copies the name and docstring of `fn` onto the wrapper","w":["It makes the function run faster","It lets the wrapper accept any arguments","It stops the function being called twice"],"why":"`wraps` keeps `__name__` and `__doc__` of the original function. Accepting any arguments is the job of `*args, **kwargs`, not `wraps`.","id":"py.a.22.4"},
        {"t":"bug","topic":"Python","q":"Python raises `TypeError: shout() missing 1 required positional argument: 'fn'`. Which line is wrong?","code":["def shout(fn):","    def wrapper():","        return fn().upper()","    return wrapper","@shout()","def hi():","    return \"hi\""],"lang":"python","bad":4,"why":"Without brackets, `@shout` passes the function to `shout`. With `@shout()` Python calls `shout` with no argument first, which fails.","fix":"@shout","id":"py.a.22.5"},
        {"t":"order","topic":"Python","q":"Put the lines of a decorator in the right order. It must work for any function arguments.","items":["def timer(fn):","def wrapper(*args, **kwargs):","return fn(*args, **kwargs)","return wrapper"],"why":"The outer function receives `fn`. The wrapper takes any arguments and calls `fn`. The outer function returns the wrapper itself, without brackets.","id":"py.a.22.6"},
        {"t":"output","topic":"Python","q":"Two decorators are stacked. What does this program print?","lang":"python","code":"def tag_a(fn):\n    return lambda: \"A\" + fn()\ndef tag_b(fn):\n    return lambda: \"B\" + fn()\n@tag_a\n@tag_b\ndef f():\n    return \"x\"\nprint(f())","a":"ABx","w":["BAx","xAB","Ax"],"why":"The decorator nearest the function runs first. So `f = tag_a(tag_b(f))`, and the outer `tag_a` adds its letter at the front.","id":"py.a.22.7"},
        {"t":"indent","topic":"Python","step":4,"q":"Fix the indentation: a decorator that calls the function two times, one inside the other.","lang":"python","lines":[[0,"def twice(fn):"],[4,"def wrapper(x):"],[8,"return fn(fn(x))"],[4,"return wrapper"],[0,"@twice"],[0,"def inc(x):"],[4,"return x + 1"],[0,"print(inc(1))"]],"why":"`wrapper` is inside `twice`, so it is 4 spaces in. `return wrapper` belongs to `twice`, not to `wrapper`.","id":"py.a.22.8"},
        {"t":"check","topic":"Python","q":"What does this program print?","lang":"python","code":"def doubled(fn):\n    def wrapper(*args, **kwargs):\n        return fn(*args, **kwargs) * 2\n    return wrapper\n@doubled\ndef add(x, y):\n    return x + y\nprint(add(2, 3))","a":"10","w":["5","6","TypeError"],"why":"`add(2, 3)` calls the wrapper. The wrapper passes both arguments on, gets 5, and doubles it to 10.","id":"py.a.22.9"},
        {"t":"check","topic":"Python","q":"`repeat` is a decorator that takes an argument. What does `@repeat(3)` do?","a":"Calls `repeat(3)` first; the result is the real decorator for the function","w":["Passes 3 as the first argument of the decorated function","Wraps the function 3 separate times","Runs `repeat` only when the function is first called"],"why":"A decorator with arguments is a function that returns a decorator. Python first evaluates `repeat(3)`, then applies the result to the function.","id":"py.a.22.10"}
      ] },
      { id: "py.a.23", level: "a", n: 23, title: "Context managers", cards: [
        {"t":"learn","topic":"Python","title":"Context managers","body":"A **context manager** sets something up and cleans it up. `with open(...) as f:` closes the file when the block ends, **even if an error happens**. It works because the object has `__enter__` and `__exit__` methods.","code":"with open(\"notes.txt\") as f:\n    data = f.read()\n# the file is closed here","lang":"python","id":"py.a.23.1"},
        {"t":"output","topic":"Python","q":"What does this program print?","lang":"python","code":"class Box:\n    def __enter__(self):\n        print(\"open\")\n        return self\n    def __exit__(self, *exc):\n        print(\"close\")\nwith Box():\n    print(\"work\")","a":"open\nwork\nclose","w":["work\nopen\nclose","open\nclose\nwork","open\nwork"],"why":"`__enter__` runs when the block starts, then the body, then `__exit__` when the block ends.","id":"py.a.23.2"},
        {"t":"output","topic":"Python","q":"What does this program print?","lang":"python","code":"from contextlib import suppress\nwith suppress(ZeroDivisionError):\n    print(1 / 0)\n    print(\"never\")\nprint(\"done\")","a":"done","w":["never\ndone","ZeroDivisionError","never"],"why":"`suppress` ignores the named error and leaves the block. The line after the error is skipped, and the program carries on.","id":"py.a.23.3"},
        {"t":"bug","topic":"Python","q":"Python raises `ValueError: I/O operation on closed file`. Which line is wrong?","code":["import io","with io.StringIO() as f:","    f.write(\"hi\")","f.write(\"bye\")"],"lang":"python","bad":3,"why":"After the block ends, `__exit__` has closed `f`. Do all the work inside the block by indenting the line.","fix":"    f.write(\"bye\")","id":"py.a.23.4"},
        {"t":"order","topic":"Python","q":"Put these in the order they happen for `with Box() as b:`.","items":["`Box()` creates the context manager","`__enter__` runs","Its return value is stored in `b`","The body of the block runs","`__exit__` runs"],"why":"Set-up comes first, then your code, then clean-up. `b` gets what `__enter__` returns, and `__exit__` runs even if the body raised an error.","id":"py.a.23.5"},
        {"t":"indent","topic":"Python","step":4,"q":"Fix the indentation: a context manager written with `contextlib`. It prints before and after the body.","lang":"python","lines":[[0,"from contextlib import contextmanager"],[0,"@contextmanager"],[0,"def tag():"],[4,"print(\"start\")"],[4,"yield"],[4,"print(\"end\")"],[0,"with tag():"],[4,"print(\"body\")"]],"why":"Code before `yield` is the set-up, the `with` body runs at the `yield`, and code after it is the clean-up. Put the `yield` in `try`/`finally` if the clean-up must run even after an error.","id":"py.a.23.6"},
        {"t":"choice","topic":"Python","q":"Which line opens `a.txt` and `b.txt` in one `with` statement?","a":"`with open(\"a.txt\") as a, open(\"b.txt\") as b:`","w":["`with open(\"a.txt\") as a and open(\"b.txt\") as b:`","`with open(\"a.txt\", \"b.txt\") as a, b:`","`with open(\"a.txt\") as a; open(\"b.txt\") as b:`"],"why":"Separate the managers with a comma. Each one has its own `as` name, and both are closed at the end of the block.","id":"py.a.23.7"},
        {"t":"check","topic":"Python","q":"What does this program print?","lang":"python","code":"from contextlib import contextmanager\n@contextmanager\ndef shout():\n    yield \"namaste\"\nwith shout() as word:\n    print(word.upper())","a":"NAMASTE","w":["namaste","None","SHOUT"],"why":"The value after `yield` is given to the name after `as`. So `word` is \"namaste\".","id":"py.a.23.8"},
        {"t":"check","topic":"Python","q":"Which code does the same job as `with open(p) as f: data = f.read()`?","a":"`f = open(p)`, then `try:` read the file, `finally:` `f.close()`","w":["`f = open(p)`, read the file, then `f.close()` on the next line, with no `try`","`f = open(p)` and let Python close it some day","`try:` open and read the file, `except:` `f.close()`"],"why":"`finally` always runs, so the file is closed even after an error. `except` runs only when there is an error, and a plain `close()` is skipped if reading fails.","id":"py.a.23.9"}
      ] },
      { id: "py.a.24", level: "a", n: 24, title: "Type hints", cards: [
        {"t":"learn","topic":"Python","title":"Type hints","body":"**Type hints** tell readers (and tools) what types a function expects and returns. Python does **not** check them when the program runs. A tool such as `mypy` reads them and warns you before you run.","code":"def price(qty: int, rate: float) -> float:\n    return qty * rate","lang":"python","id":"py.a.24.1"},
        {"t":"choice","topic":"Python","q":"A function says `n: int`. Ravi calls it with a string. What does Python do at run time?","a":"It runs the code normally; hints are not enforced","w":["It stops with a `TypeError` before the first line","It converts the string to `int` for him","It prints a warning and skips the call"],"why":"The Python runtime does not enforce type hints. An error appears only if the code itself fails with that value.","id":"py.a.24.2"},
        {"t":"output","topic":"Python","q":"What does this program print?","lang":"python","code":"def double(n: int) -> int:\n    return n * 2\nprint(double(\"ab\"))","a":"abab","w":["TypeError","ab","4"],"why":"The hint says `int`, but Python does not check it. A string times 2 repeats the string, so the result is \"abab\".","id":"py.a.24.3"},
        {"t":"output","topic":"Python","q":"What does this program print? (`X | None` needs Python 3.10 or newer.)","lang":"python","code":"def first(items: list[int]) -> int | None:\n    return items[0] if items else None\nprint(first([]), first([7]))","a":"None 7","w":["7 None","None None","[] 7"],"why":"`int | None` means \"an int or None\" (older code writes `Optional[int]`). An empty list gives `None`, and `[7]` gives 7.","id":"py.a.24.4"},
        {"t":"bug","topic":"Python","q":"This program fails with `TypeError: can only concatenate str (not \"int\") to str`. The hint `-> int` was a clue. Which line is wrong?","code":["def total(nums: list[int]) -> int:","    return sum(nums)","print(\"Total: \" + total([1, 2]))"],"lang":"python","bad":2,"why":"A str and an int cannot be added with `+`. Change the number to text with `str(...)` first.","fix":"print(\"Total: \" + str(total([1, 2])))","id":"py.a.24.5"},
        {"t":"order","topic":"Python","q":"Put these steps in the order you would use type hints in a project.","items":["Add type hints to your functions","Run a type checker such as mypy","Read the type mismatches it reports","Fix the code or the hint"],"why":"Hints do nothing alone. A type checker reads them and finds mistakes before the program runs.","id":"py.a.24.6"},
        {"t":"indent","topic":"Python","step":4,"q":"Fix the indentation: a `dataclass` whose fields are written with type hints.","lang":"python","lines":[[0,"from dataclasses import dataclass"],[0,"@dataclass"],[0,"class Student:"],[4,"name: str"],[4,"marks: int"],[0,"s = Student(\"Asha\", 90)"],[0,"print(s.name, s.marks)"]],"why":"A dataclass reads the hinted fields inside the class and makes `__init__` for you. The fields are 4 spaces in.","id":"py.a.24.7"},
        {"t":"check","topic":"Python","q":"Ravi keeps marks like `{\"Asha\": 90, \"Ravi\": 75}`. Which type hint describes this dict?","a":"`dict[str, int]`","w":["`dict[int, str]`","`list[str, int]`","`dict(str, int)`"],"why":"In `dict[K, V]` the key type comes first and the value type second: names are str and marks are int. Hints use square brackets, not round ones.","id":"py.a.24.8"},
        {"t":"check","topic":"Python","q":"Which tool can warn you about a wrong type before the program runs?","a":"A type checker such as `mypy`","w":["The Python interpreter itself","The `typing` module at import time","The `dataclasses` module"],"why":"Python ignores hints at run time. The `typing` module only gives the names, so a separate checker like `mypy` is needed.","id":"py.a.24.9"}
      ] },
      { id: "py.a.25", level: "a", n: 25, title: "Testing with pytest", cards: [
        {"t":"learn","topic":"Python","title":"Testing with pytest","body":"A **test** is a small function that checks your code. With pytest, put tests in a file named `test_*.py`, name each function `test_*`, and use a plain `assert`. Run `pytest` in the terminal and it finds and runs every test.","code":"# test_cart.py\ndef test_total():\n    total = 100 + 50\n    assert total == 150","lang":"python","id":"py.a.25.1"},
        {"t":"choice","topic":"Python","q":"Asha runs `pytest` with no file name. Which file will pytest pick up by default?","a":"`test_prices.py`","w":["`prices.py`","`check_prices.py`","`tests.py`"],"why":"By default pytest looks for files named `test_*.py` or `*_test.py`. A file that does not match is skipped, so its tests never run.","id":"py.a.25.2"},
        {"t":"bug","topic":"Python","q":"Ravi runs `pytest` and it says \"no tests ran\" for this file. Which line stops pytest from finding the test?","code":["from calc import add","","def check_add():","    assert add(2, 3) == 5"],"lang":"python","bad":2,"why":"pytest only collects functions whose names start with `test`. `check_add` is ignored, so nothing runs and nothing fails.","fix":"def test_add():","id":"py.a.25.3"},
        {"t":"order","topic":"Python","q":"Put the parts of one good test in order (arrange, act, assert).","items":["Arrange: make a cart with two items","Act: call `cart.total()`","Assert: check the total is correct"],"why":"First set up the data, then do the one thing you test, then check the result. Testing one behaviour per test makes a failure easy to read.","id":"py.a.25.4"},
        {"t":"choice","topic":"Python","q":"What does `@pytest.mark.parametrize(\"n, expected\", [(1, 1), (2, 4), (3, 9)])` do on a test function?","a":"Runs the test three times, once for each pair","w":["Runs the test once with all three pairs together","Runs only the last pair","Marks the test as skipped"],"why":"Each pair becomes a separate test case, so you can see exactly which pair failed. This avoids copy-pasting the same test.","id":"py.a.25.5"},
        {"t":"pipe","topic":"Python","q":"Build the pytest loop: put the steps in order. One block does not belong.","steps":[["TEST","Write test_cart.py","src"],["RUN","Run pytest"],["FAIL","Read the failure"],["FIX","Fix the code"]],"extra":[["SKIP","Delete the failing test"]],"why":"A failing test is a clue, not a problem to hide. Deleting it only hides the bug.","id":"py.a.25.6"},
        {"t":"choice","topic":"Python","q":"Lakshmi needs the same sample list in many tests. What is a pytest **fixture** for?","a":"It provides setup data to tests that ask for it by argument name","w":["It repeats a test many times","It checks that a function raises an error","It skips tests that are slow"],"why":"Mark a function with `@pytest.fixture`, then add its name as a test argument. pytest runs the fixture and passes in what it returns.","id":"py.a.25.7"},
        {"t":"check","topic":"Python","q":"Which code checks that `divide(1, 0)` raises `ZeroDivisionError` in pytest?","a":"`with pytest.raises(ZeroDivisionError): divide(1, 0)`","w":["`assert divide(1, 0) == ZeroDivisionError`","`try: divide(1, 0)` with no `except`","`pytest.raises(divide(1, 0))`"],"why":"`pytest.raises` is used as a `with` block. The test passes only if that error is raised inside the block.","id":"py.a.25.8"},
        {"t":"check","topic":"Python","q":"A test function has no `assert` and raises no error. What does pytest report?","a":"The test passes","w":["The test fails","The test is skipped","pytest shows a syntax error"],"why":"A test fails only when an error is raised, and a failed `assert` raises one. If nothing goes wrong, the test passes, so an empty test proves nothing.","id":"py.a.25.9"}
      ] },
      { id: "py.a.26", level: "a", n: 26, title: "Async basics", cards: [
        {"t":"learn","topic":"Python","title":"Async basics","body":"An `async def` function makes a **coroutine**. It does nothing until you `await` it or give it to `asyncio.run()`. While one coroutine waits (for a network reply, say), the event loop runs others, so **async helps with waiting, not with heavy CPU work**.","code":"import asyncio\nasync def main():\n    await asyncio.sleep(1)\n    print('done')\nasyncio.run(main())","lang":"python","id":"py.a.26.1"},
        {"t":"output","topic":"Python","q":"What does this print?","code":"import asyncio\nasync def greet():\n    return \"Hi\"\nasync def main():\n    print(await greet())\nasyncio.run(main())","lang":"python","a":"Hi","w":["<coroutine object greet>","None","Nothing is printed"],"why":"`await greet()` runs the coroutine and gives back its return value, `\"Hi\"`. Without `await` you would get a coroutine object instead.","id":"py.a.26.2"},
        {"t":"output","topic":"Python","q":"What does this print?","code":"async def greet():\n    return \"Hi\"\n\nx = greet()\nprint(type(x).__name__)\nx.close()","lang":"python","a":"coroutine","w":["str","function","Task"],"why":"Calling an `async def` function does not run its body. It returns a coroutine object that must be awaited; `x.close()` just tidies up the unused coroutine.","id":"py.a.26.3"},
        {"t":"bug","topic":"Python","q":"Ravi forgot something in `main()`. This crashes with `TypeError` on one line. Which one?","code":["import asyncio","async def get_marks():","    return 40","async def main():","    print(get_marks() + 10)","asyncio.run(main())"],"lang":"python","bad":4,"why":"`get_marks()` is a coroutine, and you cannot add a number to it. Use `await get_marks()` to get the 40 first.","fix":"    print(await get_marks() + 10)","id":"py.a.26.4"},
        {"t":"output","topic":"Python","q":"What does this print? (`gather` runs the three jobs together.)","code":"import asyncio\nasync def sq(n):\n    await asyncio.sleep(0.01 * (3 - n))\n    return n * n\nasync def main():\n    print(*await asyncio.gather(sq(1), sq(2), sq(3)))\nasyncio.run(main())","lang":"python","a":"1 4 9","w":["9 4 1","1 2 3","[1, 4, 9]"],"why":"Job 1 sleeps longest, so it finishes last. But `asyncio.gather` returns results in the same order as the arguments, not the finishing order.","id":"py.a.26.5"},
        {"t":"choice","topic":"Python","q":"Inside an `async def`, why is `time.sleep(2)` a bad idea?","a":"It blocks the whole event loop, so no other task can run","w":["It pauses only this coroutine while others keep running","It raises a SyntaxError inside async code","It is the same as `await asyncio.sleep(2)`"],"why":"`time.sleep` is a normal blocking call. `await asyncio.sleep` lets the event loop run other tasks during the wait.","id":"py.a.26.6"},
        {"t":"choice","topic":"Python","q":"Which job gets the most help from `async` / `await`?","a":"Waiting for 100 web pages to reply","w":["Multiplying a huge matrix in pure Python","Counting primes up to ten crore","Compressing a big video on the CPU"],"why":"Async switches tasks while they wait for input or output. CPU-heavy work never waits, so async gives no speed-up there.","id":"py.a.26.7"},
        {"t":"check","topic":"Python","q":"What does `asyncio.run(main())` do?","a":"Starts the event loop and runs `main()` until it finishes","w":["Runs `main()` in a new operating system process","Only creates the coroutine without running it","Makes `main()` run faster with no event loop"],"why":"`asyncio.run` is the usual entry point of an async program. It sets up the event loop, runs the coroutine, then closes the loop.","id":"py.a.26.8"},
        {"t":"check","topic":"Python","q":"What does this print?","code":"import asyncio\nasync def one():\n    await asyncio.sleep(0.01)\n    return 5\nasync def main():\n    print(sum(await asyncio.gather(one(), one())))\nasyncio.run(main())","lang":"python","a":"10","w":["5","[5, 5]","It raises a TypeError"],"why":"`gather` returns a list of results, `[5, 5]`. `sum` of that list is 10.","id":"py.a.26.9"}
      ] },
      { id: "py.a.27", level: "a", n: 27, title: "pandas for data", cards: [
        {"t":"learn","topic":"Python","title":"pandas for data","body":"A **DataFrame** is a table with named columns. `df[\"marks\"]` picks a column and `df[df[\"marks\"] > 50]` keeps only some rows. Do maths on whole columns at once (**vectorised**) instead of writing a loop.","code":"import pandas as pd\ndf = pd.DataFrame({\"name\": [\"Asha\", \"Ravi\"], \"marks\": [72, 45]})\nprint(df[\"marks\"].max())","lang":"python","id":"py.a.27.1"},
        {"t":"output","topic":"Python","q":"What does this program print?","code":"import pandas as pd\ndf = pd.DataFrame({\"name\": [\"Asha\", \"Ravi\", \"Lakshmi\"], \"marks\": [72, 45, 88]})\nprint(len(df[df[\"marks\"] > 50]))","lang":"python","a":"2","w":["1","3","88"],"why":"Marks 72 and 88 are above 50, so two rows are kept. `len(df)` counts rows.","id":"py.a.27.2"},
        {"t":"output","topic":"Python","q":"What does this print? (`groupby` sorts group names A to Z.)","code":"import pandas as pd\ndf = pd.DataFrame({\"city\": [\"Guntur\", \"Pune\", \"Guntur\"], \"sales\": [10, 20, 30]})\nprint(df.groupby(\"city\")[\"sales\"].sum().tolist())","lang":"python","a":"[40, 20]","w":["[60]","[20, 40]","[10, 20, 30]"],"why":"Guntur has 10 + 30 = 40 and Pune has 20. Groups come in sorted order, so Guntur is first.","id":"py.a.27.3"},
        {"t":"output","topic":"Python","q":"How many missing values does this print?","code":"import pandas as pd\ndf = pd.DataFrame({\"marks\": [50, None, 70]})\nprint(int(df[\"marks\"].isna().sum()))","lang":"python","a":"1","w":["0","2","3"],"why":"`isna()` gives True for each missing value. `sum()` counts the True values, which is 1 here.","id":"py.a.27.4"},
        {"t":"output","topic":"Python","q":"What does this print?","code":"import io\nimport pandas as pd\ntext = \"name,marks\\nAsha,72\\nRavi,45\"\ndf = pd.read_csv(io.StringIO(text))\nprint(df[\"marks\"].mean())","lang":"python","a":"58.5","w":["117","72","45"],"why":"`read_csv` turns CSV text into a DataFrame. The mean of 72 and 45 is 117 / 2 = 58.5.","id":"py.a.27.5"},
        {"t":"bug","topic":"Python","q":"Lakshmi gets a `KeyError` on one line. Which line is wrong?","code":["import pandas as pd","df = pd.DataFrame({\"marks\": [72, 45]})","top = df[\"Marks\"].max()","print(top)"],"lang":"python","bad":2,"why":"Column names are case-sensitive. The column is `\"marks\"`, not `\"Marks\"`.","fix":"top = df[\"marks\"].max()","id":"py.a.27.6"},
        {"t":"choice","topic":"Python","q":"Ravi wants to add 10 grace marks to every value in the `marks` column. Which is the vectorised pandas way?","a":"`df[\"marks\"] = df[\"marks\"] + 10`","w":["`for i in range(len(df)): df[\"marks\"][i] += 10`","`df.marks.append(10)`","`df[\"marks\"] = 10`"],"why":"Vectorised code works on the whole column at once, and it is shorter and faster than a loop. In pandas 3 the loop with `df[\"marks\"][i]` does not even change `df` (Copy-on-Write).","id":"py.a.27.7"},
        {"t":"check","topic":"Python","q":"What does this print?","code":"import pandas as pd\na = pd.DataFrame({\"id\": [1, 2], \"name\": [\"Asha\", \"Ravi\"]})\nb = pd.DataFrame({\"id\": [1, 2], \"marks\": [72, 45]})\nprint(a.merge(b, on=\"id\")[\"marks\"].tolist())","lang":"python","a":"[72, 45]","w":["[1, 2]","['Asha', 'Ravi']","[117]"],"why":"`merge` joins two tables on the shared `id` column, like a SQL join. The marks come along with each name.","id":"py.a.27.8"},
        {"t":"check","topic":"Python","q":"What does this print?","code":"import pandas as pd\ndf = pd.DataFrame({\"marks\": [50, None, 70]})\nprint(df[\"marks\"].fillna(0).tolist())","lang":"python","a":"[50.0, 0.0, 70.0]","w":["[50, 0, 70]","[50.0, None, 70.0]","[50, 70]"],"why":"`fillna(0)` replaces missing values with 0. A column with a missing value holds decimals, so the numbers show as 50.0 and 70.0.","id":"py.a.27.9"}
      ] },
      { id: "py.a.28", level: "a", n: 28, title: "Project structure and packaging", cards: [
        {"t":"learn","topic":"Python","title":"Project structure and packaging","body":"Each `.py` file is a **module**, and a folder of modules is a **package**. Code under `if __name__ == \"__main__\":` runs only when you start the file directly, not when another file imports it. Keep each project in its own **virtual environment**: `python -m venv .venv`.","code":"def main():\n    print(\"Hello Guntur\")\n\nif __name__ == \"__main__\":\n    main()","lang":"python","id":"py.a.28.1"},
        {"t":"output","topic":"Python","q":"You run `python app.py`. What does this file print?","code":"print(__name__)","lang":"python","a":"__main__","w":["app","app.py","__name__"],"why":"When a file is run directly, Python sets `__name__` to `\"__main__\"`. When another file imports it, `__name__` is the module name instead.","id":"py.a.28.2"},
        {"t":"indent","topic":"Python","step":4,"q":"Fix the indentation so this program runs and prints `Hello`.","lang":"python","lines":[[0,"def main():"],[4,"print(\"Hello\")"],[0,"if __name__ == \"__main__\":"],[4,"main()"]],"why":"The body of `def` and of `if` must be indented. The `if` line itself starts at column 0, outside `main`.","id":"py.a.28.3"},
        {"t":"order","topic":"Python","q":"Put the steps to start working on a new Python project in order.","items":["`python -m venv .venv`","Activate the environment","`pip install -r requirements.txt`","`python app.py`"],"why":"Create the environment first, activate it, then install packages into it. Installing before activating puts packages in the wrong place.","id":"py.a.28.4"},
        {"t":"choice","topic":"Python","q":"Asha starts a Git repo for her Python project. Which two entries belong in `.gitignore`?","a":"`.venv/` and `.env` (secrets)","w":["`requirements.txt` and `README.md`","`pyproject.toml` and `src/`","`app.py` and `tests/`"],"why":"The virtual environment is big and can be rebuilt, and `.env` may hold passwords or keys. Never commit either one.","id":"py.a.28.5"},
        {"t":"choice","topic":"Python","q":"What is `pyproject.toml` used for in a modern Python project?","a":"It holds project details (name, version, dependencies) and build settings","w":["It stores the virtual environment files","It lists secret passwords for the app","It runs the program when you open the folder"],"why":"Tools like pip read `pyproject.toml` to build and install your project. It is plain text and safe to commit.","id":"py.a.28.6"},
        {"t":"bug","topic":"Python","q":"Ravi runs this file and gets a `SyntaxError`. Which line is wrong?","code":["def main():","    print(\"Hello Pune\")","if __name__ = \"__main__\":","    main()"],"lang":"python","bad":2,"why":"One `=` assigns a value, and you cannot do that inside an `if`. Use `==` to compare.","fix":"if __name__ == \"__main__\":","id":"py.a.28.7"},
        {"t":"check","topic":"Python","q":"What does `python -m venv .venv` do?","a":"Creates a private virtual environment in the `.venv` folder","w":["Installs every package in `requirements.txt`","Upgrades Python for the whole computer","Runs the file named `venv`"],"why":"A virtual environment keeps each project's packages separate. After creating it, activate it and then install packages.","id":"py.a.28.8"},
        {"t":"check","topic":"Python","q":"`tool.py` has the line `print(__name__)`. Ravi runs `python main.py`, and `main.py` has `import tool`. What does that line in `tool.py` print?","a":"tool","w":["__main__","main","tool.py"],"why":"An imported module gets its own name in `__name__`, without `.py`. Only the file you run directly gets `\"__main__\"`.","id":"py.a.28.9"}
      ] }
    ] },
    { id: "az", name: "Azure Data Engineering", topic: "Azure", blurb: "Storage, Data Factory, Databricks, streaming and Fabric, step by step.", units: [
      { id: "az.b.01", level: "b", n: 1, title: "Cloud basics and the Azure hierarchy", cards: [
        {"t":"learn","topic":"Azure","title":"How Azure is organised","body":"**Management groups** hold **subscriptions** (where billing happens). A subscription holds **resource groups**, and each resource group holds resources like VMs, storage and databases. Serverless services like **Azure Functions** run, and cost, only when something triggers them.","id":"az.b.01.1"},
        {"t":"choice","topic":"Azure","q":"Which Azure service runs a small piece of code only when something happens, like a file upload, and charges only for that run?","a":"Azure Functions","w":["Azure Virtual Machines","Azure SQL Database","Power BI"],"why":"**Azure Functions** is serverless: no server to manage, it wakes up on an event and you pay per run. A VM runs (and costs) all the time.","id":"az.b.01.2"},
        {"t":"choice","topic":"Azure","q":"In Azure, what groups the resources of one project (VM, disk, network) so you can manage or delete them together?","a":"Resource group","w":["Management group","Tenant","Region"],"why":"A **resource group** is a folder for related resources. Delete the group and everything in it goes. Management groups sit higher and hold subscriptions.","id":"az.b.01.3"},
        {"t":"order","topic":"Azure","q":"Order Azure’s levels from the biggest container to a single resource.","items":["Management group","Subscription","Resource group","Resource (e.g. a VM)"],"why":"Management groups hold subscriptions, a subscription holds resource groups (and gets the bill), and resource groups hold the actual resources.","id":"az.b.01.4"},
        {"t":"choice","topic":"Azure","q":"Which tool is the website where you create and manage Azure resources by clicking?","a":"The Azure portal","w":["Azure Key Vault","Power BI","Azure Event Hubs"],"why":"The **Azure portal** (portal.azure.com) is the web console. The Azure CLI and PowerShell do the same from a terminal or script.","id":"az.b.01.5"},
        {"t":"choice","topic":"Azure","q":"What is an Azure region?","a":"A set of datacenters in one area, like Central India","w":["A billing account","A folder for files","A type of virtual machine"],"why":"A **region** is a geographic location with datacenters. Pick one close to your users for speed, and one that meets your data rules.","id":"az.b.01.6"},
        {"t":"check","topic":"Azure","q":"You delete a resource group. What happens to the resources inside it?","a":"They are deleted too","w":["They move to another group","Nothing happens to them","They are stopped but kept"],"why":"Deleting a resource group deletes **everything** in it. That makes clean-up easy, and mistakes costly, so be careful.","id":"az.b.01.7"},
        {"t":"check","topic":"Azure","q":"What is an Azure subscription mainly for?","a":"Billing and access: costs are tracked and paid per subscription","w":["Storing files","Running Python code","Drawing reports"],"why":"A **subscription** is the billing and access boundary. Teams often use separate subscriptions for dev and production.","id":"az.b.01.8"}
      ] },
      { id: "az.b.02", level: "b", n: 2, title: "Storage accounts and tiers", cards: [
        {"t":"learn","topic":"Azure","title":"Pay less for cold data","body":"Blob storage has **access tiers**: **Hot** for data used every day, **Cool** and **Cold** for data used now and then, and **Archive** for data you almost never read: cheapest to keep, but slow and costly to read back.","id":"az.b.02.1"},
        {"t":"choice","topic":"Azure","q":"Which storage tier is the cheapest for files you almost never read, like 7-year-old backups?","a":"Archive","w":["Hot","Cool","Premium"],"why":"**Archive** is the cheapest to store but slow and costly to read back (hours). Hot is for daily use, Cool and Cold for occasional use.","id":"az.b.02.2"},
        {"t":"choice","topic":"Azure","q":"Inside a storage account, where are files (blobs) kept?","a":"In containers","w":["In resource groups","In subscriptions","In virtual machines"],"why":"Blob storage holds files in **containers** (like top-level folders). A storage account can have many containers.","id":"az.b.02.3"},
        {"t":"order","topic":"Azure","q":"Order the access tiers from cheapest to store data to most expensive to store data.","items":["Archive","Cold","Cool","Hot"],"why":"Storing is cheapest in **Archive** and most expensive in **Hot**. Reading works the other way round: Hot is cheapest to read.","id":"az.b.02.4"},
        {"t":"choice","topic":"Azure","q":"A report reads last month’s sales files every day. Which tier fits?","a":"Hot","w":["Archive","Cold","Cool"],"why":"Data used **every day** belongs in **Hot**: the lowest cost to read. Cool, Cold and Archive charge more each time you read.","id":"az.b.02.5"},
        {"t":"choice","topic":"Azure","q":"What does LRS (locally redundant storage) mean?","a":"Azure keeps copies of your data inside one datacenter","w":["Only one copy is kept, with no backup","A copy is kept in every Azure region","Old files are deleted automatically"],"why":"**LRS** copies your data within one datacenter, which protects against a failed drive or server. **ZRS** spreads copies over zones, and **GRS** also copies to a second region for disasters.","id":"az.b.02.6"},
        {"t":"check","topic":"Azure","q":"Why can reading a file from the Archive tier take hours?","a":"It must be rehydrated (moved to an online tier) before it can be read","w":["Archive files are stored on paper","Archive works only in the USA","The file must be unzipped by hand"],"why":"Archive data is offline. You **rehydrate** it to Hot, Cool or Cold first, which can take up to several hours.","id":"az.b.02.7"},
        {"t":"check","topic":"Azure","q":"Backups are read only a few times a year, kept at least 90 days, and must be readable straight away when needed. Which tier?","a":"Cold","w":["Hot","Archive","Premium"],"why":"**Cold** is cheap for rarely used data and still online, so reads are immediate. Archive is cheaper but needs hours of rehydration.","id":"az.b.02.8"}
      ] },
      { id: "az.b.03", level: "b", n: 3, title: "Data Lake Gen2", cards: [
        {"t":"learn","topic":"Azure","title":"The data lake","body":"**Azure Data Lake Storage Gen2** is storage built for analytics: cheap, huge, with real folders and permissions. Raw files of any format (CSV, JSON, Parquet) usually land here first.","id":"az.b.03.1"},
        {"t":"choice","topic":"Azure","q":"You must store millions of raw CSV and JSON files cheaply, ready for analytics. Which Azure service?","a":"Azure Data Lake Storage Gen2","w":["Azure SQL Database","Power BI","Azure Functions"],"why":"**Data Lake Storage Gen2** is cheap file storage built for big data. SQL Database is for tables, Power BI is for reports, Functions run code.","id":"az.b.03.2"},
        {"t":"choice","topic":"Azure","q":"What makes Data Lake Storage Gen2 different from plain blob storage?","a":"A hierarchical namespace: real folders that can be renamed and secured","w":["It stores only CSV files","It runs SQL queries by itself","It is free"],"why":"The **hierarchical namespace** gives true folders, fast renames and folder-level permissions, which big data tools rely on.","id":"az.b.03.3"},
        {"t":"choice","topic":"Azure","q":"How do you create Data Lake Storage Gen2?","a":"Create a storage account and turn on the hierarchical namespace","w":["Install it on a virtual machine","Buy it inside Power BI","Create an Event Hub"],"why":"Data Lake Gen2 is a **storage account** setting, not a separate product. Tick \"hierarchical namespace\" when you create it.","id":"az.b.03.4"},
        {"t":"order","topic":"Azure","q":"Order a common data lake folder layout, from where data first lands to report-ready.","items":["raw (as it arrived)","cleaned","curated (ready for reports)"],"why":"Keep the raw copy untouched, clean it in a second area, and publish curated data for reports. The same idea is called bronze, silver and gold.","id":"az.b.03.5"},
        {"t":"choice","topic":"Azure","q":"Who can read a folder in the data lake?","a":"Only identities given access with Azure roles or folder permissions (ACLs)","w":["Anyone on the internet","Only the person who uploaded it, always","Everyone in the same city"],"why":"Access is controlled with **Azure RBAC roles** and folder **ACLs**, so each team or app sees only what it needs.","id":"az.b.03.6"},
        {"t":"check","topic":"Azure","q":"Which kinds of files can a data lake store?","a":"Any kind: CSV, JSON, Parquet, images, logs","w":["Only CSV","Only Parquet","Only database tables"],"why":"A data lake stores files **as they are**, in any format. Tools like Spark or SQL read them later.","id":"az.b.03.7"},
        {"t":"check","topic":"Azure","q":"Why do data lakes often use folders like raw/2026/11/15/?","a":"So jobs can find and process one day of data easily","w":["Because Azure requires dates in names","To make the files bigger","To hide files from Power BI"],"why":"Date folders let a daily job read just one day, and let queries skip the folders they do not need.","id":"az.b.03.8"}
      ] },
      { id: "az.b.04", level: "b", n: 4, title: "Data Factory pipelines", cards: [
        {"t":"learn","topic":"Azure","title":"Moving data with pipelines","body":"**Azure Data Factory** builds pipelines that copy and transform data on a schedule or trigger, from many sources into the lake or a database. The classic batch pattern is **ETL**: extract, transform, load.","id":"az.b.04.1"},
        {"t":"choice","topic":"Azure","q":"What is Azure Data Factory mainly used for?","a":"Moving data between systems on a schedule (pipelines)","w":["Drawing dashboards","Hosting websites","Training AI models"],"why":"**Data Factory** builds pipelines that copy and move data, for example from a database into a data lake every night.","id":"az.b.04.2"},
        {"t":"order","topic":"Azure","q":"Order a simple Azure data pipeline from start to finish.","items":["Ingest: Data Factory","Store: Data Lake","Transform: Databricks","Show: Power BI"],"why":"Collect the data, store it, clean and shape it, then show it in reports. You can practise this in the **Build the Data Pipeline** game.","id":"az.b.04.3"},
        {"t":"order","topic":"Azure","q":"ETL is a classic data job. Order its three steps.","items":["Extract: read data from the source","Transform: clean and change it","Load: write it to the target"],"why":"**E**xtract, **T**ransform, **L**oad. In modern lakes you also see ELT: load the raw data first, then transform it inside the lake.","id":"az.b.04.4"},
        {"t":"pipe","topic":"Azure","q":"Daily sales CSV files must reach a Power BI report every night. Build the pipeline.","steps":[["CSV","Sales CSV files","src"],["ADF","Data Factory"],["ADLS","Data Lake Storage"],["BI","Power BI"]],"extra":[["EH","Event Hubs"]],"why":"**Data Factory** copies the files each night, the **Data Lake** stores them, and **Power BI** shows the report. Event Hubs is for live streams, not nightly files.","id":"az.b.04.5"},
        {"t":"choice","topic":"Azure","q":"In Data Factory, what starts a pipeline every night at 2 AM?","a":"A schedule trigger","w":["A linked service","A dataset","An integration runtime"],"why":"A **schedule trigger** runs a pipeline at set times. Event triggers can also start one when a new file arrives.","id":"az.b.04.6"},
        {"t":"check","topic":"Azure","q":"In Data Factory, what holds the connection details for a source, like a SQL database?","a":"A linked service","w":["A trigger","A pipeline","A Power BI report"],"why":"A **linked service** is like a connection string. Datasets and activities use it to reach the data.","id":"az.b.04.7"},
        {"t":"check","topic":"Azure","q":"Which Data Factory activity copies data from a source to a destination?","a":"The Copy activity","w":["The Lookup activity","The Wait activity","The Delete activity"],"why":"The **Copy activity** moves data between many kinds of sources and destinations, such as a SQL database to the data lake.","id":"az.b.04.8"}
      ] },
      { id: "az.b.05", level: "b", n: 5, title: "Azure SQL basics", cards: [
        {"t":"learn","topic":"Azure","title":"Databases in Azure","body":"**Azure SQL Database** is a managed SQL Server database: Microsoft looks after the server, updates and backups, and you work with **tables** using SQL. A table has rows and columns, and each row is found by its **primary key**.","code":"SELECT name, city\nFROM customers\nWHERE city = 'Guntur';","lang":"sql","id":"az.b.05.1"},
        {"t":"choice","topic":"Azure","q":"What does \"managed\" mean for Azure SQL Database?","a":"Microsoft looks after the server, updates and backups for you","w":["You must install SQL Server yourself","It works only on weekdays","It has no security"],"why":"With a **managed** (PaaS) database you focus on tables and queries; Microsoft runs the server underneath.","id":"az.b.05.2"},
        {"t":"choice","topic":"Azure","q":"Which language do you use to read data from Azure SQL Database?","a":"SQL","w":["YAML","HTML","Bash"],"why":"**SQL** (Structured Query Language) is how you read and change data in tables: `SELECT`, `INSERT`, `UPDATE`, `DELETE`.","id":"az.b.05.3"},
        {"t":"choice","topic":"Azure","q":"What is a primary key?","a":"A column whose value is unique for every row, like customer_id","w":["The password of the database","The first table you create","A key for the server room"],"why":"A **primary key** identifies each row exactly once, so no two customers can share the same `customer_id`.","id":"az.b.05.4"},
        {"t":"choice","topic":"Azure","q":"Which data suits Azure SQL Database best?","a":"A shop’s orders: one row per sale, updated all day","w":["5 years of raw click logs for big analytics","Millions of images","Live sensor events every second"],"why":"SQL Database is great for **transactional** app data. Huge raw logs and images go to a data lake; live events go to Event Hubs.","id":"az.b.05.5"},
        {"t":"order","topic":"Azure","q":"Put this query in the right order.","items":["SELECT name, city","FROM customers","WHERE city = 'Guntur'"],"why":"A query is always written `SELECT` (which columns), `FROM` (which table), then `WHERE` (which rows).","id":"az.b.05.6"},
        {"t":"check","topic":"Azure","q":"Which Azure service would you pick for an app’s customer and order tables?","a":"Azure SQL Database","w":["Azure Event Hubs","Azure Data Factory","Azure Key Vault"],"why":"Tables of customers and orders that the app reads and updates all day are a classic fit for **Azure SQL Database**.","id":"az.b.05.7"},
        {"t":"check","topic":"Azure","q":"In a customers table, what is one row?","a":"One customer","w":["One column","The whole table","One database"],"why":"Each **row** is one record (one customer); each **column** is one detail about it, like name or city.","id":"az.b.05.8"}
      ] },
      { id: "az.b.06", level: "b", n: 6, title: "Power BI basics", cards: [
        {"t":"learn","topic":"Azure","title":"Reports and dashboards","body":"**Power BI** turns data into interactive **reports**: charts, tables and filters. You build them in **Power BI Desktop**, publish them to the **Power BI service** to share, and set a **scheduled refresh** so the numbers stay up to date.","id":"az.b.06.1"},
        {"t":"choice","topic":"Azure","q":"Where do you usually build a Power BI report?","a":"Power BI Desktop","w":["Azure Key Vault","The Azure CLI","Notepad"],"why":"**Power BI Desktop** is the free Windows app for building reports. You then publish them to the Power BI service.","id":"az.b.06.2"},
        {"t":"choice","topic":"Azure","q":"Which chart best shows sales over 12 months?","a":"A line chart","w":["A pie chart","A map","A single number card"],"why":"A **line chart** shows change over time. Pie charts compare parts of a whole; cards show one number.","id":"az.b.06.3"},
        {"t":"choice","topic":"Azure","q":"A report must show fresh numbers every morning. What do you set up?","a":"A scheduled refresh","w":["A new report every day","A Key Vault secret","A pie chart"],"why":"A **scheduled refresh** reloads the data automatically, for example every morning at 6.","id":"az.b.06.4"},
        {"t":"order","topic":"Azure","q":"Order the steps to make a Power BI report.","items":["Connect to the data","Clean and shape it in Power Query","Build the visuals","Publish and share"],"why":"Get the data, tidy it in **Power Query**, design the visuals, then publish to the Power BI service so others can see it.","id":"az.b.06.5"},
        {"t":"pipe","topic":"Azure","q":"Show gold data from the lake in a Power BI report for the team.","steps":[["ADLS","Data Lake: gold files","gold"],["SYN","Synapse serverless SQL"],["BI","Power BI semantic model"],["RPT","Report for the team"]],"extra":[["EH","Event Hubs"]],"why":"**Synapse serverless SQL** can query lake files as tables, Power BI loads them into a **semantic model**, and the report is shared from it. Event Hubs is for live streams.","id":"az.b.06.6"},
        {"t":"check","topic":"Azure","q":"What does a slicer do in a Power BI report?","a":"Lets viewers filter the report, for example by year or city","w":["Deletes rows from the database","Cuts the report into PDF pages","Encrypts the data"],"why":"A **slicer** is an on-page filter. Picking \"2026\" or \"Guntur\" updates every visual on the page.","id":"az.b.06.7"},
        {"t":"check","topic":"Azure","q":"Where do people open a shared Power BI report?","a":"In the Power BI service (app.powerbi.com) or the Power BI mobile app","w":["Only on the computer that built it","In Azure Key Vault","In Notepad"],"why":"Published reports live in the **Power BI service**, so people open them in a browser or the mobile app.","id":"az.b.06.8"}
      ] },
      { id: "az.i.07", level: "i", n: 7, title: "Databricks and Spark", cards: [
        {"t":"learn","topic":"Azure","title":"Big data with Spark","body":"**Azure Databricks** runs Apache Spark: notebooks and jobs that clean, join and summarise huge data by splitting the work across many machines at once.","id":"az.i.07.1"},
        {"t":"choice","topic":"Azure","q":"Which Azure service is a managed Apache Spark platform for cleaning and transforming big data?","a":"Azure Databricks","w":["Azure Event Hubs","Azure Blob Storage","Azure Monitor"],"why":"**Databricks** runs Spark: notebooks and jobs that clean, join and transform large data. Event Hubs receives streams, Blob Storage stores files, Monitor collects logs.","id":"az.i.07.2"},
        {"t":"choice","topic":"Azure","q":"In Spark, you write a step like 'keep only rows where city is Guntur'. Nothing runs yet. Why?","a":"Spark is lazy: it plans transformations and runs them only when an action asks for a result","w":["The cluster is switched off until midnight","Spark can only run one step per day","Transformations always fail until you restart the notebook"],"why":"Spark uses lazy evaluation. Transformations (filter, select) only build a plan. An action such as `show` or `count` makes it run.","id":"az.i.07.3"},
        {"t":"choice","topic":"Azure","q":"In Databricks, where do you usually write and run Spark code step by step?","a":"In a notebook attached to a cluster","w":["In the Azure billing page","In a Key Vault secret","In a Power BI report"],"why":"A notebook holds your code cells. The cluster is the group of machines that runs them.","id":"az.i.07.4"},
        {"t":"choice","topic":"Azure","q":"Ravi wants his Databricks notebook to run every night at 2 AM without him. What should he create?","a":"A job that runs the notebook on a schedule","w":["A bigger DataFrame","A new cluster name only","A second notebook with the same code"],"why":"A Databricks job runs a notebook or other task on a schedule or trigger, so nobody has to click Run.","id":"az.i.07.5"},
        {"t":"choice","topic":"Azure","q":"What is a Spark DataFrame?","a":"A table-like set of data with named columns, spread across the cluster","w":["A single file on your laptop","A password stored in Azure","A picture of your data"],"why":"A DataFrame looks like a table with rows and columns, but Spark can split it over many machines.","id":"az.i.07.6"},
        {"t":"check","topic":"Azure","q":"Lakshmi runs `df.filter(...)` and then `df.count()`. Which step makes Spark actually do the work?","a":"`df.count()` because it is an action","w":["`df.filter(...)` because filter always runs first","Neither, Spark never runs code","Both run at once the moment the notebook opens"],"why":"Filter is a transformation, so it is only planned. Count is an action, so Spark runs the whole plan.","id":"az.i.07.7"},
        {"t":"check","topic":"Azure","q":"Why does Databricks use a cluster of many machines for big data?","a":"Each machine works on a part of the data at the same time","w":["Each machine stores a full copy and works alone","It makes the data smaller before reading","It removes the need to write any code"],"why":"Spark splits data into parts and processes them in parallel on the cluster's machines, which is faster for big data.","id":"az.i.07.8"}
      ] },
      { id: "az.i.08", level: "i", n: 8, title: "Medallion and Delta Lake", cards: [
        {"t":"learn","topic":"Azure","title":"Bronze, silver, gold","body":"The **medallion** design keeps data in layers: **bronze** is raw, as it arrived; **silver** is cleaned and checked; **gold** is shaped for reports. **Delta Lake** tables add safe updates and history on top of the lake.","id":"az.i.08.1"},
        {"t":"order","topic":"Azure","q":"Medallion architecture: order the layers from raw data to report-ready data.","items":["Bronze: raw data, as it arrived","Silver: cleaned and joined","Gold: ready for reports"],"why":"Data gets better at each step: **bronze** keeps the raw copy, **silver** cleans it, **gold** shapes it for business reports.","id":"az.i.08.2"},
        {"t":"order","topic":"Azure","q":"A new file arrives in bronze. Order the data quality steps.","items":["Load the new file","Check row counts and empty values","Fix or reject the bad rows","Write clean data to silver"],"why":"Check data **before** it moves on. Bad rows that reach gold end up in wrong reports and bad decisions.","id":"az.i.08.3"},
        {"t":"pipe","topic":"Azure","q":"Build a medallion pipeline: from the company database to report-ready gold data.","steps":[["ERP","Company database","src"],["ADF","Data Factory"],["B","Bronze: raw","bronze"],["S","Silver: clean","silver"],["G","Gold: ready","gold"]],"extra":[["EH","Event Hubs"]],"why":"**Data Factory** copies the database into **bronze**. Then the data is cleaned into **silver** and shaped for reports in **gold**.","id":"az.i.08.4"},
        {"t":"choice","topic":"Azure","q":"What does ACID support in a Delta table protect you from?","a":"Half-finished writes: a change either fully happens or does not happen","w":["Slow internet connections","Typing mistakes in column names","Having more than one user"],"why":"ACID means transactions are Atomic, Consistent, Isolated and Durable. Readers never see a half-written change.","id":"az.i.08.5"},
        {"t":"choice","topic":"Azure","q":"Asha changed a Delta table by mistake yesterday. Which Delta feature lets her read the older version?","a":"Time travel","w":["Cluster sleep","Streaming windows","Key rotation"],"why":"Delta keeps a history of versions. With time travel you can query the table as it was at an earlier version or time.","id":"az.i.08.6"},
        {"t":"choice","topic":"Azure","q":"New customer rows must be added and changed rows must be updated in one step. Which Delta command does this upsert?","a":"`MERGE INTO`","w":["`DROP TABLE`","`SELECT DISTINCT`","`CREATE SCHEMA`"],"why":"`MERGE INTO` matches source rows to the table: matching rows are updated and new rows are inserted.","id":"az.i.08.7"},
        {"t":"choice","topic":"Azure","q":"A Delta table is stored in the data lake as what?","a":"Parquet data files plus a transaction log","w":["Only one big text file","Only Excel sheets","A video file"],"why":"Delta Lake keeps data in Parquet files and records every change in a transaction log. This log makes ACID and time travel possible.","id":"az.i.08.8"},
        {"t":"check","topic":"Azure","q":"In the medallion idea, which layer is best for Power BI reports?","a":"Gold, because it is clean and ready for business use","w":["Bronze, because it is the raw copy","Silver, because it still has duplicates","None, reports cannot use a lake"],"why":"Gold holds the final, report-ready tables. Bronze is raw and silver is cleaned but not yet shaped for reports.","id":"az.i.08.9"},
        {"t":"check","topic":"Azure","q":"Why is Delta Lake better than plain Parquet files for a table that changes every day?","a":"It adds ACID transactions, updates and history on top of the files","w":["It stores the data without any files","It makes every column a string","It stops anyone from reading the table"],"why":"Plain Parquet files are not designed for safe updates. Delta adds a log so changes are safe and versions can be read.","id":"az.i.08.10"}
      ] },
      { id: "az.i.09", level: "i", n: 9, title: "Synapse", cards: [
        {"t":"learn","topic":"Azure","title":"Azure Synapse Analytics","body":"**Synapse** is one Azure workspace for analytics. It has a **dedicated SQL pool** (reserved power for a data warehouse), a **serverless SQL pool** (query files in the data lake with no servers to set up), **Spark pools** for big data code, and **pipelines** to move data. Microsoft Fabric is Microsoft's newer analytics platform.","id":"az.i.09.1"},
        {"t":"choice","topic":"Azure","q":"Pune team wants to run SQL directly on Parquet files in the data lake without setting up servers. Which Synapse option fits?","a":"Serverless SQL pool","w":["Dedicated SQL pool","Key Vault","Event Hubs"],"why":"A serverless SQL pool queries files in the lake on demand. A dedicated SQL pool is for a provisioned data warehouse.","id":"az.i.09.2"},
        {"t":"choice","topic":"Azure","q":"Which Synapse part is a provisioned data warehouse with tables you load data into?","a":"Dedicated SQL pool","w":["Serverless SQL pool","Pipeline trigger","Managed identity"],"why":"A dedicated SQL pool is reserved warehouse power with its own stored tables.","id":"az.i.09.3"},
        {"t":"choice","topic":"Azure","q":"In Synapse, which part do you use to copy data from sources and run activities on a schedule?","a":"Pipelines","w":["Dedicated SQL pool","Time travel","Row-level security"],"why":"Synapse pipelines work like Azure Data Factory pipelines: they copy and move data and run steps in order.","id":"az.i.09.4"},
        {"t":"choice","topic":"Azure","q":"Which Synapse part runs Apache Spark code on big data?","a":"Spark pool","w":["Serverless SQL pool","Dedicated SQL pool","Gateway"],"why":"Spark pools run Spark notebooks and jobs. The SQL pools run T-SQL queries.","id":"az.i.09.5"},
        {"t":"choice","topic":"Azure","q":"Where does a Synapse serverless SQL pool read its data from?","a":"Files in the data lake, such as Parquet or CSV","w":["Only its own reserved disks","Power BI reports","Key Vault secrets"],"why":"A **serverless SQL pool** has no storage of its own: it runs SQL directly over files in the data lake. A **dedicated SQL pool** stores data in its own tables.","id":"az.i.09.6"},
        {"t":"check","topic":"Azure","q":"Which is the newer Microsoft analytics platform that brings many data tools together?","a":"Microsoft Fabric","w":["Azure Key Vault","Azure DevOps Boards","Windows Defender"],"why":"Microsoft Fabric is Microsoft's newer analytics platform. It is separate from Azure Synapse Analytics.","id":"az.i.09.7"},
        {"t":"check","topic":"Azure","q":"Which pair is correct for Synapse?","a":"Dedicated SQL pool for a warehouse, serverless SQL pool for querying lake files","w":["Dedicated SQL pool for streaming, serverless for passwords","Serverless SQL pool for a warehouse, dedicated for secrets","Both only store images"],"why":"Dedicated pools hold warehouse tables. Serverless pools query files that stay in the data lake.","id":"az.i.09.8"}
      ] },
      { id: "az.i.10", level: "i", n: 10, title: "Event Hubs and Stream Analytics", cards: [
        {"t":"learn","topic":"Azure","title":"Live data streams","body":"**Event Hubs** receives millions of events per second from apps, sensors or payments. **Stream Analytics** runs SQL-like queries on the stream as it flows and sends results to Power BI, storage or Functions.","id":"az.i.10.1"},
        {"t":"choice","topic":"Azure","q":"An app sends millions of live events every second. Which service should receive them?","a":"Azure Event Hubs","w":["Azure Data Factory","Azure Synapse Analytics","Power BI"],"why":"**Event Hubs** takes in huge streams of live events. Data Factory moves data in batches on a schedule; Synapse and Power BI come later in the pipeline.","id":"az.i.10.2"},
        {"t":"pipe","topic":"Azure","q":"Temperature sensors send readings every second. Show them live on a dashboard.","steps":[["IoT","Sensors","src"],["EH","Event Hubs"],["ASA","Stream Analytics"],["BI","Power BI"]],"extra":[["ADF","Data Factory"]],"why":"Live data needs streaming services: **Event Hubs** receives the readings, **Stream Analytics** calculates averages as they arrive, and **Power BI** shows them live.","id":"az.i.10.3"},
        {"t":"pipe","topic":"Azure","q":"Card payments must be checked live, and a fraud alert sent within seconds. Build the pipeline.","steps":[["PAY","Card payments","src"],["EH","Event Hubs"],["ASA","Stream Analytics"],["FN","Functions: send alert"]],"extra":[["ADF","Data Factory"]],"why":"**Event Hubs** receives each payment, **Stream Analytics** spots suspicious patterns in real time, and an **Azure Function** sends the alert. Data Factory runs batches, too slow here.","id":"az.i.10.4"},
        {"t":"choice","topic":"Azure","q":"Why does Event Hubs split a stream into partitions?","a":"So many readers can process parts of the stream at the same time","w":["To delete old events faster","To make events secret","To change events into tables"],"why":"Partitions let events be read in parallel, so large streams can be handled faster.","id":"az.i.10.5"},
        {"t":"choice","topic":"Azure","q":"Two apps, one for the dashboard and one for fraud alerts, both need to read the same events from Event Hubs. What lets each read independently?","a":"A separate consumer group for each app","w":["A separate Key Vault for each app","A separate partition key only","Deleting the events after the first app reads them"],"why":"A consumer group gives each app its own view of the stream, so one app does not take events away from the other.","id":"az.i.10.6"},
        {"t":"choice","topic":"Azure","q":"In Stream Analytics, a tumbling window counts clicks every 5 minutes with no overlap. What does that mean?","a":"Each event falls in exactly one fixed 5-minute window","w":["Each event is counted in many overlapping windows","The window stops when no one clicks","The window only works for one event"],"why":"Tumbling windows are fixed-size and do not overlap. A hopping window also has a fixed size but can overlap.","id":"az.i.10.7"},
        {"t":"choice","topic":"Azure","q":"Which window type is fixed in size but moves forward by a smaller step, so windows overlap?","a":"Hopping window","w":["Tumbling window","Delta window","Gold window"],"why":"A hopping window has a size and a hop. When the hop is smaller than the size, windows overlap.","id":"az.i.10.8"},
        {"t":"check","topic":"Azure","q":"Which window shows 'total sales every hour, each hour counted once'?","a":"Tumbling window of 1 hour","w":["Hopping window with a hop of 5 minutes","A Delta table","A consumer group"],"why":"A tumbling window has fixed, non-overlapping blocks, so every event is counted once.","id":"az.i.10.9"},
        {"t":"check","topic":"Azure","q":"What is a consumer group in Event Hubs?","a":"A separate view of the event stream for one reading application","w":["A group of senders sharing one password","A copy of the data in Parquet","A rule to block events"],"why":"Consumer groups let different applications read the same events at their own pace.","id":"az.i.10.10"}
      ] },
      { id: "az.i.11", level: "i", n: 11, title: "Key Vault and security", cards: [
        {"t":"learn","topic":"Azure","title":"Keep secrets out of code","body":"**Azure Key Vault** stores passwords, keys and certificates. Apps and pipelines read them at run time using their own identity, so no secret ever sits in your code or on GitHub.","id":"az.i.11.1"},
        {"t":"choice","topic":"Azure","q":"Which Azure service keeps passwords, keys and certificates safe, so they are not written in your code?","a":"Azure Key Vault","w":["Azure Blob Storage","Azure Monitor","Azure DevOps Boards"],"why":"**Key Vault** stores secrets securely. Your app or pipeline reads them at run time, so no password sits in the code or on GitHub.","id":"az.i.11.2"},
        {"t":"choice","topic":"Azure","q":"Which three kinds of items does Azure Key Vault store?","a":"Secrets, keys and certificates","w":["Tables, views and indexes","Notebooks, jobs and clusters","Windows, hops and tumbles"],"why":"Key Vault stores secrets such as passwords and connection strings, encryption keys, and certificates.","id":"az.i.11.3"},
        {"t":"choice","topic":"Azure","q":"An Azure app needs to read a secret, but you do not want to put any password in its code. What can you give the app?","a":"A managed identity","w":["A copy of your own password","A public link","A bigger cluster"],"why":"A managed identity is an identity Azure manages for the app. The app signs in without any password stored in code.","id":"az.i.11.4"},
        {"t":"choice","topic":"Azure","q":"What does RBAC (role-based access control) do?","a":"Gives people and apps only the roles they need, at a chosen scope","w":["Makes data files smaller","Moves events between partitions","Changes DAX into SQL"],"why":"RBAC assigns roles to identities. The role decides what actions they may do, so each person gets least access.","id":"az.i.11.5"},
        {"t":"pipe","topic":"Azure","q":"A data app needs a database password. Keep it safe with no password in code.","steps":[["APP","App with a managed identity","src"],["AUTH","Azure checks the identity"],["KV","Key Vault"],["SEC","Secret returned to the app"]],"extra":[["COPY","Password pasted into code"]],"why":"The app proves who it is with its managed identity, and Key Vault returns the secret. Pasting passwords in code puts them in files others can see.","id":"az.i.11.6"},
        {"t":"check","topic":"Azure","q":"Which is the safest place for a database password used by your Azure pipeline?","a":"In Key Vault as a secret","w":["Inside the code in GitHub","In a notebook comment","In an email to your team"],"why":"Key Vault controls who can read the secret and records access. Code in GitHub can be seen by many people.","id":"az.i.11.7"},
        {"t":"check","topic":"Azure","q":"Why is a managed identity better than a password stored in an app's settings file?","a":"There is no password to leak or to rotate by hand","w":["It makes the app run faster","It gives the app full access to everything","It lets anyone sign in without a name"],"why":"Azure handles the credentials behind the scenes, so nothing secret sits in code or files.","id":"az.i.11.8"}
      ] },
      { id: "az.i.12", level: "i", n: 12, title: "Parquet and partitioning", cards: [
        {"t":"learn","topic":"Azure","title":"Columnar files","body":"**Parquet** stores data column by column, compressed, so a query reads only the columns it needs. Splitting files into folders by date (**partitioning**) lets queries skip whole folders.","id":"az.i.12.1"},
        {"t":"choice","topic":"Azure","q":"Which file format stores data by column and is best for big analytics tables?","a":"Parquet","w":["CSV","TXT","JSON"],"why":"**Parquet** is columnar and compressed, so a query that needs 2 columns reads only those 2. CSV and JSON store row by row as plain text.","id":"az.i.12.2"},
        {"t":"choice","topic":"Azure","q":"A report only needs 2 columns out of 50. Why is Parquet faster for this?","a":"It reads only those columns, because data is stored by column","w":["It reads every column and then deletes them","It changes the columns to text first","It needs a gateway"],"why":"Columnar storage lets the engine skip columns the query does not need.","id":"az.i.12.3"},
        {"t":"choice","topic":"Azure","q":"Predicate pushdown means what?","a":"The filter is used at the file level so unneeded data is skipped when reading","w":["The data is copied twice","Passwords are pushed into the lake","All filters are removed"],"why":"Parquet keeps statistics, so the reader can skip blocks that cannot match a filter such as `date = 2026-11-15`.","id":"az.i.12.4"},
        {"t":"choice","topic":"Azure","q":"You often filter sales by date. How should you organise the files in the lake?","a":"Partition by date, such as folders per year, month and day","w":["Keep one huge file for everything","Put every row in its own file","Name files with random letters"],"why":"Date folders let a query read only the days it needs and skip the rest.","id":"az.i.12.5"},
        {"t":"choice","topic":"Azure","q":"What is the small-files problem?","a":"Having thousands of tiny files makes reading slow","w":["Files are too large to open","Parquet cannot hold small numbers","Partitions delete old files"],"why":"Each file has a cost to open. Many tiny files are slower than fewer, larger ones.","id":"az.i.12.6"},
        {"t":"check","topic":"Azure","q":"Why does Parquet compress well?","a":"Values in one column are alike, so they pack tightly together","w":["It removes half of your rows","It only stores text","It encrypts the data"],"why":"Similar values stored together compress better than mixed row data.","id":"az.i.12.7"},
        {"t":"check","topic":"Azure","q":"A table is partitioned by date, and the query filters one day. What happens?","a":"Only that day's folder is read","w":["All folders are always read","The table is deleted","The query needs a Key Vault"],"why":"This is partition pruning: files in other date folders are skipped.","id":"az.i.12.8"}
      ] },
      { id: "az.a.13", level: "a", n: 13, title: "Microsoft Fabric", cards: [] },
      { id: "az.a.14", level: "a", n: 14, title: "Governance and Unity Catalog", cards: [] },
      { id: "az.a.15", level: "a", n: 15, title: "CI/CD for data", cards: [] },
      { id: "az.a.16", level: "a", n: 16, title: "Bicep (infrastructure as code)", cards: [
        {"t":"learn","topic":"Azure","title":"Infrastructure as code","body":"Instead of clicking in the portal, describe your resources in a **Bicep** file and deploy it. The same file creates the same setup every time, and it lives in Git like any other code.","id":"az.a.16.1"},
        {"t":"choice","topic":"Azure","q":"Which Azure language lets you write your infrastructure as code, so the same setup can be created again and again?","a":"Bicep","w":["Power BI","Excel","Azure Key Vault"],"why":"**Bicep** files describe resources (storage, networks, VMs). Deploy the file and Azure creates them, the same way every time. Terraform does the same across clouds.","id":"az.a.16.2"}
      ] },
      { id: "az.a.17", level: "a", n: 17, title: "Cost optimisation", cards: [] },
      { id: "az.a.18", level: "a", n: 18, title: "Monitoring", cards: [] }
    ] },
    { id: "de", name: "Data Engineering", topic: "DataEng", blurb: "Move, clean and organise data on any cloud: the core ideas every data engineer uses.", units: [
      { id: "de.b.01", level: "b", n: 1, title: "What a data engineer does", cards: [
        {"t":"learn","topic":"DataEng","title":"What a data engineer does","body":"A **data engineer** moves data from **sources** (apps, files, APIs) to a place where people can use it. The moving path is called a **pipeline**. The people and tools that use the data are **consumers**: analysts, dashboards and ML models.","code":"App / files / API -> Pipeline -> Warehouse -> Dashboard","lang":"text","id":"de.b.01.1"},
        {"t":"choice","topic":"DataEng","q":"What is the main job of a data engineer?","a":"Build pipelines that move and prepare data for others to use","w":["Draw charts for the CEO every morning","Train machine learning models to predict sales","Design the colours of a mobile app"],"why":"Engineers build and run the pipes. Charts are the analyst's work and models are the data scientist's work.","id":"de.b.01.2"},
        {"t":"pipe","topic":"DataEng","q":"Asha's shop app saves orders. Build the pipeline so the manager sees sales on a dashboard.","steps":[["APP","Orders app","src"],["PIPE","Pipeline"],["WH","Warehouse"],["DASH","Dashboard"]],"extra":[["PRNT","Print server"]],"why":"Data flows from the source, through a pipeline, into a store, and then to a consumer. A print server is not part of this flow.","id":"de.b.01.3"},
        {"t":"choice","topic":"DataEng","q":"Ravi uses past customer data to build a model that predicts who will stop buying. Which role is this?","a":"Data scientist","w":["Data engineer","Data analyst","Database backup operator"],"why":"Building predictive models is the data scientist's job. Engineers only supply clean data; analysts explain what already happened.","id":"de.b.01.4"},
        {"t":"output","topic":"DataEng","q":"A data analyst runs this query on an orders table with 3 rows: Guntur, Guntur, Pune. What does it return?","code":"SELECT city, COUNT(*)\nFROM orders\nGROUP BY city\nORDER BY city;","lang":"sql","a":"Guntur 2, Pune 1","w":["Guntur 1, Pune 2","Guntur 3, Pune 3","Guntur, Pune (no counts)"],"why":"GROUP BY makes one row per city and COUNT(*) counts the rows in each group.","id":"de.b.01.5"},
        {"t":"order","topic":"DataEng","q":"Put the pipeline steps in the right order.","items":["Collect data from the source app","Clean and join the data","Store it in the warehouse","Dashboard reads the stored data"],"why":"Data must be collected before it can be cleaned, and it must be stored before anyone can query it.","id":"de.b.01.6"},
        {"t":"choice","topic":"DataEng","q":"Which one is a data consumer (someone or something that uses the data at the end)?","a":"A dashboard that managers open every day","w":["A CSV file exported from the app","The orders app that creates the data","A log file written by a web server"],"why":"Files, apps and logs are sources. Consumers are dashboards, analysts and ML models.","id":"de.b.01.7"},
        {"t":"check","topic":"DataEng","q":"Lakshmi's team needs sales data from an app and a CSV file in one place every day. Who builds the daily flow of that data?","a":"Data engineer","w":["Data analyst","Data scientist","Dashboard viewer"],"why":"Moving and preparing data on a regular basis is the data engineer's job.","id":"de.b.01.8"},
        {"t":"check","topic":"DataEng","q":"What is a pipeline in data engineering?","a":"Automated steps that move data from sources to where it is used","w":["A water pipe that cools the servers","A chart that shows sales","A model that predicts the future"],"why":"A pipeline is a set of steps that moves and prepares data automatically.","id":"de.b.01.9"}
      ] },
      { id: "de.b.02", level: "b", n: 2, title: "ETL vs ELT", cards: [
        {"t":"learn","topic":"DataEng","title":"ETL vs ELT","body":"**ETL** means Extract, Transform, Load: clean the data first, then load it. **ELT** loads the **raw** data first and transforms it **inside the warehouse** with SQL. ELT is common with modern cloud warehouses; ETL fits when data must be cleaned or masked before it is stored.","code":"-- ELT: transform inside the warehouse\nCREATE TABLE clean_orders AS\nSELECT id, UPPER(city) AS city\nFROM raw_orders;","lang":"sql","id":"de.b.02.1"},
        {"t":"choice","topic":"DataEng","q":"What does the T in ETL stand for?","a":"Transform","w":["Transfer","Table","Trigger"],"why":"ETL = Extract, Transform, Load. Transform means cleaning, joining and reshaping data.","id":"de.b.02.2"},
        {"t":"order","topic":"DataEng","q":"Put the ETL steps in the right order.","items":["Extract data from the source","Transform it (clean, join)","Load it into the warehouse"],"why":"In ETL the transform happens before the load. In ELT the last two steps swap.","id":"de.b.02.3"},
        {"t":"pipe","topic":"DataEng","q":"Build an ELT pipeline: raw data is loaded first, then changed inside the warehouse.","steps":[["SRC","Source app","src"],["EXT","Extract"],["LOAD","Load raw data"],["TRN","Transform with SQL"]],"extra":[["FAX","Fax the report"]],"why":"ELT loads raw data before the transform step, and the transform runs inside the warehouse. Faxing is not part of it.","id":"de.b.02.4"},
        {"t":"bug","topic":"DataEng","q":"This ELT step should build clean_orders from the raw table, but it reads the wrong table. Which line is wrong?","code":["CREATE TABLE clean_orders AS","SELECT id, UPPER(city) AS city","FROM clean_orders","WHERE amount > 0;"],"lang":"sql","bad":2,"why":"The step must read from the raw table that was loaded earlier, not from the table it is creating.","id":"de.b.02.5"},
        {"t":"output","topic":"DataEng","q":"The raw_orders table has 3 rows. What does this ELT query return?","code":"SELECT id, UPPER(city)\nFROM raw_orders\nWHERE amount > 0\nORDER BY id;","lang":"sql","a":"1 GUNTUR, 3 PUNE","w":["1 guntur, 3 pune","1 GUNTUR, 2 GUNTUR, 3 PUNE","2 GUNTUR"],"why":"Row 2 has amount 0, so WHERE removes it. UPPER changes the city to capital letters.","id":"de.b.02.6"},
        {"t":"choice","topic":"DataEng","q":"A bank must remove customers' card numbers before data reaches the warehouse. Which approach fits?","a":"ETL: transform (mask) first, then load","w":["ELT: load raw card numbers first and hide them later","Skip the transform step completely","Copy the data to a USB drive"],"why":"When sensitive data must not be stored raw, transform it before loading. That is ETL.","id":"de.b.02.7"},
        {"t":"check","topic":"DataEng","q":"What is the main difference between ELT and ETL?","a":"ELT loads raw data first and transforms it inside the warehouse","w":["ELT never uses SQL","ELT transforms data before extracting it","ELT only works with images"],"why":"The order of Load and Transform is swapped in ELT.","id":"de.b.02.8"},
        {"t":"check","topic":"DataEng","q":"Why do many teams like ELT with a cloud warehouse?","a":"Raw data is kept, so they can re-run or change transforms later","w":["It needs no warehouse at all","It removes the need for any source data","It makes data always real time"],"why":"Since raw data is stored, a changed rule can simply be run again with SQL.","id":"de.b.02.9"}
      ] },
      { id: "de.b.03", level: "b", n: 3, title: "Batch vs streaming", cards: [
        {"t":"learn","topic":"DataEng","title":"Batch vs streaming","body":"**Batch** processing handles data in scheduled chunks, like a nightly sales report. **Streaming** processes each event as it arrives, in seconds or less. The time between an event and its result is the **latency**; streaming has lower latency but is usually more complex to run.","code":"Batch:     all day sales -> 2 AM job -> report\nStreaming: card swipe -> check now -> alert","lang":"text","id":"de.b.03.1"},
        {"t":"choice","topic":"DataEng","q":"Which job is a good fit for batch processing?","a":"A nightly report of the day's sales for each store","w":["Blocking a stolen card while it is being used","Showing a live cricket score every second","Alerting a driver the moment a bus is late"],"why":"A report that is read once a day can wait for a nightly job. The other three need results at once.","id":"de.b.03.2"},
        {"t":"choice","topic":"DataEng","q":"A bank wants to stop card fraud within seconds. Which should it use?","a":"Streaming","w":["A weekly batch job","A monthly report","A manual Excel check"],"why":"Fraud must be caught while it happens, so the data must be processed event by event.","id":"de.b.03.3"},
        {"t":"pipe","topic":"DataEng","q":"Build a streaming pipeline that sends an SMS alert for a suspicious card swipe.","steps":[["SWP","Card swipe","src"],["BUS","Event stream"],["RULE","Fraud check"],["SMS","Alert"]],"extra":[["MON","Monthly report"]],"why":"Each event flows through the stream and is checked immediately. A monthly report is a batch idea.","id":"de.b.03.4"},
        {"t":"order","topic":"DataEng","q":"Put the steps of a nightly batch job in order.","items":["Sales are collected during the day","The job starts at its scheduled time","The job processes all the collected data","Managers read the report next morning"],"why":"Batch jobs wait, collect, run on a schedule and then publish all results together.","id":"de.b.03.5"},
        {"t":"output","topic":"DataEng","q":"A batch job totals sales per day. What does this query return?","code":"SELECT day, SUM(amount)\nFROM sales\nGROUP BY day\nORDER BY day;","lang":"sql","a":"Mon 300, Tue 50","w":["Mon 150, Tue 50","Mon 350","Mon 300, Tue 300"],"why":"Monday has two rows (100 and 200) which add up to 300. Tuesday has one row of 50.","id":"de.b.03.6"},
        {"t":"choice","topic":"DataEng","q":"What does latency mean in a data pipeline?","a":"The delay between an event happening and its result being ready","w":["The size of the data in GB","The number of tables in the warehouse","The number of users of a dashboard"],"why":"Low latency means results come quickly. Streaming aims for low latency; batch accepts higher latency.","id":"de.b.03.7"},
        {"t":"check","topic":"DataEng","q":"Which statement about batch and streaming is true?","a":"Batch works on chunks of data on a schedule; streaming works on events as they arrive","w":["Streaming runs only once a month","Batch always has lower latency than streaming","Batch cannot use SQL"],"why":"Batch = chunks on a schedule. Streaming = continuous, event by event.","id":"de.b.03.8"},
        {"t":"check","topic":"DataEng","q":"Which task needs streaming rather than batch?","a":"A ride app showing live driver location","w":["A yearly tax summary","A weekly sales report","A monthly salary sheet"],"why":"Live locations are useful only for a few seconds, so they must be processed continuously.","id":"de.b.03.9"}
      ] },
      { id: "de.b.04", level: "b", n: 4, title: "Warehouse, lake and lakehouse", cards: [
        {"t":"learn","topic":"DataEng","title":"Warehouse, lake and lakehouse","body":"A **data warehouse** stores clean, structured **tables** for analytics. A **data lake** stores raw **files** of any type (CSV, JSON, images) cheaply. A **lakehouse** keeps files in a lake but adds table features such as transactions, using formats like **Delta Lake** or **Apache Iceberg**.","code":"Warehouse: tables (schema first)\nLake:      raw files (any type)\nLakehouse: lake files + table features","lang":"text","id":"de.b.04.1"},
        {"t":"choice","topic":"DataEng","q":"Which store is best for clean, structured tables used by analysts for reports?","a":"Data warehouse","w":["Data lake only","A photo folder","An email inbox"],"why":"Warehouses are built for structured tables and fast SQL reports.","id":"de.b.04.2"},
        {"t":"choice","topic":"DataEng","q":"Ravi must keep server logs, JSON files and photos in one cheap place, without fixing columns first. What fits?","a":"Data lake","w":["Data warehouse","A spreadsheet","A star schema"],"why":"A lake holds raw files of any type. A warehouse expects structured tables.","id":"de.b.04.3"},
        {"t":"choice","topic":"DataEng","q":"Schema-on-write means the table structure (columns and types) is...","a":"Defined before data is written, and data must match it","w":["Decided only when someone reads the data","Never defined","Copied from the dashboard"],"why":"Warehouses use schema-on-write. Lakes usually use schema-on-read, where structure is applied when reading.","id":"de.b.04.4"},
        {"t":"bug","topic":"DataEng","q":"In a warehouse table with schema-on-write, amount is an INT. One line below would be rejected. Which one?","code":["CREATE TABLE sales (id INT, amount INT);","INSERT INTO sales VALUES (1, 500);","INSERT INTO sales VALUES (2, 'five hundred');","INSERT INTO sales VALUES (3, 250);"],"lang":"sql","bad":2,"why":"Text cannot be stored in an INT column when the schema is enforced when writing.","id":"de.b.04.5"},
        {"t":"order","topic":"DataEng","q":"Put the steps of schema-on-read in order.","items":["Save the raw file in the lake as it is","Decide which columns to read from it","Query the file using those columns"],"why":"With schema-on-read the structure is applied at query time, not when the file is saved.","id":"de.b.04.6"},
        {"t":"choice","topic":"DataEng","q":"What does a lakehouse add on top of plain files in a lake?","a":"Table features such as transactions, using formats like Delta Lake or Apache Iceberg","w":["A paper backup of every file","A ban on structured data","A requirement to delete raw files"],"why":"Open table formats like Delta Lake and Iceberg bring table behaviour to files in a lake.","id":"de.b.04.7"},
        {"t":"check","topic":"DataEng","q":"Which pair is matched correctly?","a":"Lake - raw files of any type","w":["Warehouse - only images","Lakehouse - no tables at all","Lake - schema-on-write only"],"why":"A lake keeps raw files of any type; warehouses and lakehouses add structure.","id":"de.b.04.8"},
        {"t":"check","topic":"DataEng","q":"When is the structure of the data applied in schema-on-read?","a":"When the data is read (queried)","w":["When the source app is installed","Never","When the server is switched off"],"why":"Schema-on-read applies columns and types at query time.","id":"de.b.04.9"}
      ] },
      { id: "de.b.05", level: "b", n: 5, title: "Data modelling: star schema", cards: [
        {"t":"learn","topic":"DataEng","title":"Star schema","body":"A **star schema** has one **fact table** in the middle (events and numbers, like sales amount) and **dimension tables** around it (who, what, where, when). The fact table holds **keys** that point to each dimension. Reports become simple: JOIN the fact to a dimension, then GROUP BY.","code":"SELECT d.city, SUM(f.amount)\nFROM fact_sales f\nJOIN dim_store d ON f.store_id = d.store_id\nGROUP BY d.city;","lang":"sql","id":"de.b.05.1"},
        {"t":"choice","topic":"DataEng","q":"What does a fact table usually store?","a":"Events and numbers, such as each sale and its amount","w":["Only the names of cities","Only passwords","Only images"],"why":"Facts are the measurable events. Descriptions like city or product name go in dimensions.","id":"de.b.05.2"},
        {"t":"choice","topic":"DataEng","q":"Which table is a dimension table?","a":"dim_store: store_id, city, state","w":["fact_sales: sale_id, store_id, amount","A log of every click with timestamps only","A file of raw JSON events"],"why":"Dimensions describe who, what, where or when. They have descriptive columns like city.","id":"de.b.05.3"},
        {"t":"output","topic":"DataEng","q":"Stores: 1 Guntur, 2 Pune. Sales: (store 1, 100), (store 1, 200), (store 2, 50). What does this query return?","code":"SELECT d.city, SUM(f.amount)\nFROM fact_sales f\nJOIN dim_store d ON f.store_id = d.store_id\nGROUP BY d.city\nORDER BY d.city;","lang":"sql","a":"Guntur 300, Pune 50","w":["Guntur 100, Pune 50","Guntur 350, Pune 350","Guntur 2, Pune 1"],"why":"The join matches each sale to its city, then SUM adds the amounts per city.","id":"de.b.05.4"},
        {"t":"bug","topic":"DataEng","q":"This report should show total sales per city, but the join links the wrong columns. Which line is wrong?","code":["SELECT d.city, SUM(f.amount)","FROM fact_sales f","JOIN dim_store d ON f.sale_id = d.store_id","GROUP BY d.city;"],"lang":"sql","bad":2,"why":"The fact table's store_id key must match the dimension's store_id. sale_id identifies the sale, not the store.","id":"de.b.05.5"},
        {"t":"order","topic":"DataEng","q":"Put the steps of answering 'sales per city' in a star schema in order.","items":["Start from the fact table","JOIN the store dimension using store_id","GROUP BY city and SUM the amount","Show one row per city"],"why":"Join the descriptive dimension to the facts first, then group and add up.","id":"de.b.05.6"},
        {"t":"choice","topic":"DataEng","q":"Why do star schemas make reports simple and fast?","a":"Few joins: facts connect straight to each dimension","w":["All data is stored in one text file","They avoid using keys","They store each sale in every dimension"],"why":"A star needs only one join per dimension, so queries stay short and easy to read.","id":"de.b.05.7"},
        {"t":"check","topic":"DataEng","q":"In a star schema, what links a fact row to a dimension row?","a":"A key (such as store_id)","w":["A dashboard colour","A file name","A server password"],"why":"The fact table stores the dimension's key, and a JOIN matches the two.","id":"de.b.05.8"},
        {"t":"check","topic":"DataEng","q":"Which of these belongs in a date dimension (dim_date)?","a":"Month name and year","w":["The sale amount","The customer's phone number","The store's address"],"why":"A date dimension describes the 'when': day, month, year. Amounts are facts.","id":"de.b.05.9"}
      ] },
      { id: "de.b.06", level: "b", n: 6, title: "Pipelines and scheduling", cards: [
        {"t":"learn","topic":"DataEng","title":"Pipelines and scheduling","body":"A pipeline is a set of **tasks** with **dependencies**, drawn as a **DAG** (a graph with arrows and no loops). A scheduler runs it at set times, **retries** failed tasks and sends **alerts**. Re-runs should be **idempotent**: running twice gives the same result. **Apache Airflow** is a common scheduler.","code":"extract >> transform >> load","lang":"python","id":"de.b.06.1"},
        {"t":"choice","topic":"DataEng","q":"What does DAG stand for?","a":"Directed Acyclic Graph","w":["Data Access Group","Daily Automatic Gateway","Direct Archive Gate"],"why":"Tasks have a direction (arrows) and no cycles, so a task never waits for itself.","id":"de.b.06.2"},
        {"t":"pipe","topic":"DataEng","q":"Build the daily sales pipeline in the right task order.","steps":[["EXT","Extract orders","src"],["TRN","Transform"],["LOAD","Load to warehouse"],["ALRT","Alert if failed"]],"extra":[["GAME","Play a game"]],"why":"Each task waits for the one before it. Alerting on failure is part of a good pipeline; a game is not.","id":"de.b.06.3"},
        {"t":"choice","topic":"DataEng","q":"A task is idempotent. What happens if it runs twice for the same day?","a":"The final result is the same as running it once","w":["Every row is duplicated","The warehouse is deleted","The second run always fails"],"why":"Idempotent re-runs are safe. A common mistake is plain INSERT, which adds duplicate rows on a re-run.","id":"de.b.06.4"},
        {"t":"choice","topic":"DataEng","q":"The source API was down for one minute and the extract task failed. What helps most?","a":"Set retries so the task tries again automatically","w":["Delete the whole pipeline","Ignore failures forever","Run every task twice at once"],"why":"Temporary problems often go away. Retries (and an alert if all retries fail) keep the pipeline healthy.","id":"de.b.06.5"},
        {"t":"indent","topic":"DataEng","step":2,"q":"Fix the indentation of this example pipeline config (not tied to any tool).","lang":"yaml","lines":[[0,"pipeline:"],[2,"name: daily_sales"],[2,"schedule: \"0 2 * * *\""],[2,"retries: 2"],[2,"tasks:"],[4,"- extract"],[4,"- load"]],"why":"Settings sit under pipeline, and the list items sit under tasks. YAML uses spaces, never tabs.","id":"de.b.06.6"},
        {"t":"order","topic":"DataEng","q":"A task fails. Put the sensible handling in order.","items":["Task fails","Scheduler retries the task","Retries are used up and it still fails","An alert is sent to the team"],"why":"Retry first for small problems, and alert people only when retries do not fix it.","id":"de.b.06.7"},
        {"t":"check","topic":"DataEng","q":"In Airflow, what does extract >> transform >> load mean?","a":"transform runs after extract, and load runs after transform","w":["All three run at the same moment","load runs first","The tasks are deleted after one run"],"why":"The >> sign sets the order: the task on the left runs before the task on the right.","id":"de.b.06.8"},
        {"t":"check","topic":"DataEng","q":"Why should a pipeline task be idempotent?","a":"So a retry or re-run does not create duplicate or wrong data","w":["So it runs faster than any other task","So it never needs a schedule","So it can skip the warehouse"],"why":"Retries and manual re-runs are normal, so each run must be safe to repeat.","id":"de.b.06.9"}
      ] },
      { id: "de.i.07", level: "i", n: 7, title: "Data quality checks", cards: [
        {"t":"learn","topic":"DataEng","title":"Data quality checks","body":"Bad data in means bad reports out. A pipeline runs **checks** on the data: **not null**, **unique**, **accepted values**, **referential** (every order has a real customer), **row count** against the source and **freshness** (is the data recent?). Check **early**, before loading the warehouse, then **fail** the pipeline or **quarantine** the bad rows.","code":"SELECT COUNT(*)\nFROM orders\nWHERE customer_id IS NULL;","lang":"sql","id":"de.i.07.1"},
        {"t":"choice","topic":"DataEng","q":"Ravi loads orders and finds two rows with the same order_id. Which check would have caught this?","a":"A unique check on order_id","w":["A not-null check on order_id","A freshness check on the table","An accepted-values check on city"],"why":"A unique check finds repeated keys. A not-null check only finds empty values, a common mix-up.","id":"de.i.07.2"},
        {"t":"output","topic":"DataEng","q":"What does this check return?","code":"SELECT COUNT(*)\nFROM orders\nWHERE customer_id IS NULL;","lang":"sql","a":"2","w":["1","3","4"],"why":"Only rows 2 and 3 have an empty customer_id. Use IS NULL, because = NULL never matches.","id":"de.i.07.3"},
        {"t":"output","topic":"DataEng","q":"This duplicate check runs on a table with order_id values 1, 2, 2, 3. What row does it return?","code":"SELECT order_id, COUNT(*)\nFROM orders\nGROUP BY order_id\nHAVING COUNT(*) > 1;","lang":"sql","a":"2 | 2","w":["1 | 1","2 | 1","3 | 2"],"why":"order_id 2 appears twice, so its count is 2. HAVING keeps only groups with more than one row.","id":"de.i.07.4"},
        {"t":"bug","topic":"DataEng","q":"This referential check should list orders whose customer does not exist in customers. It returns the wrong rows. Which line is wrong?","code":["SELECT o.order_id","FROM orders o","LEFT JOIN customers c","  ON o.customer_id = c.customer_id","WHERE o.customer_id IS NULL;"],"lang":"sql","bad":4,"why":"A missing customer shows up as NULL on the customers side (c), not on the orders side.","fix":"WHERE c.customer_id IS NULL;","id":"de.i.07.5"},
        {"t":"pipe","topic":"DataEng","q":"Build a pipeline that stops bad data from reaching the dashboard.","steps":[["EXT","Extract orders","src"],["CHK","Run quality checks"],["LOAD","Load to warehouse"],["BI","Refresh dashboard"]],"extra":[["DEL","Delete the source"]],"why":"Checks run before loading, so bad rows never reach the warehouse. Deleting the source is never part of the flow.","id":"de.i.07.6"},
        {"t":"order","topic":"DataEng","q":"A quality check finds bad rows. Put the sensible handling in order.","items":["Check runs on the staged data","Check finds bad rows","Bad rows go to a quarantine table","The team gets an alert"],"why":"Keep bad rows aside instead of throwing them away, so people can fix and reload them.","id":"de.i.07.7"},
        {"t":"check","topic":"DataEng","q":"Sales data is expected every morning, but the newest row is three days old. Which check catches this?","a":"A freshness check","w":["A unique check","A referential check","An accepted-values check"],"why":"Freshness compares the newest timestamp with the time now. The other checks look at values, not age.","id":"de.i.07.8"},
        {"t":"check","topic":"DataEng","q":"Why run quality checks before loading the warehouse?","a":"So bad data never reaches the reports people trust","w":["So the warehouse needs less storage than before","So the source system can be switched off","So the pipeline never needs a schedule"],"why":"Fixing bad data after it is in reports is harder and hurts trust.","id":"de.i.07.9"}
      ] },
      { id: "de.i.08", level: "i", n: 8, title: "Incremental loads and CDC", cards: [
        {"t":"learn","topic":"DataEng","title":"Incremental loads and CDC","body":"A **full load** copies everything each time. An **incremental load** copies only new or changed rows, using a **watermark** column such as `updated_at` and saving the last value. A simple watermark misses **deletes**, so **CDC** (change data capture) reads the database log to see every insert, update and delete.","code":"SELECT *\nFROM orders\nWHERE updated_at > '2026-11-23 00:00:00';","lang":"sql","id":"de.i.08.1"},
        {"t":"choice","topic":"DataEng","q":"In an incremental load, what is the watermark?","a":"The last updated_at value already loaded, saved for the next run","w":["The name of the target warehouse","A password for the source database","The number of failed tasks"],"why":"The next run asks only for rows newer than the saved watermark.","id":"de.i.08.2"},
        {"t":"output","topic":"DataEng","q":"The saved watermark is 2026-11-23. How many rows does this query pick up?","code":"SELECT COUNT(*)\nFROM orders\nWHERE updated_at > '2026-11-23';","lang":"sql","a":"2","w":["1","3","4"],"why":"Only rows dated after 2026-11-23 count. A row dated exactly 2026-11-23 is not greater than it.","id":"de.i.08.3"},
        {"t":"choice","topic":"DataEng","q":"Lakshmi deletes an order in the source database. What does a simple updated_at watermark do?","a":"It misses the delete, because the row no longer exists","w":["It copies the delete to the warehouse","It marks the row as updated tomorrow","It stops the whole pipeline"],"why":"A deleted row has no updated_at to read. CDC from the database log, or a soft-delete flag, catches deletes.","id":"de.i.08.4"},
        {"t":"bug","topic":"DataEng","q":"This job saves the wrong watermark, so every run reloads old rows. Which line is wrong?","code":["last = read_watermark()","rows = extract(since=last)","load(rows)","save_watermark(last)"],"lang":"python","bad":3,"why":"Saving the old value means the next run starts from the same place. Save the newest updated_at that was loaded (or keep the old one if no rows came).","fix":"save_watermark(max((r[\"updated_at\"] for r in rows), default=last))","id":"de.i.08.5"},
        {"t":"pipe","topic":"DataEng","q":"Build a CDC pipeline from a database to a warehouse table.","steps":[["LOG","Database log","src"],["CDC","Capture changes"],["STG","Staging table"],["MRG","Merge into target"]],"extra":[["CSV","Email a CSV file"]],"why":"CDC reads the log, lands changes in staging, then merges them into the target. Emailing files is not a pipeline step.","id":"de.i.08.6"},
        {"t":"choice","topic":"DataEng","q":"A batch of orders is loaded twice by mistake. Which load style keeps the table correct?","a":"An upsert (MERGE) on order_id","w":["A plain INSERT of every row again","A TRUNCATE of the source table","A new column for each load"],"why":"An upsert updates a row if its key exists and inserts it if not, so reruns are idempotent. Plain INSERT adds duplicates.","id":"de.i.08.7"},
        {"t":"check","topic":"DataEng","q":"Which method sees inserts, updates and deletes from a database?","a":"Change data capture (CDC) from the database log","w":["A watermark on updated_at only","A full load of one column","A row count at midnight"],"why":"CDC reads the log, so it records every kind of change. A watermark misses deletes.","id":"de.i.08.8"},
        {"t":"check","topic":"DataEng","q":"What does an upsert do when the key already exists in the target table?","a":"Updates that row instead of adding a duplicate","w":["Adds a second row with the same key","Deletes the whole table","Skips the pipeline run"],"why":"Upsert means update or insert. It is what makes incremental reruns safe.","id":"de.i.08.9"}
      ] },
      { id: "de.i.09", level: "i", n: 9, title: "Partitioning and file formats", cards: [
        {"t":"learn","topic":"DataEng","title":"Partitioning and file formats","body":"**CSV** and **JSON** are easy to read but big. **Parquet** is **columnar** and compressed, so queries read only the columns they need. **Avro** is **row-based** and suits streams. **Partition** files into date folders like `dt=2026-11-24/`, so a query that filters on date reads fewer files (**partition pruning**).","code":"sales/dt=2026-11-23/part-0001.parquet\nsales/dt=2026-11-24/part-0001.parquet","lang":"text","id":"de.i.09.1"},
        {"t":"choice","topic":"DataEng","q":"Why is Parquet good for analytics queries?","a":"It stores data by column, so a query reads only the columns it needs","w":["It stores each row as one text line","It cannot be compressed","It only works for a single small file"],"why":"Columnar storage skips unused columns. A common mistake is thinking Parquet is a text format like CSV.","id":"de.i.09.2"},
        {"t":"choice","topic":"DataEng","q":"Which binary format is row-based, stores its schema with the data, and is often used for events in streams?","a":"Avro","w":["Parquet","CSV","JSON"],"why":"Avro writes whole records one after another with a schema, which suits streams. Parquet is columnar; CSV and JSON are text with no built-in schema.","id":"de.i.09.3"},
        {"t":"choice","topic":"DataEng","q":"Reports always filter by date. The data is in folders like dt=2026-11-24/. Asha queries one day. What happens?","a":"Only that day's folder is read (partition pruning)","w":["Every folder is read anyway","The query fails without a join","All files are converted to CSV first"],"why":"Partition pruning skips folders that cannot match the filter, so less data is read.","id":"de.i.09.4"},
        {"t":"bug","topic":"DataEng","q":"Queries filter by date, but this write creates millions of tiny folders. Which line is wrong?","code":["df = spark.read.parquet(\"raw/sales/\")","df = df.filter(df.amount > 0)","df.write.partitionBy(\"customer_id\").parquet(\"clean/sales/\")"],"lang":"python","bad":2,"why":"Partition by a column that queries filter on and that has few values, like the date. customer_id has too many values.","fix":"df.write.partitionBy(\"dt\").parquet(\"clean/sales/\")","id":"de.i.09.5"},
        {"t":"indent","topic":"DataEng","step":2,"q":"Fix the indentation of this example table config (not tied to one tool).","lang":"yaml","lines":[[0,"table: sales"],[0,"format: parquet"],[0,"partition_by:"],[2,"- dt"],[0,"storage:"],[2,"path: s3://lake/sales/"],[2,"compression: snappy"]],"why":"The list item sits under partition_by, and the path and compression sit under storage. YAML uses spaces, never tabs.","id":"de.i.09.6"},
        {"t":"pipe","topic":"DataEng","q":"Build a pipeline that turns daily CSV files into fast, partitioned data.","steps":[["RAW","Raw CSV lands","src"],["CNV","Convert to Parquet"],["PRT","Partition by date"],["QRY","Query with a date filter"]],"extra":[["ZIP","Zip all days into one file"]],"why":"Convert, partition, then query with a filter that prunes. One giant zip would be slower to read.","id":"de.i.09.7"},
        {"t":"check","topic":"DataEng","q":"Why is a folder with millions of tiny files a problem?","a":"Opening so many files is slow, even if the data is small","w":["Parquet cannot hold small files","The data becomes unreadable","Partitions are not allowed in lakes"],"why":"Each file has a fixed cost to open and list. Fewer, larger files are faster.","id":"de.i.09.8"},
        {"t":"check","topic":"DataEng","q":"Which column is usually the best partition column for daily reports?","a":"The date the queries filter on","w":["A unique order_id","A free-text comment","The customer's phone number"],"why":"Pick a column that queries filter on and that has a modest number of values. Unique columns create too many partitions.","id":"de.i.09.9"}
      ] },
      { id: "de.i.10", level: "i", n: 10, title: "Orchestration with Airflow", cards: [
        {"t":"learn","topic":"DataEng","title":"Orchestration with Airflow","body":"In **Apache Airflow** a pipeline is a **DAG** written in Python. Each step is a **task** made with an **operator**. Set order with `>>`, set a `schedule`, and add **retries**. Airflow should **schedule and watch** work; heavy processing belongs in Spark or the warehouse. (Airflow 2 imports `from airflow import DAG`.)","code":"from datetime import datetime\nfrom airflow.sdk import DAG\nwith DAG(\"daily_sales\", schedule=\"@daily\", start_date=datetime(2026, 11, 1)) as dag:\n    # extract and load are tasks made with operators\n    extract >> load","lang":"python","id":"de.i.10.1"},
        {"t":"choice","topic":"DataEng","q":"A DAG has a start_date of 1 November and catchup is on. You turn it on on 24 November. What does Airflow do?","a":"Runs the missed schedule intervals since the start date (catchup)","w":["Runs only today's interval and ignores the rest","Deletes all earlier runs","Waits one year before the first run"],"why":"With catchup=True, the scheduler creates runs for past intervals it has not run yet. In Airflow 3 catchup is off by default; a backfill is a re-run you start yourself.","id":"de.i.10.2"},
        {"t":"bug","topic":"DataEng","q":"The load task starts before the extract task finishes. Which line has the wrong order?","code":["with DAG(\"etl\", schedule=\"@daily\", start_date=datetime(2026, 11, 1)) as dag:","    extract = PythonOperator(task_id=\"extract\", python_callable=get_data)","    load = PythonOperator(task_id=\"load\", python_callable=put_data)","    load >> extract"],"lang":"python","bad":3,"why":"The task on the left runs first. Here load would run before extract.","fix":"    extract >> load","id":"de.i.10.3"},
        {"t":"choice","topic":"DataEng","q":"Ravi must process 200 GB of files every night. What is the best use of Airflow?","a":"Airflow starts a Spark or warehouse job and waits for it to finish","w":["Airflow loads all 200 GB into one Python task","Airflow replaces the warehouse","Airflow stores the 200 GB itself"],"why":"Airflow is an orchestrator: it schedules and watches. Heavy data work runs in a processing engine.","id":"de.i.10.4"},
        {"t":"choice","topic":"DataEng","q":"What does retries=2 mean on a task?","a":"A failed task is tried again up to 2 more times","w":["The task runs twice every day","The task is copied to two DAGs","The DAG is deleted after 2 failures"],"why":"Retries handle short problems like a brief network error. After they are used up, the task fails and can alert people.","id":"de.i.10.5"},
        {"t":"order","topic":"DataEng","q":"Put these steps in the order Airflow handles a new DAG.","items":["You save the DAG file in the dags folder","Airflow's DAG processor parses the file","The scheduler creates a DAG run at the scheduled time","Tasks run in dependency order"],"why":"Airflow finds and parses the file first, then the scheduler creates runs, then the tasks run in the order set by >>.","id":"de.i.10.6"},
        {"t":"choice","topic":"DataEng","q":"Why use the run date template {{ ds }} in a file name, like sales_{{ ds }}.csv?","a":"Each run reads or writes its own day, so reruns give the same result","w":["So the DAG runs faster","So retries are turned off","So every run overwrites yesterday's file"],"why":"Using the run's date, not today's date, makes a task idempotent. A backfill then fills the right days.","id":"de.i.10.7"},
        {"t":"check","topic":"DataEng","q":"What is the main job of Airflow in a data platform?","a":"To schedule tasks, run them in order and alert on failure","w":["To store petabytes of data","To replace SQL","To draw dashboards for managers"],"why":"Airflow orchestrates other tools. It does not store or process big data itself.","id":"de.i.10.8"},
        {"t":"check","topic":"DataEng","q":"What does this line mean?","code":"extract >> [clean, validate]","lang":"python","a":"clean and validate both run after extract","w":["extract runs after clean and validate","Only one of the two tasks ever runs","All three tasks must run at the same moment"],"why":"A list on the right means both tasks depend on extract. They may run in parallel once it is done.","id":"de.i.10.9"}
      ] },
      { id: "de.i.11", level: "i", n: 11, title: "Slowly changing dimensions", cards: [
        {"t":"learn","topic":"DataEng","title":"Slowly changing dimensions","body":"Dimension data changes slowly: a customer moves from Guntur to Pune. **Type 1** overwrites the old value, so history is lost. **Type 2** closes the old row and adds a **new row** with `valid_from`, `valid_to` and `is_current`. Each version gets its own **surrogate key**.","code":"-- key | customer | city   | valid_from | valid_to   | current\n-- 1   | C1       | Guntur | 2024-01-01 | 2026-06-30 | 0\n-- 2   | C1       | Pune   | 2026-07-01 | 9999-12-31 | 1","lang":"sql","id":"de.i.11.1"},
        {"t":"choice","topic":"DataEng","q":"Asha's phone number had a typing mistake. You only want the correct number, with no history. Which type fits?","a":"Type 1: overwrite the old value","w":["Type 2: add a new row with dates","Type 2: copy the whole fact table","No type: delete the customer"],"why":"For corrections, or values where history does not matter, Type 1 overwriting is simple and enough.","id":"de.i.11.2"},
        {"t":"choice","topic":"DataEng","q":"Asha moves from Guntur to Pune. In Type 2, what happens?","a":"The old row is closed and a new row for Pune is added","w":["The Guntur row is overwritten with Pune","The customer is removed from the table","The fact table is deleted and rebuilt"],"why":"Type 2 keeps every version, so old sales still show the city at that time.","id":"de.i.11.3"},
        {"t":"output","topic":"DataEng","q":"Which city does this query return for the customer's current row?","code":"SELECT city\nFROM dim_customer\nWHERE customer_id = 'C1'\n  AND is_current = 1;","lang":"sql","a":"Pune","w":["Guntur","Both Guntur and Pune","No rows"],"why":"Only the newest version has is_current = 1. The Guntur row was closed with is_current = 0.","id":"de.i.11.4"},
        {"t":"output","topic":"DataEng","q":"A sale happened on 2026-03-10. Which city does this join show for it?","code":"SELECT d.city\nFROM fact_sales f\nJOIN dim_customer d\n  ON f.customer_id = d.customer_id\n AND f.sale_date BETWEEN d.valid_from AND d.valid_to;","lang":"sql","a":"Guntur","w":["Pune","Guntur and Pune","No rows"],"why":"March 2026 falls inside the Guntur row's dates (2024-01-01 to 2026-06-30), so the sale keeps the city of that time.","id":"de.i.11.5"},
        {"t":"bug","topic":"DataEng","q":"This statement should close the old row, but it leaves it marked as current. Which line is wrong?","code":["UPDATE dim_customer","SET valid_to = '2026-06-30',","    is_current = 1","WHERE customer_id = 'C1'","  AND is_current = 1;"],"lang":"sql","bad":2,"why":"A closed row must no longer be current, so is_current becomes 0.","fix":"    is_current = 0","id":"de.i.11.6"},
        {"t":"pipe","topic":"DataEng","q":"Build the Type 2 load for a customer dimension.","steps":[["STG","Staged changes","src"],["CMP","Compare with current rows"],["CLS","Close the old row"],["INS","Insert the new version"]],"extra":[["DEL","Delete all history"]],"why":"Find what changed, close the old version, then add the new one. Deleting history defeats Type 2.","id":"de.i.11.7"},
        {"t":"check","topic":"DataEng","q":"Why do Type 2 dimensions use a surrogate key?","a":"Each version of the same customer needs its own unique key","w":["Because customer_id can never be repeated in any table","To make the fact table smaller than the dimension","To avoid using a date at all"],"why":"The business id C1 appears in several rows, so each version gets its own key that fact rows can point to.","id":"de.i.11.8"},
        {"t":"check","topic":"DataEng","q":"Which column shows the version that is valid today in a Type 2 dimension?","a":"is_current = 1 (or valid_to far in the future)","w":["The smallest customer_key","The oldest valid_from","The row with the longest city name"],"why":"The newest version is flagged current and has an open-ended valid_to. The oldest row is the first version, not the latest.","id":"de.i.11.9"}
      ] },
      { id: "de.i.12", level: "i", n: 12, title: "Spark basics", cards: [
        {"t":"learn","topic":"DataEng","title":"Spark basics","body":"**Apache Spark** processes big data on many machines. The **driver** plans the job and **executors** do the work on **partitions** of the data. Transformations like `filter` are **lazy**, so nothing runs until an **action** like `count()`, `show()` or `write`.","code":"df = spark.read.parquet(\"sales/\")\ndf.filter(df.amount > 0).groupBy(\"city\").count().show()","lang":"python","id":"de.i.12.1"},
        {"t":"choice","topic":"DataEng","q":"In Spark, what do the executors do?","a":"They run the tasks on partitions of the data","w":["They plan the job and hold the final result","They give machines to applications, like a cluster manager","They read the code and turn it into a plan"],"why":"The driver plans and coordinates. Executors on worker machines do the real processing.","id":"de.i.12.2"},
        {"t":"choice","topic":"DataEng","q":"Which of these is a Spark action, so it starts the job?","a":"count()","w":["filter()","select()","groupBy()"],"why":"filter, select and groupBy are lazy transformations. An action such as count() makes Spark run the plan.","id":"de.i.12.3"},
        {"t":"bug","topic":"DataEng","q":"Lakshmi runs this code, but nothing prints and no job runs. Which line is wrong?","code":["from pyspark.sql import functions as F","df = spark.read.parquet(\"sales/\")","totals = df.groupBy(\"city\").agg(F.sum(\"amount\").alias(\"total\"))","totals.show"],"lang":"python","bad":3,"why":"`totals.show` without brackets only names the method. Call it with () to run the action.","fix":"totals.show()","id":"de.i.12.4"},
        {"t":"choice","topic":"DataEng","q":"Which step usually causes an expensive shuffle?","a":"A groupBy on a large DataFrame, because rows with the same key move across the network","w":["A select of two columns","A filter on one column","A withColumn that adds a new column"],"why":"Shuffles move data between executors, which is slow. select, filter and withColumn work inside each partition.","id":"de.i.12.5"},
        {"t":"order","topic":"DataEng","q":"Put what happens in this PySpark job in order.","items":["You write transformations such as filter","Spark records them as a plan and does nothing yet","You call an action such as count()","Executors run the tasks and the result returns"],"why":"Lazy evaluation lets Spark see the whole plan first and optimise it before running.","id":"de.i.12.6"},
        {"t":"pipe","topic":"DataEng","q":"Build a simple Spark job that writes city totals.","steps":[["READ","Read Parquet","src"],["FLT","Filter rows"],["AGG","Group and aggregate"],["WRT","Write the result"]],"extra":[["COLL","Collect all rows to the driver"]],"why":"Read, filter, aggregate and write. Collecting a huge result into the driver can crash it.","id":"de.i.12.7"},
        {"t":"check","topic":"DataEng","q":"What is a Spark partition?","a":"A chunk of the data that one task processes","w":["A copy of the Python script","A folder on the driver's laptop","A type of SQL table"],"why":"Spark splits data into partitions so many tasks can work at the same time.","id":"de.i.12.8"},
        {"t":"check","topic":"DataEng","q":"Why does Spark wait until an action before running?","a":"It can plan the whole job first and run it efficiently","w":["Because the driver is always asleep","Because transformations are not allowed","Because data is deleted after each step"],"why":"Lazy transformations let Spark join steps together and skip work it does not need.","id":"de.i.12.9"}
      ] },
      { id: "de.a.13", level: "a", n: 13, title: "Data contracts and governance", cards: [] },
      { id: "de.a.14", level: "a", n: 14, title: "Cost and performance", cards: [] },
      { id: "de.a.15", level: "a", n: 15, title: "Streaming with Kafka", cards: [] },
      { id: "de.a.16", level: "a", n: 16, title: "dbt and analytics engineering", cards: [] }
    ] },
    { id: "snow", name: "Snowflake", topic: "Snowflake", blurb: "The cloud data warehouse: load, query and share data with SQL.", units: [
      { id: "snow.b.01", level: "b", n: 1, title: "What Snowflake is", cards: [
        {"t":"learn","topic":"Snowflake","title":"What is Snowflake?","body":"**Snowflake** is a cloud data platform where you store data and ask questions with **SQL**. It runs on **AWS, Azure or Google Cloud**, so you install nothing. **Storage** (where data lives) and **compute** (what runs queries) are **separate**, so each can grow on its own.","code":"SELECT COUNT(*) FROM orders;","lang":"sql","id":"snow.b.01.1"},
        {"t":"choice","topic":"Snowflake","q":"What is Snowflake?","a":"A cloud data platform you query with SQL","w":["A mobile phone brand","A program you install only on your own laptop","A tool that only draws charts"],"why":"Snowflake is a cloud service for storing and analysing data. Nothing is installed on your computer; you use a browser or a client.","id":"snow.b.01.2"},
        {"t":"choice","topic":"Snowflake","q":"Asha says \"Snowflake separates storage and compute.\" What does this mean?","a":"Data is kept in one layer and queries run on a different layer, so each can change on its own","w":["Data and queries must always run on the same machine","Snowflake cannot store data, it only runs queries","Snowflake can only compute when data is on your laptop"],"why":"Storage and compute are separate layers. You can add more compute without moving or copying your data.","id":"snow.b.01.3"},
        {"t":"choice","topic":"Snowflake","q":"Which cloud providers can Snowflake run on?","a":"AWS, Microsoft Azure and Google Cloud","w":["Only AWS","Only your own office server","Only Google Cloud and Azure, never AWS"],"why":"Snowflake is available on all three big clouds. You pick one when you create your account.","id":"snow.b.01.4"},
        {"t":"choice","topic":"Snowflake","q":"Which language do you mainly use to ask questions in Snowflake?","a":"SQL","w":["Only Java","Only HTML","Only Excel formulas"],"why":"Snowflake works with standard SQL, such as `SELECT`. Other languages can call Snowflake too, but SQL is the main one.","id":"snow.b.01.5"},
        {"t":"order","topic":"Snowflake","q":"Ravi opens Snowflake for the first time. Put his steps in order.","items":["Sign in to Snowflake","Pick a warehouse to run the query","Run a SELECT query","Read the result"],"why":"You sign in, choose compute (a warehouse), then run the query and read the result. A query needs compute to run.","id":"snow.b.01.6"},
        {"t":"bug","topic":"Snowflake","q":"Asha wants to read all rows from `orders`, but the query gives a syntax error. Which line is wrong?","code":["USE WAREHOUSE my_wh;","USE DATABASE shop;","SELECT * FORM orders;"],"lang":"sql","bad":2,"why":"The keyword is `FROM`, not `FORM`. A small spelling mistake in a keyword is a very common error.","id":"snow.b.01.7"},
        {"t":"check","topic":"Snowflake","q":"Why is \"storage and compute are separate\" useful?","a":"You can change the compute power without moving your data","w":["You must copy all data each time you run a query","Your data is deleted when compute stops","Only one person can use the data at a time"],"why":"Because data stays in storage, compute can be added or stopped without touching the data.","id":"snow.b.01.8"},
        {"t":"check","topic":"Snowflake","q":"Which statement about Snowflake is true?","a":"It is a cloud service that runs on AWS, Azure or Google Cloud","w":["It runs only on a single laptop","It stores data and runs queries in the same fixed machine","It works only with spreadsheets"],"why":"Snowflake is delivered as a cloud service on the big three clouds, with separate storage and compute.","id":"snow.b.01.9"}
      ] },
      { id: "snow.b.02", level: "b", n: 2, title: "Virtual warehouses", cards: [
        {"t":"learn","topic":"Snowflake","title":"Virtual warehouses","body":"A **virtual warehouse** is the **compute** that runs your queries. It is **not** where data is stored. Bigger sizes give more power. **AUTO_SUSPEND** (in seconds) pauses an idle warehouse and **AUTO_RESUME** wakes it when a query comes, so you do not pay for idle time.","code":"CREATE WAREHOUSE my_wh\n  WAREHOUSE_SIZE = 'XSMALL'\n  AUTO_SUSPEND = 60\n  AUTO_RESUME = TRUE;","lang":"sql","id":"snow.b.02.1"},
        {"t":"choice","topic":"Snowflake","q":"What is a virtual warehouse in Snowflake?","a":"A cluster of compute that runs queries","w":["A building where tables are stored","A backup copy of the database","A folder for CSV files"],"why":"A warehouse provides compute (CPU and memory) for queries. The common mistake is to think it stores data.","id":"snow.b.02.2"},
        {"t":"choice","topic":"Snowflake","q":"Ravi stops his warehouse. What happens to the data in his tables?","a":"Nothing, the data stays in storage","w":["All tables are deleted","The tables become read-only forever","The data moves to his laptop"],"why":"Data is stored separately from the warehouse. Stopping compute does not remove data.","id":"snow.b.02.3"},
        {"t":"choice","topic":"Snowflake","q":"What does `AUTO_SUSPEND = 60` do?","a":"Suspends the warehouse after 60 seconds of no activity","w":["Deletes the warehouse after 60 days","Limits each query to 60 rows","Runs the warehouse for only 60 queries"],"why":"The value is in seconds. A suspended warehouse stops using compute, which helps control cost.","id":"snow.b.02.4"},
        {"t":"bug","topic":"Snowflake","q":"Asha wants the warehouse to pause after 60 seconds idle, but this statement fails. Which line is wrong?","code":["CREATE WAREHOUSE asha_wh","  WAREHOUSE_SIZE = 'XSMALL'","  AUTO_SUSPEND = TRUE","  AUTO_RESUME = TRUE;"],"lang":"sql","bad":2,"why":"`AUTO_SUSPEND` takes a number of seconds, not TRUE. Use `AUTO_RESUME` for the TRUE/FALSE switch.","id":"snow.b.02.5"},
        {"t":"pipe","topic":"Snowflake","q":"A warehouse is suspended and Lakshmi runs a query. Build what happens.","steps":[["SUSP","Warehouse suspended","src"],["QRY","Query is sent"],["WAKE","Auto-resume starts it"],["RUN","Query runs"]],"extra":[["DROP","Table is dropped"]],"why":"With `AUTO_RESUME = TRUE` the warehouse starts by itself when a query arrives. Dropping a table has nothing to do with it.","id":"snow.b.02.6"},
        {"t":"order","topic":"Snowflake","q":"Put these warehouse sizes in order from smallest to largest.","items":["X-Small","Small","Medium","Large"],"why":"Sizes go X-Small, Small, Medium, Large and up. A bigger size gives more compute power.","id":"snow.b.02.7"},
        {"t":"check","topic":"Snowflake","q":"Which statement about warehouses is true?","a":"A warehouse runs queries but does not store the data","w":["A warehouse stores all the tables and files","A warehouse can run only when it is never suspended","A warehouse is the same thing as a database"],"why":"Warehouse means compute. Databases hold the data.","id":"snow.b.02.8"},
        {"t":"check","topic":"Snowflake","q":"Ravi forgets his warehouse after work. Which setting helps avoid paying for idle compute?","a":"AUTO_SUSPEND","w":["WAREHOUSE_SIZE = 'LARGE'","CREATE SCHEMA","COPY INTO"],"why":"`AUTO_SUSPEND` pauses an idle warehouse. A larger size only adds power and does not stop idle use.","id":"snow.b.02.9"}
      ] },
      { id: "snow.b.03", level: "b", n: 3, title: "Databases, schemas and tables", cards: [
        {"t":"learn","topic":"Snowflake","title":"Databases, schemas, tables","body":"Snowflake data is organised as **database > schema > table**. A **database** holds **schemas**, and a schema holds **tables**. A full name looks like `database.schema.table`. A new database gets a schema called **PUBLIC**.","code":"CREATE DATABASE shop;\nCREATE SCHEMA shop.sales;\nCREATE TABLE shop.sales.orders (id NUMBER, item VARCHAR);","lang":"sql","id":"snow.b.03.1"},
        {"t":"choice","topic":"Snowflake","q":"What is the correct order of the Snowflake object hierarchy, from biggest to smallest?","a":"Database, schema, table","w":["Table, schema, database","Schema, database, table","Database, table, schema"],"why":"A database contains schemas and a schema contains tables.","id":"snow.b.03.2"},
        {"t":"choice","topic":"Snowflake","q":"What does the full name `shop.sales.orders` mean?","a":"Table `orders` in schema `sales` of database `shop`","w":["Schema `orders` in table `sales` of database `shop`","Database `orders` in schema `sales` of table `shop`","Warehouse `shop` running table `orders`"],"why":"The name is `database.schema.table`, read from left to right.","id":"snow.b.03.3"},
        {"t":"choice","topic":"Snowflake","q":"Lakshmi creates a new database `shop`. Which schema exists inside it by default?","a":"PUBLIC","w":["ORDERS","DEFAULT_TABLES","There is no schema until you create one"],"why":"Every new database comes with a schema named `PUBLIC`.","id":"snow.b.03.4"},
        {"t":"choice","topic":"Snowflake","q":"What does `USE DATABASE shop;` do?","a":"Sets `shop` as the current database for your session","w":["Creates a new database called shop","Deletes the database called shop","Starts a warehouse called shop"],"why":"`USE` only sets the context. After it you can write shorter names without the database part.","id":"snow.b.03.5"},
        {"t":"order","topic":"Snowflake","q":"Put the steps in order to create a table inside a new database and schema.","items":["CREATE DATABASE shop;","CREATE SCHEMA shop.sales;","CREATE TABLE shop.sales.orders (id NUMBER);"],"why":"You must create the container first. A schema needs its database and a table needs its schema.","id":"snow.b.03.6"},
        {"t":"bug","topic":"Snowflake","q":"Ravi wants table `orders` in schema `sales` of database `shop`. The last line fails. Which line is wrong?","code":["CREATE DATABASE shop;","CREATE SCHEMA shop.sales;","USE SCHEMA shop.sales;","CREATE TABLE shop.orders (id NUMBER);"],"lang":"sql","bad":3,"why":"A two-part name is `schema.table`, so `shop.orders` looks for a schema named `shop`. After `USE SCHEMA`, a plain table name is enough.","id":"snow.b.03.7"},
        {"t":"check","topic":"Snowflake","q":"Which statement creates a schema named `sales` inside database `shop`?","a":"CREATE SCHEMA shop.sales;","w":["CREATE DATABASE shop.sales;","CREATE TABLE shop.sales;","USE SCHEMA sales.shop;"],"why":"Use `CREATE SCHEMA database.schema`. `USE` only sets context and does not create anything.","id":"snow.b.03.8"},
        {"t":"check","topic":"Snowflake","q":"Where does a table belong in Snowflake?","a":"In a schema, which is inside a database","w":["Directly inside a warehouse","Directly inside an account with no schema","Inside another table"],"why":"Tables live in schemas and schemas live in databases. Warehouses only provide compute.","id":"snow.b.03.9"}
      ] },
      { id: "snow.b.04", level: "b", n: 4, title: "Loading data: stages and COPY INTO", cards: [
        {"t":"learn","topic":"Snowflake","title":"Load data with stages and COPY INTO","body":"A **stage** is a place that holds data files before loading. It can be **internal** (inside Snowflake) or **external** (like cloud storage). Use `PUT` from the **Snowflake CLI** (`snow sql`) or a driver to upload a local file to an internal stage, then `COPY INTO` the table. Formats include **CSV, JSON and Parquet**.","code":"CREATE STAGE my_stage;\nPUT file:///tmp/orders.csv @my_stage;\nCOPY INTO orders\n  FROM @my_stage\n  FILE_FORMAT = (TYPE = CSV SKIP_HEADER = 1);","lang":"sql","id":"snow.b.04.1"},
        {"t":"choice","topic":"Snowflake","q":"What is a stage in Snowflake?","a":"A place where data files wait before they are loaded","w":["A type of warehouse size","A kind of SQL query","A backup of a deleted table"],"why":"Files go to a stage first. Then `COPY INTO` loads them into a table.","id":"snow.b.04.2"},
        {"t":"choice","topic":"Snowflake","q":"Asha has `orders.csv` on her laptop. Which command uploads it to an internal stage? (run from the Snowflake CLI)","a":"PUT","w":["GET","SELECT","UNDROP"],"why":"`PUT` uploads local files to an internal stage; `GET` downloads from it. It cannot be run from a Snowsight worksheet; use the Snowflake CLI (`snow sql`) or a driver.","id":"snow.b.04.3"},
        {"t":"choice","topic":"Snowflake","q":"Which of these file formats can Snowflake load?","a":"CSV, JSON and Parquet","w":["Only Word documents","Only images","Only CSV"],"why":"Snowflake loads structured and semi-structured formats such as CSV, JSON, Avro, ORC and Parquet.","id":"snow.b.04.4"},
        {"t":"choice","topic":"Snowflake","q":"Ravi's files are already in cloud storage. Which stage type points to them without uploading?","a":"An external stage","w":["A suspended stage","A warehouse stage","A result cache stage"],"why":"An external stage refers to files kept in cloud storage. An internal stage keeps files inside Snowflake.","id":"snow.b.04.5"},
        {"t":"pipe","topic":"Snowflake","q":"Build the path of a CSV file into a Snowflake table.","steps":[["FILE","CSV file on laptop","src"],["STG","Stage"],["COPY","COPY INTO"],["TBL","Table"]],"extra":[["UND","UNDROP"]],"why":"The file goes to a stage, then `COPY INTO` loads it into the table. `UNDROP` restores dropped objects and is not part of loading.","id":"snow.b.04.6"},
        {"t":"bug","topic":"Snowflake","q":"Ravi wants to load the staged file into table `orders`, but the last line does the opposite. Which line is wrong?","code":["PUT file:///tmp/orders.csv @my_stage;","COPY INTO @my_stage FROM orders FILE_FORMAT = (TYPE = CSV);"],"lang":"sql","bad":1,"why":"`COPY INTO @stage` unloads data from a table into files. To load, write `COPY INTO table FROM @stage`.","id":"snow.b.04.7"},
        {"t":"check","topic":"Snowflake","q":"Which statement loads staged files into a table?","a":"COPY INTO orders FROM @my_stage;","w":["COPY INTO @my_stage FROM orders;","PUT orders @my_stage;","USE TABLE orders;"],"why":"Loading is `COPY INTO <table> FROM @<stage>`. `PUT` only uploads files to the stage.","id":"snow.b.04.8"},
        {"t":"check","topic":"Snowflake","q":"Asha ran `PUT` and the file is now in the stage. What must she run to fill the table?","a":"COPY INTO orders FROM @my_stage;","w":["PUT orders @my_stage;","USE STAGE my_stage;","SELECT * FROM @my_stage INTO orders;"],"why":"`PUT` only uploads the file. `COPY INTO <table> FROM @<stage>` loads it into the table.","id":"snow.b.04.9"}
      ] },
      { id: "snow.b.05", level: "b", n: 5, title: "Querying with SQL", cards: [
        {"t":"learn","topic":"Snowflake","title":"Querying with SQL","body":"Use `SELECT` to read data, `WHERE` to filter rows, `GROUP BY` to summarise, `ORDER BY` to sort and `LIMIT` to cap rows. Use the **full name** `database.schema.table` or run `USE` first. If you run the **same query** again and the data has not changed, Snowflake can reuse the saved **result**.","code":"SELECT item, SUM(qty)\nFROM shop.sales.orders\nGROUP BY item\nORDER BY item;","lang":"sql","id":"snow.b.05.1"},
        {"t":"choice","topic":"Snowflake","q":"Asha wants only the first 5 rows of a table. Which clause does this?","a":"LIMIT 5","w":["GROUP BY 5","WHERE LIMIT","USE 5"],"why":"`LIMIT` caps the number of rows returned. Put it at the end of the query.","id":"snow.b.05.2"},
        {"t":"choice","topic":"Snowflake","q":"Ravi is not in database `shop`. How can he read table `orders` in schema `sales`?","a":"SELECT * FROM shop.sales.orders;","w":["SELECT * FROM orders.sales.shop;","SELECT * FROM shop WHERE orders;","SELECT shop.sales FROM orders;"],"why":"A full name is `database.schema.table`. It works even if you have not run `USE`.","id":"snow.b.05.3"},
        {"t":"choice","topic":"Snowflake","q":"Lakshmi runs the same query twice and the table data did not change. What can Snowflake do the second time?","a":"Reuse the saved result from the first run","w":["Always delete the table first","Refuse to run the query","Ask her to load the data again"],"why":"Snowflake can return a persisted (cached) result when the query is the same and the data has not changed.","id":"snow.b.05.4"},
        {"t":"output","topic":"Snowflake","q":"The `orders` table has rows: (1, pen, 10), (2, book, 3), (3, pen, 5), (4, bag, 1). What does this return?","code":"SELECT item, SUM(qty) AS total\nFROM orders\nGROUP BY item\nORDER BY item;","lang":"sql","a":"bag 1, book 3, pen 15","w":["pen 10, book 3, bag 1","bag 1, book 3, pen 5","pen 15, book 3, bag 1"],"why":"`GROUP BY item` adds the quantities per item: pen is 10 + 5 = 15. `ORDER BY item` sorts bag, book, pen.","id":"snow.b.05.5"},
        {"t":"output","topic":"Snowflake","q":"Same `orders` table. What does this return?","code":"SELECT item\nFROM orders\nWHERE qty > 4\nORDER BY id\nLIMIT 1;","lang":"sql","a":"pen","w":["book","bag","pen and pen"],"why":"`WHERE qty > 4` keeps rows 1 and 3 (both pen). `ORDER BY id` puts id 1 first and `LIMIT 1` keeps one row.","id":"snow.b.05.6"},
        {"t":"bug","topic":"Snowflake","q":"Asha wants the number of orders per item, but this query fails. Which line is wrong?","code":["SELECT item, COUNT(*)","FROM orders","GROUP BY qty;"],"lang":"sql","bad":2,"why":"A column next to an aggregate like `COUNT(*)` must be in `GROUP BY`. Here `item` is selected, so group by `item`.","id":"snow.b.05.7"},
        {"t":"check","topic":"Snowflake","q":"Which clause filters rows BEFORE they are grouped?","a":"WHERE","w":["ORDER BY","LIMIT","USE"],"why":"`WHERE` filters rows first, then `GROUP BY` groups what is left. `ORDER BY` only sorts the result.","id":"snow.b.05.8"},
        {"t":"check","topic":"Snowflake","q":"What is the full name format for a table in Snowflake?","a":"database.schema.table","w":["table.schema.database","warehouse.table","schema.database.table"],"why":"Names go from big to small: database, schema, table.","id":"snow.b.05.9"}
      ] },
      { id: "snow.b.06", level: "b", n: 6, title: "Time Travel and cloning", cards: [
        {"t":"learn","topic":"Snowflake","title":"Time Travel and cloning","body":"**Time Travel** lets you query old data with `AT` or `BEFORE`, and bring back a dropped table with `UNDROP TABLE`. It works only inside the **retention period**. `CLONE` makes a **zero-copy** copy: no data is copied at first, so it is fast and uses almost no extra storage.","code":"SELECT * FROM orders AT(OFFSET => -60*5);\nUNDROP TABLE orders;\nCREATE TABLE orders_test CLONE orders;","lang":"sql","id":"snow.b.06.1"},
        {"t":"choice","topic":"Snowflake","q":"What does `SELECT * FROM orders AT(OFFSET => -60*5);` return?","a":"The table as it was 5 minutes ago","w":["The table as it will be in 5 minutes","Only 5 rows of the table","A copy of the table saved to a stage"],"why":"`OFFSET` is in seconds in the past, so `-60*5` means 300 seconds (5 minutes) ago.","id":"snow.b.06.2"},
        {"t":"choice","topic":"Snowflake","q":"Ravi dropped table `orders` by mistake today. Which command can bring it back?","a":"UNDROP TABLE orders;","w":["RESTORE orders;","CREATE TABLE orders CLONE orders;","COPY INTO orders;"],"why":"`UNDROP TABLE` restores a dropped table, if it is still inside the Time Travel retention period.","id":"snow.b.06.3"},
        {"t":"choice","topic":"Snowflake","q":"Asha runs `CREATE TABLE orders_test CLONE orders;`. What does \"zero-copy\" mean?","a":"No data is physically copied at first; the clone shares the same stored data","w":["The new table is always empty","The data is copied to her laptop","The original table is deleted"],"why":"A clone starts by pointing to the same data. Storage grows only when you change one of the tables.","id":"snow.b.06.4"},
        {"t":"choice","topic":"Snowflake","q":"Lakshmi changes rows in the clone `orders_test`. What happens to the original `orders`?","a":"Nothing, changes to a clone do not affect the original","w":["The original changes too","The original is dropped","The original becomes read-only"],"why":"After cloning, the two tables are independent. This is why clones are good for safe testing.","id":"snow.b.06.5"},
        {"t":"bug","topic":"Snowflake","q":"Ravi dropped `orders` and wants it back. This command gives a syntax error. Which line is wrong?","code":["-- orders was dropped by mistake","UNDROP orders;"],"lang":"sql","bad":1,"why":"The command needs the object type: `UNDROP TABLE`. Also remember it works only within the retention period.","id":"snow.b.06.6"},
        {"t":"pipe","topic":"Snowflake","q":"Asha wants to test changes safely on a copy of her table. Build the steps.","steps":[["PROD","Production table","src"],["CLN","CLONE it"],["TEST","Test changes on the clone"],["SAFE","Original stays unchanged"]],"extra":[["PUT","PUT file"]],"why":"A clone is a zero-copy, independent table, so tests do not touch the original. `PUT` is for uploading files and is not needed here.","id":"snow.b.06.7"},
        {"t":"check","topic":"Snowflake","q":"Which is TRUE about Time Travel?","a":"It works only within the retention period","w":["It keeps every old version forever","It works only for views, never tables","It needs a Large warehouse"],"why":"After the retention period ends, old data can no longer be queried or restored with Time Travel.","id":"snow.b.06.8"},
        {"t":"check","topic":"Snowflake","q":"What is the main benefit of `CLONE`?","a":"A fast copy that does not copy the data at first","w":["It deletes the original after copying","It loads files from a stage","It makes the warehouse bigger"],"why":"Because it is zero-copy, a clone is created quickly and uses extra storage only when data changes.","id":"snow.b.06.9"}
      ] },
      { id: "snow.i.07", level: "i", n: 7, title: "Roles and access control", cards: [
        {"t":"learn","topic":"Snowflake","title":"Roles and access control","body":"In Snowflake you normally grant **privileges to a role**, then grant the **role to a user**. Users switch roles with `USE ROLE`. Use **SYSADMIN** to create objects and keep **ACCOUNTADMIN** for rare admin work.","code":"CREATE ROLE analyst;\nGRANT USAGE ON WAREHOUSE my_wh TO ROLE analyst;\nGRANT ROLE analyst TO USER asha;","lang":"sql","id":"snow.i.07.1"},
        {"t":"choice","topic":"Snowflake","q":"In Snowflake role-based access control (RBAC), what is the recommended grantee for privileges such as SELECT on a table?","a":"A role","w":["A warehouse","A schema","A stage"],"why":"Grant privileges to roles, then grant roles to users. Snowflake can also grant some privileges straight to a user, but roles are the recommended way.","id":"snow.i.07.2"},
        {"t":"bug","topic":"Snowflake","q":"Ravi wants the role analyst to read the `orders` table, but Snowflake rejects one line. Which line is wrong?","code":["CREATE ROLE analyst;","GRANT USAGE ON TABLE shop.public.orders TO ROLE analyst;","GRANT ROLE analyst TO USER asha;"],"lang":"sql","bad":1,"why":"To read a table a role needs `SELECT`. `USAGE` is a privilege for warehouses, databases and schemas, not tables.","fix":"GRANT SELECT ON TABLE shop.public.orders TO ROLE analyst;","id":"snow.i.07.3"},
        {"t":"order","topic":"Snowflake","q":"Lakshmi sets up a new analyst. Put the steps in order.","items":["Create the role analyst","Grant privileges to the role","Grant the role to the user","The user runs USE ROLE analyst"],"why":"Build the role first, give it privileges, then give the role to the user. The user then switches to it.","id":"snow.i.07.4"},
        {"t":"choice","topic":"Snowflake","q":"Which system role should you use for daily work like creating tables and warehouses?","a":"SYSADMIN","w":["ACCOUNTADMIN","PUBLIC","SECURITYADMIN"],"why":"SYSADMIN creates and manages objects. ACCOUNTADMIN is the most powerful role and should be used only when really needed.","id":"snow.i.07.5"},
        {"t":"choice","topic":"Snowflake","q":"Asha has the role analyst but still cannot run a query. The query needs compute. Which grant is missing?","a":"GRANT USAGE ON WAREHOUSE my_wh TO ROLE analyst;","w":["GRANT SELECT ON WAREHOUSE my_wh TO ROLE analyst;","GRANT ROLE my_wh TO USER asha;","GRANT USAGE ON ROLE analyst TO my_wh;"],"why":"A role needs the `USAGE` privilege on a warehouse to run queries on it. `SELECT` is for tables and views.","id":"snow.i.07.6"},
        {"t":"choice","topic":"Snowflake","q":"What does the command `USE ROLE sysadmin;` do?","a":"Makes sysadmin the active role for your session","w":["Creates a new role named sysadmin","Deletes all roles except sysadmin","Gives every user the sysadmin role"],"why":"`USE ROLE` only switches which of your granted roles is active in the current session. It does not create or grant anything.","id":"snow.i.07.7"},
        {"t":"check","topic":"Snowflake","q":"Which best follows least privilege for Snowflake daily work?","a":"Use a role that has only the privileges you need, not ACCOUNTADMIN","w":["Always sign in with ACCOUNTADMIN so nothing is blocked","Grant every privilege to PUBLIC","Share one user login with the whole team"],"why":"Give each person only the access they need. ACCOUNTADMIN for daily tasks is risky, and PUBLIC is given to every user.","id":"snow.i.07.8"},
        {"t":"check","topic":"Snowflake","q":"How does a user get the privileges of the role analyst?","a":"GRANT ROLE analyst TO USER asha;","w":["GRANT USER asha TO ROLE analyst;","GRANT analyst ON asha;","CREATE USER analyst;"],"why":"The syntax is `GRANT ROLE <role> TO USER <user>`. The role comes first, then the user.","id":"snow.i.07.9"}
      ] },
      { id: "snow.i.08", level: "i", n: 8, title: "Semi-structured data (VARIANT)", cards: [
        {"t":"learn","topic":"Snowflake","title":"Semi-structured data with VARIANT","body":"A **VARIANT** column can hold JSON. Use `:` to reach a key, `.` to go deeper, and `::string` to **cast** the result. Without a cast, text values show in double quotes.","code":"SELECT src:customer.name::string AS name\nFROM raw_orders;","lang":"sql","id":"snow.i.08.1"},
        {"t":"choice","topic":"Snowflake","q":"Which column type stores a JSON document in Snowflake?","a":"VARIANT","w":["VARCHAR(1)","BOOLEAN","DATE"],"why":"`VARIANT` holds semi-structured data like JSON. You can then query inside it with path syntax.","id":"snow.i.08.2"},
        {"t":"choice","topic":"Snowflake","q":"The column `src` holds `{\"customer\":{\"name\":\"Asha\"}}`. Which query returns the name as plain text?","a":"SELECT src:customer.name::string FROM raw_orders;","w":["SELECT src.customer.name::int FROM raw_orders;","SELECT src->customer->name FROM raw_orders;","SELECT customer.name FROM src;"],"why":"Use `column:key.key` for the path and `::string` to cast. `->` is not Snowflake path syntax.","id":"snow.i.08.3"},
        {"t":"choice","topic":"Snowflake","q":"Ravi has a JSON string. Which function turns it into a VARIANT value?","a":"PARSE_JSON","w":["TO_DATE","SPLIT_PART","JSON_TO_ROWS"],"why":"`PARSE_JSON` reads a JSON string and returns a VARIANT. It is often used when inserting JSON into a VARIANT column.","id":"snow.i.08.4"},
        {"t":"bug","topic":"Snowflake","q":"Asha wants one row for each item in the array `src:items`, but this query gives an 'invalid identifier' error. Which line is wrong?","code":["SELECT f.value:name::string","FROM raw_orders,","  LATERAL FLATTEN(input => src.items) f;"],"lang":"sql","bad":2,"why":"The first step from a VARIANT column into its JSON uses `:`, so write `src:items`. With `src.items` Snowflake looks for a table named src.","fix":"  LATERAL FLATTEN(input => src:items) f;","id":"snow.i.08.5"},
        {"t":"order","topic":"Snowflake","q":"Lakshmi loads JSON orders and reads the customer names. Put the steps in order.","items":["Create a table with a VARIANT column","Load the JSON into that column","Query a path such as src:customer.name","Cast the result with ::string"],"why":"First you need the column, then the data. Then you read a path and cast it to the type you want.","id":"snow.i.08.6"},
        {"t":"choice","topic":"Snowflake","q":"What does `LATERAL FLATTEN` do with a JSON array?","a":"Turns each array element into its own row","w":["Sorts the array alphabetically","Deletes empty elements from the table","Joins the array into one long string"],"why":"`FLATTEN` expands an array into rows, and `LATERAL` lets it use the current row of the table. Each element is then available as `value`.","id":"snow.i.08.7"},
        {"t":"check","topic":"Snowflake","q":"Why is the result of `src:customer.name` shown as \"Asha\" with double quotes?","a":"It is still a VARIANT value, so it needs a cast like ::string","w":["The name has a spelling error","Snowflake always adds quotes to every column","The table is locked"],"why":"VARIANT text values show in double quotes. Add `::string` to get a normal text value.","id":"snow.i.08.8"},
        {"t":"check","topic":"Snowflake","q":"Which part of `src:items[0].price` picks the first item of the array?","a":"[0]","w":[":items",".price","src"],"why":"Array positions start at 0 and are written in square brackets. So `[0]` is the first element.","id":"snow.i.08.9"}
      ] },
      { id: "snow.i.09", level: "i", n: 9, title: "Snowpipe", cards: [
        {"t":"learn","topic":"Snowflake","title":"Snowpipe: continuous loading","body":"**Snowpipe** loads files from a stage soon after they arrive. You create a **pipe** that holds a `COPY INTO` statement. Snowpipe uses **serverless** compute managed by Snowflake, so you do not name a warehouse.","code":"CREATE PIPE orders_pipe AUTO_INGEST = TRUE AS\n  COPY INTO orders FROM @orders_stage\n  FILE_FORMAT = (TYPE = 'CSV');","lang":"sql","id":"snow.i.09.1"},
        {"t":"choice","topic":"Snowflake","q":"When is Snowpipe a better fit than a bulk `COPY INTO` run once a day?","a":"Small files keep arriving all day and you want them loaded soon","w":["You load one huge file once a year","You never want to load any files","You want to run `PUT` from your laptop"],"why":"Snowpipe is for continuous loading of files as they arrive. Bulk `COPY INTO` suits scheduled loads.","id":"snow.i.09.2"},
        {"t":"choice","topic":"Snowflake","q":"Which compute does Snowpipe use to load files?","a":"Serverless compute managed by Snowflake","w":["Your warehouse named in the pipe","Your own laptop","A Kubernetes cluster you create"],"why":"Snowpipe does not use your virtual warehouse. Snowflake provides the compute and charges for it separately.","id":"snow.i.09.3"},
        {"t":"bug","topic":"Snowflake","q":"Ravi tries to create a pipe, but Snowflake rejects the statement. Which line is wrong?","code":["CREATE PIPE orders_pipe WAREHOUSE = my_wh","AS COPY INTO orders FROM @orders_stage","  FILE_FORMAT = (TYPE = 'CSV');"],"lang":"sql","bad":0,"why":"`CREATE PIPE` has no `WAREHOUSE` option. Snowpipe uses Snowflake-managed compute.","fix":"CREATE PIPE orders_pipe","id":"snow.i.09.4"},
        {"t":"pipe","topic":"Snowflake","q":"Build the auto-ingest flow: a CSV file lands in an S3 bucket.","steps":[["FILE","File lands in the stage","src"],["EVT","Event notification sent"],["PIPE","Pipe runs its COPY"],["TBL","Rows appear in the table"]],"extra":[["WH","Your warehouse starts"]],"why":"The cloud sends an event when a file arrives, and the pipe runs its `COPY INTO`. Your own warehouse is not part of Snowpipe.","id":"snow.i.09.5"},
        {"t":"choice","topic":"Snowflake","q":"What does `AUTO_INGEST = TRUE` rely on?","a":"Event notifications from the cloud storage about new files","w":["A task that runs every second","A manual PUT command from each user","Time Travel on the stage"],"why":"With auto-ingest, the cloud storage tells Snowpipe when new files arrive, so loading starts without a manual call.","id":"snow.i.09.6"},
        {"t":"order","topic":"Snowflake","q":"Lakshmi sets up Snowpipe for a new feed. Put the steps in order.","items":["Create the target table and the external stage","Create the pipe with AUTO_INGEST = TRUE","Run SHOW PIPES to get the notification_channel","Set up S3 event notifications to that channel"],"why":"The pipe refers to the stage and table, so they come first. SHOW PIPES gives the queue (notification_channel) that the bucket events must be sent to.","id":"snow.i.09.7"},
        {"t":"check","topic":"Snowflake","q":"What is inside a pipe definition?","a":"A COPY INTO statement","w":["A virtual warehouse","A list of users","A Time Travel setting"],"why":"A pipe is a named wrapper around a `COPY INTO` statement that Snowpipe runs for new files.","id":"snow.i.09.8"},
        {"t":"check","topic":"Snowflake","q":"Asha loads one big CSV by hand once a month. Which is the simpler choice?","a":"A bulk COPY INTO on a warehouse","w":["A Snowpipe with auto-ingest","A stream on the stage","A resource monitor"],"why":"Snowpipe is for files that keep arriving. A one-time or scheduled load is fine with plain `COPY INTO`.","id":"snow.i.09.9"}
      ] },
      { id: "snow.i.10", level: "i", n: 10, title: "Streams and tasks", cards: [
        {"t":"learn","topic":"Snowflake","title":"Streams and tasks","body":"A **stream** records the changes (inserts, updates, deletes) made to a table. A **task** runs SQL on a **schedule** or after another task. A new task starts **suspended**, so you must run `ALTER TASK ... RESUME`.","code":"CREATE STREAM orders_stream ON TABLE orders;\nSELECT * FROM orders_stream;","lang":"sql","id":"snow.i.10.1"},
        {"t":"choice","topic":"Snowflake","q":"What does a stream on a table record?","a":"The changes made to the table rows","w":["A copy of the whole table every minute","The list of users who logged in","The size of the warehouse"],"why":"A stream tracks change data (inserts, updates, deletes) after a point in time. It is not a full copy of the table.","id":"snow.i.10.2"},
        {"t":"choice","topic":"Snowflake","q":"Which column in a stream tells you if a row was added or removed?","a":"METADATA$ACTION","w":["METADATA$SIZE","ROW_TYPE","STREAM_ID"],"why":"`METADATA$ACTION` shows INSERT or DELETE. An update appears as a DELETE and an INSERT of the new values.","id":"snow.i.10.3"},
        {"t":"bug","topic":"Snowflake","q":"Asha tries to create a stream on her table, but gets a syntax error. Which line is wrong?","code":["CREATE STREAM orders_stream ON orders;"],"lang":"sql","bad":0,"why":"The syntax is `ON TABLE <name>`. The word `TABLE` is required.","fix":"CREATE STREAM orders_stream ON TABLE orders;","id":"snow.i.10.4"},
        {"t":"bug","topic":"Snowflake","q":"Ravi created a task, but it never runs. Which line is the problem?","code":["CREATE TASK load_orders","  WAREHOUSE = my_wh","  SCHEDULE = '5 MINUTE'","AS INSERT INTO orders_copy SELECT * FROM orders;","ALTER TASK load_orders SUSPEND;"],"lang":"sql","bad":4,"why":"A new task starts suspended and only runs after `RESUME`. Here it is suspended again.","fix":"ALTER TASK load_orders RESUME;","id":"snow.i.10.5"},
        {"t":"pipe","topic":"Snowflake","q":"Build a simple incremental pipeline inside Snowflake.","steps":[["ROWS","Rows change in orders","src"],["STRM","Stream records the changes"],["TASK","Task runs on a schedule"],["MRG","MERGE updates the target"]],"extra":[["PIPE","Snowpipe loads files"]],"why":"The stream captures changes and a task reads them on schedule to MERGE into the target. Snowpipe is for loading files, not this flow.","id":"snow.i.10.6"},
        {"t":"choice","topic":"Snowflake","q":"How can a task run only when its stream has new data?","a":"Add WHEN SYSTEM$STREAM_HAS_DATA('orders_stream')","w":["Add WHERE STREAM = TRUE","Set AUTO_SUSPEND on the stream","Use GRANT STREAM TO TASK"],"why":"The `WHEN` clause with `SYSTEM$STREAM_HAS_DATA` skips runs when there is nothing new, which saves compute.","id":"snow.i.10.7"},
        {"t":"check","topic":"Snowflake","q":"How do you make a task run after another task finishes?","a":"Create it with AFTER parent_task","w":["Give it a smaller warehouse","Create it with ON TABLE parent_task","Use LATERAL FLATTEN"],"why":"The `AFTER` option makes a task depend on another, so they run as a chain.","id":"snow.i.10.8"},
        {"t":"check","topic":"Snowflake","q":"You just ran CREATE TASK with a SCHEDULE. What must you do before it runs?","a":"ALTER TASK ... RESUME","w":["Nothing, it starts at once","DROP the stream","GRANT ROLE to the task"],"why":"Tasks are created suspended. `ALTER TASK <name> RESUME` starts the schedule.","id":"snow.i.10.9"}
      ] },
      { id: "snow.i.11", level: "i", n: 11, title: "Performance and caching", cards: [
        {"t":"learn","topic":"Snowflake","title":"Performance and caching","body":"Snowflake keeps table data in **micro-partitions** and skips those a query does not need (**pruning**). Repeating a query on unchanged data can use the **result cache**. A running warehouse also keeps a **local disk cache**, which is lost when it suspends.","code":"ALTER TABLE sales CLUSTER BY (sale_date);","lang":"sql","id":"snow.i.11.1"},
        {"t":"choice","topic":"Snowflake","q":"Ravi runs the same query twice and the data has not changed. What can help the second run?","a":"The result cache","w":["A bigger monitor","The Time Travel stream","A new schema"],"why":"Snowflake can return saved results for a repeated query when the data has not changed. This needs no warehouse work.","id":"snow.i.11.2"},
        {"t":"choice","topic":"Snowflake","q":"What is pruning?","a":"Skipping micro-partitions that cannot contain the rows you need","w":["Deleting old rows from a table","Removing unused warehouses","Shortening column names"],"why":"Snowflake keeps min and max values per micro-partition and ignores the ones that do not match your filter. Less data is scanned.","id":"snow.i.11.3"},
        {"t":"bug","topic":"Snowflake","q":"Asha wants to add a clustering key to a very large table, but gets a syntax error. Which line is wrong?","code":["ALTER TABLE sales CLUSTER ON (sale_date);"],"lang":"sql","bad":0,"why":"The syntax is `CLUSTER BY (...)`, not `CLUSTER ON`.","fix":"ALTER TABLE sales CLUSTER BY (sale_date);","id":"snow.i.11.4"},
        {"t":"choice","topic":"Snowflake","q":"Many analysts run queries at the same time and queries wait in a queue. Which fix fits best?","a":"Scale out with a multi-cluster warehouse","w":["Add a clustering key to every column","Delete the result cache","Rename the warehouse"],"why":"Scale out adds more clusters to handle many users at once. Scale up (bigger size) helps one heavy query.","id":"snow.i.11.5"},
        {"t":"choice","topic":"Snowflake","q":"One complex query is slow on a small warehouse. What is the usual first step?","a":"Scale up to a larger warehouse size","w":["Add many clusters","Turn off AUTO_RESUME","Create a new database"],"why":"A bigger size gives that single query more compute. More clusters help many users, not one slow query.","id":"snow.i.11.6"},
        {"t":"choice","topic":"Snowflake","q":"Which Snowflake tool shows how a query ran, step by step, so you can find slow parts?","a":"Query Profile","w":["Resource Profile","Stream Viewer","Pipe Monitor"],"why":"Query Profile shows the operators of a query and where time is spent, such as scans and joins.","id":"snow.i.11.7"},
        {"t":"check","topic":"Snowflake","q":"What happens to the warehouse local disk cache when the warehouse suspends?","a":"It is dropped","w":["It is kept forever","It is copied to the result cache","It is moved to another schema"],"why":"The local cache lives with the running warehouse. After resuming, the first queries may be slower while it fills again.","id":"snow.i.11.8"},
        {"t":"check","topic":"Snowflake","q":"When should you consider a clustering key?","a":"On a very large table where queries filter on a certain column","w":["On every small table","On a table with 10 rows","Only on views"],"why":"Clustering helps pruning on big tables. On small tables it adds cost with little benefit.","id":"snow.i.11.9"}
      ] },
      { id: "snow.i.12", level: "i", n: 12, title: "Cost control", cards: [
        {"t":"learn","topic":"Snowflake","title":"Cost control","body":"A warehouse uses **credits** while it runs, and **serverless** features such as Snowpipe also use credits. Set `AUTO_SUSPEND` (in seconds) and `AUTO_RESUME`, pick the right size, and add a **resource monitor** to get alerts or suspend at a limit.","code":"ALTER WAREHOUSE my_wh SET\n  AUTO_SUSPEND = 60\n  AUTO_RESUME = TRUE;","lang":"sql","id":"snow.i.12.1"},
        {"t":"choice","topic":"Snowflake","q":"When does a virtual warehouse use credits?","a":"While it is running","w":["Only when tables are created","Only at the end of the month","Never, storage pays for it"],"why":"A warehouse consumes credits while it is started. Suspending it when idle stops that use.","id":"snow.i.12.2"},
        {"t":"bug","topic":"Snowflake","q":"Ravi wants his warehouse to suspend after 5 minutes idle, but this fails. Which line is wrong?","code":["ALTER WAREHOUSE my_wh SET AUTO_SUSPEND = '5 MINUTES';"],"lang":"sql","bad":0,"why":"`AUTO_SUSPEND` takes a number of seconds. Five minutes is `300`.","fix":"ALTER WAREHOUSE my_wh SET AUTO_SUSPEND = 300;","id":"snow.i.12.3"},
        {"t":"choice","topic":"Snowflake","q":"What can a resource monitor do?","a":"Send a notification or suspend warehouses when credit use reaches a set level","w":["Increase warehouse size automatically","Delete tables that cost too much","Speed up the result cache"],"why":"A resource monitor watches credit use and can notify, or suspend, at the thresholds you set.","id":"snow.i.12.4"},
        {"t":"choice","topic":"Snowflake","q":"Which role is needed to create a resource monitor?","a":"ACCOUNTADMIN","w":["PUBLIC","Any user with SELECT","USERADMIN"],"why":"By default only account administrators can create resource monitors. Others can be given privileges on an existing monitor.","id":"snow.i.12.5"},
        {"t":"order","topic":"Snowflake","q":"Asha puts a spending limit on a warehouse. Put the steps in order.","items":["Switch to ACCOUNTADMIN","CREATE RESOURCE MONITOR with a quota and triggers","ALTER WAREHOUSE to set RESOURCE_MONITOR","Switch back to a daily role"],"why":"A monitor must exist before you attach it to a warehouse. Go back to a lower role after the admin work is done.","id":"snow.i.12.6"},
        {"t":"choice","topic":"Snowflake","q":"Lakshmi loads files with Snowpipe all day. Does this use credits?","a":"Yes, serverless compute also uses credits","w":["No, only warehouses use credits","No, pipes are always free","Only when the pipe is dropped"],"why":"Serverless features like Snowpipe have their own compute use, which is billed in credits. Watch them as well as warehouses.","id":"snow.i.12.7"},
        {"t":"check","topic":"Snowflake","q":"A big warehouse stays on all night with no queries. Which setting stops this waste?","a":"AUTO_SUSPEND","w":["AUTO_CLUSTER","MAX_DATA_EXTENSION","COMMENT"],"why":"`AUTO_SUSPEND` stops an idle warehouse after the time you set. Use it with `AUTO_RESUME` so users are not blocked.","id":"snow.i.12.8"},
        {"t":"check","topic":"Snowflake","q":"Which choice is a good habit to keep Snowflake cost under control?","a":"Start with a smaller warehouse and size up only if needed","w":["Use the largest size for every task","Disable AUTO_SUSPEND","Share ACCOUNTADMIN so anyone can change settings"],"why":"Right-sizing avoids paying for compute you do not use. Auto-suspend and monitors add safety.","id":"snow.i.12.9"}
      ] },
      { id: "snow.a.13", level: "a", n: 13, title: "Data sharing", cards: [] },
      { id: "snow.a.14", level: "a", n: 14, title: "Snowpark", cards: [] },
      { id: "snow.a.15", level: "a", n: 15, title: "Security and governance", cards: [] }
    ] },
    { id: "aws", name: "AWS", topic: "AWS", blurb: "The most used cloud: servers, storage, networks and safe billing.", units: [
      { id: "aws.b.01", level: "b", n: 1, title: "Cloud, regions and Availability Zones", cards: [
        {"t":"learn","topic":"AWS","title":"Cloud, Regions and Availability Zones","body":"With the **cloud**, you rent servers and storage on demand and pay for what you use. AWS runs in **Regions**, which are separate places in the world, like Mumbai (`ap-south-1`). Each Region has several **Availability Zones (AZs)**, which are isolated groups of data centres.","code":"aws ec2 describe-availability-zones --region ap-south-1","lang":"shell","id":"aws.b.01.1"},
        {"t":"choice","topic":"AWS","q":"Asha in Guntur builds a website for users in India. Why does she pick the Mumbai Region?","a":"It is close to her users, so the site responds faster","w":["Mumbai is the only Region that has Availability Zones","Resources in Mumbai are always free","Resources in one Region are copied to all Regions by default"],"why":"Choosing a Region near your users lowers delay. Your resources stay in the Region you pick unless you copy them yourself.","id":"aws.b.01.2"},
        {"t":"choice","topic":"AWS","q":"What is an Availability Zone (AZ)?","a":"One or more separate data centres inside a Region","w":["A country where AWS sells services","A single physical server in a Region","A type of AWS support plan"],"why":"AZs are isolated from each other inside one Region. Do not confuse an AZ with a Region, which contains several AZs.","id":"aws.b.01.3"},
        {"t":"choice","topic":"AWS","q":"Which of these is a global service, not tied to one Region?","a":"AWS IAM (users and permissions)","w":["Amazon EC2 instances","Amazon EBS volumes","Amazon VPC"],"why":"IAM is global, so one user or role works across Regions. EC2, EBS and VPC resources live in a specific Region.","id":"aws.b.01.4"},
        {"t":"order","topic":"AWS","q":"Put these in order from smallest to biggest.","items":["Data centre","Availability Zone","Region"],"why":"A Region has several AZs, and each AZ is made of one or more data centres.","id":"aws.b.01.5"},
        {"t":"bug","topic":"AWS","q":"Ravi wants Mumbai as his default Region. One line uses a wrong value. Which one?","code":["aws configure set region mumbai","aws configure get region"],"lang":"shell","bad":0,"why":"AWS CLI needs the Region code, like `ap-south-1`, not the city name.","id":"aws.b.01.6"},
        {"t":"choice","topic":"AWS","q":"Lakshmi wants her app to keep working if one data centre group has a power failure. What should she do?","a":"Run the app in two or more Availability Zones","w":["Run a bigger server in one Availability Zone","Run the app only on her laptop","Turn on MFA for the root user"],"why":"AZs are isolated, so a problem in one is unlikely to hit the other. A bigger server in one AZ is still a single point of failure.","id":"aws.b.01.7"},
        {"t":"check","topic":"AWS","q":"Which is the Region code for Asia Pacific (Mumbai)?","a":"ap-south-1","w":["ap-mumbai-1","in-west-1","us-east-1"],"why":"Mumbai is `ap-south-1`. `us-east-1` is N. Virginia in the USA.","id":"aws.b.01.8"},
        {"t":"check","topic":"AWS","q":"Which statement about Regions and AZs is true?","a":"A Region has multiple AZs that are isolated from each other","w":["An AZ has multiple Regions inside it","All Regions share one single data centre","You pay nothing for using any Region"],"why":"The hierarchy is Region, then AZs, then data centres. You pay for what you use, in any Region.","id":"aws.b.01.9"}
      ] },
      { id: "aws.b.02", level: "b", n: 2, title: "IAM: users, groups and roles", cards: [
        {"t":"learn","topic":"AWS","title":"IAM: users, groups and roles","body":"**IAM** controls who can do what in your AWS account. A **user** is a person, a **group** is a set of users that share **policies**, and a **role** gives **temporary credentials** to a service or app. Keep your **root user** locked with MFA and do not use it daily.","code":"aws iam create-user --user-name ravi","lang":"shell","id":"aws.b.02.1"},
        {"t":"choice","topic":"AWS","q":"Asha just created an AWS account. What should she do with the root user?","a":"Turn on MFA and avoid using it for daily work","w":["Share its password with her team","Use it for all daily tasks to avoid permission errors","Delete it after creating one IAM user"],"why":"The root user has full access that cannot be limited. Use an IAM identity for daily work. Root cannot be deleted.","id":"aws.b.02.2"},
        {"t":"choice","topic":"AWS","q":"Ten testers need the same permissions. What is the easiest way to manage this?","a":"Put them in an IAM group and attach the policy to the group","w":["Share one IAM user between all ten","Give all ten the root password","Create ten roles with the same name"],"why":"A policy on a group applies to every user in it. Sharing one user hides who did what.","id":"aws.b.02.3"},
        {"t":"choice","topic":"AWS","q":"An app on an EC2 server must read files from S3. What is the safest way to give it access?","a":"Attach an IAM role to the instance","w":["Save an access key in the app code","Make the S3 bucket public for everyone","Run the app as the root user"],"why":"A role gives the app temporary credentials that rotate automatically. Keys in code can leak.","id":"aws.b.02.4"},
        {"t":"bug","topic":"AWS","q":"Ravi runs these to add a user to a group. One command is incomplete. Which one?","code":["aws iam create-user --user-name ravi","aws iam create-group --group-name developers","aws iam add-user-to-group --user-name ravi"],"lang":"shell","bad":2,"why":"`add-user-to-group` needs both `--user-name` and `--group-name`. Without the group, the command fails.","id":"aws.b.02.5"},
        {"t":"pipe","topic":"AWS","q":"Build how Ravi gets read-only access to S3 through a group.","steps":[["USR","Ravi (IAM user)","src"],["GRP","Developers group"],["POL","ReadOnly policy"],["S3","Can read S3"]],"extra":[["ROOT","Root password"]],"why":"A user in a group gets the group's policies, and the policy decides what is allowed. Sharing the root password is never the answer.","id":"aws.b.02.6"},
        {"t":"choice","topic":"AWS","q":"What does \"least privilege\" mean?","a":"Give only the permissions needed for the task, nothing more","w":["Give everyone full access so work is not blocked","Give permissions only to the root user","Remove all passwords from users"],"why":"Start with small permissions and add more only when needed. Full access for everyone is the common mistake.","id":"aws.b.02.7"},
        {"t":"check","topic":"AWS","q":"What do IAM roles give to an app or service?","a":"Temporary security credentials","w":["A permanent password that never changes","Full root access to the account","A free EC2 instance"],"why":"Roles use temporary credentials that expire and refresh. This is safer than long-term keys.","id":"aws.b.02.8"},
        {"t":"check","topic":"AWS","q":"Which action follows IAM best practice?","a":"Use MFA on the root user and use IAM identities for daily work","w":["Create access keys for the root user and share them","Use one shared IAM user for the whole team","Attach AdministratorAccess to every user"],"why":"Protect root with MFA and give each person their own identity with the permissions they need.","id":"aws.b.02.9"}
      ] },
      { id: "aws.b.03", level: "b", n: 3, title: "EC2: virtual servers", cards: [
        {"t":"learn","topic":"AWS","title":"EC2: virtual servers","body":"**EC2** gives you virtual servers called **instances**. You pick an **AMI** (the starting image with the operating system), an **instance type** (CPU and memory), a **key pair** to log in, and a **security group** (a virtual firewall).","code":"aws ec2 describe-instances","lang":"shell","id":"aws.b.03.1"},
        {"t":"choice","topic":"AWS","q":"What is an AMI?","a":"A template with the operating system and software used to launch an instance","w":["A firewall that protects an instance","The file you use to log in to an instance","A bill for your EC2 usage"],"why":"An AMI is the starting image. The firewall is a security group and the login file is a key pair.","id":"aws.b.03.2"},
        {"t":"choice","topic":"AWS","q":"Asha launches an instance with a security group that has no inbound rules. What happens to inbound traffic?","a":"It is blocked until she adds a rule that allows it","w":["All inbound traffic is allowed","Only traffic from India is allowed","Only SSH is allowed"],"why":"Security groups deny inbound traffic by default. You add allow rules only; there are no deny rules.","id":"aws.b.03.3"},
        {"t":"choice","topic":"AWS","q":"Ravi no longer needs an instance and its data. Which action ends it for good?","a":"Terminate the instance","w":["Stop the instance","Reboot the instance","Detach the key pair"],"why":"Terminate deletes the instance and it cannot be started again. A stopped instance can be started later.","id":"aws.b.03.4"},
        {"t":"order","topic":"AWS","q":"Put the main steps of launching an EC2 instance in order.","items":["Choose an AMI","Choose an instance type","Choose a key pair","Set the security group","Launch"],"why":"You pick the image and size first, then login and network settings, then launch.","id":"aws.b.03.5"},
        {"t":"bug","topic":"AWS","q":"Lakshmi runs this command to launch an instance, but it fails. One line has a wrong option. Which?","code":["aws ec2 run-instances \\","  --image-id ami-0abcdef1234567890 \\","  --instance-type t3.micro \\","  --key-pair mykey"],"lang":"shell","bad":3,"why":"The option is `--key-name`, not `--key-pair`. The key pair name goes there.","id":"aws.b.03.6"},
        {"t":"choice","topic":"AWS","q":"Which part of an EC2 key pair does AWS keep?","a":"The public key; you keep the private key safe","w":["The private key; you keep the public key","Neither; keys are not used","Both keys, so you can download them any time"],"why":"AWS stores only the public key. If you lose the private key file, you cannot log in with that key pair.","id":"aws.b.03.7"},
        {"t":"check","topic":"AWS","q":"You stop an EC2 instance for the night. What is true?","a":"You can start it again later","w":["It is deleted and cannot be started","Its AMI is deleted","Its security group is deleted"],"why":"Stopping is a pause; terminating deletes the instance. Attached EBS volumes can still add storage cost while stopped.","id":"aws.b.03.8"},
        {"t":"check","topic":"AWS","q":"What does a security group do for an EC2 instance?","a":"It works as a virtual firewall for the instance","w":["It chooses the operating system","It stores the instance data","It sets the CPU and memory"],"why":"Security groups control allowed traffic. The AMI sets the OS and the instance type sets CPU and memory.","id":"aws.b.03.9"}
      ] },
      { id: "aws.b.04", level: "b", n: 4, title: "S3: storage", cards: [
        {"t":"learn","topic":"AWS","title":"S3: storage in buckets","body":"**S3** stores files, called **objects**, inside **buckets**. Each object has a **key**, which is its name. A bucket name must be **globally unique**, and **Block Public Access** helps keep your data private.","code":"aws s3 cp notes.pdf s3://asha-notes-2026/\naws s3 ls s3://asha-notes-2026/","lang":"shell","id":"aws.b.04.1"},
        {"t":"choice","topic":"AWS","q":"Ravi tries to create a bucket named `data` and gets an error that the name is taken. Why?","a":"Bucket names must be unique across all AWS accounts","w":["Bucket names must have only capital letters","Each account can have only one bucket","A bucket cannot be made in the Mumbai Region"],"why":"Bucket names are global, so someone else may already use it. Try a more specific name.","id":"aws.b.04.2"},
        {"t":"choice","topic":"AWS","q":"In S3, what is an object key?","a":"The unique name of an object in a bucket","w":["The password of the bucket","The size of the object","The Region code of the bucket"],"why":"A key like `photos/pic1.jpg` identifies the object. S3 has no real folders; the `photos/` part is a prefix in the key.","id":"aws.b.04.3"},
        {"t":"choice","topic":"AWS","q":"What does S3 Block Public Access do?","a":"It stops buckets and objects from becoming public through ACLs or policies","w":["It blocks the account owner from reading the data","It deletes old objects every day","It encrypts the bucket name"],"why":"It is a safety switch against accidental public data. Owners and permitted users can still read the data.","id":"aws.b.04.4"},
        {"t":"bug","topic":"AWS","q":"Asha wants to upload a file, but one command fails. Which line is wrong?","code":["aws s3 mb s3://asha-reports-2026","aws s3 cp report.csv asha-reports-2026/","aws s3 ls s3://asha-reports-2026/"],"lang":"shell","bad":1,"why":"S3 locations in the CLI must start with `s3://`. Without it, the CLI treats it as a local folder.","id":"aws.b.04.5"},
        {"t":"pipe","topic":"AWS","q":"Build the path of Asha's file into S3.","steps":[["FILE","notes.pdf on laptop","src"],["CLI","aws s3 cp"],["BKT","S3 bucket"],["OBJ","Object with a key"]],"extra":[["EC2","EC2 instance"]],"why":"`aws s3 cp` sends the file to a bucket, where it is stored as an object with a key. An EC2 instance is not needed to store a file in S3.","id":"aws.b.04.6"},
        {"t":"choice","topic":"AWS","q":"Which command lists all the buckets in your account?","a":"aws s3 ls","w":["aws s3 list-all","aws s3 show buckets","aws bucket ls"],"why":"`aws s3 ls` with no path lists your buckets. Add `s3://bucket-name/` to list objects inside one.","id":"aws.b.04.7"},
        {"t":"check","topic":"AWS","q":"Lakshmi wants to copy `notes.pdf` from her laptop into bucket `lakshmi-notes`. Which command does it?","a":"aws s3 cp notes.pdf s3://lakshmi-notes/","w":["aws s3 cp s3://lakshmi-notes/ notes.pdf","aws s3 ls notes.pdf s3://lakshmi-notes/","aws s3 mb notes.pdf"],"why":"`cp` takes source first, then destination. The reverse order downloads from S3.","id":"aws.b.04.8"},
        {"t":"check","topic":"AWS","q":"What are S3 storage classes for?","a":"Choosing how objects are stored based on how often you access them","w":["Setting which Region the bucket is in","Choosing the operating system of the bucket","Limiting which users can log in"],"why":"Different classes suit frequent or rare access. The Region and permissions are separate settings.","id":"aws.b.04.9"}
      ] },
      { id: "aws.b.05", level: "b", n: 5, title: "VPC: networking basics", cards: [
        {"t":"learn","topic":"AWS","title":"VPC: your private network","body":"A **VPC** is your own private network in AWS. It is split into **subnets**. A **public subnet** has a route to an **internet gateway**, and a **private subnet** does not. **Security groups** and **network ACLs** control traffic.","id":"aws.b.05.1"},
        {"t":"choice","topic":"AWS","q":"What is a subnet in a VPC?","a":"A range of IP addresses in the VPC, inside one Availability Zone","w":["A password for the VPC","A copy of the VPC in another Region","A type of EC2 server"],"why":"A VPC is divided into subnets, and each subnet lives in one AZ.","id":"aws.b.05.2"},
        {"t":"choice","topic":"AWS","q":"What makes a subnet public?","a":"Its route table has a route to an internet gateway","w":["Its name starts with \"public\"","It has no security group","It is in the Mumbai Region"],"why":"The route to the internet gateway is what matters, not the name. Without it, the subnet is private.","id":"aws.b.05.3"},
        {"t":"choice","topic":"AWS","q":"What does a route table do?","a":"It has rules that decide where network traffic from a subnet goes","w":["It stores the data of your EC2 instances","It lists the users who can log in","It sets the price of the VPC"],"why":"Each rule says a destination range and a target, like an internet gateway.","id":"aws.b.05.4"},
        {"t":"choice","topic":"AWS","q":"How do security groups and network ACLs differ?","a":"Security groups are stateful; network ACLs are stateless","w":["Security groups are stateless; network ACLs are stateful","Both are stateless","Security groups work on subnets and network ACLs on instances"],"why":"Stateful means reply traffic is allowed automatically. Security groups work at the instance level and network ACLs at the subnet level.","id":"aws.b.05.5"},
        {"t":"order","topic":"AWS","q":"Put the steps in order to give a subnet internet access.","items":["Create an internet gateway","Attach it to the VPC","Add a route to the gateway in the subnet route table","Give the instance a public IP address"],"why":"The gateway must be attached before the route can use it. Instances also need a public IP to be reached from the internet.","id":"aws.b.05.6"},
        {"t":"pipe","topic":"AWS","q":"Build the path for a visitor reaching a web server in a public subnet.","steps":[["NET","Internet","src"],["IGW","Internet gateway"],["PUB","Public subnet"],["EC2","Web server"]],"extra":[["PRIV","Private subnet"]],"why":"Traffic enters through the internet gateway to the public subnet. A private subnet has no route to the gateway.","id":"aws.b.05.7"},
        {"t":"check","topic":"AWS","q":"Which resource gives a VPC a path to the internet?","a":"An internet gateway","w":["A security group alone","An AMI","An IAM group"],"why":"The internet gateway links the VPC to the internet. Routes must also point to it.","id":"aws.b.05.8"},
        {"t":"check","topic":"AWS","q":"Ravi allows inbound traffic in a security group. What about the reply traffic?","a":"It is allowed automatically, because security groups are stateful","w":["It is blocked until he adds an outbound rule","It is blocked because security groups are stateless","It needs a network ACL deny rule"],"why":"Stateful filtering tracks the connection and allows replies. Stateless network ACLs need rules for both directions.","id":"aws.b.05.9"}
      ] },
      { id: "aws.b.06", level: "b", n: 6, title: "Billing and Free Tier safety", cards: [
        {"t":"learn","topic":"AWS","title":"Billing and cost safety","body":"AWS is **pay-as-you-go**, so unused resources can still cost money. Set up **AWS Budgets** to get alerts, add **tags** to see who spends what, and **terminate** things you no longer need. Never share your **access keys**.","id":"aws.b.06.1"},
        {"t":"choice","topic":"AWS","q":"Asha wants an email when her spend goes above a set limit. What should she use?","a":"AWS Budgets with an alert","w":["An IAM group","An S3 bucket policy","A VPC route table"],"why":"AWS Budgets tracks cost and sends alerts when you pass the thresholds you set.","id":"aws.b.06.2"},
        {"t":"choice","topic":"AWS","q":"Ravi tags every resource with `project=college-demo`. What does this help with?","a":"Finding and grouping costs by project","w":["Making the resource faster","Hiding the resource from other users","Making the resource free"],"why":"Tags are labels. After you activate cost allocation tags, you can see cost by tag in billing reports.","id":"aws.b.06.3"},
        {"t":"choice","topic":"AWS","q":"Lakshmi finished her test and stopped her EC2 instance. What can still cost money?","a":"The EBS storage volume attached to the instance","w":["The instance CPU time","Nothing at all","Her AWS password"],"why":"A stopped instance does not charge for instance usage, but its EBS volumes stay. Terminate and delete what you do not need.","id":"aws.b.06.4"},
        {"t":"choice","topic":"AWS","q":"A friend asks Asha to send her AWS access key and secret key \"to help fix a bug\". What should she do?","a":"Refuse. Keys must never be shared","w":["Send them by WhatsApp","Post them in the group chat","Email them with a short subject"],"why":"Anyone with your keys can use your account and run up bills. Give a friend their own IAM user with limited rights instead.","id":"aws.b.06.5"},
        {"t":"bug","topic":"AWS","q":"Asha will push this file to GitHub. Which line is unsafe?","code":["import boto3","s3 = boto3.client('s3', aws_access_key_id='YOUR_KEY', aws_secret_access_key='YOUR_SECRET')","print(s3.list_buckets()['Buckets'])"],"lang":"python","bad":1,"why":"Keys written in code can leak when the code is shared. Let boto3 read credentials from a role or the AWS config instead.","id":"aws.b.06.6"},
        {"t":"order","topic":"AWS","q":"Rotate an access key safely. Put the steps in order.","items":["Create a new access key","Update the app to use the new key","Deactivate the old key","Delete the old key"],"why":"Switch the app first so nothing breaks, then deactivate and delete the old key.","id":"aws.b.06.7"},
        {"t":"check","topic":"AWS","q":"You finish a practice project. What is the best way to avoid surprise bills?","a":"Delete or terminate the resources you no longer need","w":["Leave them running in case you need them","Change your password","Rename the resources"],"why":"Running resources keep costing money. Clean up after practice and keep a budget alert on.","id":"aws.b.06.8"},
        {"t":"check","topic":"AWS","q":"Ravi wants to see how much he spent last month, service by service. Which AWS tool helps?","a":"AWS Cost Explorer","w":["AWS IAM","Amazon S3 Block Public Access","An EC2 key pair"],"why":"Cost Explorer shows past costs and usage by service, tag and more. Budgets (alerts) and Cost Explorer (reports) work well together.","id":"aws.b.06.9"}
      ] },
      { id: "aws.i.07", level: "i", n: 7, title: "RDS databases", cards: [
        {"t":"learn","topic":"AWS","title":"RDS: managed databases","body":"**Amazon RDS** runs relational databases such as MySQL, PostgreSQL, MariaDB, Oracle, SQL Server and Db2; **Amazon Aurora** is AWS's own MySQL- and PostgreSQL-compatible engine. AWS handles patching and **automated backups**, and you can also take manual **snapshots**. Connect with the **endpoint** (a DNS name) and keep the database in a **private subnet**.","code":"aws rds describe-db-instances \\\n  --query \"DBInstances[].Endpoint.Address\"","lang":"shell","src":"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/AmazonRDS/latest/UserGuide/Welcome.html","id":"aws.i.07.1"},
        {"t":"choice","topic":"AWS","q":"Asha moves her database to RDS. Which job does AWS now do for her?","a":"Patching the database software and taking backups","w":["Designing her tables and writing her queries","Deciding which users may see which rows","Fixing bugs in her application code"],"why":"RDS manages routine work like patching and backups. Your schema, queries and app are still your job.","src":"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/AmazonRDS/latest/UserGuide/Welcome.html","id":"aws.i.07.2"},
        {"t":"choice","topic":"AWS","q":"Ravi's database must keep working if its Availability Zone fails. Which RDS feature helps?","a":"Multi-AZ deployment, which keeps a standby copy in another AZ","w":["A read replica used only for reports","A bigger instance type in the same AZ","Turning off automated backups"],"why":"In Multi-AZ, RDS can fail over to the standby in another AZ. A bigger instance in one AZ is still one AZ.","src":"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/AmazonRDS/latest/UserGuide/Concepts.MultiAZ.html","id":"aws.i.07.3"},
        {"t":"choice","topic":"AWS","q":"Lakshmi's shop database is slow because many people run read-only reports. What is the best fit?","a":"Add a read replica and send the report queries to it","w":["Add a Multi-AZ DB instance standby and send the reports to it","Delete old automated backups","Move the database to a public subnet"],"why":"Read replicas scale reads. The standby in a Multi-AZ DB instance deployment is only for failover and does not serve read traffic.","src":"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/AmazonRDS/latest/UserGuide/Concepts.MultiAZ.html","id":"aws.i.07.4"},
        {"t":"pipe","topic":"AWS","q":"Build how Ravi's web server reaches his RDS database safely.","steps":[["APP","Web server","src"],["END","RDS endpoint (DNS name)"],["SG","DB security group allows the port"],["DB","DB instance in private subnet"]],"extra":[["S3","S3 bucket"]],"why":"The app looks up the endpoint, its traffic is checked by the security group attached to the DB instance, and only then reaches the database. An S3 bucket is not part of this path.","src":"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/AmazonRDS/latest/UserGuide/USER_VPC.WorkingWithRDSInstanceinaVPC.html","id":"aws.i.07.5"},
        {"t":"bug","topic":"AWS","q":"Asha wants a manual snapshot of her database, but one line has a wrong option. Which?","code":["aws rds create-db-snapshot \\","  --db-instance-identifier asha-db \\","  --db-snapshot-name asha-snap"],"lang":"shell","bad":2,"why":"The option is `--db-snapshot-identifier`. There is no `--db-snapshot-name` option.","fix":"  --db-snapshot-identifier asha-snap","src":"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/cli/latest/reference/rds/create-db-snapshot.html","id":"aws.i.07.6"},
        {"t":"choice","topic":"AWS","q":"Where should Lakshmi place a database that only her app servers use?","a":"In a private subnet, with a security group that allows only the app","w":["In a public subnet open to all IP addresses","On the same server as her laptop","In the root account with no firewall"],"why":"A private subnet has no direct route from the internet. Opening the database to everyone is a common and risky mistake.","src":"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/AmazonRDS/latest/UserGuide/USER_VPC.WorkingWithRDSInstanceinaVPC.html","id":"aws.i.07.7"},
        {"t":"check","topic":"AWS","q":"In a Multi-AZ DB instance deployment, what does the standby do?","a":"It takes over if the primary DB instance fails","w":["It answers report queries to speed up reads","It stores backups in S3 for you to download","It makes the database public"],"why":"This standby gives failover support and does not serve reads. Read replicas are the way to scale reads.","src":"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/AmazonRDS/latest/UserGuide/Concepts.MultiAZ.html","id":"aws.i.07.8"},
        {"t":"check","topic":"AWS","q":"How should an app find its RDS database?","a":"Use the DB instance endpoint (DNS name)","w":["Use the private IP of the underlying server","Use the name of the AMI","Use the root user email address"],"why":"The endpoint stays valid even when RDS replaces the underlying host during failover.","src":"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/AmazonRDS/latest/UserGuide/Welcome.html","id":"aws.i.07.9"}
      ] },
      { id: "aws.i.08", level: "i", n: 8, title: "Lambda (serverless)", cards: [
        {"t":"learn","topic":"AWS","title":"Lambda: code without servers","body":"**AWS Lambda** runs your code only when an **event** happens, for example a file upload to S3 or a schedule from EventBridge. You write a **handler** function and Lambda runs it. An **execution role** gives it permission to use other AWS services.","code":"def lambda_handler(event, context):\n    return \"Hello from Guntur\"","lang":"python","src":"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/lambda/latest/dg/welcome.html","id":"aws.i.08.1"},
        {"t":"choice","topic":"AWS","q":"What is the main idea of AWS Lambda?","a":"Run code in response to events without managing servers","w":["Rent a virtual server that runs all the time","Store files in buckets","Give users permissions to the account"],"why":"Lambda runs your code when triggered and you do not manage the servers. EC2 is the always-on server service.","src":"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/lambda/latest/dg/welcome.html","id":"aws.i.08.2"},
        {"t":"choice","topic":"AWS","q":"Ravi's Lambda function must read a file from S3. How does it get permission?","a":"Through the permissions in its execution role","w":["By saving access keys inside the function code","By using the root user password","Lambda can always read every bucket by default"],"why":"The execution role gives the function its permissions and temporary credentials. Keys in code can leak.","src":"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/lambda/latest/dg/lambda-intro-execution-role.html","id":"aws.i.08.3"},
        {"t":"order","topic":"AWS","q":"Asha uploads a photo to S3 and a Lambda function makes a thumbnail. Put the steps in order.","items":["Photo is uploaded to S3","S3 event triggers the function","The handler runs and makes a thumbnail","Logs go to CloudWatch Logs"],"why":"The upload is the event, the event triggers the function, then the handler runs and logs its output.","src":"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/lambda/latest/dg/monitoring-cloudwatchlogs.html","id":"aws.i.08.4"},
        {"t":"bug","topic":"AWS","q":"This Python handler fails when Lambda calls it. Which line is wrong?","code":["def lambda_handler(event):","    name = event.get(\"name\", \"friend\")","    return \"Hello \" + name"],"lang":"python","bad":0,"why":"A Python handler receives two arguments: `event` and `context`. Lambda passes both when it calls the function.","fix":"def lambda_handler(event, context):","src":"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/lambda/latest/dg/python-handler.html","id":"aws.i.08.5"},
        {"t":"choice","topic":"AWS","q":"Lakshmi's function keeps a counter in a Python variable and expects it to grow forever. What goes wrong?","a":"Lambda is stateless, so the value is not reliable between runs","w":["Lambda blocks all variables","Counters are only allowed in the root account","The Region deletes the counter every hour"],"why":"Do not depend on memory between invocations. Keep state in a service like S3 or DynamoDB.","src":"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/lambda/latest/dg/foundation-progmodel.html","id":"aws.i.08.6"},
        {"t":"choice","topic":"AWS","q":"A function runs too long and stops with a timeout error. Which setting can Asha change?","a":"The function timeout in its configuration","w":["The Region code of her account","The name of the S3 bucket","The MFA device of the root user"],"why":"Timeout and memory are function settings. Better still, check whether the work can be made shorter.","src":"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/lambda/latest/dg/configuration-timeout.html","id":"aws.i.08.7"},
        {"t":"cmd","topic":"AWS","q":"Ravi wants to run his Lambda function `hello` once from the terminal and save its answer in `out.json`. Build the command (the output file goes last).","parts":["aws","lambda","invoke","--function-name","hello","out.json"],"extra":["run","--name"],"why":"`aws lambda invoke` runs the function. `--function-name` names it, and the last word is the file where the response is saved.","src":"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/cli/latest/reference/lambda/invoke.html","id":"aws.i.08.10"},
        {"t":"check","topic":"AWS","q":"Which of these can trigger a Lambda function?","a":"A schedule from Amazon EventBridge","w":["A security group rule","An AMI name","A key pair file"],"why":"Lambda is triggered by events from services such as S3, API Gateway and EventBridge schedules.","src":"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/lambda/latest/dg/lambda-services.html","id":"aws.i.08.8"},
        {"t":"check","topic":"AWS","q":"How does AWS charge for a Lambda function?","a":"By the number of requests and how long the code runs","w":["A fixed monthly fee for each function, even if it never runs","By the number of lines in the code","By the number of S3 buckets in the account"],"why":"You pay per request plus run time (duration, which also depends on the memory you choose). Check the pricing page for amounts when you plan.","src":"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/lambda/latest/dg/welcome.html","id":"aws.i.08.9"}
      ] },
      { id: "aws.i.09", level: "i", n: 9, title: "CloudWatch monitoring", cards: [
        {"t":"learn","topic":"AWS","title":"CloudWatch: watch your cloud","body":"**Amazon CloudWatch** collects **metrics** (numbers like CPU use), **logs** and **alarms**. An alarm can notify you through **SNS**, for example by email. A **dashboard** shows your metrics on one screen.","code":"aws logs describe-log-groups","lang":"shell","src":"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/AmazonCloudWatch/latest/monitoring/WhatIsCloudWatch.html","id":"aws.i.09.1"},
        {"t":"choice","topic":"AWS","q":"Which one is a CloudWatch metric?","a":"CPU utilization of an EC2 instance over time","w":["The text of an error message from the app","The name of an IAM user","The AMI used to launch the server"],"why":"A metric is a number measured over time. Text lines like errors belong in logs.","src":"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/AmazonCloudWatch/latest/monitoring/cloudwatch_concepts.html","id":"aws.i.09.2"},
        {"t":"pipe","topic":"AWS","q":"Build how Asha gets an email when her server CPU is too high.","steps":[["CPU","CPU metric","src"],["ALRM","CloudWatch alarm"],["SNS","SNS topic"],["MAIL","Email to Asha"]],"extra":[["S3","S3 bucket"]],"why":"An alarm watches a metric and sends a message to an SNS topic, which emails the subscribers. An S3 bucket is not needed.","src":"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/AmazonCloudWatch/latest/monitoring/AlarmThatSendsEmail.html","id":"aws.i.09.3"},
        {"t":"choice","topic":"AWS","q":"Someone deleted an S3 bucket. Which service helps Ravi find who made that API call?","a":"AWS CloudTrail","w":["CloudWatch metrics","An EC2 key pair","A Lambda execution role"],"why":"CloudTrail records API calls (who did what). CloudWatch tells you how things perform.","src":"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/awscloudtrail/latest/userguide/cloudtrail-user-guide.html","id":"aws.i.09.4"},
        {"t":"choice","topic":"AWS","q":"Where does a Lambda function write its logs?","a":"To a CloudWatch Logs log group for that function","w":["To the S3 bucket that triggered it","To the root user mailbox","To an EC2 key pair"],"why":"Lambda sends logs to CloudWatch Logs. The role needs permission to write them.","src":"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/lambda/latest/dg/monitoring-cloudwatchlogs.html","id":"aws.i.09.5"},
        {"t":"bug","topic":"AWS","q":"Lakshmi wants alarm emails. One line uses a wrong protocol value. Which?","code":["aws sns create-topic --name alerts","aws sns subscribe --topic-arn <TOPIC_ARN> --protocol mail --notification-endpoint lakshmi@example.com","aws sns list-subscriptions"],"lang":"shell","bad":1,"why":"The protocol for email is `email`. SNS also asks the person to confirm the subscription from the email.","fix":"aws sns subscribe --topic-arn <TOPIC_ARN> --protocol email --notification-endpoint lakshmi@example.com","src":"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/cli/latest/reference/sns/subscribe.html","id":"aws.i.09.6"},
        {"t":"choice","topic":"AWS","q":"In CloudWatch Logs, what is a log group?","a":"A collection of log streams that share the same settings","w":["A group of IAM users who can read logs","A group of EC2 instances in one AZ","A bill for log storage"],"why":"Log events are stored in log streams, and streams are collected in a log group.","src":"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/AmazonCloudWatch/latest/logs/CloudWatchLogsConcepts.html","id":"aws.i.09.7"},
        {"t":"check","topic":"AWS","q":"Asha wants one screen that shows her server CPU and her alarms together. What should she create?","a":"A CloudWatch dashboard","w":["A CloudTrail trail","A log stream","An SNS topic"],"why":"A dashboard shows chosen metrics and alarms in a single view. CloudTrail records API calls, and SNS only sends messages.","src":"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/AmazonCloudWatch/latest/monitoring/CloudWatch_Dashboards.html","id":"aws.i.09.8"},
        {"t":"check","topic":"AWS","q":"What can a CloudWatch alarm do when a metric crosses its threshold?","a":"Send a notification through an SNS topic","w":["Rewrite the application code","Delete the IAM user","Change the Region of the account"],"why":"Alarms watch a metric and can send an SNS notification, such as an email.","src":"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/AmazonCloudWatch/latest/monitoring/AlarmThatSendsEmail.html","id":"aws.i.09.9"}
      ] },
      { id: "aws.i.10", level: "i", n: 10, title: "Load balancers and Auto Scaling", cards: [
        {"t":"learn","topic":"AWS","title":"Load balancers and Auto Scaling","body":"A **load balancer** shares traffic between healthy servers in a **target group**. An **Auto Scaling group** keeps a **minimum**, **desired** and **maximum** number of instances and adds or removes them as load changes. Use two or more Availability Zones for safety.","code":"aws elbv2 describe-target-health \\\n  --target-group-arn <TG_ARN>","lang":"shell","src":"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/elasticloadbalancing/latest/userguide/what-is-load-balancing.html","id":"aws.i.10.1"},
        {"t":"choice","topic":"AWS","q":"Ravi wants to send `/api` requests to one group of servers and `/images` to another. Which load balancer fits?","a":"Application Load Balancer, which can route by path","w":["Network Load Balancer, which balances TCP and UDP connections","An S3 bucket policy","A VPC route table"],"why":"An Application Load Balancer works with HTTP/HTTPS requests and has path rules. A Network Load Balancer works at layer 4 (connections such as TCP, UDP and TLS) and does not route by URL path.","src":"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/elasticloadbalancing/latest/application/introduction.html","id":"aws.i.10.2"},
        {"t":"choice","topic":"AWS","q":"One server in a target group stops answering its health check. What does the load balancer do?","a":"Stops sending requests to it until it is healthy again","w":["Sends all the traffic to it to wake it up","Deletes the server","Turns off the other servers too"],"why":"Health checks let the load balancer send traffic only to healthy targets.","src":"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/elasticloadbalancing/latest/application/target-group-health-checks.html","id":"aws.i.10.3"},
        {"t":"order","topic":"AWS","q":"Asha's website gets busy. Put the scale-out steps in order.","items":["Load rises and a scaling rule is met","Auto Scaling launches an instance from the launch template","The new instance is registered in the target group","The load balancer sends traffic to it"],"why":"The group launches instances from the launch template, and the load balancer sends traffic once they are registered and healthy.","src":"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/autoscaling/ec2/userguide/auto-scaling-groups.html","id":"aws.i.10.4"},
        {"t":"bug","topic":"AWS","q":"Lakshmi's command to create an Auto Scaling group fails because of a wrong size setting. Which line?","code":["aws autoscaling create-auto-scaling-group \\","  --auto-scaling-group-name web-asg \\","  --min-size 4 --max-size 2 \\","  --launch-template LaunchTemplateName=web-lt \\","  --vpc-zone-identifier \"subnet-aaa,subnet-bbb\""],"lang":"shell","bad":2,"why":"The minimum size cannot be more than the maximum size. Here min is 4 and max is 2.","fix":"  --min-size 2 --max-size 4 \\","src":"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/cli/latest/reference/autoscaling/create-auto-scaling-group.html","id":"aws.i.10.5"},
        {"t":"choice","topic":"AWS","q":"Why spread an Auto Scaling group across subnets in two Availability Zones?","a":"The app can keep running if one AZ has a problem","w":["It makes the instance type bigger","It removes the need for a security group","It makes the Region cheaper"],"why":"AZs are isolated from each other, so two AZs avoid a single point of failure.","src":"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/autoscaling/ec2/userguide/auto-scaling-groups.html","id":"aws.i.10.6"},
        {"t":"choice","topic":"AWS","q":"What is \"scale in\" in Auto Scaling?","a":"Removing instances when the load goes down","w":["Adding instances when the load goes up","Making one instance bigger","Moving instances to another Region"],"why":"Scale out adds instances and scale in removes them. Both stay between the minimum and maximum size.","src":"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/autoscaling/ec2/userguide/auto-scaling-groups.html","id":"aws.i.10.7"},
        {"t":"check","topic":"AWS","q":"Which load balancer type is made for TCP and UDP traffic?","a":"Network Load Balancer","w":["Application Load Balancer","Auto Scaling group","Internet gateway"],"why":"A Network Load Balancer works with TCP and UDP. An Application Load Balancer works with HTTP and HTTPS.","src":"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/elasticloadbalancing/latest/network/introduction.html","id":"aws.i.10.8"},
        {"t":"check","topic":"AWS","q":"In an Auto Scaling group, what does the launch template describe?","a":"How new instances are launched, such as the AMI and instance type","w":["Which users can log in to the console","How much the bill will be","Which Region has the most AZs"],"why":"The launch template holds the settings for new instances, so every new server looks the same.","src":"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/autoscaling/ec2/userguide/launch-templates.html","id":"aws.i.10.9"}
      ] },
      { id: "aws.i.11", level: "i", n: 11, title: "AWS CLI", cards: [
        {"t":"learn","topic":"AWS","title":"The AWS CLI","body":"The **AWS CLI** lets you control AWS from a terminal. Run `aws configure` to set your credentials, default **Region** and output format. Use a **profile** with `--profile` to switch between accounts. Prefer **roles** or **IAM Identity Center** over long-term keys.","code":"aws configure --profile asha\naws sts get-caller-identity --profile asha","lang":"shell","src":"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/cli/latest/userguide/cli-configure-files.html","id":"aws.i.11.1"},
        {"t":"choice","topic":"AWS","q":"Which command shows which account and identity the CLI is using right now?","a":"aws sts get-caller-identity","w":["aws iam show-me","aws ec2 whoami","aws configure identity"],"why":"`aws sts get-caller-identity` returns the account ID and the ARN of the caller. It is a quick check before you run anything risky.","src":"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/cli/latest/reference/sts/get-caller-identity.html","id":"aws.i.11.2"},
        {"t":"choice","topic":"AWS","q":"Ravi's CLI works in Mumbai, but this once he needs Singapore. What can he add to a single command?","a":"--region ap-southeast-1","w":["--zone singapore","--country sg","--location asia"],"why":"The `--region` option overrides the default Region for that one command.","src":"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/cli/latest/userguide/cli-configure-options.html","id":"aws.i.11.3"},
        {"t":"bug","topic":"AWS","q":"Asha wants to upload a local folder to S3, but one command is incomplete. Which?","code":["aws sts get-caller-identity --profile asha","aws ec2 describe-instances --region ap-south-1 --output table","aws s3 sync --profile asha"],"lang":"shell","bad":2,"why":"`aws s3 sync` needs a source and a destination, for example a local folder and an S3 path.","fix":"aws s3 sync ./site s3://asha-site-2026 --profile asha","src":"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/cli/latest/reference/s3/sync.html","id":"aws.i.11.4"},
        {"t":"choice","topic":"AWS","q":"Which CLI option picks only some fields from the output?","a":"--query","w":["--select","--only","--pick"],"why":"`--query` uses a JMESPath expression to keep just the fields you need.","src":"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/cli/latest/userguide/cli-usage-filter.html","id":"aws.i.11.5"},
        {"t":"choice","topic":"AWS","q":"Lakshmi wants the output as a readable table. Which option does it?","a":"--output table","w":["--format grid","--pretty on","--show table"],"why":"The `--output` option supports formats such as `json`, `text`, `table` and `yaml`.","src":"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/cli/latest/userguide/cli-usage-output-format.html","id":"aws.i.11.6"},
        {"t":"choice","topic":"AWS","q":"Ravi's script needs AWS access. What is the safest approach?","a":"Use a role or IAM Identity Center, and keep keys out of the code","w":["Paste his access key in the script and push to GitHub","Use the root user access key","Email the secret key to his team"],"why":"Keys in code or in a public repo can leak and be misused. Temporary credentials from roles are safer.","src":"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/IAM/latest/UserGuide/best-practices.html","id":"aws.i.11.7"},
        {"t":"cmd","topic":"AWS","q":"Asha wants to see her EC2 instances in the Mumbai Region as a neat table. Build the command: Region first, then the output format.","parts":["aws","ec2","describe-instances","--region","ap-south-1","--output","table"],"extra":["list-instances","--format"],"why":"EC2 lists things with `describe-...` commands. `--region` picks the Region and `--output table` changes how the result looks.","src":"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/cli/latest/reference/ec2/describe-instances.html","id":"aws.i.11.10"},
        {"t":"check","topic":"AWS","q":"What does `aws configure` ask for?","a":"Access key ID, secret access key, default Region and output format","w":["Only the name of the S3 bucket","The AMI and the key pair","The root user MFA code"],"why":"It saves these settings in your profile. With IAM Identity Center you use `aws configure sso` instead.","src":"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/cli/latest/userguide/cli-configure-files.html","id":"aws.i.11.8"},
        {"t":"check","topic":"AWS","q":"You have a `dev` and a `prod` profile. How do you run a command with `prod`?","a":"Add --profile prod to the command","w":["Add --account prod to the command","Rename the folder to prod","Run it twice"],"why":"The `--profile` option picks the named profile for that command.","src":"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/cli/latest/userguide/cli-configure-files.html","id":"aws.i.11.9"}
      ] },
      { id: "aws.i.12", level: "i", n: 12, title: "Data on AWS: Glue, Athena, Redshift", cards: [
        {"t":"learn","topic":"AWS","title":"Data on AWS: S3, Glue, Athena, Redshift","body":"Many teams keep raw data files in **S3** as a data lake. The **Glue Data Catalog** stores table definitions, and a **Glue crawler** can find the schema of your files. **Athena** then runs SQL directly on the files in S3, and **Redshift** is the data warehouse.","code":"aws glue start-crawler --name sales-crawler","lang":"shell","src":"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/glue/latest/dg/catalog-and-crawler.html","id":"aws.i.12.1"},
        {"t":"choice","topic":"AWS","q":"What does a Glue crawler do?","a":"Scans data in S3 and adds table definitions to the Data Catalog","w":["Copies the files into Redshift automatically","Deletes old files in S3","Sends emails about data errors"],"why":"A crawler reads your data, works out the schema and writes table metadata in the Glue Data Catalog. The data stays where it is.","src":"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/glue/latest/dg/add-crawler.html","id":"aws.i.12.2"},
        {"t":"choice","topic":"AWS","q":"Ravi has CSV sales files in S3 and wants to query them with SQL, with no server to manage. What fits?","a":"Amazon Athena","w":["Amazon EC2 with a database installed","AWS CloudTrail","Amazon Route 53"],"why":"Athena is a serverless query service for data in S3 using standard SQL. You are charged by the data your queries scan.","src":"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/athena/latest/ug/what-is.html","id":"aws.i.12.3"},
        {"t":"pipe","topic":"AWS","q":"Build how Asha queries her CSV files in S3 with SQL.","steps":[["S3","CSV files in S3","src"],["CRWL","Glue crawler"],["CAT","Data Catalog table"],["ATH","Athena SQL query"]],"extra":[["EC2","EC2 server"]],"why":"The crawler creates a catalog table for the files, and Athena uses that table to run SQL. No EC2 server is needed.","src":"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/athena/latest/ug/what-is.html","id":"aws.i.12.4"},
        {"t":"choice","topic":"AWS","q":"Lakshmi wants her Athena queries to scan less data. What helps?","a":"Store data in a columnar format like Parquet and use partitions","w":["Store everything in one huge CSV file","Always run SELECT * on all the data","Turn off the Data Catalog"],"why":"Partitions let Athena skip data, and columnar formats read only the columns you ask for.","src":"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/athena/latest/ug/performance-tuning.html","id":"aws.i.12.5"},
        {"t":"choice","topic":"AWS","q":"Which service is the AWS data warehouse for fast analytics on large structured data?","a":"Amazon Redshift","w":["Amazon Athena","Amazon Kinesis","AWS Glue crawler"],"why":"Redshift is the data warehouse. Athena queries files in S3 in place, and Kinesis streams data.","src":"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/redshift/latest/mgmt/welcome.html","id":"aws.i.12.6"},
        {"t":"choice","topic":"AWS","q":"Ravi needs to collect a continuous stream of events, like clicks from a website. Which AWS service fits?","a":"Amazon Kinesis Data Streams","w":["Amazon S3 Glacier","AWS Budgets","Amazon RDS read replica"],"why":"Kinesis collects and processes streaming data in real time. It is the streaming side of the data services.","src":"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/streams/latest/dev/introduction.html","id":"aws.i.12.7"},
        {"t":"cmd","topic":"AWS","q":"Every evening Lakshmi copies only the new and changed files from her `./sales` folder to the data lake bucket `s3://guntur-lake/sales`. Build the command.","parts":["aws","s3","sync","./sales","s3://guntur-lake/sales"],"extra":["upload","glue"],"why":"`aws s3 sync` copies only new and changed files, from the first path (source) to the second (destination).","src":"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/cli/latest/reference/s3/sync.html","id":"aws.i.12.10"},
        {"t":"check","topic":"AWS","q":"Every night Ravi must clean his CSV files in S3 and write them back as Parquet, with no servers to manage. What fits?","a":"An AWS Glue ETL job","w":["A Glue crawler","AWS CloudTrail","An Amazon RDS read replica"],"why":"A Glue job runs a script that reads, transforms and writes data, and a trigger can start it on a schedule. A crawler only adds table definitions to the Data Catalog.","src":"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/glue/latest/dg/author-job-glue.html","id":"aws.i.12.8"},
        {"t":"check","topic":"AWS","q":"What does the Glue Data Catalog store?","a":"Metadata about your data, such as table and column definitions","w":["The data files themselves","Your IAM access keys","Your EC2 instances"],"why":"The catalog holds metadata. The data files stay in S3.","src":"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/glue/latest/dg/catalog-and-crawler.html","id":"aws.i.12.9"}
      ] },
      { id: "aws.a.13", level: "a", n: 13, title: "CloudFormation", cards: [
        {"t":"learn","topic":"AWS","title":"CloudFormation: infrastructure as code","body":"**AWS CloudFormation** builds AWS resources from a **template** written in YAML or JSON. The resources made from one template are managed together as a **stack**. In a template, `Resources` is required, while `Parameters` and `Outputs` are optional. Use `!Ref` and `!GetAtt` to link resources.","code":"Resources:\n  NotesBucket:\n    Type: AWS::S3::Bucket","lang":"yaml","src":"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/AWSCloudFormation/latest/UserGuide/template-anatomy.html","id":"aws.a.13.1"},
        {"t":"choice","topic":"AWS","q":"Ravi creates the same S3 bucket and EC2 server by clicking in the console every time. What does CloudFormation give him?","a":"He writes the setup once in a template and can create it again the same way","w":["The resources become free to run","AWS stops him from changing resources in the console","His resources are copied to every Region automatically"],"why":"A template is code you can keep, review and reuse. It does not change what the resources cost.","src":"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/AWSCloudFormation/latest/UserGuide/Welcome.html","id":"aws.a.13.2"},
        {"t":"choice","topic":"AWS","q":"Which section of a CloudFormation template is required?","a":"Resources","w":["Parameters","Outputs","Description"],"why":"`Resources` is the only required section, because a template with no resources builds nothing.","src":"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/AWSCloudFormation/latest/UserGuide/template-anatomy.html","id":"aws.a.13.3"},
        {"t":"indent","topic":"AWS","step":2,"q":"Fix the indentation of this template so that the bucket has versioning turned on.","lang":"yaml","lines":[[0,"Resources:"],[2,"NotesBucket:"],[4,"Type: AWS::S3::Bucket"],[4,"Properties:"],[6,"VersioningConfiguration:"],[8,"Status: Enabled"]],"why":"`Type` and `Properties` sit under the resource name. `Status` sits under `VersioningConfiguration`, which sits under `Properties`.","src":"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/AWSCloudFormation/latest/UserGuide/aws-properties-s3-bucket-versioningconfiguration.html","id":"aws.a.13.4"},
        {"t":"bug","topic":"AWS","q":"Asha wants the Outputs section to show the real bucket name. Now it shows the plain text NotesBucket. Which line is wrong?","code":["Resources:","  NotesBucket:","    Type: AWS::S3::Bucket","Outputs:","  BucketName:","    Value: NotesBucket"],"lang":"yaml","bad":5,"why":"A plain word is just text. `Ref` returns the resource name for an S3 bucket, so the output shows the real bucket name.","fix":"    Value: {Ref: NotesBucket}","src":"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/AWSCloudFormation/latest/UserGuide/intrinsic-function-reference-ref.html","id":"aws.a.13.5"},
        {"t":"pipe","topic":"AWS","q":"Build a safe way to update a stack: preview first, then apply.","steps":[["TMPL","Edit the template","src"],["CSET","Create a change set"],["REVW","Review the changes"],["EXEC","Execute the change set"]],"extra":[["DEL","Delete the stack"]],"why":"A **change set** shows what CloudFormation will add, change or remove before anything happens. Deleting the stack would remove all its resources.","src":"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/AWSCloudFormation/latest/UserGuide/using-cfn-updating-stacks-changesets.html","id":"aws.a.13.6"},
        {"t":"choice","topic":"AWS","q":"Lakshmi's teammate changed a security group by hand in the console. What does CloudFormation drift detection do?","a":"It finds resources whose real settings differ from the stack template","w":["It changes the resource back to the template settings by itself","It checks the template for spelling mistakes","It deletes resources that were changed by hand"],"why":"Drift detection only reports the differences. You fix them yourself, for example by updating the stack or the resource.","src":"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/AWSCloudFormation/latest/UserGuide/using-cfn-stack-drift.html","id":"aws.a.13.7"},
        {"t":"check","topic":"AWS","q":"What is the main use of a change set?","a":"To preview the changes an update will make to a stack","w":["To roll back a stack after it is deleted","To store passwords for the stack","To copy a stack to another account"],"why":"You create a change set, read it, and then choose whether to execute it.","src":"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/AWSCloudFormation/latest/UserGuide/using-cfn-updating-stacks-changesets.html","id":"aws.a.13.8"},
        {"t":"check","topic":"AWS","q":"After practice, Ravi deletes his CloudFormation stack. What happens to the resources it created?","a":"They are deleted too, unless a resource is set to be kept","w":["They stay and keep running without the stack","Only the template file is deleted","They are moved to a new stack automatically"],"why":"Deleting a stack removes its resources, which is a clean way to stop practice costs. Some resources can be kept with a deletion policy.","src":"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/AWSCloudFormation/latest/UserGuide/cfn-console-delete-stack.html","id":"aws.a.13.9"}
      ] },
      { id: "aws.a.14", level: "a", n: 14, title: "EKS (Kubernetes on AWS)", cards: [
        {"t":"learn","topic":"AWS","title":"EKS: Kubernetes on AWS","body":"**Amazon EKS** runs the Kubernetes **control plane** for you. Your pods run on worker nodes, which can be EC2 instances in a **node group**, or **AWS Fargate**. Images are usually stored in **Amazon ECR**. Delete the cluster after practice, because it keeps costing money while it exists.","code":"aws eks update-kubeconfig --name demo --region ap-south-1\nkubectl get nodes","lang":"shell","src":"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/eks/latest/userguide/what-is-eks.html","id":"aws.a.14.1"},
        {"t":"choice","topic":"AWS","q":"In Amazon EKS, which part does AWS manage for Asha?","a":"The Kubernetes control plane","w":["Her application code","The Docker images she builds","The Kubernetes manifests she writes"],"why":"Asha still writes and updates her app and manifests. AWS runs the control plane that stores cluster state and schedules pods.","src":"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/eks/latest/userguide/what-is-eks.html","id":"aws.a.14.2"},
        {"t":"choice","topic":"AWS","q":"Ravi does not want to manage any EC2 worker servers for his pods. Which EKS option fits?","a":"AWS Fargate","w":["A key pair for SSH","An internet gateway","A read replica"],"why":"With Fargate, EKS runs pods without you managing the nodes. Managed node groups still use EC2 instances in your account.","src":"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/eks/latest/userguide/fargate.html","id":"aws.a.14.3"},
        {"t":"order","topic":"AWS","q":"Put these steps in order to run an app on a new EKS cluster.","items":["Create the EKS cluster","Run `aws eks update-kubeconfig`","Run `kubectl apply -f app.yaml`","Run `kubectl get pods`"],"why":"`update-kubeconfig` lets `kubectl` talk to the cluster. Then you apply the manifest and check the pods.","src":"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/eks/latest/userguide/create-kubeconfig.html","id":"aws.a.14.4"},
        {"t":"indent","topic":"AWS","step":2,"q":"Fix the indentation of this Kubernetes Pod manifest.","lang":"k8s","lines":[[0,"apiVersion: v1"],[0,"kind: Pod"],[0,"metadata:"],[2,"name: web"],[0,"spec:"],[2,"containers:"],[4,"- name: web"],[6,"image: nginx"]],"why":"`name` sits under `metadata`. `image` belongs to the container, so it lines up with `name` inside the list item.","src":"https://kubernetes.io/docs/concepts/workloads/pods/","id":"aws.a.14.5"},
        {"t":"bug","topic":"AWS","q":"Lakshmi runs this to connect `kubectl` to her EKS cluster named demo, but the first command fails. Which line is wrong?","code":["aws eks update-kubeconfig --cluster-name demo --region ap-south-1","kubectl get nodes"],"lang":"shell","bad":0,"why":"The option that gives the cluster name is `--name`.","fix":"aws eks update-kubeconfig --name demo --region ap-south-1","src":"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/eks/latest/userguide/create-kubeconfig.html","id":"aws.a.14.6"},
        {"t":"choice","topic":"AWS","q":"A pod in EKS must read files from S3. What is the safest way to give it permission?","a":"Give the pod an IAM role, for example with EKS Pod Identity","w":["Put the root user's access keys inside the container image","Store an IAM user's access keys in the app code","Give every pod the same admin permissions"],"why":"A role gives the pod temporary credentials. Keys inside images or code can leak.","src":"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/eks/latest/userguide/pod-identities.html","id":"aws.a.14.7"},
        {"t":"cmd","topic":"AWS","q":"Ravi created the EKS cluster `shop-cluster` in Mumbai. Build the command that sets up `kubectl` to talk to it: Region first, then the cluster name.","parts":["aws","eks","update-kubeconfig","--region","ap-south-1","--name","shop-cluster"],"extra":["get-credentials","--cluster"],"why":"`aws eks update-kubeconfig` writes the cluster's details into your kubeconfig file. The cluster is named with `--name`.","src":"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/eks/latest/userguide/create-kubeconfig.html","id":"aws.a.14.10"},
        {"t":"check","topic":"AWS","q":"What is Amazon ECR used for in an EKS project?","a":"Storing container images that the cluster pulls","w":["Running the Kubernetes control plane","Splitting a VPC into subnets","Sending alarm emails"],"why":"ECR is a registry for container images. You push an image there, and pods pull it from it.","src":"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/AmazonECR/latest/userguide/what-is-ecr.html","id":"aws.a.14.8"},
        {"t":"check","topic":"AWS","q":"Asha finished her EKS practice. What should she do to stop the cost?","a":"Delete the cluster and the node groups or Fargate profiles she created","w":["Close the browser tab","Delete the kubeconfig file on her laptop","Run `kubectl get pods` once more"],"why":"Resources keep running and billing until they are deleted. Deleting local files does not touch the cluster.","src":"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/eks/latest/userguide/delete-cluster.html","id":"aws.a.14.9"}
      ] },
      { id: "aws.a.15", level: "a", n: 15, title: "Security best practices", cards: [
        {"t":"learn","topic":"AWS","title":"Security best practices on AWS","body":"AWS secures the cloud itself, and you secure what you put in it. This is the **shared responsibility model**. A policy lists `Effect`, `Action` and `Resource`. Give only the permissions needed (**least privilege**), use **MFA** and roles, and turn on **CloudTrail**.","code":"{\n  \"Effect\": \"Allow\",\n  \"Action\": \"s3:GetObject\",\n  \"Resource\": \"arn:aws:s3:::asha-notes/*\"\n}","lang":"text","src":"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/IAM/latest/UserGuide/best-practices.html","id":"aws.a.15.1"},
        {"t":"choice","topic":"AWS","q":"In an IAM policy statement, what does `Effect` say?","a":"Whether the statement allows or denies access","w":["Which AWS service is used","Which Region the policy works in","When the policy expires"],"why":"`Effect` is either `Allow` or `Deny`. `Action` says what can be done, and `Resource` says on what.","src":"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/IAM/latest/UserGuide/reference_policies_elements.html","id":"aws.a.15.2"},
        {"t":"bug","topic":"AWS","q":"Ravi's app only needs to read files from one bucket. Which line gives it far too much access?","code":["{ \"Version\": \"2012-10-17\", \"Statement\": {","\"Effect\": \"Allow\",","\"Action\": \"s3:*\",","\"Resource\": \"arn:aws:s3:::asha-notes/*\"","} }"],"lang":"text","bad":2,"why":"`s3:*` allows every S3 action. Least privilege means allowing only what is needed, like `s3:GetObject`.","fix":"\"Action\": \"s3:GetObject\",","src":"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/IAM/latest/UserGuide/best-practices.html","id":"aws.a.15.3"},
        {"t":"choice","topic":"AWS","q":"Lakshmi wants one place that collects findings from GuardDuty and other services and checks her account against security best practices. Which service?","a":"AWS Security Hub","w":["AWS CloudFormation","Amazon CloudWatch Logs","AWS Budgets"],"why":"Security Hub (named Security Hub CSPM in the docs) gathers findings in one view and runs checks such as AWS Foundational Security Best Practices. GuardDuty finds threats; Security Hub brings the findings together.","src":"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/securityhub/latest/userguide/what-is-securityhub.html","id":"aws.a.15.4"},
        {"t":"choice","topic":"AWS","q":"Lakshmi wants to encrypt her data and control who can use the encryption keys. Which service helps?","a":"AWS Key Management Service (KMS)","w":["Amazon CloudWatch","AWS Budgets","Elastic Load Balancing"],"why":"KMS creates and controls encryption keys. CloudWatch is for metrics and logs.","src":"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/kms/latest/developerguide/overview.html","id":"aws.a.15.5"},
        {"t":"choice","topic":"AWS","q":"Asha wants an alert if an EC2 instance seems to talk to a known bad IP address. Which service is made for this?","a":"Amazon GuardDuty","w":["AWS CloudTrail","Amazon Route 53","AWS Glue"],"why":"GuardDuty is threat detection. CloudTrail records API calls, so it shows who did what but does not hunt threats itself.","src":"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/guardduty/latest/ug/what-is-guardduty.html","id":"aws.a.15.6"},
        {"t":"choice","topic":"AWS","q":"Ravi's app needs a database password. Where should he keep it?","a":"In AWS Secrets Manager","w":["Written in the source code on GitHub","In the name of an S3 bucket","In a tag on the EC2 instance"],"why":"Secrets Manager stores secrets safely, and the app reads them when it runs. Code and tags can be seen by many people.","src":"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/secretsmanager/latest/userguide/intro.html","id":"aws.a.15.7"},
        {"t":"check","topic":"AWS","q":"Under the shared responsibility model, who must set up the IAM permissions in Asha's account?","a":"Asha, the customer","w":["AWS, in every case","Her internet provider","The data centre staff in the Region"],"why":"AWS secures the cloud infrastructure. The customer secures what they build in it, like permissions and data.","src":"https://aws.amazon.com/compliance/shared-responsibility-model/","id":"aws.a.15.8"},
        {"t":"check","topic":"AWS","q":"Which setting helps stop an S3 bucket from being made public by mistake?","a":"S3 Block Public Access","w":["S3 storage classes","A bigger EC2 instance","Using one Availability Zone"],"why":"Block Public Access overrides public settings on buckets and objects. Storage classes only change cost and speed.","src":"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/AmazonS3/latest/userguide/access-control-block-public-access.html","id":"aws.a.15.9"}
      ] },
      { id: "aws.a.16", level: "a", n: 16, title: "Well-Architected Framework", cards: [
        {"t":"learn","topic":"AWS","title":"The Well-Architected Framework","body":"The **AWS Well-Architected Framework** is a set of good design habits. It has six pillars: **operational excellence**, **security**, **reliability**, **performance efficiency**, **cost optimization** and **sustainability**. You can review a workload against them with the **AWS Well-Architected Tool**.","src":"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/wellarchitected/latest/framework/welcome.html","id":"aws.a.16.1"},
        {"t":"choice","topic":"AWS","q":"Which of these is NOT one of the six Well-Architected pillars?","a":"Portability","w":["Reliability","Sustainability","Cost optimization"],"why":"The six pillars are operational excellence, security, reliability, performance efficiency, cost optimization and sustainability.","src":"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/wellarchitected/latest/framework/welcome.html","id":"aws.a.16.2"},
        {"t":"choice","topic":"AWS","q":"Ravi runs his app in two Availability Zones so it keeps working if one fails. Which pillar is this?","a":"Reliability","w":["Cost optimization","Sustainability","Operational excellence"],"why":"The reliability pillar is about recovering from failures and meeting demand. Using more than one AZ is a common habit.","src":"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/wellarchitected/latest/framework/reliability.html","id":"aws.a.16.3"},
        {"t":"choice","topic":"AWS","q":"Asha turns off test servers at night and removes unused resources. Which pillar does this support most?","a":"Cost optimization","w":["Security","Performance efficiency","Reliability"],"why":"Cost optimization means paying only for what you need. Stopping unused resources is a simple habit that helps.","src":"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/wellarchitected/latest/framework/cost-optimization.html","id":"aws.a.16.4"},
        {"t":"choice","topic":"AWS","q":"Lakshmi uses CloudFormation to build her app the same way every time and improves her operations steps after each problem. Which pillar is this?","a":"Operational excellence","w":["Security","Sustainability","Performance efficiency"],"why":"Operational excellence is about running workloads well and learning from events. Automating with code is a common habit here.","src":"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/wellarchitected/latest/framework/operational-excellence.html","id":"aws.a.16.5"},
        {"t":"pipe","topic":"AWS","q":"Build a simple review flow with the Well-Architected Tool.","steps":[["DEF","Define the workload","src"],["ASK","Answer the questions"],["RISK","See risks and the improvement plan"],["SAVE","Save a milestone"]],"extra":[["EC2","Launch more EC2 servers"]],"why":"The tool asks questions about your workload, shows risks, and lets you save milestones to track progress. It does not need new servers.","src":"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/wellarchitected/latest/userguide/tool.html","id":"aws.a.16.6"},
        {"t":"choice","topic":"AWS","q":"Ravi tests different instance types and picks the one that answers his users fastest. Which pillar is this?","a":"Performance efficiency","w":["Security","Reliability","Operational excellence"],"why":"Performance efficiency is about using computing resources efficiently to meet your requirements, such as choosing the right compute. Test and measure instead of guessing.","src":"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/wellarchitected/latest/framework/performance-efficiency.html","id":"aws.a.16.7"},
        {"t":"check","topic":"AWS","q":"Which pillar asks you to protect data and use least privilege?","a":"Security","w":["Sustainability","Performance efficiency","Cost optimization"],"why":"The security pillar covers protecting data and systems, such as strong identity and encryption.","src":"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/wellarchitected/latest/framework/security.html","id":"aws.a.16.8"},
        {"t":"check","topic":"AWS","q":"Which pillar is about using resources efficiently to lower environmental impact?","a":"Sustainability","w":["Reliability","Operational excellence","Security"],"why":"The sustainability pillar focuses on reducing the environmental impact of running cloud workloads.","src":"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/wellarchitected/latest/framework/sustainability.html","id":"aws.a.16.9"}
      ] }
    ] },
    { id: "gcp", name: "Google Cloud", topic: "GCP", blurb: "Google Cloud: projects, Compute Engine, Cloud Storage and BigQuery.", units: [
      { id: "gcp.b.01", level: "b", n: 1, title: "Projects, regions and zones", cards: [
        {"t":"learn","topic":"GCP","title":"Everything lives in a project","body":"In Google Cloud, every VM, bucket and table lives in a **project**. A project has a name, a **project ID** you choose once and cannot change, and a **project number** Google gives it. Resources run in **regions** (like Mumbai `asia-south1`), and each region has **zones** (like `asia-south1-a`).","code":"gcloud projects list\ngcloud config set project my-project-id\ngcloud config get-value project","lang":"shell","id":"gcp.b.01.1"},
        {"t":"choice","topic":"GCP","q":"Asha creates a VM and a bucket in Google Cloud. What holds both of them?","a":"A project","w":["A zone","A billing account","A Cloud Storage bucket"],"why":"Every Google Cloud resource belongs to exactly one project. A billing account only pays for projects; it does not hold resources.","id":"gcp.b.01.2"},
        {"t":"choice","topic":"GCP","q":"Which part of a project do you choose yourself and can never change later?","a":"The project ID","w":["The project name","The project number","The billing account ID"],"why":"The project ID is chosen at creation and is permanent. The name can be edited, and Google assigns the project number.","id":"gcp.b.01.3"},
        {"t":"order","topic":"GCP","q":"Put the Google Cloud resource hierarchy in order, from the top to the bottom.","items":["Organization","Folder","Project","Resource (like a VM)"],"why":"An organization holds folders, folders hold projects, and projects hold resources. Folders are optional but help group teams.","id":"gcp.b.01.4"},
        {"t":"choice","topic":"GCP","q":"What is `asia-south1-a`?","a":"A zone inside the Mumbai region `asia-south1`","w":["A region in Delhi","A project ID in Mumbai","A folder in an organization"],"why":"A zone name is the region name plus a letter. Regions are bigger areas that contain zones.","id":"gcp.b.01.5"},
        {"t":"bug","topic":"GCP","q":"Ravi wants to set his default project, but the command fails with an invalid command error. Which line is wrong?","code":["gcloud projects list","gcloud config get-value project","gcloud set project my-project-id","gcloud config list"],"lang":"shell","bad":2,"why":"Defaults are stored with `gcloud config set`. There is no `gcloud set` command.","id":"gcp.b.01.6"},
        {"t":"choice","topic":"GCP","q":"A role is granted to a folder. What happens to the projects inside that folder?","a":"They inherit the role","w":["Nothing, each project must be granted separately","The projects are deleted","The role moves to the organization"],"why":"Policies set on a higher level are inherited by everything below it. This saves time but also means a wide grant gives wide access.","id":"gcp.b.01.7"},
        {"t":"check","topic":"GCP","q":"Lakshmi creates a VM in zone `asia-south1-a`. Which statement is true?","a":"The VM belongs to one project and runs in one zone","w":["The VM belongs to a region only and has no project","The VM is stored inside her billing account","A zone is made of many projects"],"why":"Every resource belongs to one project, and a VM runs in one zone of a region. A billing account only pays.","id":"gcp.b.01.8"},
        {"t":"check","topic":"GCP","q":"Which command lists the projects you can see?","a":"`gcloud projects list`","w":["`gcloud list projects`","`gcloud project show`","`gcloud config projects`"],"why":"The pattern is `gcloud <group> <action>`: group `projects`, action `list`.","id":"gcp.b.01.9"}
      ] },
      { id: "gcp.b.02", level: "b", n: 2, title: "IAM roles", cards: [
        {"t":"learn","topic":"GCP","title":"IAM: who can do what","body":"**IAM** decides who can do what on which resource. A **principal** (a person, a group or a **service account**) gets a **role**, which is a set of permissions. Always give the smallest role that does the job: this is **least privilege**.","code":"gcloud projects add-iam-policy-binding my-project-id \\\n  --member=\"user:asha@example.com\" \\\n  --role=\"roles/storage.objectViewer\"","lang":"shell","id":"gcp.b.02.1"},
        {"t":"choice","topic":"GCP","q":"In IAM, what is a principal?","a":"Who gets access, like a user, group or service account","w":["A set of permissions","A group of VMs","A billing report"],"why":"A principal is the identity. The role is the set of permissions you give it.","id":"gcp.b.02.2"},
        {"t":"choice","topic":"GCP","q":"Ravi's report app only needs to read objects in Cloud Storage. Which role follows least privilege?","a":"`roles/storage.objectViewer`","w":["`roles/owner`","`roles/editor`","`roles/resourcemanager.projectIamAdmin`"],"why":"A predefined role made for the job is narrower than the basic roles. Owner and Editor give power over many services.","id":"gcp.b.02.3"},
        {"t":"choice","topic":"GCP","q":"What is a service account used for?","a":"An identity for an app or VM, not for a person","w":["A person's personal login","A paid support plan","A place to store files"],"why":"Apps and VMs use service accounts to call Google Cloud. Give each one only the roles it needs.","id":"gcp.b.02.4"},
        {"t":"pipe","topic":"GCP","q":"Build how Asha gets permission to read files in a project.","steps":[["ASHA","Asha (principal)","src"],["ROLE","Storage Object Viewer role"],["GRNT","Role granted on the project"],["READ","Asha can read objects"]],"extra":[["BILL","Billing account"]],"why":"A principal gets a role granted on a resource, and then the permissions apply. A billing account only pays for usage.","id":"gcp.b.02.5"},
        {"t":"bug","topic":"GCP","q":"One line fails with an invalid command error. Which line is wrong?","code":["gcloud iam service-accounts create report-app","gcloud iam service-accounts list","gcloud iam role describe roles/storage.objectViewer","gcloud iam roles list"],"lang":"shell","bad":2,"why":"The command group is `roles` (plural), like `service-accounts`.","id":"gcp.b.02.6"},
        {"t":"choice","topic":"GCP","q":"Why should you avoid the basic roles Owner, Editor and Viewer for production work?","a":"They are very broad and cover many services","w":["They only work on Fridays","They cannot be granted to users","They always cost extra"],"why":"Basic roles mix lots of permissions. Predefined or custom roles let you give just what is needed.","id":"gcp.b.02.7"},
        {"t":"check","topic":"GCP","q":"Where do you grant a role so it applies to every project in a team's folder?","a":"On the folder","w":["On each VM","On the billing account only","In the bucket name"],"why":"Roles granted on a folder are inherited by the projects inside it.","id":"gcp.b.02.8"},
        {"t":"check","topic":"GCP","q":"Asha's app on a VM must read from Cloud Storage. What is the safest identity setup?","a":"Attach a service account that has only the needed storage role","w":["Copy Asha's own password onto the VM","Give the VM an Owner key and email it to the team","Make the bucket public so no identity is needed"],"why":"A service account with a narrow role avoids sharing personal credentials. Never copy passwords or keys around.","id":"gcp.b.02.9"}
      ] },
      { id: "gcp.b.03", level: "b", n: 3, title: "Compute Engine VMs", cards: [
        {"t":"learn","topic":"GCP","title":"Compute Engine: virtual machines","body":"**Compute Engine** runs virtual machines (VMs) in a **zone**. You pick a **machine type** (CPU and memory) and an **image** (the operating system for the boot disk). Create one with `gcloud compute instances create`.","code":"gcloud compute instances create web-1 \\\n  --zone=asia-south1-a \\\n  --machine-type=e2-medium \\\n  --image-family=debian-12 \\\n  --image-project=debian-cloud","lang":"shell","id":"gcp.b.03.1"},
        {"t":"choice","topic":"GCP","q":"What does the machine type of a VM decide?","a":"How many vCPUs and how much memory it has","w":["Which region the project lives in","Who can log in to it","Which bucket it uses"],"why":"Machine types are sets of virtual CPUs and memory. Pick a small one while learning.","id":"gcp.b.03.2"},
        {"t":"choice","topic":"GCP","q":"What is an image used for when you create a VM?","a":"It gives the boot disk its operating system","w":["It sets the firewall rules","It stores your bills","It chooses the zone"],"why":"A public image such as Debian is copied to the boot disk of the new VM.","id":"gcp.b.03.3"},
        {"t":"choice","topic":"GCP","q":"Ravi finished his lab and will never use `web-1` again. Which command removes it for good?","a":"`gcloud compute instances delete web-1 --zone=asia-south1-a`","w":["`gcloud compute instances stop web-1 --zone=asia-south1-a`","`gcloud compute instances reset web-1 --zone=asia-south1-a`","`gcloud compute instances list`"],"why":"`stop` keeps the VM and its disks so you can start it later. `delete` removes the VM, which is the cleanup you want.","id":"gcp.b.03.4"},
        {"t":"pipe","topic":"GCP","q":"Build the life of a practice VM, from start to cleanup.","steps":[["PROJ","Select the project","src"],["CRT","Create the VM"],["RUN","VM is running"],["DEL","Delete the VM"]],"extra":[["BKT","Create a bucket"]],"why":"Pick the project, create the VM, use it, then delete it so it stops costing money. A bucket is not part of the VM life.","id":"gcp.b.03.5"},
        {"t":"choice","topic":"GCP","q":"Asha's web server on port 80 cannot be opened from the internet. What is the likely fix?","a":"Add an ingress firewall rule that allows `tcp:80`","w":["Change the image family","Rename the project","Stop the VM and start it again"],"why":"Incoming traffic is blocked unless a firewall rule allows it. Open only the ports you need.","id":"gcp.b.03.6"},
        {"t":"order","topic":"GCP","q":"Put these commands in the order you would use them in a VM's life.","items":["`gcloud compute instances create`","`gcloud compute instances list`","`gcloud compute instances stop`","`gcloud compute instances delete`"],"why":"Create the VM, check it is running, stop it when you pause work, and delete it when you are done for good.","id":"gcp.b.03.7"},
        {"t":"check","topic":"GCP","q":"Which flag tells `gcloud compute instances create` where to place the VM?","a":"`--zone=asia-south1-a`","w":["`--region=asia-south1-a`","`--location=asia-south1-a`","`--area=asia-south1-a`"],"why":"VMs live in a zone, and `asia-south1-a` is a zone. A region name has no letter at the end.","id":"gcp.b.03.8"},
        {"t":"check","topic":"GCP","q":"You finished practice and will not need the VM again. What is the best step to avoid surprise bills?","a":"Delete the VM (and any disks you do not need)","w":["Close the browser tab","Remove your own IAM role","Change the VM's zone"],"why":"Closing the browser does not stop anything. Delete resources you no longer need.","id":"gcp.b.03.9"}
      ] },
      { id: "gcp.b.04", level: "b", n: 4, title: "Cloud Storage buckets", cards: [
        {"t":"learn","topic":"GCP","title":"Cloud Storage buckets","body":"**Cloud Storage** keeps files, called **objects**, inside **buckets**. A bucket name must be **globally unique** and the bucket has a **location**. Use `gcloud storage cp` to upload and `gcloud storage ls` to list.","code":"gcloud storage buckets create gs://asha-notes-2026 --location=asia-south1\ngcloud storage cp notes.pdf gs://asha-notes-2026/\ngcloud storage ls gs://asha-notes-2026","lang":"shell","id":"gcp.b.04.1"},
        {"t":"choice","topic":"GCP","q":"Bucket names in Cloud Storage must be unique where?","a":"Across all of Cloud Storage, worldwide","w":["Only inside your project","Only inside your region","Only inside your folder"],"why":"Names share one global space, so a common name like `photos` is probably taken.","id":"gcp.b.04.2"},
        {"t":"choice","topic":"GCP","q":"What is an object in Cloud Storage?","a":"A file and its metadata stored in a bucket","w":["A virtual machine","A billing account","A firewall rule"],"why":"Photos, PDFs and CSV files are all objects. Buckets are the containers for them.","id":"gcp.b.04.3"},
        {"t":"choice","topic":"GCP","q":"Which storage class is meant for data you access less than once a year?","a":"Archive","w":["Standard","Nearline","Coldline"],"why":"Standard is for data used often. Nearline, Coldline and Archive are for data used less and less often.","id":"gcp.b.04.4"},
        {"t":"bug","topic":"GCP","q":"Asha's upload command fails because the command name does not exist. Which line is wrong?","code":["gcloud storage buckets create gs://asha-notes-2026 --location=asia-south1","gcloud storage copy notes.pdf gs://asha-notes-2026/","gcloud storage ls gs://asha-notes-2026","gcloud storage rm gs://asha-notes-2026/notes.pdf"],"lang":"shell","bad":1,"why":"The copy command is short: `cp`. It copies to and from buckets.","id":"gcp.b.04.5"},
        {"t":"choice","topic":"GCP","q":"What does public access prevention do on a bucket?","a":"It stops the bucket and its objects from being made public on the internet","w":["It makes every object download faster","It deletes old objects","It moves the bucket to another region"],"why":"Turn it on for private data so a wrong setting cannot expose it.","id":"gcp.b.04.6"},
        {"t":"order","topic":"GCP","q":"Put these steps in order to store a file in Cloud Storage and clean up.","items":["Create a bucket","Upload the file with `gcloud storage cp`","Check it with `gcloud storage ls`","Remove it with `gcloud storage rm` when done"],"why":"The bucket must exist before you upload. Remove test files when you no longer need them.","id":"gcp.b.04.7"},
        {"t":"check","topic":"GCP","q":"Why does Ravi choose `--location=asia-south1` for his bucket?","a":"The data is stored in Mumbai, close to his users in India","w":["It makes the bucket name unique","It makes all objects public","It turns the bucket into a VM"],"why":"A bucket has a location, chosen when it is created. Pick one near your users or where the data must stay.","id":"gcp.b.04.8"},
        {"t":"check","topic":"GCP","q":"Which command uploads a local file to a bucket?","a":"`gcloud storage cp report.csv gs://my-bucket/`","w":["`gcloud storage ls report.csv gs://my-bucket/`","`gcloud storage rm report.csv gs://my-bucket/`","`gcloud storage upload gs://my-bucket/ report.csv`"],"why":"`cp` copies from source to destination. `ls` lists and `rm` deletes.","id":"gcp.b.04.9"}
      ] },
      { id: "gcp.b.05", level: "b", n: 5, title: "BigQuery basics", cards: [
        {"t":"learn","topic":"GCP","title":"BigQuery: SQL on big data","body":"**BigQuery** is a **serverless** data warehouse: you write SQL and Google runs it. Tables live in **datasets** and are named `project.dataset.table`. You pay attention to how much data a query **scans**, so select only the columns you need.","code":"SELECT word, word_count\nFROM `bigquery-public-data.samples.shakespeare`\nLIMIT 10;","lang":"sql","id":"gcp.b.05.1"},
        {"t":"choice","topic":"GCP","q":"What does serverless mean for BigQuery?","a":"You do not set up or manage servers","w":["It works without internet","It has no tables","It only runs on your laptop"],"why":"Google manages the machines. You focus on your data and SQL.","id":"gcp.b.05.2"},
        {"t":"choice","topic":"GCP","q":"In the table name `my-project.sales.orders`, what is `sales`?","a":"The dataset","w":["The project","The column","The region"],"why":"The pattern is project, then dataset, then table.","id":"gcp.b.05.3"},
        {"t":"choice","topic":"GCP","q":"Which change makes a query scan less data?","a":"Select only the columns you need","w":["Add `LIMIT 10` to a normal table query","Use `SELECT *`","Add `ORDER BY` at the end"],"why":"BigQuery reads the columns you ask for. `LIMIT` stops the output but not the scanning on a normal table.","id":"gcp.b.05.4"},
        {"t":"output","topic":"GCP","q":"Run this on a table with 4 orders. What does it return?","code":"SELECT COUNT(*)\nFROM orders\nWHERE city = 'Guntur'\n  AND status = 'paid';","lang":"sql","a":"2","w":["1","3","4"],"why":"Only rows that match both conditions are counted. Guntur has 3 orders but one was cancelled.","id":"gcp.b.05.5"},
        {"t":"pipe","topic":"GCP","q":"Build the steps to analyse your own data in BigQuery.","steps":[["DSET","Create a dataset","src"],["TBL","Create a table"],["LOAD","Load data into it"],["SQL","Run a SELECT query"]],"extra":[["VM","Create a VM"]],"why":"A table lives in a dataset, and you load data before you query it. BigQuery is serverless, so no VM is needed.","id":"gcp.b.05.6"},
        {"t":"bug","topic":"GCP","q":"Lakshmi counts paid orders per city, but the query gives a syntax error. Which line is wrong?","code":["SELECT city, COUNT(*) AS orders","FROM sales.orders","WHERE status = 'paid'","GROUP city;"],"lang":"sql","bad":3,"why":"Grouping needs the words `GROUP BY`, not `GROUP` alone.","id":"gcp.b.05.7"},
        {"t":"check","topic":"GCP","q":"Asha only needs `city` and `amount` from a very large table. Which query is best?","a":"`SELECT city, amount FROM sales.orders`","w":["`SELECT * FROM sales.orders`","`SELECT * FROM sales.orders LIMIT 10`","`SELECT * FROM sales.orders ORDER BY city`"],"why":"Asking for just two columns reads less data. `LIMIT` on its own does not reduce what is scanned.","id":"gcp.b.05.8"},
        {"t":"check","topic":"GCP","q":"How can you see roughly how much data a query will process before you run it?","a":"Use a dry run (or check the estimate shown in the console)","w":["Run it and look at the bill later","Use `bq head` on the table","Add `LIMIT 1` to it"],"why":"A dry run checks the query and estimates bytes processed without running it. `bq head` only previews rows.","id":"gcp.b.05.9"}
      ] },
      { id: "gcp.b.06", level: "b", n: 6, title: "Billing and budgets", cards: [
        {"t":"learn","topic":"GCP","title":"Billing accounts and budgets","body":"A **billing account** pays for the projects linked to it. A **budget** sends **alerts** when spending reaches your thresholds, but it does not stop your resources by itself. So delete or stop what you do not use.","code":"gcloud billing accounts list\ngcloud billing projects describe my-project-id","lang":"shell","id":"gcp.b.06.1"},
        {"t":"choice","topic":"GCP","q":"What pays for the resources in a project?","a":"The billing account linked to the project","w":["The project number","The zone","The service account"],"why":"A project needs a linked billing account to use paid services.","id":"gcp.b.06.2"},
        {"t":"choice","topic":"GCP","q":"By default, what does a budget do when spending reaches the threshold?","a":"It sends an alert","w":["It deletes all resources","It stops all VMs","It closes the billing account"],"why":"Budgets notify you. They do not cap usage unless you build extra automation.","id":"gcp.b.06.3"},
        {"t":"choice","topic":"GCP","q":"Ravi finished his practice and wants no more charges from it. What is the best step?","a":"Delete the resources you made, or shut down the practice project","w":["Only close the console tab","Rename the project","Turn off email alerts"],"why":"Resources keep running until you stop or delete them, even when you are not logged in.","id":"gcp.b.06.4"},
        {"t":"bug","topic":"GCP","q":"Asha wants to shut down her practice project, but one line gives an invalid command error. Which line is wrong?","code":["gcloud billing accounts list","gcloud billing projects describe my-project-id","gcloud project delete my-project-id","gcloud projects list"],"lang":"shell","bad":2,"why":"The group name is `projects`, plural.","id":"gcp.b.06.5"},
        {"t":"order","topic":"GCP","q":"Put these cleanup steps in a sensible order after a practice lab.","items":["List what you created","Delete VMs and disks you do not need","Delete test buckets","Shut down the practice project"],"why":"First see what exists, then remove it. Shutting down the project is the final cleanup.","id":"gcp.b.06.6"},
        {"t":"pipe","topic":"GCP","q":"Build the steps to get a spending alert for your project.","steps":[["BILL","Open the billing account","src"],["BUD","Create a budget"],["THR","Set alert thresholds"],["MAIL","Receive the email alert"]],"extra":[["SSH","Open an SSH session"]],"why":"A budget belongs to a billing account and you pick thresholds to alert on. SSH is for logging in to a VM.","id":"gcp.b.06.7"},
        {"t":"check","topic":"GCP","q":"Ravi's new project has no billing account linked. What happens when he tries to use a paid service like Compute Engine?","a":"It does not work until a billing account is linked","w":["It works and Google pays","It is billed to Lakshmi's project","The project is deleted at once"],"why":"Paid services need billing enabled on the project, which means linking a billing account.","id":"gcp.b.06.8"},
        {"t":"check","topic":"GCP","q":"What does this command do?","code":"gcloud projects delete my-practice-project","lang":"shell","a":"Shuts down the project, and it can be restored for a limited time","w":["Deletes only the billing account","Renames the project","Deletes every project in the organization"],"why":"The project and its resources are shut down, so check the project ID first. Never run it on a project you still need.","id":"gcp.b.06.9"}
      ] },
      { id: "gcp.i.07", level: "i", n: 7, title: "VPC networks and firewall rules", cards: [
        {"t":"learn","topic":"GCP","title":"VPC networks and firewall rules","body":"A **VPC network** is your private network in Google Cloud. The network is **global**, but each **subnet** lives in one **region**. **Firewall rules** decide which traffic is allowed: by default incoming traffic is blocked and outgoing traffic is allowed.","code":"gcloud compute firewall-rules create allow-http \\\n  --network=my-vpc --direction=INGRESS \\\n  --allow=tcp:80 --target-tags=web","lang":"shell","src":"https://cloud.google.com/vpc/docs/vpc","id":"gcp.i.07.1"},
        {"t":"choice","topic":"GCP","q":"What is the scope of a VPC network and of its subnets in Google Cloud?","a":"The network is global and each subnet is regional","w":["The network is regional and each subnet is zonal","The network is zonal and each subnet is global","Both the network and its subnets belong to one project folder"],"why":"One VPC network can span all regions. You create subnets inside it, and each subnet is in a single region.","src":"https://cloud.google.com/vpc/docs/vpc","id":"gcp.i.07.2"},
        {"t":"choice","topic":"GCP","q":"Lakshmi wants to choose her own subnet IP ranges instead of getting automatic ones. Which VPC mode should she use?","a":"Custom mode","w":["Auto mode","Regional mode","Zonal mode"],"why":"Auto mode creates one subnet in every region for you. In custom mode you create only the subnets you want, with the ranges you want.","src":"https://cloud.google.com/vpc/docs/vpc#subnet-ranges","id":"gcp.i.07.3"},
        {"t":"choice","topic":"GCP","q":"A new VPC network has no extra firewall rules added by you. What do the implied rules do?","a":"Block incoming traffic and allow outgoing traffic","w":["Allow incoming and outgoing traffic","Block incoming and outgoing traffic","Allow incoming traffic and block outgoing traffic"],"why":"Every VPC network has an implied rule that denies incoming (ingress) traffic and one that allows outgoing (egress) traffic. You add rules to open what you need.","src":"https://cloud.google.com/firewall/docs/firewalls","id":"gcp.i.07.4"},
        {"t":"choice","topic":"GCP","q":"Two firewall rules match the same traffic. One has priority 100 and denies it. The other has priority 1000 and allows it. What happens?","a":"The traffic is denied, because the lower number has higher priority","w":["The traffic is allowed, because 1000 is the higher priority","The rule created first always wins","Both rules are ignored and the traffic is allowed"],"why":"In firewall rules a lower priority number is checked first. A common mistake is to think a bigger number means more important.","src":"https://cloud.google.com/firewall/docs/firewalls","id":"gcp.i.07.5"},
        {"t":"pipe","topic":"GCP","q":"Build a small network for a web server, from the first step to the VM.","steps":[["VPC","Create a custom VPC network","src"],["SUB","Add a subnet in a region"],["FW","Add a rule allowing tcp:80 to tag web"],["VM","Create a VM with the tag web"]],"extra":[["BKT","Create a bucket"]],"why":"The network comes first, then a subnet in it, then a firewall rule that targets the network tag, then a VM in the subnet carrying that tag. A bucket is not part of the network.","src":"https://cloud.google.com/firewall/docs/firewalls","id":"gcp.i.07.6"},
        {"t":"bug","topic":"GCP","q":"Ravi's command to create a subnet fails because of one wrong flag. Which line is wrong?","code":["gcloud compute networks create my-vpc --subnet-mode=custom","gcloud compute networks subnets create web-subnet --network=my-vpc --zone=asia-south1-a --range=10.0.1.0/24","gcloud compute firewall-rules create allow-http --network=my-vpc --allow=tcp:80 --target-tags=web","gcloud compute firewall-rules list"],"lang":"shell","bad":1,"why":"A subnet belongs to a region, not a zone, so the flag is `--region`.","fix":"gcloud compute networks subnets create web-subnet --network=my-vpc --region=asia-south1 --range=10.0.1.0/24","src":"https://cloud.google.com/sdk/gcloud/reference/compute/networks/subnets/create","id":"gcp.i.07.7"},
        {"t":"cmd","topic":"GCP","q":"Asha's VPC `my-vpc` must allow web traffic on TCP port 80. Build the command that creates the firewall rule `allow-http`: network first, then the allowed traffic.","parts":["gcloud","compute","firewall-rules","create","allow-http","--network=my-vpc","--allow=tcp:80"],"extra":["--open=80","aws"],"why":"`gcloud compute firewall-rules create` makes the rule. `--network` picks the VPC and `--allow=tcp:80` says which traffic is allowed.","src":"https://cloud.google.com/sdk/gcloud/reference/compute/firewall-rules/create","id":"gcp.i.07.10"},
        {"t":"check","topic":"GCP","q":"Asha's private VM has no external IP address but must download software updates from the internet. What should she set up?","a":"Cloud NAT","w":["Cloud CDN","Cloud Armor","Cloud Load Balancing"],"why":"Cloud NAT lets VMs without external IPs make outgoing connections. The other services do other jobs, like caching, protection and spreading incoming traffic.","src":"https://cloud.google.com/nat/docs/overview","id":"gcp.i.07.8"},
        {"t":"check","topic":"GCP","q":"Which flag limits a firewall rule so that it applies only to VMs that carry the network tag `web`?","a":"`--target-tags=web`","w":["`--network-tag=web`","`--tags=web`","`--target-vms=web`"],"why":"Targets of a rule are chosen with `--target-tags` (or target service accounts). The other names are not firewall rule flags.","src":"https://cloud.google.com/sdk/gcloud/reference/compute/firewall-rules/create","id":"gcp.i.07.9"}
      ] },
      { id: "gcp.i.08", level: "i", n: 8, title: "Cloud Run", cards: [
        {"t":"learn","topic":"GCP","title":"Cloud Run: containers without servers","body":"**Cloud Run** runs your container without you managing servers. It gives a **service** a web address and scales the number of instances with the requests, even **down to zero** when nobody calls it. Your app must listen on the port given in the `PORT` environment variable.","code":"gcloud run deploy hello \\\n  --source . \\\n  --region=asia-south1","lang":"shell","src":"https://cloud.google.com/run/docs/overview/what-is-cloud-run","id":"gcp.i.08.1"},
        {"t":"choice","topic":"GCP","q":"A Cloud Run service with default settings gets no requests for a while. What can happen?","a":"It scales down to zero instances","w":["It is deleted automatically","It keeps ten instances running forever","It moves to another project"],"why":"Cloud Run can scale to zero when there is no traffic, so you do not keep a server running for nothing. The service itself stays and starts again on the next request.","src":"https://cloud.google.com/run/docs/overview/what-is-cloud-run","id":"gcp.i.08.2"},
        {"t":"choice","topic":"GCP","q":"Ravi's container is deployed, but Cloud Run says it could not start and listen for requests. What is a likely cause?","a":"The app does not listen on the port given in the `PORT` variable","w":["The service name has too many letters","The region name has no zone letter","The project has no folder"],"why":"Cloud Run tells your container which port to use through `PORT`. Read that value in your code instead of using a fixed port that Cloud Run does not expect.","src":"https://cloud.google.com/run/docs/container-contract","id":"gcp.i.08.3"},
        {"t":"choice","topic":"GCP","q":"Lakshmi has a script that cleans data once a night and then exits. It does not serve web requests. Which Cloud Run option fits?","a":"A Cloud Run job","w":["A Cloud Run service","A Cloud Run revision","A VPC subnet"],"why":"Services answer requests. Jobs run a task to completion and stop.","src":"https://cloud.google.com/run/docs/overview/what-is-cloud-run","id":"gcp.i.08.4"},
        {"t":"order","topic":"GCP","q":"Put these steps in order to release a new version slowly and safely.","items":["Deploy a new revision with `--no-traffic`","Test the new revision","Send some traffic with `gcloud run services update-traffic`","Move all traffic to the new revision"],"why":"Each deploy creates a revision. You can keep it away from users, test it, then move traffic in steps and roll back if something looks wrong.","src":"https://cloud.google.com/run/docs/rollouts-rollbacks-traffic-migration","id":"gcp.i.08.5"},
        {"t":"pipe","topic":"GCP","q":"Build what happens when Asha deploys with `--source .`.","steps":[["CODE","Your source code folder","src"],["BLD","Cloud Build builds a container image"],["AR","Image is stored in Artifact Registry"],["RUN","A Cloud Run revision serves it"]],"extra":[["VM","Create a Compute Engine VM"]],"why":"A source deploy builds an image for you, stores it, and starts a new revision from it. If you already have an image, deploy it with `--image` instead; no VM is needed either way.","src":"https://cloud.google.com/run/docs/deploying-source-code","id":"gcp.i.08.6"},
        {"t":"bug","topic":"GCP","q":"Asha wants to delete her practice service, but one command fails with an invalid command error. Which line is wrong?","code":["gcloud run services list","gcloud run services describe hello --region=asia-south1","gcloud run service delete hello --region=asia-south1","gcloud run revisions list --region=asia-south1"],"lang":"shell","bad":2,"why":"The command group is `services` (plural), like in the other lines.","fix":"gcloud run services delete hello --region=asia-south1","src":"https://cloud.google.com/sdk/gcloud/reference/run/services/delete","id":"gcp.i.08.7"},
        {"t":"cmd","topic":"GCP","q":"Ravi is in his app folder and wants Cloud Run to build and deploy it as the service `hello` in Mumbai (`asia-south1`). Build the command: source first, then the region.","parts":["gcloud","run","deploy","hello","--source",".","--region","asia-south1"],"extra":["create","--zone"],"why":"`gcloud run deploy` with `--source .` builds a container from the current folder and deploys it. Cloud Run uses a region, not a zone.","src":"https://cloud.google.com/run/docs/deploying-source-code","id":"gcp.i.08.10"},
        {"t":"check","topic":"GCP","q":"What does the flag `--allow-unauthenticated` do when you deploy a Cloud Run service?","a":"Anyone on the internet can call the service without signing in","w":["It makes the service private to your project","It turns off logging for the service","It lets the service run without a container"],"why":"It makes the service public. Use it only for apps that are meant to be public, never for private data.","src":"https://cloud.google.com/run/docs/authenticating/public","id":"gcp.i.08.8"},
        {"t":"check","topic":"GCP","q":"What is a Cloud Run revision?","a":"A snapshot of your code and settings that is created on each deploy","w":["A copy of your billing account","A firewall rule for the service","A bucket that stores the service logs"],"why":"Every deploy creates a new revision. Traffic can be split between revisions, which also makes rollbacks easy.","src":"https://cloud.google.com/run/docs/resource-model","id":"gcp.i.08.9"}
      ] },
      { id: "gcp.i.09", level: "i", n: 9, title: "Cloud SQL", cards: [
        {"t":"learn","topic":"GCP","title":"Cloud SQL: managed databases","body":"**Cloud SQL** runs **MySQL, PostgreSQL or SQL Server** for you. Google handles patches, backups and failover, and you keep the data and the SQL. You can connect with `gcloud sql connect`, or with the **Cloud SQL Auth Proxy** from an app.","code":"gcloud sql instances list\ngcloud sql connect asha-db --user=postgres","lang":"shell","src":"https://cloud.google.com/sql/docs/introduction","id":"gcp.i.09.1"},
        {"t":"choice","topic":"GCP","q":"What does Cloud SQL manage for Asha, so she does not have to do it herself?","a":"Database software patching, backups and failover","w":["Writing her application code","Choosing her table columns","Paying her billing account"],"why":"A managed service takes over the server work. You still design the tables and write the SQL.","src":"https://cloud.google.com/sql/docs/introduction","id":"gcp.i.09.2"},
        {"t":"choice","topic":"GCP","q":"Which Cloud SQL feature gives a standby copy that takes over automatically if the main instance's zone has a problem?","a":"High availability (a regional instance)","w":["A read replica","A backup","A private IP address"],"why":"A high availability instance keeps a standby in another zone of the same region. A read replica is for spreading reads, not for automatic failover.","src":"https://cloud.google.com/sql/docs/postgres/high-availability","id":"gcp.i.09.3"},
        {"t":"choice","topic":"GCP","q":"Ravi's main database is slow because many people run reports on it. These reports only read data. What helps most?","a":"Add a read replica and send the reports to it","w":["Add a bigger billing account","Turn on public access prevention","Delete the old backups"],"why":"A read replica is a copy that serves read-only queries, so the main instance has less work.","src":"https://cloud.google.com/sql/docs/postgres/replication","id":"gcp.i.09.4"},
        {"t":"choice","topic":"GCP","q":"What do automated backups let Lakshmi do?","a":"Restore the instance data from an earlier point","w":["Make every query run faster","Add a standby in another region by themselves","Remove the need for database passwords"],"why":"Backups are for recovery after mistakes or failures. They do not speed up queries or replace security.","src":"https://cloud.google.com/sql/docs/postgres/backup-recovery/backups","id":"gcp.i.09.5"},
        {"t":"order","topic":"GCP","q":"Put these steps in order to connect an app safely to a Cloud SQL instance and clean up.","items":["Create the Cloud SQL instance","Start the Cloud SQL Auth Proxy with the instance connection name","Point the app to the proxy's local address","Stop or delete the instance when practice is done"],"why":"The proxy opens a secure local door to the instance, so the app connects to the proxy. Do not forget to stop practice instances.","src":"https://cloud.google.com/sql/docs/postgres/sql-proxy","id":"gcp.i.09.6"},
        {"t":"bug","topic":"GCP","q":"Asha wants to stop her practice instance, but one command fails because it does not exist. Which line is wrong?","code":["gcloud sql instances list","gcloud sql instances describe asha-db","gcloud sql instances stop asha-db","gcloud sql backups list --instance=asha-db"],"lang":"shell","bad":2,"why":"There is no `stop` command. An instance is stopped by setting its activation policy to `NEVER` with `patch`.","fix":"gcloud sql instances patch asha-db --activation-policy=NEVER","src":"https://cloud.google.com/sql/docs/postgres/start-stop-restart-instance","id":"gcp.i.09.7"},
        {"t":"check","topic":"GCP","q":"What is the main reason to give a Cloud SQL instance a private IP address?","a":"It can be reached from your VPC network without a public internet address","w":["It makes the database free","It turns the instance into a read replica","It replaces the need for backups"],"why":"A private IP keeps the database inside your private network. Fewer public doors means less risk.","src":"https://cloud.google.com/sql/docs/postgres/private-ip","id":"gcp.i.09.8"},
        {"t":"check","topic":"GCP","q":"What does the Cloud SQL Auth Proxy give an app?","a":"A secure, encrypted connection that uses IAM, without managing IP allow-lists","w":["A faster copy of the database","An automatic standby instance","A free public IP for every query"],"why":"The proxy handles encryption and checks permissions through IAM. You do not need to open the database to a list of IP addresses.","src":"https://cloud.google.com/sql/docs/postgres/sql-proxy","id":"gcp.i.09.9"}
      ] },
      { id: "gcp.i.10", level: "i", n: 10, title: "Pub/Sub", cards: [
        {"t":"learn","topic":"GCP","title":"Pub/Sub: send messages between apps","body":"**Pub/Sub** passes messages between apps. A **publisher** sends a message to a **topic**, and each **subscription** on that topic gets a copy for its **subscribers**. A subscriber must **ack** (acknowledge) a message when it is done.","code":"gcloud pubsub topics create orders\ngcloud pubsub subscriptions create orders-sub --topic=orders\ngcloud pubsub topics publish orders --message=\"order 101\"\ngcloud pubsub subscriptions pull orders-sub --auto-ack","lang":"shell","src":"https://cloud.google.com/pubsub/docs/overview","id":"gcp.i.10.1"},
        {"t":"choice","topic":"GCP","q":"In Pub/Sub, where does a publisher send a message, and how does a subscriber get it?","a":"It sends to a topic, and a subscriber reads from a subscription","w":["It sends to a subscription, and a subscriber reads from a bucket","It sends to a subscriber, and a subscriber reads from a topic","It sends to a project, and a subscriber reads from a zone"],"why":"Publishers know only the topic. Each subscription belongs to a topic and holds the messages for its subscribers.","src":"https://cloud.google.com/pubsub/docs/overview","id":"gcp.i.10.2"},
        {"t":"choice","topic":"GCP","q":"The topic `orders` has two subscriptions: `email-sub` and `report-sub`. A message is published once. Who gets it?","a":"Both subscriptions get a copy","w":["Only the first subscription created","Each subscription gets half of it","Neither, until the topic is deleted"],"why":"Every subscription on a topic receives every message. That is how one event can feed many apps.","src":"https://cloud.google.com/pubsub/docs/subscription-overview","id":"gcp.i.10.3"},
        {"t":"choice","topic":"GCP","q":"Which delivery type has Pub/Sub send each message to an HTTPS address of your service?","a":"Push","w":["Pull","Ack","Topic"],"why":"With push, Pub/Sub calls your endpoint. With pull, your app asks Pub/Sub for messages.","src":"https://cloud.google.com/pubsub/docs/subscription-overview","id":"gcp.i.10.4"},
        {"t":"choice","topic":"GCP","q":"Ravi's app sometimes receives the same message twice. What should he do?","a":"Make the processing safe to repeat","w":["Delete the topic every day","Ignore it, because it can never happen","Publish fewer messages"],"why":"Pub/Sub delivers at least once by default, so duplicates can happen. Design the work so that doing it twice gives the same result.","src":"https://cloud.google.com/pubsub/docs/subscription-overview","id":"gcp.i.10.5"},
        {"t":"pipe","topic":"GCP","q":"Build the journey of one message through Pub/Sub.","steps":[["PUB","Publisher sends the message","src"],["TOP","Topic receives it"],["SUB","Subscription holds a copy"],["ACK","Subscriber processes and acks it"]],"extra":[["FW","Firewall rule"]],"why":"The message goes to the topic, each subscription keeps a copy, and the subscriber acks after processing. A firewall rule is not part of this flow.","src":"https://cloud.google.com/pubsub/docs/overview","id":"gcp.i.10.6"},
        {"t":"bug","topic":"GCP","q":"Asha's last command says the subscription was not found. Which line is wrong?","code":["gcloud pubsub topics create orders","gcloud pubsub subscriptions create orders-sub --topic=orders","gcloud pubsub topics publish orders --message=\"order 101\"","gcloud pubsub subscriptions pull orders --auto-ack"],"lang":"shell","bad":3,"why":"`pull` reads from a subscription, not from a topic. The subscription here is `orders-sub`.","fix":"gcloud pubsub subscriptions pull orders-sub --auto-ack","src":"https://cloud.google.com/sdk/gcloud/reference/pubsub/subscriptions/pull","id":"gcp.i.10.7"},
        {"t":"cmd","topic":"GCP","q":"The topic `orders` exists. Lakshmi wants a subscription `orders-sub` so her app can receive its messages. Build the command.","parts":["gcloud","pubsub","subscriptions","create","orders-sub","--topic=orders"],"extra":["topics","--from=orders"],"why":"Subscriptions are created with `gcloud pubsub subscriptions create`, and `--topic` says which topic they listen to.","src":"https://cloud.google.com/sdk/gcloud/reference/pubsub/subscriptions/create","id":"gcp.i.10.10"},
        {"t":"check","topic":"GCP","q":"What does an ack (acknowledgement) tell Pub/Sub?","a":"The message was processed, so do not send it again","w":["The topic should be deleted","The message should be sent to every project","The subscriber wants the message twice"],"why":"If a message is not acked in time, Pub/Sub sends it again. Ack only after your work is done.","src":"https://cloud.google.com/pubsub/docs/subscription-overview","id":"gcp.i.10.8"},
        {"t":"check","topic":"GCP","q":"An order app publishes events and an email app sends the mails. Why put Pub/Sub between them?","a":"The order app does not wait for the email app, and messages wait if the email app is down","w":["The two apps must be on the same VM","The email app can then read the order database directly","Messages are deleted as soon as they are published"],"why":"Pub/Sub decouples the apps. Each can scale and fail on its own while messages are kept for the subscription.","src":"https://cloud.google.com/pubsub/docs/overview","id":"gcp.i.10.9"}
      ] },
      { id: "gcp.i.11", level: "i", n: 11, title: "Dataflow", cards: [
        {"t":"learn","topic":"GCP","title":"Dataflow: pipelines for big data","body":"**Dataflow** is a managed service that runs **Apache Beam** pipelines, in **batch** or **streaming** mode. A pipeline reads data into a **PCollection** and applies **transforms** to it. Dataflow adds and removes workers for you, and you stop a streaming job when you finish.","code":"gcloud dataflow jobs list --region=asia-south1\ngcloud dataflow jobs drain JOB_ID --region=asia-south1","lang":"shell","src":"https://cloud.google.com/dataflow/docs/overview","id":"gcp.i.11.1"},
        {"t":"choice","topic":"GCP","q":"What is Dataflow?","a":"A managed service that runs Apache Beam pipelines","w":["A database for small web apps","A firewall for VPC networks","A tool that stores container images"],"why":"You write the pipeline with the Beam SDK, and Dataflow runs it on workers it manages for you.","src":"https://cloud.google.com/dataflow/docs/overview","id":"gcp.i.11.2"},
        {"t":"choice","topic":"GCP","q":"In Apache Beam, what is a PCollection?","a":"The set of data that a pipeline reads and transforms","w":["A group of virtual machines","A Pub/Sub subscription","A BigQuery dataset"],"why":"A PCollection is the data inside the pipeline. Each transform takes one or more PCollections and produces new ones.","src":"https://beam.apache.org/documentation/programming-guide/#pcollections","id":"gcp.i.11.3"},
        {"t":"choice","topic":"GCP","q":"Card payments arrive all day and Ravi must check each one within seconds. Which pipeline type fits?","a":"A streaming pipeline","w":["A batch pipeline that runs once a month","A pipeline that runs only after the bank closes","A pipeline that only reads old files"],"why":"Streaming pipelines process data continuously as it arrives. Batch pipelines work on a fixed set of data.","src":"https://cloud.google.com/dataflow/docs/overview","id":"gcp.i.11.4"},
        {"t":"pipe","topic":"GCP","q":"Build a common streaming flow for Lakshmi's shop events.","steps":[["PUBS","Pub/Sub topic receives events","src"],["DF","Dataflow pipeline processes them"],["BQ","BigQuery table stores the results"],["RPT","A report reads the table"]],"extra":[["SQL","Cloud SQL backup"]],"why":"Pub/Sub collects events, Dataflow transforms them and BigQuery stores them for analysis. A Cloud SQL backup is not part of this flow.","src":"https://cloud.google.com/dataflow/docs/overview","id":"gcp.i.11.5"},
        {"t":"order","topic":"GCP","q":"Put these steps in order to stop a streaming job safely after practice.","items":["Find the job ID with `gcloud dataflow jobs list`","Run `gcloud dataflow jobs drain`","Wait until the job shows the Drained state","Check that no job is left running"],"why":"A streaming job never ends by itself, and running workers cost money. Drain it and then check.","src":"https://cloud.google.com/dataflow/docs/guides/stopping-a-pipeline","id":"gcp.i.11.6"},
        {"t":"choice","topic":"GCP","q":"Asha stops a streaming job and does not want to lose data that it already read from Pub/Sub. What should she choose?","a":"Drain the job","w":["Cancel the job","Delete the Pub/Sub topic","Close the browser tab"],"why":"Drain stops reading new data but finishes the data already in the pipeline. Cancel stops at once and can lose data that was still being processed.","src":"https://cloud.google.com/dataflow/docs/guides/stopping-a-pipeline","id":"gcp.i.11.7"},
        {"t":"check","topic":"GCP","q":"What does Dataflow autoscaling do?","a":"It adds or removes workers based on the load","w":["It adds more topics automatically","It rewrites the pipeline code for you","It moves the job to another project"],"why":"More data means more workers, and less data means fewer. You do not set the worker count by hand every time.","src":"https://cloud.google.com/dataflow/docs/horizontal-autoscaling","id":"gcp.i.11.8"},
        {"t":"check","topic":"GCP","q":"What is a Dataflow template good for?","a":"Running a ready-made pipeline by giving it parameters, without writing Beam code","w":["Storing files in a bucket","Blocking traffic with firewall rules","Creating a billing account"],"why":"Google provides templates for common jobs, like Pub/Sub to BigQuery. You choose the template and fill in the settings.","src":"https://cloud.google.com/dataflow/docs/guides/templates/provided-templates","id":"gcp.i.11.9"}
      ] },
      { id: "gcp.i.12", level: "i", n: 12, title: "gcloud CLI", cards: [
        {"t":"learn","topic":"GCP","title":"The gcloud command line","body":"**gcloud** is the command line tool for Google Cloud. `gcloud init` signs you in and sets defaults, and `gcloud config set project` picks the project. `gcloud storage` and `bq` come with it for files and BigQuery, and **Cloud Shell** gives you gcloud in the browser.","code":"gcloud init\ngcloud config set project my-project-id\ngcloud config list","lang":"shell","src":"https://cloud.google.com/sdk/docs/initializing","id":"gcp.i.12.1"},
        {"t":"choice","topic":"GCP","q":"Which command signs in with your Google account?","a":"`gcloud auth login`","w":["`gcloud login`","`gcloud config login`","`gcloud init login`"],"why":"Sign-in commands live in the `auth` group, like `gcloud auth list` that shows the accounts you used.","src":"https://cloud.google.com/sdk/gcloud/reference/auth/login","id":"gcp.i.12.2"},
        {"t":"choice","topic":"GCP","q":"Asha runs `gcloud run deploy` and gets a message that the Cloud Run API is not enabled. Which command fixes it?","a":"`gcloud services enable run.googleapis.com`","w":["`gcloud components install run`","`gcloud auth enable run`","`gcloud config set run enabled`"],"why":"APIs are switched on per project with `gcloud services enable` and the API name. Components are tools for the CLI itself.","src":"https://cloud.google.com/sdk/gcloud/reference/services/enable","id":"gcp.i.12.3"},
        {"t":"choice","topic":"GCP","q":"Which command prints only the VM names, one per line?","a":"`gcloud compute instances list --format=\"value(name)\"`","w":["`gcloud compute instances list --filter=\"value(name)\"`","`gcloud compute instances list --output=name`","`gcloud compute instances list --only=name`"],"why":"`--format` decides how the output looks. `value(name)` prints just that field.","src":"https://cloud.google.com/sdk/gcloud/reference/topic/formats","id":"gcp.i.12.4"},
        {"t":"bug","topic":"GCP","q":"Ravi wants to switch to a named configuration called `dev`, but one command fails with an invalid command error. Which line is wrong?","code":["gcloud config configurations create dev","gcloud config configurations list","gcloud config configuration activate dev","gcloud config list"],"lang":"shell","bad":2,"why":"The group is `configurations` (plural) in every line that works.","fix":"gcloud config configurations activate dev","src":"https://cloud.google.com/sdk/gcloud/reference/config/configurations/activate","id":"gcp.i.12.5"},
        {"t":"order","topic":"GCP","q":"Put these steps in order to set up gcloud on a new laptop.","items":["Install the Google Cloud CLI","Run `gcloud init` and sign in","Choose the project with `gcloud config set project`","Check the setup with `gcloud config list`"],"why":"Install first, sign in, pick your project, and then check that the settings are what you expect.","src":"https://cloud.google.com/sdk/docs/initializing","id":"gcp.i.12.6"},
        {"t":"choice","topic":"GCP","q":"Lakshmi has no laptop of her own and cannot install software. What gives her gcloud in the browser?","a":"Cloud Shell","w":["Cloud NAT","A Cloud Run job","Cloud Router"],"why":"Cloud Shell is a terminal in the Google Cloud console that already has gcloud ready to use.","src":"https://cloud.google.com/shell/docs","id":"gcp.i.12.7"},
        {"t":"cmd","topic":"GCP","q":"Asha wants every gcloud command to use her project `asha-shop-2025` by default. Build the command.","parts":["gcloud","config","set","project","asha-shop-2025"],"extra":["use","--default"],"why":"`gcloud config set project` saves the default project in the active configuration, so you don't need `--project` each time.","src":"https://cloud.google.com/sdk/gcloud/reference/config/set","id":"gcp.i.12.10"},
        {"t":"check","topic":"GCP","q":"Ravi works on a college project and an office project. Which gcloud feature keeps separate project, account and region settings for each?","a":"Named configurations","w":["gcloud components","gcloud services","The `bq` tool"],"why":"Create one configuration per setup with `gcloud config configurations create` and switch with `activate`.","src":"https://cloud.google.com/sdk/gcloud/reference/config/configurations","id":"gcp.i.12.8"},
        {"t":"check","topic":"GCP","q":"Which command shows only the VMs that are running?","a":"`gcloud compute instances list --filter=\"status=RUNNING\"`","w":["`gcloud compute instances list --format=\"status=RUNNING\"`","`gcloud compute instances list --only=\"RUNNING\"`","`gcloud compute instances list --where=\"status=RUNNING\"`"],"why":"`--filter` chooses which rows to show, and `--format` chooses how to show them. Mixing them up is a common mistake.","src":"https://cloud.google.com/sdk/gcloud/reference/topic/filters","id":"gcp.i.12.9"}
      ] },
      { id: "gcp.a.13", level: "a", n: 13, title: "GKE (Kubernetes on Google Cloud)", cards: [
        {"t":"learn","topic":"GCP","title":"GKE: Kubernetes on Google Cloud","body":"**Google Kubernetes Engine (GKE)** runs Kubernetes for you. In **Autopilot** mode Google manages the nodes too; in **Standard** mode you manage the node pools. Create a cluster, then run `gcloud container clusters get-credentials` so `kubectl` can talk to it.","code":"gcloud container clusters create-auto shop-cluster \\\n  --region=asia-south1\ngcloud container clusters get-credentials shop-cluster \\\n  --region=asia-south1\nkubectl get nodes","lang":"shell","src":"https://cloud.google.com/kubernetes-engine/docs/concepts/choose-cluster-mode","id":"gcp.a.13.1"},
        {"t":"choice","topic":"GCP","q":"Asha does not want to manage nodes or node pools. Which GKE mode fits her?","a":"Autopilot","w":["Standard","Compute Engine mode","Cloud Run mode"],"why":"In Autopilot, Google manages the cluster infrastructure, including nodes. In Standard you configure and manage node pools yourself.","src":"https://cloud.google.com/kubernetes-engine/docs/concepts/autopilot-overview","id":"gcp.a.13.2"},
        {"t":"order","topic":"GCP","q":"Ravi makes a new GKE cluster and runs his app. Put the steps in order.","items":["`gcloud container clusters create-auto`","`gcloud container clusters get-credentials`","`kubectl apply -f deployment.yaml`","`kubectl get pods`"],"why":"The cluster must exist first. `get-credentials` then writes the cluster details into your kubeconfig so `kubectl` can use it.","src":"https://cloud.google.com/kubernetes-engine/docs/how-to/cluster-access-for-kubectl","id":"gcp.a.13.3"},
        {"t":"indent","topic":"GCP","step":2,"q":"Fix the indentation of this Deployment so Kubernetes can read it.","lang":"k8s","lines":[[0,"apiVersion: apps/v1"],[0,"kind: Deployment"],[0,"metadata:"],[2,"name: web"],[0,"spec:"],[2,"replicas: 2"],[2,"selector:"],[4,"matchLabels:"],[6,"app: web"],[2,"template:"],[4,"metadata:"],[6,"labels:"],[8,"app: web"],[4,"spec:"],[6,"containers:"],[8,"- name: web"],[10,"image: nginx"]],"why":"Labels go under `metadata`, and the `containers` list goes under the pod `spec` inside `template`. The `selector` labels must match the template labels.","src":"https://kubernetes.io/docs/concepts/workloads/controllers/deployment/","id":"gcp.a.13.4"},
        {"t":"bug","topic":"GCP","q":"Lakshmi wants to point `kubectl` at her new GKE cluster, but one command is wrong. Which line fails?","code":["gcloud container clusters list","gcloud container clusters get-credentials shop-cluster --region=asia-south1","kubectl config current-context","gcloud container clusters connect shop-cluster"],"lang":"shell","bad":3,"why":"There is no `connect` command in `gcloud container clusters`. Use `describe` to see a cluster, and `get-credentials` for `kubectl` access.","fix":"gcloud container clusters describe shop-cluster --region=asia-south1","src":"https://cloud.google.com/sdk/gcloud/reference/container/clusters/get-credentials","id":"gcp.a.13.5"},
        {"t":"choice","topic":"GCP","q":"Where should a team store the container image that GKE pulls for its Deployment?","a":"Artifact Registry","w":["Cloud Storage lifecycle rules","A Kubernetes Secret","The kubeconfig file"],"why":"Artifact Registry stores and manages container images. A Secret holds small sensitive values, not images.","src":"https://cloud.google.com/artifact-registry/docs/overview","id":"gcp.a.13.6"},
        {"t":"choice","topic":"GCP","q":"A pod in GKE must read a Cloud Storage bucket. What is the recommended way to give it access without a key file?","a":"Workload Identity Federation for GKE","w":["Copy a service account key JSON into the image","Give the pod the project Owner role","Make the bucket public"],"why":"Workload Identity Federation for GKE lets workloads use Google Cloud APIs with IAM and no downloaded keys. Key files can leak, and public buckets expose everyone.","src":"https://cloud.google.com/kubernetes-engine/docs/concepts/workload-identity","id":"gcp.a.13.7"},
        {"t":"cmd","topic":"GCP","q":"Ravi's GKE cluster `shop-cluster` is in `asia-south1`. Build the command that lets `kubectl` talk to it.","parts":["gcloud","container","clusters","get-credentials","shop-cluster","--region=asia-south1"],"extra":["update-kubeconfig","eks"],"why":"`gcloud container clusters get-credentials` adds the cluster to your kubeconfig. On AWS the same job is done by `aws eks update-kubeconfig`.","src":"https://cloud.google.com/sdk/gcloud/reference/container/clusters/get-credentials","id":"gcp.a.13.10"},
        {"t":"check","topic":"GCP","q":"Ravi finished practising on GKE. What should he do so he does not pay for an idle cluster?","a":"Delete the cluster","w":["Close the terminal window","Run `kubectl get nodes` again","Rename the cluster"],"why":"A cluster keeps its resources until it is deleted. Use `gcloud container clusters delete` when practice is over.","src":"https://cloud.google.com/sdk/gcloud/reference/container/clusters/delete","id":"gcp.a.13.8"},
        {"t":"check","topic":"GCP","q":"What does `gcloud container clusters get-credentials` do?","a":"Writes cluster access details into your kubeconfig for `kubectl`","w":["Creates a new cluster","Pushes an image to Artifact Registry","Deletes old pods"],"why":"It only configures `kubectl` access to an existing cluster. It does not create or change anything in the cluster.","src":"https://cloud.google.com/sdk/gcloud/reference/container/clusters/get-credentials","id":"gcp.a.13.9"}
      ] },
      { id: "gcp.a.14", level: "a", n: 14, title: "Terraform on Google Cloud", cards: [
        {"t":"learn","topic":"GCP","title":"Terraform on Google Cloud","body":"**Terraform** lets you describe cloud resources in files written in **HCL** and create them with commands. The `google` provider talks to Google Cloud. A `resource` block describes one thing, like a bucket.","code":"resource \"google_storage_bucket\" \"logs\" {\n  name     = \"asha-demo-logs-123\"\n  location = \"ASIA-SOUTH1\"\n}","lang":"text","src":"https://registry.terraform.io/providers/hashicorp/google/latest/docs/resources/storage_bucket","id":"gcp.a.14.1"},
        {"t":"choice","topic":"GCP","q":"What does `terraform plan` do?","a":"Shows what Terraform would create, change or destroy, without doing it","w":["Creates all resources immediately","Deletes the state file","Downloads the provider plugins"],"why":"`plan` is a preview. `apply` makes the changes and `init` downloads providers, so always read the plan before applying.","src":"https://developer.hashicorp.com/terraform/cli/commands/plan","id":"gcp.a.14.2"},
        {"t":"order","topic":"GCP","q":"Put the normal Terraform workflow in order.","items":["Write the `.tf` files","`terraform init`","`terraform plan`","`terraform apply`"],"why":"`init` prepares the folder and downloads providers. You plan to preview, then apply to make the changes.","src":"https://developer.hashicorp.com/terraform/intro","id":"gcp.a.14.3"},
        {"t":"pipe","topic":"GCP","q":"Build the setup that keeps Terraform state safely in a team bucket.","steps":[["BKT","Create a Cloud Storage bucket for state","src"],["BKND","Add a backend \"gcs\" block with that bucket"],["INIT","terraform init connects to the bucket"],["APPL","terraform apply saves state in the bucket"]],"extra":[["GIT","git commit terraform.tfstate"]],"why":"The bucket must exist first, then the backend block points to it, and `init` sets it up. State can hold sensitive values, so it goes to the bucket, never into Git.","src":"https://developer.hashicorp.com/terraform/language/backend/gcs","id":"gcp.a.14.4"},
        {"t":"bug","topic":"GCP","q":"Ravi finishes his Terraform practice and wants to remove everything it created. Which command is wrong?","code":["terraform init","terraform plan","terraform apply","terraform delete"],"lang":"shell","bad":3,"why":"Terraform has no `delete` command. `terraform destroy` removes the resources it manages.","fix":"terraform destroy","src":"https://developer.hashicorp.com/terraform/cli/commands/destroy","id":"gcp.a.14.5"},
        {"t":"choice","topic":"GCP","q":"Why does a team keep Terraform state in a Cloud Storage bucket instead of on one laptop?","a":"Everyone shares the same state, and it is not lost with a laptop","w":["Terraform cannot run without a bucket","Buckets make `terraform plan` unnecessary","The state file then becomes safe to commit to Git"],"why":"A remote backend gives one shared copy of state with locking. Never commit state to Git, as it may contain secrets.","src":"https://developer.hashicorp.com/terraform/language/backend/gcs","id":"gcp.a.14.6"},
        {"t":"choice","topic":"GCP","q":"A variable named `project_id` is declared with a `variable` block. How do you use it in a resource?","a":"`var.project_id`","w":["`$project_id`","`input.project_id`","`variable.project_id`"],"why":"Input variables are referenced as `var.<name>`. This lets you reuse the same code for dev and prod.","src":"https://developer.hashicorp.com/terraform/language/values/variables","id":"gcp.a.14.7"},
        {"t":"check","topic":"GCP","q":"Lakshmi changed a `.tf` file. What should she run before `terraform apply`?","a":"`terraform plan`","w":["`terraform destroy`","`gcloud projects delete`","`terraform state rm`"],"why":"Plan shows exactly what will change, so surprises are caught before they reach your project.","src":"https://developer.hashicorp.com/terraform/cli/commands/plan","id":"gcp.a.14.8"},
        {"t":"check","topic":"GCP","q":"Which file should never be committed to a Git repository?","a":"The Terraform state file","w":["A `main.tf` file","A `variables.tf` file","The `.terraform.lock.hcl` file"],"why":"State can contain sensitive data, so keep it in a remote backend. HashiCorp recommends committing the lock file.","src":"https://developer.hashicorp.com/terraform/language/state/remote","id":"gcp.a.14.9"}
      ] },
      { id: "gcp.a.15", level: "a", n: 15, title: "Security and organization policies", cards: [
        {"t":"learn","topic":"GCP","title":"Security and organization policies","body":"IAM roles are **inherited** down the resource hierarchy: a role on a folder applies to all projects inside it. An **organization policy** is different: it sets limits on what can be done, like blocking VMs with public IPs. Keep access small and avoid long-lived keys.","code":"constraints/compute.vmExternalIpAccess\nconstraints/iam.allowedPolicyMemberDomains","lang":"text","src":"https://cloud.google.com/resource-manager/docs/organization-policy/overview","id":"gcp.a.15.1"},
        {"t":"choice","topic":"GCP","q":"Asha grants a role to a folder. Later she adds a new project to that folder. What happens?","a":"The new project inherits the role","w":["Nothing; she must grant the role again","The role is removed from the folder","Only the organization gets the role"],"why":"IAM policies are inherited from parents to children. A wide grant high in the tree gives wide access, so grant low.","src":"https://cloud.google.com/iam/docs/resource-hierarchy-access-control","id":"gcp.a.15.2"},
        {"t":"choice","topic":"GCP","q":"Ravi wants one place that lists security risks across the organization, like public buckets or open firewall rules. Which service fits?","a":"Security Command Center","w":["Secret Manager","Cloud NAT","Artifact Registry"],"why":"Security Command Center finds misconfigurations and threats and shows them as findings. Secret Manager stores secrets; it does not scan for risks.","src":"https://cloud.google.com/security-command-center/docs/security-command-center-overview","id":"gcp.a.15.3"},
        {"t":"choice","topic":"GCP","q":"An app on Compute Engine needs to call Google APIs. What is safer than downloading a service account key?","a":"Attach a service account to the VM","w":["Email the key to the team","Save the key JSON in the Git repo","Use the basic Owner role with a key"],"why":"An attached service account gives short-lived credentials automatically. Downloaded keys are long-lived and can leak.","src":"https://cloud.google.com/iam/docs/best-practices-for-managing-service-account-keys","id":"gcp.a.15.4"},
        {"t":"bug","topic":"GCP","q":"Ravi stores a database password in Secret Manager and wants to read it back. One command is wrong. Which line?","code":["gcloud secrets create db-password --replication-policy=automatic","printf 'example-value' | gcloud secrets versions add db-password --data-file=-","gcloud secrets list","gcloud secrets read db-password --version=latest"],"lang":"shell","bad":3,"why":"A value is read with `gcloud secrets versions access`. There is no `gcloud secrets read` command.","fix":"gcloud secrets versions access latest --secret=db-password","src":"https://cloud.google.com/secret-manager/docs/access-secret-version","id":"gcp.a.15.5"},
        {"t":"choice","topic":"GCP","q":"Which log records who did what, like creating or deleting a resource, and is written by default?","a":"Admin Activity audit logs in Cloud Audit Logs","w":["VM serial port output","Cloud Storage object versions","Billing budget alerts"],"why":"Admin Activity audit logs record changes to configuration and metadata. They are always written and cannot be turned off.","src":"https://cloud.google.com/logging/docs/audit","id":"gcp.a.15.6"},
        {"t":"choice","topic":"GCP","q":"What does VPC Service Controls help prevent?","a":"Data leaving a defined perimeter of Google Cloud services","w":["VMs running out of memory","Bills growing too high","Slow `terraform plan` runs"],"why":"It creates a service perimeter around projects and services to reduce the risk of data exfiltration. Security Command Center is the separate tool that shows findings and risks.","src":"https://cloud.google.com/vpc-service-controls/docs/overview","id":"gcp.a.15.7"},
        {"t":"check","topic":"GCP","q":"Lakshmi must stop people from creating VMs with public IPs across the whole company. What fits best?","a":"An organization policy on the organization","w":["A role on each project","A budget alert","A bucket lifecycle rule"],"why":"Organization policies set at the top are inherited by folders and projects, so one rule covers all.","src":"https://cloud.google.com/resource-manager/docs/organization-policy/overview","id":"gcp.a.15.8"},
        {"t":"check","topic":"GCP","q":"Where should an app keep an API password instead of putting it in code?","a":"Secret Manager","w":["A public Git repo","A comment in the Dockerfile","A bucket name"],"why":"Secret Manager stores secrets with access control and versions, so code never holds the value.","src":"https://cloud.google.com/secret-manager/docs/overview","id":"gcp.a.15.9"}
      ] }
    ] },
    { id: "git", name: "Git", topic: "Git", blurb: "Save, share and review code like a professional team.", units: [
      { id: "git.b.01", level: "b", n: 1, title: "What Git is", cards: [
        {"t":"learn","topic":"Git","title":"What Git is","body":"**Git** is a tool that saves **snapshots** of your project, so you can see its **history** and go back to an older version. A folder that Git tracks is called a **repository** (repo). **GitHub** is a different thing: a website that stores copies of Git repositories online.","code":"git --version","lang":"shell","id":"git.b.01.1"},
        {"t":"choice","topic":"Git","q":"What is a Git repository?","a":"A project folder together with its saved history","w":["A website where code is stored","A single saved file","A program that runs your code"],"why":"A repository is your project plus all the snapshots Git has saved. A website that stores repositories is GitHub, not the repository itself.","id":"git.b.01.2"},
        {"t":"choice","topic":"Git","q":"Which one is a website that stores Git repositories online?","a":"GitHub","w":["Git","A commit","A repository"],"why":"Git is the tool on your computer. GitHub is the website. Many beginners think they are the same thing, but they are not.","id":"git.b.01.3"},
        {"t":"choice","topic":"Git","q":"Asha broke her project by mistake. Why does Git help her?","a":"It keeps older snapshots, so she can go back","w":["It stops her from making mistakes","It runs the code for her","It makes the project load faster"],"why":"Git does not stop mistakes. It remembers every saved version, so you can go back to a good one.","id":"git.b.01.4"},
        {"t":"pipe","topic":"Git","q":"Build the path a change takes from your files to GitHub.","steps":[["WORK","Working folder","src"],["STG","Staging area"],["REPO","Local repository"],["HUB","GitHub"]],"extra":[["ZIP","Zip file"]],"why":"You edit in the working folder, add changes to the staging area, commit them to the local repository, then push to GitHub. A zip file is not part of Git.","id":"git.b.01.5"},
        {"t":"choice","topic":"Git","q":"Ravi has no internet. Can he still save snapshots with Git?","a":"Yes, Git saves snapshots on his own computer","w":["No, Git always needs GitHub","Yes, but only after he opens a browser","No, Git needs a Wi-Fi password first"],"why":"Git works on your computer. You only need internet when you share with GitHub.","id":"git.b.01.6"},
        {"t":"check","topic":"Git","q":"What is the main job of version control like Git?","a":"Keep the history of your project so you can go back","w":["Make your code run faster","Host your website for free","Find mistakes in your code automatically"],"why":"Version control remembers each saved version of your work. It does not run or fix the code.","id":"git.b.01.7"},
        {"t":"check","topic":"Git","q":"Which statement is correct?","a":"Git is a tool on your computer; GitHub is an online service","w":["GitHub is a tool on your computer; Git is a website","Git and GitHub are two names for the same thing","Git only works when you are on GitHub"],"why":"Git is the version control tool. GitHub is a website that stores repositories made with Git.","id":"git.b.01.8"}
      ] },
      { id: "git.b.02", level: "b", n: 2, title: "init, clone, add, commit", cards: [
        {"t":"learn","topic":"Git","title":"Saving work with Git","body":"`git clone` copies a repository to your computer. After editing, `git add` picks the changes and `git commit -m \"message\"` saves a snapshot with a note about what changed.","code":"git clone https://github.com/user/project.git\ngit add main.py\ngit commit -m \"Fix bug\"","lang":"shell","id":"git.b.02.1"},
        {"t":"choice","topic":"Git","q":"Which command downloads a full copy of a GitHub repository to your computer?","a":"git clone","w":["git push","git commit","git init"],"why":"`git clone <url>` copies the repository and its history. `git init` starts a new empty one; `push` uploads.","id":"git.b.02.2"},
        {"t":"choice","topic":"Git","q":"Which command creates a new, empty repository in the current folder?","a":"git init","w":["git start","git new","git create"],"why":"`git init` makes a hidden `.git` folder, which turns the current folder into a repository.","id":"git.b.02.3"},
        {"t":"output","topic":"Git","code":"git init -q\ngit add a.txt\ngit commit -q -m \"first\"\ngit log --oneline | wc -l","lang":"shell","a":"1","w":["0","2","first"],"why":"One commit was made, so the log has one line. `wc -l` counts the lines.","id":"git.b.02.4"},
        {"t":"bug","topic":"Git","q":"Ravi wants to save notes.txt with the message \"first version\". Which line is wrong?","code":["git init","git add notes.txt","git commit \"first version\""],"bad":2,"why":"The message must come after `-m`. Without it, Git thinks \"first version\" is a file name.","id":"git.b.02.5"},
        {"t":"output","topic":"Git","code":"git init -q\ngit add a.txt\ngit status --short","lang":"shell","a":"A  a.txt","w":["?? a.txt","M  a.txt","nothing"],"why":"`A` means the new file is added to the staging area. `??` would mean Git does not track the file yet.","id":"git.b.02.6"},
        {"t":"check","topic":"Git","q":"What does `git add notes.txt` do?","a":"Puts the changes of notes.txt in the staging area","w":["Saves the snapshot for good","Uploads notes.txt to GitHub","Creates an empty file named notes.txt"],"why":"`git add` only stages changes. The snapshot is saved when you run `git commit`.","id":"git.b.02.7"},
        {"t":"check","topic":"Git","q":"Which command saves the staged changes as a snapshot with a message?","a":"git commit -m \"message\"","w":["git save -m \"message\"","git add -m \"message\"","git push -m \"message\""],"why":"`git commit` saves the snapshot. `-m` gives the message in quotes.","id":"git.b.02.8"}
      ] },
      { id: "git.b.03", level: "b", n: 3, title: "status, log, diff", cards: [
        {"t":"learn","topic":"Git","title":"Looking at your work","body":"`git status` tells you which files are new, changed or staged. `git log --oneline` lists past commits, one line each. `git diff` shows the lines you changed that are **not staged** yet; `git diff --name-only` shows just the file names.","code":"git status\ngit log --oneline\ngit diff","lang":"shell","id":"git.b.03.1"},
        {"t":"choice","topic":"Git","q":"Which command shows which files are changed or staged?","a":"git status","w":["git log","git check","git show-files"],"why":"`git status` is the command to run when you are not sure what state your folder is in.","id":"git.b.03.2"},
        {"t":"output","topic":"Git","code":"git commit -q --allow-empty -m one\ngit commit -q --allow-empty -m two\ngit log --oneline | wc -l","lang":"shell","a":"2","w":["1","3","one two"],"why":"There are two commits, so `git log --oneline` prints two lines.","id":"git.b.03.3"},
        {"t":"output","topic":"Git","code":"echo b >> f.txt\ngit diff --name-only","lang":"shell","a":"f.txt","w":["b","+b","nothing"],"why":"f.txt was committed earlier, then changed. `--name-only` prints only the names of changed files.","id":"git.b.03.4"},
        {"t":"order","topic":"Git","q":"Put these steps in the right order to review and save a change.","items":["Edit notes.txt","git diff","git add notes.txt","git commit -m \"Update notes\""],"why":"`git diff` shows unstaged changes, so run it before `git add`. After you add, the changes are staged and wait for the commit.","id":"git.b.03.5"},
        {"t":"choice","topic":"Git","q":"Asha runs `git add notes.txt` and then `git diff`. It shows nothing. Why?","a":"`git diff` shows only changes that are not staged","w":["The file was deleted","Git found a mistake","She must run git diff twice"],"why":"After `git add`, the change is staged, so plain `git diff` has nothing to show.","id":"git.b.03.6"},
        {"t":"check","topic":"Git","q":"Which command lists your past commits, one per line?","a":"git log --oneline","w":["git status --short","git diff --oneline","git history"],"why":"`git log` shows the history. `--oneline` makes each commit one short line.","id":"git.b.03.7"},
        {"t":"check","topic":"Git","q":"You edited a file but have not run `git add`. Which command shows exactly what changed?","a":"git diff","w":["git log","git commit","git clone"],"why":"`git diff` shows changed lines. `git log` only shows commits already saved.","id":"git.b.03.8"}
      ] },
      { id: "git.b.04", level: "b", n: 4, title: "push and pull", cards: [
        {"t":"learn","topic":"Git","title":"Sharing with GitHub","body":"`git push` uploads your commits to GitHub. `git pull` downloads other people's commits and merges them into your branch. Pull before you start work to stay up to date.","id":"git.b.04.1"},
        {"t":"order","topic":"Git","q":"Put the steps in order to save a code change and share it on GitHub.","items":["Edit the file","git add main.py","git commit -m \"Fix bug\"","git push"],"why":"`git add` picks the changes, `git commit` saves them with a message on your computer, and `git push` sends them to GitHub.","id":"git.b.04.2"},
        {"t":"choice","topic":"Git","q":"What does git pull do?","a":"Downloads new commits from GitHub and merges them into your branch","w":["Uploads your commits to GitHub","Deletes your branch","Creates a new repository"],"why":"`git pull` brings your copy up to date with others’ work. `git push` is the opposite: it uploads your commits.","id":"git.b.04.3"},
        {"t":"choice","topic":"Git","q":"What does `git push` do?","a":"Sends your local commits to the GitHub repository","w":["Downloads new commits from GitHub","Creates a new commit","Stages all your files"],"why":"`push` uploads your commits. `pull` is the one that downloads.","id":"git.b.04.4"},
        {"t":"bug","topic":"Git","q":"Asha wants to upload her new commit to GitHub. Which line is wrong?","code":["git add notes.txt","git commit -m \"Add notes\"","git pull origin main"],"bad":2,"why":"`git pull` downloads. To upload, use `git push origin main`.","id":"git.b.04.5"},
        {"t":"choice","topic":"Git","q":"Ravi and a friend both work on one repository. Why should Ravi run `git pull` before `git push`?","a":"To get his friend's latest commits first","w":["To delete his old commits","To log in to GitHub","To make his commit message shorter"],"why":"If GitHub has newer commits, Git may refuse your push. Pulling first brings your copy up to date.","id":"git.b.04.6"},
        {"t":"check","topic":"Git","q":"Asha ran `git commit`. Where is the new commit right now?","a":"Only in the repository on her computer","w":["On GitHub, for everyone to see","In her friend's computer","Nowhere yet, it is waiting for `git add`"],"why":"A commit is saved locally. It reaches GitHub only after `git push`.","id":"git.b.04.7"},
        {"t":"check","topic":"Git","q":"A friend added a file on GitHub. Which command brings it to your computer?","a":"git pull","w":["git push","git init","git add"],"why":"`git pull` downloads new commits from GitHub and joins them with your work.","id":"git.b.04.8"}
      ] },
      { id: "git.i.05", level: "i", n: 5, title: "Branches", cards: [
        {"t":"learn","topic":"Git","title":"Branches","body":"A **branch** is your own line of work. Create one with `git switch -c new-feature`, commit there, and `main` stays safe until you merge.","id":"git.i.05.1"},
        {"t":"choice","topic":"Git","q":"You want to build a new feature without touching the main code yet. What should you create?","a":"A new branch","w":["A new repository","A tag","A merge conflict"],"why":"A **branch** is your own line of work. When the feature is ready and reviewed, you merge it into `main`.","id":"git.i.05.2"},
        {"t":"choice","topic":"Git","q":"Which command lists all the branches in your repository?","a":"git branch","w":["git branches","git show branches","git ls-branch"],"why":"`git branch` with no name lists branches and marks the current one with `*`. The other commands do not exist.","id":"git.i.05.3"},
        {"t":"choice","topic":"Git","q":"In Git, what is `HEAD`?","a":"A pointer to the branch (or commit) you are on right now","w":["The very first commit in the repository","The newest branch you created","The copy of your code on GitHub"],"why":"`HEAD` shows where you are. When you switch branches, `HEAD` moves to that branch.","id":"git.i.05.4"},
        {"t":"output","topic":"Git","code":"# main has f.txt\ngit switch -c login\necho b > g.txt\ngit add g.txt\ngit commit -m \"Add g\"\ngit switch main\nls","lang":"shell","a":"f.txt","w":["g.txt","f.txt\ng.txt","Nothing is printed"],"why":"g.txt was committed only on the `login` branch. Back on `main`, the folder has only f.txt.","id":"git.i.05.5"},
        {"t":"bug","topic":"Git","q":"Ravi finished his work on branch `login` and wants to go back to the existing branch `main`. Which line is wrong?","code":["git switch -c login","git add login.html","git commit -m \"Add login page\"","git switch -c main"],"bad":3,"why":"`-c` creates a new branch, and `main` already exists. To go to an existing branch, use `git switch main`.","id":"git.i.05.6"},
        {"t":"order","topic":"Git","q":"Order the steps to fix a typo on a new branch.","items":["git switch -c fix-typo","Edit notes.txt","git add notes.txt","git commit -m \"Fix typo\""],"why":"First make the branch, so your commit goes on it. Then edit, stage with `git add`, and commit.","id":"git.i.05.7"},
        {"t":"check","topic":"Git","q":"You are on `main`. You run `git switch -c cart`. What happens?","a":"Git creates a new branch `cart` and moves you to it","w":["Git deletes the branch `main`","Git copies `cart` from GitHub","Git only shows the branch list"],"why":"`git switch -c name` means create a branch and switch to it in one step.","id":"git.i.05.8"},
        {"t":"check","topic":"Git","q":"What does this print?","code":"git switch -c a\ngit switch -c b\ngit branch --show-current","lang":"shell","a":"b","w":["a","main","a\nb"],"why":"The last `git switch -c b` moves you to branch `b`, so that is the current branch.","id":"git.i.05.9"}
      ] },
      { id: "git.i.06", level: "i", n: 6, title: "Merge", cards: [
        {"t":"learn","topic":"Git","title":"Merging branches","body":"**Merge** brings the work of one branch into another. First go to the branch that should receive the work, then run `git merge name`. If the receiving branch has no new commits, Git just moves forward: this is a **fast-forward**. If both branches have new commits, Git makes a **merge commit**.","code":"git switch main\ngit merge login","lang":"shell","id":"git.i.06.1"},
        {"t":"choice","topic":"Git","q":"You are on `main` and want the work from branch `login` inside `main`. Which command do you run?","a":"git merge login","w":["git merge main","git push login","git switch login"],"why":"`git merge name` brings the named branch into the branch you are on. So you stand on `main` and merge `login`.","id":"git.i.06.2"},
        {"t":"bug","topic":"Git","q":"Asha is on branch `payments`, finished her work and committed. She wants the changes in `main`. Which line is wrong?","code":["git switch main","git merge main","git branch -d payments"],"bad":1,"why":"Merging `main` into `main` does nothing. She must merge the branch with the work: `git merge payments`.","id":"git.i.06.3"},
        {"t":"output","topic":"Git","code":"# main has 1 commit; feature has 2 more\ngit switch main\ngit merge feature\ngit log --oneline | wc -l","lang":"shell","a":"3","w":["1","2","4"],"why":"`main` had no new commits, so Git fast-forwards. `main` now has all 3 commits, and `wc -l` counts 3 lines.","id":"git.i.06.4"},
        {"t":"output","topic":"Git","code":"# commit a is on main\n# b is on feature, c is on main\ngit switch main\ngit merge feature -m \"Merge feature\"\ngit log --oneline | wc -l","lang":"shell","a":"4","w":["2","3","5"],"why":"Both branches had new commits, so Git adds a merge commit. The log now has a, b, c and the merge commit: 4 lines.","id":"git.i.06.5"},
        {"t":"choice","topic":"Git","q":"When does Git do a fast-forward merge?","a":"When the branch you are on has no new commits of its own","w":["When both branches changed the same file","When you use the word `fast` in the command","When the branch was created on GitHub"],"why":"If your branch did not move since the other branch started, Git only moves the pointer forward. No merge commit is needed.","id":"git.i.06.6"},
        {"t":"check","topic":"Git","q":"`main` has a new commit, and `login` also has its own new commits. You merge `login` into `main` with no problems. What does Git create?","a":"A merge commit","w":["A fast-forward only","A new repository","A copy of `main` named `login`"],"why":"Both branches moved, so Git cannot just slide forward. It joins them with a merge commit.","id":"git.i.06.7"},
        {"t":"check","topic":"Git","q":"You are on branch `login`. You run `git merge main`. What happens?","a":"`main` is merged into `login`, and `main` itself does not change","w":["`login` is merged into `main`","Both branches are deleted","Git pushes the code to GitHub"],"why":"The branch you are standing on receives the work. `main` stays as it was.","id":"git.i.06.8"}
      ] },
      { id: "git.i.07", level: "i", n: 7, title: "Merge conflicts", cards: [
        {"t":"learn","topic":"Git","title":"Merge conflicts","body":"A **conflict** happens when two branches changed the same lines. Git stops and marks the file with `<<<<<<<`, `=======` and `>>>>>>>`. You fix the file by hand, then `git add` it and `git commit`. To cancel, run `git merge --abort`.","code":"<<<<<<< HEAD\nblue\n=======\ngreen\n>>>>>>> feature","lang":"shell","id":"git.i.07.1"},
        {"t":"choice","topic":"Git","q":"In a conflict, what is between `<<<<<<< HEAD` and `=======`?","a":"The lines from the branch you are on","w":["The lines from the other branch","The lines Git deleted for good","The lines from GitHub"],"why":"The top part is your current branch (`HEAD`). The part after `=======` is from the branch you are merging in.","id":"git.i.07.2"},
        {"t":"output","topic":"Git","code":"# notes.txt: main says blue, feature says green\ngit merge feature\ncat notes.txt","lang":"shell","a":"<<<<<<< HEAD\nblue\n=======\ngreen\n>>>>>>> feature","w":["blue","green","blue\ngreen"],"why":"Both branches changed the same line, so Git cannot choose. It writes both versions with the markers.","id":"git.i.07.3"},
        {"t":"choice","topic":"Git","q":"You started a merge, saw a conflict, and want to go back to how things were before the merge. Which command?","a":"git merge --abort","w":["git merge --undo","git delete merge","git branch -d feature"],"why":"`git merge --abort` stops the merge and brings back your files as they were before it.","id":"git.i.07.4"},
        {"t":"order","topic":"Git","q":"Order the steps to solve a merge conflict.","items":["git merge feature (Git reports a conflict)","Open the file and choose the lines to keep","Delete the marker lines <<<<<<<, ======= and >>>>>>>","git add notes.txt","git commit"],"why":"Fix the file first and remove the markers. Then `git add` tells Git it is solved, and `git commit` finishes the merge.","id":"git.i.07.5"},
        {"t":"pipe","topic":"Git","q":"Build the steps to finish a merge that has a conflict.","steps":[["MRG","git merge"],["EDIT","Fix the file"],["ADD","git add"],["COMM","git commit"]],"extra":[["CLON","git clone"]],"why":"After the merge stops, you fix the file, add it, and commit. `git clone` copies a whole repository, which is not needed here.","id":"git.i.07.6"},
        {"t":"check","topic":"Git","q":"You fixed the conflicted file by hand and removed the markers. What must you do before `git commit`?","a":"Run `git add` on the file","w":["Run `git clone` again","Delete the whole branch","Run `git merge --abort`"],"why":"`git add` tells Git that the conflict in that file is solved.","id":"git.i.07.7"},
        {"t":"check","topic":"Git","q":"A conflict happened. You run `git merge --abort`. What does `cat notes.txt` print now?","code":"# main says blue, feature says green\ngit merge feature\ngit merge --abort\ncat notes.txt","lang":"shell","a":"blue","w":["green","<<<<<<< HEAD\nblue\n=======\ngreen\n>>>>>>> feature","blue\ngreen"],"why":"`--abort` cancels the merge. The file goes back to the version on `main`: blue.","id":"git.i.07.8"}
      ] },
      { id: "git.i.08", level: "i", n: 8, title: "Pull requests", cards: [
        {"t":"learn","topic":"Git","title":"Pull requests","body":"A **pull request** asks to merge your branch into `main`. Teammates review the changes, automated tests run, and then it is merged.","id":"git.i.08.1"},
        {"t":"order","topic":"Git","q":"Order the steps to suggest a fix to a project on GitHub.","items":["git clone the repository","git checkout -b fix-typo","Edit, then git commit","git push and open a pull request"],"why":"Clone it, make a **branch** for your change, commit, push, then open a **pull request** so others can review before it is merged.","id":"git.i.08.2"},
        {"t":"choice","topic":"Git","q":"What is a pull request on GitHub?","a":"A request to merge your branch into another branch, so others can review it","w":["A command that downloads the latest code","A copy of the repository for backup","A way to delete a branch"],"why":"You open a pull request to ask the team to review your changes and merge them. It does not download anything.","id":"git.i.08.3"},
        {"t":"choice","topic":"Git","q":"A reviewer reads your pull request and wants a change. What can the reviewer do?","a":"Leave a comment or request changes","w":["Edit your commits in your computer","Delete your branch at once","Merge it without reading"],"why":"Reviewers comment on lines and ask for changes. When happy, they approve.","id":"git.i.08.4"},
        {"t":"choice","topic":"Git","q":"A team turns on branch protection for `main`. What does it do?","a":"It can stop direct pushes to `main` and require a reviewed pull request","w":["It hides `main` from everyone","It deletes old commits","It merges every branch automatically"],"why":"Branch protection is a rule that keeps `main` safe. Changes must come in through a pull request, often with an approval.","id":"git.i.08.5"},
        {"t":"choice","topic":"Git","q":"Your pull request is open. You push one more commit to the same branch. What happens?","a":"The new commit shows up in the same pull request","w":["GitHub closes the pull request","You must open a second pull request","The new commit is lost"],"why":"A pull request follows its branch. New commits on that branch are added to it.","id":"git.i.08.6"},
        {"t":"check","topic":"Git","q":"Why do teams use pull requests?","a":"So others can review changes before they go into `main`","w":["To make the code run faster","To avoid using branches","To copy the code to a new computer"],"why":"Review catches mistakes early and lets the team talk about the change.","id":"git.i.08.7"},
        {"t":"check","topic":"Git","q":"`main` is protected. Lakshmi runs `git push origin main` directly. What may happen?","a":"GitHub can reject the push, and she must use a pull request","w":["Her code goes live with no checks","GitHub deletes her branch","Her computer is blocked from GitHub"],"why":"With branch protection, direct pushes to `main` can be refused. The change must go through a pull request.","id":"git.i.08.8"}
      ] },
      { id: "git.a.09", level: "a", n: 9, title: "Rebase", cards: [
        {"t":"learn","topic":"Git","title":"Rebase: replay your commits","body":"`git rebase main` takes your branch's commits and **replays** them on top of the latest `main`. The history becomes one straight line, with no merge commit. Rebase **rewrites** commits (they get new hashes), so use it only on commits that are still yours.","code":"git switch feature\ngit rebase main","lang":"shell","id":"git.a.09.1"},
        {"t":"output","topic":"Git","q":"Both branches start from commit `a`. Then `main` gets `m1` and `feature` gets `f1`. Asha runs this on `feature`. What does the last command print (newest first)?","code":"git switch feature\ngit rebase main\ngit log --format=%s","lang":"shell","a":"f1\nm1\na","w":["m1\nf1\na","f1\na","Merge branch 'main'\nf1\nm1"],"why":"Rebase puts `f1` on top of `m1`, so the history is one straight line with no merge commit.","id":"git.a.09.2"},
        {"t":"choice","topic":"Git","q":"Which commits should you NOT rebase?","a":"Commits you already pushed and others have pulled","w":["Commits that exist only on your own computer","Commits on a branch only you use","Commits with more than one changed file"],"why":"Rebase gives commits new hashes. If teammates already have the old ones, their history and yours no longer match.","id":"git.a.09.3"},
        {"t":"order","topic":"Git","q":"A rebase stops because of a conflict. Put Asha's steps in order.","items":["Run `git rebase main` (it stops with a conflict)","Edit the file and remove the conflict markers","Run `git add` on the fixed file","Run `git rebase --continue`"],"why":"Fix the file, stage it, then continue. Do not run `git commit` yourself in the middle of a rebase.","id":"git.a.09.4"},
        {"t":"choice","topic":"Git","q":"Asha is in the middle of a rebase with conflicts and wants everything back as it was before she started. Which command?","a":"`git rebase --abort`","w":["`git rebase --continue`","`git rebase --skip`","`git merge --abort`"],"why":"`--abort` cancels the rebase and returns the branch to its original state. `--continue` moves forward and `--skip` drops the current commit.","id":"git.a.09.5"},
        {"t":"bug","topic":"Git","q":"Asha wants `feature` to sit on top of `main`, but Git says the branch is already up to date. Which line is wrong?","code":["git switch feature","git rebase feature"],"lang":"shell","bad":1,"why":"`git rebase <branch>` replays your commits onto that branch. Rebasing onto itself changes nothing.","fix":"git rebase main","id":"git.a.09.6"},
        {"t":"choice","topic":"Git","q":"What does `git pull --rebase` do?","a":"Fetches, then replays your local commits on top of the fetched ones","w":["Fetches and always makes a merge commit","Deletes your local commits and copies the remote","Pushes your commits after rebasing them onto main"],"why":"A normal `git pull` merges. With `--rebase` your local commits are replayed on top, so history stays a straight line.","id":"git.a.09.7"},
        {"t":"check","topic":"Git","q":"After `git rebase main`, what happens to the commits of `feature`?","a":"They are copied as new commits with new hashes","w":["They keep the same hashes and move unchanged","They are merged into one commit automatically","They are deleted from the repository forever"],"why":"Rebase rewrites history: each commit is replayed as a new commit. The old ones are no longer on the branch.","id":"git.a.09.8"},
        {"t":"check","topic":"Git","q":"Ravi wants to squash his last 3 messy commits into one before opening a pull request. Which command starts that?","a":"`git rebase -i HEAD~3`","w":["`git rebase HEAD~3`","`git merge --squash HEAD~3`","`git pull --rebase HEAD~3`"],"why":"Interactive rebase (`-i`) lists the last 3 commits so you can mark them `squash`. Only do this on commits not pushed yet.","id":"git.a.09.9"}
      ] },
      { id: "git.a.10", level: "a", n: 10, title: "Stash", cards: [
        {"t":"learn","topic":"Git","title":"Stash: park unfinished work","body":"`git stash` saves your uncommitted changes and gives you a clean folder again. Bring them back later with `git stash pop` (restore and remove) or `git stash apply` (restore and keep). New files that Git does not track need `-u`.","code":"git stash push -m \"login page\"\ngit stash list\ngit stash pop","lang":"shell","id":"git.a.10.1"},
        {"t":"output","topic":"Git","q":"Asha edited `f.txt` on branch `main`, then ran `git stash push -m wip`. What does this print?","code":"git stash list","lang":"shell","a":"stash@{0}: On main: wip","w":["stash@{0}: wip","stash@{1}: On main: wip","(nothing, the stash list is empty)"],"why":"Each entry shows its name, the branch it came from and your message. The newest stash is always `stash@{0}`.","id":"git.a.10.2"},
        {"t":"output","topic":"Git","q":"One stash exists. Asha runs `git stash apply`. What does the second command print?","code":"git stash apply\ngit stash list | wc -l","lang":"shell","a":"1","w":["0","2","stash@{0}"],"why":"`apply` restores the changes but keeps the stash. `pop` would remove it, and the count would be 0.","id":"git.a.10.3"},
        {"t":"order","topic":"Git","q":"Ravi must fix an urgent bug but has unfinished work on `feature`. Put the steps in order.","items":["`git stash push -m \"login page\"`","`git switch hotfix`","Fix the bug and commit it","`git switch feature`","`git stash pop`"],"why":"Stash first so the folder is clean, do the urgent work, come back, then pop your stash.","id":"git.a.10.4"},
        {"t":"bug","topic":"Git","q":"`notes.txt` is a brand new file. After the stash it is still in Lakshmi's folder and comes along to `hotfix`. Which line misses it?","code":["printf 'draft\\n' > notes.txt","git stash","git switch hotfix"],"lang":"shell","bad":1,"why":"By default `git stash` saves only tracked files. Add `-u` (`--include-untracked`) to stash new files too.","fix":"git stash push -u","id":"git.a.10.5"},
        {"t":"choice","topic":"Git","q":"Why do many people run `git stash` before `git switch other-branch`?","a":"Git may refuse to switch if your uncommitted changes would be overwritten","w":["Switching branches always deletes uncommitted changes","Stash is required for every branch switch","Stash copies the other branch to your folder"],"why":"If the other branch has different content in a file you changed, Git stops. Stashing keeps your work safe and the folder clean.","id":"git.a.10.6"},
        {"t":"choice","topic":"Git","q":"`git stash pop` hits a merge conflict. What happens to the stash entry?","a":"It is kept in the list, so you do not lose it","w":["It is deleted at once","It is moved to a new branch","It is committed on main"],"why":"Pop removes the entry only when it applies cleanly. After a conflict, fix the files and then run `git stash drop` yourself.","id":"git.a.10.7"},
        {"t":"check","topic":"Git","q":"Ravi has 3 stashes. Which one is the newest?","a":"`stash@{0}`","w":["`stash@{2}`","`stash@{3}`","The one with the longest message"],"why":"New stashes go on top, so the newest is `stash@{0}` and older ones get higher numbers.","id":"git.a.10.8"},
        {"t":"check","topic":"Git","q":"Which command deletes a stash without restoring it?","a":"`git stash drop`","w":["`git stash pop`","`git stash apply`","`git stash list`"],"why":"`drop` throws the entry away. `pop` restores and then drops. `apply` only restores.","id":"git.a.10.9"}
      ] },
      { id: "git.a.11", level: "a", n: 11, title: "Revert vs reset", cards: [
        {"t":"learn","topic":"Git","title":"Revert vs reset: undo safely","body":"`git revert <commit>` adds a **new commit** that cancels an old one, so shared history stays safe. `git reset` moves the branch back: `--soft` keeps changes staged, the default `--mixed` keeps them unstaged, `--hard` **throws them away**.","code":"git revert HEAD\ngit reset --soft HEAD~1\ngit reflog","lang":"shell","id":"git.a.11.1"},
        {"t":"output","topic":"Git","q":"The history has `first` then `second`. Asha runs this. What does the last command print (newest first)?","code":"git revert --no-edit HEAD\ngit log --format=%s","lang":"shell","a":"Revert \"second\"\nsecond\nfirst","w":["first","second\nfirst","Revert \"first\"\nsecond\nfirst"],"why":"Revert never erases history. It adds a new commit whose message starts with `Revert` and that cancels `second`.","id":"git.a.11.2"},
        {"t":"choice","topic":"Git","q":"The last commit `second` changed `f.txt`. Ravi runs `git reset --soft HEAD~1`. What state is `f.txt` in now?","a":"Changed and staged, ready to commit again","w":["Changed but not staged","Untracked, as if it were a new file","Back to the old content, the change is lost"],"why":"`--soft` only moves the branch back and keeps the change **staged**. The default `--mixed` would keep it unstaged, and `--hard` would throw it away.","id":"git.a.11.3"},
        {"t":"choice","topic":"Git","q":"A bad commit was already pushed and teammates pulled it. How should Asha undo it?","a":"`git revert <commit>` and push the new commit","w":["`git reset --hard HEAD~1` and force push","Delete the repository and clone again","`git stash` the commit"],"why":"Revert adds history instead of rewriting it, so nobody's copy breaks. Reset plus force push changes history others already have.","id":"git.a.11.4"},
        {"t":"choice","topic":"Git","q":"What does `git reset --hard` do to uncommitted changes in your files?","a":"It discards them","w":["It stashes them safely","It keeps them staged","It commits them for you"],"why":"`--hard` makes tracked files match the target commit, so uncommitted edits are lost. New untracked files are left alone. Check `git status` first.","id":"git.a.11.5"},
        {"t":"order","topic":"Git","q":"Lakshmi ran `git reset --hard` and lost a commit. Put the recovery steps in order.","items":["Run `git reflog` to see where HEAD has been","Find the line from before the reset and copy its hash","Run `git reset --hard <hash>`","Run `git log` to check the commit is back"],"why":"The reflog keeps a private record of where HEAD pointed, so a lost commit can often be found for some time.","id":"git.a.11.6"},
        {"t":"bug","topic":"Git","q":"Lakshmi wants to undo her edits in `notes.txt` only. One line would also wipe her edits in every other tracked file. Which line?","code":["git status","git reset --hard","git status"],"lang":"shell","bad":1,"why":"`git restore <file>` discards changes in that one file. `reset --hard` affects every file.","fix":"git restore notes.txt","id":"git.a.11.7"},
        {"t":"check","topic":"Git","q":"Which is the safest way to undo a commit that is already on the shared `main`?","a":"`git revert <commit>`","w":["`git reset --hard <commit>~1`","`git reset --soft <commit>~1`","`git restore <commit>`"],"why":"Revert adds a new commit, so history is not rewritten. Reset would force everyone to repair their copy.","id":"git.a.11.8"},
        {"t":"check","topic":"Git","q":"Ravi staged `app.py` by mistake. Which command unstages it but keeps his edits?","a":"`git restore --staged app.py`","w":["`git restore app.py`","`git reset --hard app.py`","`git revert app.py`"],"why":"`--staged` removes the file from the staging area only. Plain `git restore app.py` would discard the edits.","id":"git.a.11.9"}
      ] },
      { id: "git.a.12", level: "a", n: 12, title: "Tags and releases", cards: [
        {"t":"learn","topic":"Git","title":"Tags: name a release","body":"A **tag** is a fixed name for one commit, like `v1.0`. A lightweight tag is just a name; an **annotated** tag (`-a`) also stores a message, author and date. Tags are **not pushed** by default: send one with `git push origin v1.0`.","code":"git tag -a v1.0 -m \"First release\"\ngit tag\ngit push origin v1.0","lang":"shell","id":"git.a.12.1"},
        {"t":"output","topic":"Git","q":"Ravi creates two tags in this order. What does the last command print?","code":"git tag v1.0\ngit tag v0.9\ngit tag","lang":"shell","a":"v0.9\nv1.0","w":["v1.0\nv0.9","v1.0","v0.9"],"why":"`git tag` lists names in alphabetical order, not in the order you created them.","id":"git.a.12.2"},
        {"t":"output","topic":"Git","q":"`v1.0` is annotated; `v0.9` is lightweight. What does this print?","code":"git cat-file -t v1.0\ngit cat-file -t v0.9","lang":"shell","a":"tag\ncommit","w":["commit\ntag","tag\ntag","commit\ncommit"],"why":"An annotated tag is its own object (`tag`). A lightweight tag points straight to the commit.","id":"git.a.12.3"},
        {"t":"choice","topic":"Git","q":"Asha ran `git push origin main`. Will her tag `v1.0` appear on GitHub?","a":"No, tags must be pushed on their own","w":["Yes, tags always go with the branch","Yes, but only annotated tags","No, tags can never be pushed"],"why":"Push one tag with `git push origin v1.0`, or all tags with `git push --tags`.","id":"git.a.12.4"},
        {"t":"choice","topic":"Git","q":"The version is `1.4.2`. Ravi fixes a small bug and changes no features. What is the next version in semantic versioning?","a":"`1.4.3`","w":["`2.0.0`","`1.5.0`","`1.4.1`"],"why":"MAJOR.MINOR.PATCH: bug fixes raise PATCH, new compatible features raise MINOR, breaking changes raise MAJOR.","id":"git.a.12.5"},
        {"t":"order","topic":"Git","q":"Put these release steps in order.","items":["Test and merge the work into `main`","`git tag -a v1.1.0 -m \"Release 1.1.0\"`","`git push origin v1.1.0`","Create the release on GitHub from that tag"],"why":"Tag the exact commit you tested, then push the tag so others (and GitHub) can see it.","id":"git.a.12.6"},
        {"t":"bug","topic":"Git","q":"Ravi's release tag must store a message, author and date, but he got a lightweight tag. Which line is wrong?","code":["git switch main","git tag v1.0","git push origin v1.0"],"lang":"shell","bad":1,"why":"Without `-a` (or `-m`), `git tag` makes a lightweight tag: only a name for the commit. Use `-a` with a message for releases.","fix":"git tag -a v1.0 -m \"First release\"","id":"git.a.12.7"},
        {"t":"choice","topic":"Git","q":"Ravi runs `git checkout v1.0` and Git says \"detached HEAD\". What should he do before making new commits?","a":"Create a branch, for example `git switch -c fix-1.0`","w":["Nothing, the commits are saved on main","Delete the tag first","Run `git push --tags`"],"why":"In detached HEAD, new commits belong to no branch and can be lost. A new branch keeps them.","id":"git.a.12.8"},
        {"t":"check","topic":"Git","q":"Which command creates an annotated tag with a message?","a":"`git tag -a v1.0 -m \"First release\"`","w":["`git tag v1.0 \"First release\"`","`git tag -m v1.0`","`git push -a v1.0`"],"why":"`-a` makes it annotated and `-m` gives the message, just like `git commit -m`.","id":"git.a.12.9"},
        {"t":"check","topic":"Git","q":"The version is `2.3.1`. Asha adds a new feature that does not break old code. What is the next version?","a":"`2.4.0`","w":["`2.3.2`","`3.0.0`","`2.3.1.1`"],"why":"A new compatible feature raises MINOR and resets PATCH to 0.","id":"git.a.12.10"}
      ] },
      { id: "git.a.13", level: "a", n: 13, title: "Team workflows", cards: [
        {"t":"learn","topic":"Git","title":"Team workflow with branches","body":"In **GitHub flow** nobody works directly on `main`. You make a branch, push it, open a **pull request** (PR), a teammate reviews it, then you merge. Keep PRs small so they are easy to review.","code":"git switch -c add-login\ngit push -u origin add-login\n# open a Pull Request on GitHub","lang":"shell","id":"git.a.13.1"},
        {"t":"order","topic":"Git","q":"Put the GitHub flow steps in order.","items":["Create a branch","Make commits and push the branch","Open a pull request","A teammate reviews it","Merge into `main`"],"why":"Review happens before merge. That is the whole point of a pull request.","id":"git.a.13.2"},
        {"t":"choice","topic":"Git","q":"What does a protected `main` branch usually stop?","a":"Pushing straight to `main` without a pull request and review","w":["Creating any new branch","Reading the code","Deleting your own local copy"],"why":"Branch protection rules (set on GitHub) can require reviews and passing checks before a merge.","id":"git.a.13.3"},
        {"t":"choice","topic":"Git","q":"What is the idea of trunk-based development?","a":"Short-lived branches that merge into main often, sometimes daily","w":["One long branch per developer for months","No branches and no code review","A separate main branch for each team"],"why":"Small, frequent merges mean fewer big conflicts. Long-lived branches drift away from `main`.","id":"git.a.13.4"},
        {"t":"choice","topic":"Git","q":"Which command shows who last changed each line of `app.py`?","a":"`git blame app.py`","w":["`git log app.py --blame`","`git who app.py`","`git diff --author app.py`"],"why":"`git blame` prints the commit and author for every line. Use it to find the commit that explains a change, not to blame people.","id":"git.a.13.5"},
        {"t":"output","topic":"Git","q":"`.gitignore` contains `.env`. The folder also has `.env` and `app.py`, and nothing is committed. What does `git status --short` print?","code":"git status --short","lang":"shell","a":"?? .gitignore\n?? app.py","w":["?? .env\n?? app.py","?? .gitignore\n?? .env\n?? app.py","?? app.py"],"why":"Ignored files such as `.env` are hidden from status. The `.gitignore` file itself is new and untracked, so it is listed.","id":"git.a.13.6"},
        {"t":"bug","topic":"Git","q":"Ravi's pull request never appears on GitHub because he pushed the wrong branch. Which line is wrong?","code":["git switch -c fix-typo","git commit -am \"Fix typo\"","git push origin main"],"lang":"shell","bad":2,"why":"The commit is on `fix-typo`, and local `main` has nothing new, so nothing useful is pushed. Push the branch, then open the PR from it.","fix":"git push -u origin fix-typo","id":"git.a.13.7"},
        {"t":"check","topic":"Git","q":"`.env` was committed last week. Asha now adds `.env` to `.gitignore`. Is the file still tracked?","a":"Yes, Git keeps tracking it until you run `git rm --cached .env`","w":["No, `.gitignore` stops tracking it at once","No, Git deletes it from the history","Yes, and `.gitignore` can never ignore it"],"why":"`.gitignore` only affects untracked files. If a secret was committed, also change the secret, because it is still in history.","id":"git.a.13.8"},
        {"t":"check","topic":"Git","q":"Which command shows branches and merges as a small drawing, one line per commit?","a":"`git log --oneline --graph`","w":["`git branch -a`","`git status --short`","`git log --stat`"],"why":"`--graph` draws the branch and merge lines, and `--oneline` keeps each commit to one short line. `git branch -a` only lists branch names, and `git log --stat` lists changed files.","id":"git.a.13.9"}
      ] }
    ] },
    { id: "lx", name: "Linux", topic: "Linux", blurb: "The command line every server and cloud runs on.", units: [
      { id: "lx.b.01", level: "b", n: 1, title: "Files and folders", cards: [
        {"t":"learn","topic":"Linux","title":"Moving around","body":"`pwd` shows where you are, `ls` lists files, `cd folder` moves into a folder, `mkdir` makes a new one and `touch` creates an empty file.","code":"pwd\nls\nmkdir project\ncd project\ntouch notes.txt","lang":"shell","id":"lx.b.01.1"},
        {"t":"choice","topic":"Linux","q":"Which command lists the files in the current folder?","a":"ls","w":["cd","pwd","mkdir"],"why":"`ls` lists files. `cd` changes folder, `pwd` prints where you are, `mkdir` makes a new folder.","id":"lx.b.01.2"},
        {"t":"order","topic":"Linux","q":"Order the commands: make a folder, go into it, create a file, then list what is there.","items":["mkdir project","cd project","touch notes.txt","ls"],"why":"`mkdir` makes the folder, `cd` moves into it, `touch` creates an empty file, `ls` shows `notes.txt`.","id":"lx.b.01.3"},
        {"t":"output","topic":"Linux","code":"mkdir notes\ntouch notes/a.txt\nls notes","lang":"shell","a":"a.txt","w":["notes","notes/a.txt","nothing"],"why":"`ls notes` lists what is inside the folder `notes`, which is the file `a.txt`.","id":"lx.b.01.4"},
        {"t":"choice","topic":"Linux","q":"What does `cd ..` do?","a":"Moves you one folder up","w":["Creates a folder named ..","Shows the current folder","Goes back to your home folder"],"why":"`..` means the parent folder. `cd ~` or plain `cd` goes to your home folder.","id":"lx.b.01.5"},
        {"t":"bug","topic":"Linux","q":"Ravi wants to make a folder `photos`, go inside it and create a file. Which line is wrong?","code":["cd photos","mkdir photos","touch a.txt"],"bad":0,"why":"The folder does not exist yet. Run `mkdir photos` first, then `cd photos`.","id":"lx.b.01.6"},
        {"t":"check","topic":"Linux","q":"Which command prints the full path of the folder you are in?","a":"pwd","w":["ls","cd","where"],"why":"`pwd` means print working directory. `ls` lists files.","id":"lx.b.01.7"},
        {"t":"check","topic":"Linux","q":"Which command creates an empty file named notes.txt?","a":"touch notes.txt","w":["mkdir notes.txt","cd notes.txt","ls notes.txt"],"why":"`touch` creates an empty file. `mkdir` makes a folder instead.","id":"lx.b.01.8"}
      ] },
      { id: "lx.b.02", level: "b", n: 2, title: "Reading files", cards: [
        {"t":"learn","topic":"Linux","title":"Reading files","body":"`cat` prints a whole file. `head` shows the first 10 lines and `tail` shows the last 10; `-n 3` changes that to 3. `wc -l` counts lines, `less` lets you scroll (press `q` to quit), and `grep word file` prints only the lines that contain the word. The pipe `|` sends the output of one command to the next.","code":"cat notes.txt\nhead -n 3 notes.txt\ngrep ERROR app.log | wc -l","lang":"shell","id":"lx.b.02.1"},
        {"t":"choice","topic":"Linux","q":"Which command shows the first lines of a file (10 by default)?","a":"head","w":["tail","cat -first","less -f"],"why":"`head` shows the start of a file. `tail` shows the end.","id":"lx.b.02.2"},
        {"t":"output","topic":"Linux","code":"printf 'a\\nb\\nc\\n' > f.txt\nwc -l f.txt","lang":"shell","a":"3 f.txt","w":["3","f.txt 3","a b c"],"why":"`wc -l` counts lines and prints the file name after the count.","id":"lx.b.02.3"},
        {"t":"output","topic":"Linux","code":"printf 'one\\ntwo\\nthree\\n' > f.txt\ntail -n 1 f.txt","lang":"shell","a":"three","w":["one","two","one two three"],"why":"`tail -n 1` prints only the last line.","id":"lx.b.02.4"},
        {"t":"output","topic":"Linux","code":"printf 'apple\\nbanana\\napricot\\n' > f.txt\ngrep ap f.txt | wc -l","lang":"shell","a":"2","w":["1","3","apple apricot"],"why":"`grep ap` keeps `apple` and `apricot`. `wc -l` then counts those 2 lines.","id":"lx.b.02.5"},
        {"t":"pipe","topic":"Linux","q":"Count the lines that contain ERROR in server.log.","steps":[["CAT","cat server.log","src"],["GREP","grep ERROR"],["WC","wc -l"]],"extra":[["RM","rm server.log"]],"why":"`cat` reads the file, `grep` keeps only the ERROR lines and `wc -l` counts them. `rm` would delete the file.","id":"lx.b.02.6"},
        {"t":"check","topic":"Linux","q":"Which command lets you scroll through a long file page by page?","a":"less","w":["cat","tail -n 1","wc -l"],"why":"`less` opens the file in a viewer. `cat` prints everything at once.","id":"lx.b.02.7"},
        {"t":"check","topic":"Linux","q":"What does this print?","code":"printf 'red\\ngreen\\nblue\\n' > c.txt\nhead -n 2 c.txt | tail -n 1","lang":"shell","a":"green","w":["red","blue","red green"],"why":"`head -n 2` gives red and green. `tail -n 1` keeps the last of those, green.","id":"lx.b.02.8"}
      ] },
      { id: "lx.b.03", level: "b", n: 3, title: "Permissions", cards: [
        {"t":"learn","topic":"Linux","title":"Permissions","body":"Every file has **r** (read), **w** (write) and **x** (execute) for three groups: the **owner**, the **group** and **others**. `ls -l` shows this as a string like `-rwxr-xr--`. `chmod` changes it: `u+x` adds execute for the owner, and numbers add up (r=4, w=2, x=1), so `755` is rwxr-xr-x.","code":"ls -l run.sh\nchmod u+x run.sh\nchmod 644 notes.txt","lang":"shell","id":"lx.b.03.1"},
        {"t":"output","topic":"Linux","code":"touch f\nchmod 640 f\nls -l f | cut -c1-10","lang":"shell","a":"-rw-r-----","w":["-rwxr-----","-rw-rw----","-rw-r--r--"],"why":"6 is rw- for the owner, 4 is r-- for the group and 0 is --- for others.","id":"lx.b.03.2"},
        {"t":"choice","topic":"Linux","q":"What do r, w and x mean?","a":"read, write, execute","w":["run, write, exit","read, work, extract","root, write, execute"],"why":"r lets you see the content, w lets you change it and x lets you run it as a program.","id":"lx.b.03.3"},
        {"t":"choice","topic":"Linux","q":"What does `chmod 755 run.sh` give?","a":"Owner can read, write, run; group and others can read and run","w":["Everyone can read, write and run","Only the owner can do anything","Owner can only read; others can run"],"why":"7 = rwx for the owner. 5 = r-x (read and execute) for the group and for others.","id":"lx.b.03.4"},
        {"t":"output","topic":"Linux","code":"touch f\nchmod 600 f\nchmod u+x f\nls -l f | cut -c1-10","lang":"shell","a":"-rwx------","w":["-rw-------","-rwxr-xr-x","---x------"],"why":"600 gives rw- to the owner only. Then `u+x` adds x for the owner.","id":"lx.b.03.5"},
        {"t":"bug","topic":"Linux","q":"Ravi wants to run his script run.sh. Which line stops it from working?","code":["echo 'echo hello' > run.sh","chmod 644 run.sh","./run.sh"],"bad":1,"why":"644 has no x, so the file cannot be run. Use `chmod u+x run.sh` or `chmod 755 run.sh`.","id":"lx.b.03.6"},
        {"t":"check","topic":"Linux","q":"What does this print?","code":"touch f\nchmod 755 f\nls -l f | cut -c1-10","lang":"shell","a":"-rwxr-xr-x","w":["-rwxrwxrwx","-rw-r--r--","-rwx------"],"why":"7 = rwx, 5 = r-x, 5 = r-x. The first dash means it is a normal file.","id":"lx.b.03.7"},
        {"t":"check","topic":"Linux","q":"Which number lets the owner read and write, and everyone else only read?","a":"644","w":["755","600","444"],"why":"6 = rw- for the owner, then 4 = r-- for the group and 4 = r-- for others.","id":"lx.b.03.8"}
      ] },
      { id: "lx.b.04", level: "b", n: 4, title: "Users and sudo", cards: [
        {"t":"learn","topic":"Linux","title":"Users and sudo","body":"`whoami` prints your user name. **root** is the all-powerful administrator user. Do not work as root every day; use `sudo` before one command to run just that command as root. On Ubuntu, users in the **sudo** group can use it, and `groups` lists your groups.","code":"whoami\nsudo apt update\ngroups\nsudo adduser lakshmi","lang":"shell","id":"lx.b.04.1"},
        {"t":"choice","topic":"Linux","q":"What does `whoami` print?","a":"The name of the user you are logged in as","w":["The name of your computer","The full path of your folder","The list of all users"],"why":"`whoami` answers \"which user am I?\". It does not show the computer name.","id":"lx.b.04.2"},
        {"t":"choice","topic":"Linux","q":"What does `sudo` do?","a":"Runs one command with administrator (root) rights","w":["Switches off the computer","Shows who is logged in","Deletes all your permissions"],"why":"`sudo` lets an allowed user run a single command as root, after typing their own password.","id":"lx.b.04.3"},
        {"t":"choice","topic":"Linux","q":"Why is it better not to log in as root for daily work?","a":"One small mistake can damage the whole system","w":["Root cannot open any files","Root is slower than other users","Root cannot use the internet"],"why":"Root can change or delete anything. With normal user rights, mistakes are limited.","id":"lx.b.04.4"},
        {"t":"choice","topic":"Linux","q":"Which command is the friendly way on Ubuntu to add a new user, asking for details?","a":"adduser","w":["whoami","chmod","groups"],"why":"`adduser` asks for a password and details, and creates the home folder. `useradd` is a lower-level command.","id":"lx.b.04.5"},
        {"t":"choice","topic":"Linux","q":"What does the `groups` command show?","a":"The groups your user belongs to","w":["All files you own","The users logged in now","The permissions of a file"],"why":"Groups decide shared rights. On Ubuntu, being in the `sudo` group allows you to use `sudo`.","id":"lx.b.04.6"},
        {"t":"check","topic":"Linux","q":"Ravi runs `apt install git` and gets \"Permission denied\". What should he try?","a":"sudo apt install git","w":["chmod apt install git","whoami apt install git","cd apt install git"],"why":"Installing software needs administrator rights. `sudo` runs the command as root.","id":"lx.b.04.7"},
        {"t":"check","topic":"Linux","q":"Which statement about root is correct?","a":"Root can do anything on the system, including deleting system files","w":["Root is just a normal user with a short name","Root can only read files","Root is the group every user belongs to"],"why":"Root is the administrator user with full power, so use it carefully, only through `sudo`.","id":"lx.b.04.8"}
      ] },
      { id: "lx.i.05", level: "i", n: 5, title: "Processes", cards: [
        {"t":"learn","topic":"Linux","title":"What is running?","body":"Every running program is a **process**. `top` (or `htop`) shows them live with CPU and memory use, `ps aux` lists them once, and `kill <pid>` stops one.","id":"lx.i.05.1"},
        {"t":"choice","topic":"Linux","q":"Which command shows running processes and CPU use, updating live?","a":"top","w":["ls","cat","chmod"],"why":"`top` (or the friendlier `htop`) shows what is using CPU and memory right now. Press `q` to quit.","id":"lx.i.05.2"},
        {"t":"choice","topic":"Linux","q":"A program is stuck. Which command asks it politely to stop, using its PID?","a":"kill 1234","w":["stop 1234","ps 1234","top 1234"],"why":"`kill PID` sends a normal stop signal. It is the first thing to try.","id":"lx.i.05.3"},
        {"t":"choice","topic":"Linux","q":"A program ignores a normal `kill`. Which command forces it to stop and cannot be ignored?","a":"kill -9 1234","w":["kill -15 1234","kill 1234","ps -9 1234"],"why":"`kill -9` sends SIGKILL, which the program cannot ignore. Use it as a last resort, because the program cannot clean up.","id":"lx.i.05.4"},
        {"t":"output","topic":"Linux","code":"sleep 1 &\necho main\nwait\necho done","lang":"shell","a":"main\ndone","w":["done\nmain","main","done"],"why":"`&` starts `sleep` in the background, so `echo main` runs at once. `wait` pauses until the background job ends, then `done` prints.","id":"lx.i.05.5"},
        {"t":"pipe","topic":"Linux","q":"Count the lines of the process list that mention nginx.","steps":[["PS","ps aux"],["GREP","grep nginx"],["WC","wc -l"]],"extra":[["CAT","cat"]],"why":"`ps aux` lists all processes, `grep nginx` keeps the lines with nginx, and `wc -l` counts them. `cat` is not needed.","id":"lx.i.05.6"},
        {"t":"check","topic":"Linux","q":"What is a PID?","a":"A unique number given to each running process","w":["The name of a program file","The password of a user","The size of a process in memory"],"why":"PID means process ID. You use it with commands like `kill`.","id":"lx.i.05.7"},
        {"t":"check","topic":"Linux","q":"You ran a command and the terminal is stuck. Which keys stop the command?","a":"Ctrl + C","w":["Ctrl + P","Ctrl + S","Ctrl + A"],"why":"Ctrl + C sends a stop signal to the program running in the foreground.","id":"lx.i.05.8"}
      ] },
      { id: "lx.i.06", level: "i", n: 6, title: "Services (systemctl)", cards: [
        {"t":"learn","topic":"Linux","title":"Services with systemctl","body":"Background programs like nginx are **services**. `sudo systemctl restart nginx` restarts one, `systemctl status nginx` checks it, and `enable` starts it at boot. You reach a server with `ssh user@server`.","id":"lx.i.06.1"},
        {"t":"order","topic":"Linux","q":"Order the steps to log in to a server and restart nginx.","items":["ssh user@server","sudo systemctl restart nginx","systemctl status nginx","exit"],"why":"`ssh` logs in, `systemctl restart` restarts the service (it needs `sudo`), `status` checks it is running again, and `exit` logs out.","id":"lx.i.06.2"},
        {"t":"choice","topic":"Linux","q":"Which command shows whether the nginx service is running?","a":"systemctl status nginx","w":["systemctl list nginx","systemctl show-running","service nginx.log"],"why":"`systemctl status nginx` shows if the service is active, and its latest log lines.","id":"lx.i.06.3"},
        {"t":"choice","topic":"Linux","q":"Which command makes nginx start automatically when the server boots?","a":"sudo systemctl enable nginx","w":["sudo systemctl start nginx","sudo systemctl boot nginx","sudo systemctl restart nginx"],"why":"`enable` sets it to start at boot. `start` only starts it now.","id":"lx.i.06.4"},
        {"t":"choice","topic":"Linux","q":"Which command shows the logs of the nginx service?","a":"journalctl -u nginx","w":["journalctl nginx.txt","systemctl logs nginx","cat nginx.log"],"why":"`journalctl -u name` shows the logs of one unit (service).","id":"lx.i.06.5"},
        {"t":"bug","topic":"Linux","q":"Ravi changed the nginx settings. He wants to apply them and then read the nginx logs. Which line is wrong?","code":["sudo nano /etc/nginx/nginx.conf","sudo systemctl restart nginx","sudo journalctl nginx","sudo systemctl status nginx"],"bad":2,"why":"To see one service's logs you need the `-u` option: `sudo journalctl -u nginx`.","id":"lx.i.06.6"},
        {"t":"check","topic":"Linux","q":"A service is running now but is disabled. The server reboots. What happens?","a":"It does not start automatically at boot","w":["It starts automatically at boot","It is deleted from the server","It starts after one hour"],"why":"Only an enabled service starts at boot. `start` and `enable` are different.","id":"lx.i.06.7"},
        {"t":"check","topic":"Linux","q":"What does `sudo systemctl restart nginx` do?","a":"Stops nginx and starts it again","w":["Installs nginx again","Deletes nginx settings","Only shows the nginx status"],"why":"`restart` is a stop followed by a start. It is used after changing settings.","id":"lx.i.06.8"}
      ] },
      { id: "lx.i.07", level: "i", n: 7, title: "Packages (apt)", cards: [
        {"t":"learn","topic":"Linux","title":"Install programs with apt","body":"On Ubuntu, `apt` installs and removes programs, called **packages**. First run `apt update` to refresh the list of available packages. Then `apt install name` installs one. You need `sudo`, because it changes the system.","code":"sudo apt update\nsudo apt install nginx","lang":"shell","id":"lx.i.07.1"},
        {"t":"choice","topic":"Linux","q":"What does `sudo apt update` do?","a":"It refreshes the list of available packages and versions, and installs nothing","w":["It upgrades every installed program","It installs the newest Ubuntu","It removes unused programs"],"why":"`update` only downloads the package list. `upgrade` is the command that installs newer versions.","id":"lx.i.07.2"},
        {"t":"choice","topic":"Linux","q":"Which command installs the program `curl`?","a":"sudo apt install curl","w":["sudo apt get curl","sudo apt add curl","sudo install apt curl"],"why":"The pattern is `sudo apt install name`.","id":"lx.i.07.3"},
        {"t":"choice","topic":"Linux","q":"Which command removes the package `nginx`?","a":"sudo apt remove nginx","w":["sudo apt delete nginx","sudo apt uninstall nginx","sudo rm nginx"],"why":"`apt remove name` uninstalls a package. `delete` and `uninstall` are not apt commands.","id":"lx.i.07.4"},
        {"t":"choice","topic":"Linux","q":"You forgot the package name but know it is about \"editor\". Which command looks for packages?","a":"apt search editor","w":["apt find editor","apt lookup editor","apt get editor"],"why":"`apt search word` lists packages that match the word.","id":"lx.i.07.5"},
        {"t":"order","topic":"Linux","q":"Order the steps to install and check nginx on Ubuntu.","items":["sudo apt update","sudo apt install nginx","nginx -v"],"why":"Refresh the list, install, and then check the installed version with `nginx -v`.","id":"lx.i.07.6"},
        {"t":"check","topic":"Linux","q":"Why do we run `sudo apt update` before `sudo apt install`?","a":"So apt knows the latest packages and versions","w":["To delete old programs","To give your user admin rights","To restart the server"],"why":"Without a fresh list, apt may look for an old version that is no longer there.","id":"lx.i.07.7"},
        {"t":"check","topic":"Linux","q":"Which command installs newer versions of the programs that are already installed?","a":"sudo apt upgrade","w":["sudo apt update","sudo apt search","sudo apt remove"],"why":"`update` refreshes the list. `upgrade` installs the newer versions.","id":"lx.i.07.8"}
      ] },
      { id: "lx.i.08", level: "i", n: 8, title: "Networking commands", cards: [
        {"t":"learn","topic":"Linux","title":"Network commands","body":"`ip a` shows your IP addresses. `ping` checks if another computer answers, and `curl` fetches a web page. `ss -tuln` lists the ports that are **listening**, which means waiting for connections.","code":"ip a\nping -c 3 8.8.8.8\ncurl -I https://example.com\nss -tuln","lang":"shell","id":"lx.i.08.1"},
        {"t":"choice","topic":"Linux","q":"Which command shows the IP addresses of your computer?","a":"ip a","w":["ping ip","curl ip","ss -ip"],"why":"`ip a` (short for `ip addr`) lists each network card and its addresses.","id":"lx.i.08.2"},
        {"t":"choice","topic":"Linux","q":"Which command lists the ports that are listening for connections?","a":"ss -tuln","w":["ping -c 3","curl -I","ip route list ports"],"why":"`ss -tuln` shows listening TCP and UDP ports, with numbers instead of names.","id":"lx.i.08.3"},
        {"t":"choice","topic":"Linux","q":"What does `-c 3` mean in `ping -c 3 8.8.8.8`?","a":"Send 3 packets and then stop","w":["Wait 3 minutes","Use channel 3","Check 3 computers"],"why":"Without `-c`, `ping` on Linux keeps going until you press Ctrl + C.","id":"lx.i.08.4"},
        {"t":"choice","topic":"Linux","q":"Which command fetches a web page from the terminal?","a":"curl https://example.com","w":["ss https://example.com","ip https://example.com","ls https://example.com"],"why":"`curl` makes a request to a URL and prints the answer.","id":"lx.i.08.5"},
        {"t":"choice","topic":"Linux","q":"What does DNS do?","a":"It turns a name like example.com into an IP address","w":["It makes the internet faster","It blocks bad websites","It gives your computer a password"],"why":"Computers connect with IP addresses. DNS is like a phone book from names to numbers.","id":"lx.i.08.6"},
        {"t":"order","topic":"Linux","q":"The internet is not working. Order the checks from the closest to the farthest.","items":["ip a (do I have an IP address?)","ping 8.8.8.8 (can I reach the internet?)","ping google.com (does DNS work?)"],"why":"Check yourself first, then reach the internet by number, and then by name. This shows where the problem is.","id":"lx.i.08.7"},
        {"t":"check","topic":"Linux","q":"`ping 8.8.8.8` works, but `ping google.com` says it cannot find the name. What is most likely wrong?","a":"DNS","w":["The network cable","The IP address of 8.8.8.8","The hard disk"],"why":"Reaching an IP number works, so the network is fine. Only turning a name into a number fails: that is DNS.","id":"lx.i.08.8"},
        {"t":"check","topic":"Linux","q":"`ss -tuln` shows `0.0.0.0:80`. What does it mean?","a":"Something is listening on port 80 on all addresses","w":["Port 80 is closed","Your computer has no IP address","Port 80 is blocked by DNS"],"why":"`0.0.0.0` means all addresses on this computer. Port 80 is where a web server often listens.","id":"lx.i.08.9"}
      ] },
      { id: "lx.i.09", level: "i", n: 9, title: "Shell scripts", cards: [
        {"t":"learn","topic":"Linux","title":"Your first shell script","body":"A **shell script** is a file of commands. The first line `#!/bin/bash` (the **shebang**) says to run it with bash. Set a variable with no spaces around `=`, and read it with `$`. Run the script with `bash script.sh`.","code":"#!/bin/bash\nname=\"Asha\"\necho \"Hello $name\"","lang":"shell","id":"lx.i.09.1"},
        {"t":"output","topic":"Linux","code":"# hi.sh has one line:\necho \"Hello $1\"\n# run it with:\nbash hi.sh Ravi","lang":"shell","a":"Hello Ravi","w":["Hello $1","Hello hi.sh","Hello"],"why":"`$1` is the first word after the script name. Here it is `Ravi`.","id":"lx.i.09.2"},
        {"t":"output","topic":"Linux","code":"for n in 1 2 3\ndo\n  echo \"Day $n\"\ndone","lang":"shell","a":"Day 1\nDay 2\nDay 3","w":["Day n\nDay n\nDay n","Day 3","Day 1 2 3"],"why":"A `for` loop runs the commands once for each value. `$n` becomes 1, then 2, then 3.","id":"lx.i.09.3"},
        {"t":"output","topic":"Linux","code":"age=20\nif [ $age -ge 18 ]; then\n  echo \"adult\"\nelse\n  echo \"minor\"\nfi","lang":"shell","a":"adult","w":["minor","adult\nminor","Nothing is printed"],"why":"`-ge` means greater than or equal. 20 is at least 18, so the `then` part runs.","id":"lx.i.09.4"},
        {"t":"output","topic":"Linux","code":"# cities.txt has Guntur and Pune\ngrep -q Mumbai cities.txt\necho $?","lang":"shell","a":"1","w":["0","Mumbai","Nothing is printed"],"why":"`$?` is the exit code of the last command. 0 means success. `grep` returns 1 when it finds no match.","id":"lx.i.09.5"},
        {"t":"output","topic":"Linux","code":"printf 'Guntur\\nPune\\nGuntur\\n' | sort | uniq","lang":"shell","a":"Guntur\nPune","w":["Guntur\nPune\nGuntur","Guntur\nGuntur\nPune","Pune"],"why":"`sort` puts the same lines next to each other. Then `uniq` removes the repeated neighbours.","id":"lx.i.09.6"},
        {"t":"bug","topic":"Linux","q":"This script gives the error `name: command not found`. Which line is wrong?","code":["#!/bin/bash","name = \"Asha\"","echo \"Hello $name\""],"bad":1,"why":"In bash there must be no spaces around `=`. Write `name=\"Asha\"`.","id":"lx.i.09.7"},
        {"t":"check","topic":"Linux","q":"What does the first line `#!/bin/bash` do?","a":"It tells the system to run the script with bash","w":["It makes the script run faster","It hides the script","It creates a folder named bash"],"why":"The shebang names the program that should run the file.","id":"lx.i.09.8"},
        {"t":"check","topic":"Linux","q":"What does this print?","code":"x=5\nfor i in 1 2 3; do\n  x=$((x+i))\ndone\necho $x","lang":"shell","a":"11","w":["8","6","5"],"why":"x starts at 5. The loop adds 1, 2 and 3, so x becomes 5 + 6 = 11.","id":"lx.i.09.9"}
      ] },
      { id: "lx.a.10", level: "a", n: 10, title: "cron", cards: [
        {"t":"learn","topic":"Linux","title":"cron: run jobs on a schedule","body":"`crontab -e` opens your list of scheduled jobs, and `crontab -l` shows it. Each line has **5 time fields** (minute, hour, day of month, month, day of week) and then the command. Example: `0 2 * * *` runs every day at 2:00 AM.","code":"crontab -e\n# m h dom mon dow  command\n0 2 * * * /home/asha/backup.sh","lang":"shell","id":"lx.a.10.1"},
        {"t":"choice","topic":"Linux","q":"What does `0 2 * * *` mean?","a":"Every day at 2:00 AM","w":["Every 2 minutes","Every day at 2:00 PM","At 2:00 AM only on the 1st of the month"],"why":"Fields are minute, hour, day of month, month, day of week. Hours use the 24-hour clock, so 2 PM would be `14`.","id":"lx.a.10.2"},
        {"t":"choice","topic":"Linux","q":"What does `*/15 * * * *` mean?","a":"Every 15 minutes","w":["At minute 15 of every hour","Every 15 hours","Only on the 15th day of the month"],"why":"`*/15` in the minute field means every 15 minutes: 0, 15, 30 and 45.","id":"lx.a.10.3"},
        {"t":"choice","topic":"Linux","q":"Which line runs a job every Monday at 9:00 AM?","a":"`0 9 * * 1`","w":["`9 0 * * 1`","`0 9 1 * *`","`0 9 * 1 *`"],"why":"Minute 0, hour 9, any day of month, any month, day of week 1 (Monday). The others swap fields or pick the 1st of the month or January.","id":"lx.a.10.4"},
        {"t":"pipe","topic":"Linux","q":"Build a cron line: put the five time fields in the order cron reads them. One block does not belong.","steps":[["MIN","minute"],["HOUR","hour"],["DOM","day of month"],["MON","month"],["DOW","day of week"]],"extra":[["YEAR","year"]],"why":"A standard crontab line has no year field. After the five fields comes the command.","id":"lx.a.10.5"},
        {"t":"bug","topic":"Linux","q":"Ravi's backup works in his terminal but never runs from cron, because cron has a very small PATH. Which line is wrong?","code":["# m h dom mon dow  command","30 1 * * * backup.sh >> /home/ravi/backup.log 2>&1"],"lang":"shell","bad":1,"why":"Cron does not search your usual folders. Use the full path to the script (and to other programs when needed).","fix":"30 1 * * * /home/ravi/bin/backup.sh >> /home/ravi/backup.log 2>&1","id":"lx.a.10.6"},
        {"t":"order","topic":"Linux","q":"Put the steps for adding a new cron job in order.","items":["Write the script and test it by hand","Run `crontab -e`","Add the line with full paths and a log file","Run `crontab -l` to check it was saved"],"why":"Test the script first, so a failure is not mistaken for a cron problem.","id":"lx.a.10.7"},
        {"t":"choice","topic":"Linux","q":"Which is an alternative to cron on Linux systems that use systemd?","a":"systemd timers","w":["`journalctl`","`logrotate`","`ssh-agent`"],"why":"A systemd timer unit starts a service on a schedule. It also keeps its logs in the journal.","id":"lx.a.10.8"},
        {"t":"check","topic":"Linux","q":"What does `>> /var/log/job.log 2>&1` at the end of a cron line do?","a":"Appends normal output and errors to `job.log`","w":["Overwrites `job.log` every time","Sends only errors to `job.log`","Stops the job from running twice"],"why":"`>>` appends and `2>&1` sends errors to the same place. Without a log, cron may try to email the output or lose it.","id":"lx.a.10.9"},
        {"t":"check","topic":"Linux","q":"Which command removes ALL of your cron jobs?","a":"`crontab -r`","w":["`crontab -l`","`crontab -e`","`crontab -v`"],"why":"`-r` removes the whole crontab, and it sits right next to `-e` on the keyboard. Save a copy first with `crontab -l > backup.txt`.","id":"lx.a.10.10"}
      ] },
      { id: "lx.a.11", level: "a", n: 11, title: "Logs and journalctl", cards: [
        {"t":"learn","topic":"Linux","title":"Reading logs","body":"Programs write logs in `/var/log/` and in the systemd journal. Use `tail -f` to watch new lines live, `grep -i error` to find lines, and `journalctl -u nginx` for one service. `--since \"1 hour ago\"` narrows the time.","code":"tail -f /var/log/syslog\ngrep -i error app.log\njournalctl -u nginx --since \"1 hour ago\"","lang":"shell","id":"lx.a.11.1"},
        {"t":"output","topic":"Linux","q":"`app.log` has four lines: `INFO started`, `ERROR disk full`, `info retry` and `Error timeout`. What does this print?","code":"grep -i error app.log","lang":"shell","a":"ERROR disk full\nError timeout","w":["ERROR disk full","ERROR disk full\ninfo retry\nError timeout","INFO started\ninfo retry"],"why":"`-i` ignores upper and lower case, so both `ERROR` and `Error` match. Without `-i`, only `ERROR disk full` would match.","id":"lx.a.11.2"},
        {"t":"output","topic":"Linux","q":"The same `app.log` has four lines: `INFO started`, `ERROR disk full`, `info retry` and `Error timeout`. What does this print?","code":"tail -n 2 app.log","lang":"shell","a":"info retry\nError timeout","w":["INFO started\nERROR disk full","ERROR disk full\ninfo retry","Error timeout"],"why":"`tail -n 2` prints the last 2 lines. Recent events are at the end of a log file.","id":"lx.a.11.3"},
        {"t":"order","topic":"Linux","q":"The website is failing. Put Asha's calm log search in order.","items":["Find the right file in `/var/log`","Read the end with `tail -n 50`","Narrow down with `grep -i error`","Watch new lines with `tail -f` while retrying"],"why":"Go from wide to narrow, and watch live only when you know which file matters.","id":"lx.a.11.4"},
        {"t":"choice","topic":"Linux","q":"Which command shows only error messages (and worse) of the `nginx` service from this boot?","a":"`journalctl -u nginx -p err -b`","w":["`journalctl -u nginx -f`","`journalctl nginx -b err`","`journalctl --unit=err nginx`"],"why":"`-u` picks the unit, `-p err` filters by priority and `-b` means this boot. `-f` only follows new lines.","id":"lx.a.11.5"},
        {"t":"bug","topic":"Linux","q":"Ravi wants to watch new lines arrive, but the command prints 20 lines and stops. Which line is wrong?","code":["cd /var/log","tail -n 20 syslog"],"lang":"shell","bad":1,"why":"`tail -f` keeps the file open and prints new lines as they are added. Press Ctrl+C to stop.","fix":"tail -f syslog","id":"lx.a.11.6"},
        {"t":"choice","topic":"Linux","q":"What does `logrotate` do?","a":"Renames, compresses and removes old log files so they do not fill the disk","w":["Sends logs to another server every hour","Shows log lines as they arrive","Encrypts log files with a password"],"why":"Without rotation, a busy service can slowly fill the disk with log files.","id":"lx.a.11.7"},
        {"t":"check","topic":"Linux","q":"Which command follows the new log messages of `sshd` live?","a":"`journalctl -u sshd -f`","w":["`journalctl -u sshd -b`","`journalctl -u sshd -p err`","`tail sshd`"],"why":"`-f` follows the journal like `tail -f`. `-b` and `-p` only filter old messages.","id":"lx.a.11.8"},
        {"t":"check","topic":"Linux","q":"`app.log` has four lines: `INFO started`, `ERROR disk full`, `info retry` and `Error timeout`. What does this print?","code":"grep -c ERROR app.log","lang":"shell","a":"1","w":["2","3","4"],"why":"`-c` counts matching lines. Without `-i`, only `ERROR disk full` matches. `Error` and `info` are different in case.","id":"lx.a.11.9"}
      ] },
      { id: "lx.a.12", level: "a", n: 12, title: "SSH keys", cards: [
        {"t":"learn","topic":"Linux","title":"SSH keys: log in without a password","body":"An SSH key is a **pair**: the **private key** stays on your computer and is never shared, and the **public key** (`.pub`) is copied to the server. The server keeps public keys in `~/.ssh/authorized_keys`. Keys are safer than passwords.","code":"ssh-keygen -t ed25519\nssh-copy-id asha@203.0.113.10\nssh asha@203.0.113.10","lang":"shell","id":"lx.a.12.1"},
        {"t":"choice","topic":"Linux","q":"Which file does Asha copy to the server?","a":"The public key, `id_ed25519.pub`","w":["The private key, `id_ed25519`","Both files together","Her `known_hosts` file"],"why":"Anyone with your private key can log in as you. Share only the `.pub` file.","id":"lx.a.12.2"},
        {"t":"choice","topic":"Linux","q":"Which file on the server holds the public keys that may log in to that account?","a":"`~/.ssh/authorized_keys`","w":["`~/.ssh/known_hosts`","`~/.ssh/id_ed25519`","`/etc/passwd`"],"why":"`ssh-copy-id` appends your public key to this file. `known_hosts` is on your laptop and remembers servers you trusted.","id":"lx.a.12.3"},
        {"t":"order","topic":"Linux","q":"Put the steps for key login in order.","items":["Create the key pair with `ssh-keygen -t ed25519`","Copy the public key with `ssh-copy-id asha@server`","Log in with the key to test it","Set `PasswordAuthentication no` and reload `sshd`"],"why":"Turn off password login only at the end, after you have seen key login work.","id":"lx.a.12.4"},
        {"t":"bug","topic":"Linux","q":"SSH says UNPROTECTED PRIVATE KEY FILE and ignores the key. Which line gives the private key the wrong permission?","code":["chmod 700 ~/.ssh","chmod 644 ~/.ssh/id_ed25519"],"lang":"shell","bad":1,"why":"A private key must be readable only by you: `600`. `700` on the `~/.ssh` folder is right.","fix":"chmod 600 ~/.ssh/id_ed25519","id":"lx.a.12.5"},
        {"t":"choice","topic":"Linux","q":"Asha's `~/.ssh/config` has `Host web`, `HostName 203.0.113.10` and `User asha`. What can she type now?","a":"`ssh web`","w":["`ssh config web`","`ssh -h web`","`ssh asha@web.config`"],"why":"A Host alias in `~/.ssh/config` gives a short name, so you do not repeat the address and user every time.","id":"lx.a.12.6"},
        {"t":"choice","topic":"Linux","q":"When should you set `PasswordAuthentication no` in `sshd_config`?","a":"Only after you tested that key login works","w":["Before copying any key, to be safe","Right after creating the server, always","Never, because keys already block passwords"],"why":"If keys do not work and passwords are off, you are locked out. Test first.","id":"lx.a.12.7"},
        {"t":"check","topic":"Linux","q":"Lakshmi accidentally emailed her private key `id_ed25519` to a friend. What should she do?","a":"Treat it as leaked: remove its public key from servers and create a new pair","w":["Nothing, the key is useless without the `.pub` file","Rename the file to `id_ed25519.old`","Change only her computer's password"],"why":"Anyone holding a private key can log in wherever its public key is trusted. Replace the pair.","id":"lx.a.12.8"},
        {"t":"check","topic":"Linux","q":"Asha edited `sshd_config` on a remote server. What should she keep open while she tests the new setting?","a":"Her current logged-in session","w":["Nothing, she can close all sessions","Only a password-reset page","The `known_hosts` file"],"why":"If the new setting is wrong, the open session lets her undo it. A closed session may mean a locked-out server.","id":"lx.a.12.9"}
      ] },
      { id: "lx.a.13", level: "a", n: 13, title: "Troubleshooting a slow server", cards: [
        {"t":"learn","topic":"Linux","title":"A slow server: stay calm, check in order","body":"First look at the big picture: `uptime` (load average), `free -h` (memory) and `df -h` (disk). Then find the culprit with `top` or `du`. Change **one thing at a time** and check the result.","code":"uptime\nfree -h\ndf -h\ndu -sh * | sort -rh | head","lang":"shell","id":"lx.a.13.1"},
        {"t":"order","topic":"Linux","q":"A server feels slow. Put a calm order of checks.","items":["`uptime`: look at the load average","`free -h` and `df -h`: check memory and disk","`top`: find the process using CPU or memory","Read that program's logs for errors","Change one thing, then check again"],"why":"Start wide and cheap, then go deeper. Logs help most once you know which program to look at.","id":"lx.a.13.2"},
        {"t":"pipe","topic":"Linux","q":"The disk is full. Build the pipeline that lists the 5 biggest folders here. One block does not belong.","steps":[["DU","du -sh *"],["SORT","sort -rh"],["HEAD","head -n 5"]],"extra":[["WC","wc -l"]],"why":"`du -sh *` gives folder sizes, `sort -rh` puts the biggest first and `head` keeps the top 5. `wc -l` only counts lines.","id":"lx.a.13.3"},
        {"t":"choice","topic":"Linux","q":"A 4-core server shows a load average of 12. What does that suggest?","a":"More tasks are running or waiting than the 4 cores can handle","w":["Only 12% of memory is free","The server has been up for 12 days","12 users are logged in"],"why":"Load average counts tasks that are running or waiting. Compare it with the number of CPU cores.","id":"lx.a.13.4"},
        {"t":"choice","topic":"Linux","q":"In the output of `free -h`, which column best shows memory still available for new programs?","a":"`available`","w":["`free`","`used`","`shared`"],"why":"Linux uses spare memory as cache, so `free` looks small. `available` estimates what programs can really get.","id":"lx.a.13.5"},
        {"t":"bug","topic":"Linux","q":"Ravi wants the 5 processes using the most memory, but the list shows the smallest first. Which line is wrong?","code":["uptime","ps aux --sort=%mem | head -n 5"],"lang":"shell","bad":1,"why":"A minus sign reverses the order: `--sort=-%mem` puts the biggest first.","fix":"ps aux --sort=-%mem | head -n 5","id":"lx.a.13.6"},
        {"t":"choice","topic":"Linux","q":"You found three possible causes of a slow server. What is the calm way to continue?","a":"Change one thing, check the result, then continue","w":["Change all three and restart the server","Reboot first, then read the logs","Kill every process that uses CPU"],"why":"If you change many things at once, you will not know which one helped or caused a new problem.","id":"lx.a.13.7"},
        {"t":"check","topic":"Linux","q":"Which command shows how full each disk or partition is?","a":"`df -h`","w":["`du -sh *`","`free -h`","`uptime`"],"why":"`df` reports file system space. `du` adds up the size of folders, `free` is memory and `uptime` is load.","id":"lx.a.13.8"},
        {"t":"check","topic":"Linux","q":"`top` shows one process using 99% CPU. What is the best first step?","a":"Find out what it is (name, user, `ps`) before stopping it","w":["Run `kill -9` on it at once","Reboot the server","Delete its program file"],"why":"It may be a database or a backup that is needed. Understand it first, then stop it gently if that is right.","id":"lx.a.13.9"}
      ] }
    ] },
    { id: "rh", name: "Red Hat", topic: "RedHat", blurb: "Red Hat Enterprise Linux and OpenShift: the Linux and Kubernetes many big companies run.", units: [
      { id: "rh.b.01", level: "b", n: 1, title: "RHEL and other Linux", cards: [
        {"t":"learn","topic":"RedHat","title":"RHEL and other Linux","body":"**Red Hat Enterprise Linux (RHEL)** is a Linux system for companies. You buy a **subscription**, and it gives you updates and support from Red Hat. Use `cat /etc/redhat-release` to see which RHEL version a server runs.","code":"cat /etc/redhat-release","lang":"shell","id":"rh.b.01.1"},
        {"t":"choice","topic":"RedHat","q":"What does a RHEL subscription give a company?","a":"Updates and support from Red Hat","w":["Permission to use basic commands like `ls`","A free laptop with Linux installed","A faster Linux kernel than all other Linux systems"],"why":"The subscription is for updates, security fixes and support. Basic Linux commands are free for everyone.","id":"rh.b.01.2"},
        {"t":"choice","topic":"RedHat","q":"Which project is a community Linux where new features are tried before they reach RHEL?","a":"Fedora","w":["Windows Server","Android","Excel"],"why":"Fedora is the community project. CentOS Stream tracks just ahead of RHEL.","id":"rh.b.01.3"},
        {"t":"choice","topic":"RedHat","q":"Asha logs in to a new server. Which command shows the RHEL version?","a":"`cat /etc/redhat-release`","w":["`rhel --version`","`ls /etc/redhat`","`whoami`"],"why":"The file `/etc/redhat-release` holds the release name. `whoami` only shows your user name.","id":"rh.b.01.4"},
        {"t":"bug","topic":"RedHat","q":"Ravi wants to read the OS details, but one command fails with 'No such file'. Which line has the wrong file name?","code":["cat /etc/os-release","cat /etc/redhat-release","cat /etc/release-redhat"],"lang":"shell","bad":2,"why":"The correct file is `/etc/redhat-release`. The words in the name must be in the right order.","id":"rh.b.01.5"},
        {"t":"choice","topic":"RedHat","q":"Ravi knows `ls`, `cd` and `cp` from another Linux. Will they work on RHEL?","a":"Yes, basic Linux commands are the same","w":["No, RHEL has its own different commands for these","Only after buying a subscription","Only for the root user"],"why":"All Linux systems share the same basics. The package tool is different: RHEL uses `dnf`.","id":"rh.b.01.6"},
        {"t":"check","topic":"RedHat","q":"Which one is an enterprise Linux with a paid subscription and support?","a":"Red Hat Enterprise Linux","w":["Fedora Workstation","Windows 11","Google Chrome"],"why":"RHEL is the Red Hat product with a subscription. Fedora is the community project.","id":"rh.b.01.7"},
        {"t":"check","topic":"RedHat","q":"Which is true about CentOS Stream?","a":"It is a free Linux that tracks just ahead of RHEL","w":["It is a paid Windows edition","It is the oldest RHEL version","It is a package manager like dnf"],"why":"CentOS Stream sits between Fedora and RHEL and tracks just ahead of RHEL development.","id":"rh.b.01.8"}
      ] },
      { id: "rh.b.02", level: "b", n: 2, title: "Packages with dnf", cards: [
        {"t":"learn","topic":"RedHat","title":"Packages with dnf","body":"**dnf** installs, updates and removes software on RHEL from **repositories**. Changing software needs admin rights, so use `sudo`. The older name was `yum`; on RHEL 9 `yum` is just another name for `dnf`.","code":"sudo dnf install httpd\ndnf search nginx\ndnf info httpd\ndnf list installed","lang":"shell","id":"rh.b.02.1"},
        {"t":"choice","topic":"RedHat","q":"Lakshmi wants to install the `httpd` package. Which command is right?","a":"`sudo dnf install httpd`","w":["`dnf get httpd`","`sudo dnf run httpd`","`dnf add-repo httpd`"],"why":"Software is added with `dnf install`, and it needs `sudo`.","id":"rh.b.02.2"},
        {"t":"bug","topic":"RedHat","q":"One line fails with a permission error because it changes the system without admin rights. Which line?","code":["sudo dnf search nginx","dnf install nginx","sudo dnf remove nginx"],"lang":"shell","bad":1,"why":"Installing changes the system, so it needs `sudo`.","id":"rh.b.02.3"},
        {"t":"pipe","topic":"RedHat","q":"Build the safe way to add software: look, check details, install, then confirm.","steps":[["SRCH","dnf search","src"],["INFO","dnf info"],["INST","sudo dnf install"],["LIST","dnf list installed"]],"extra":[["RMV","sudo dnf remove"]],"why":"Search, read the details, install, then list installed packages to confirm. `remove` does the opposite of install.","id":"rh.b.02.4"},
        {"t":"choice","topic":"RedHat","q":"Which command shows the packages already installed?","a":"`dnf list installed`","w":["`dnf show all`","`dnf installed`","`dnf repo list-files`"],"why":"`dnf list installed` lists installed packages. Many beginners type `dnf installed`, which is not a command.","id":"rh.b.02.5"},
        {"t":"choice","topic":"RedHat","q":"Ravi finds an old guide that uses `yum install`. What should he know about RHEL 9?","a":"`yum` is the older name and works as an alias of `dnf`","w":["`yum` is a different tool for Windows","`yum` can only remove packages","`yum` is a firewall command"],"why":"On RHEL 9, `yum` points to `dnf`, so old guides still work. Prefer typing `dnf`.","id":"rh.b.02.6"},
        {"t":"check","topic":"RedHat","q":"What is a repository (repo) for dnf?","a":"A place that holds packages dnf can download","w":["A folder where you save your own files","A list of running services","A type of firewall zone"],"why":"dnf downloads packages from the repositories set up on the system.","id":"rh.b.02.7"},
        {"t":"check","topic":"RedHat","q":"Which command removes the `httpd` package?","a":"`sudo dnf remove httpd`","w":["`sudo dnf delete-all httpd`","`sudo dnf purge httpd`","`sudo dnf stop httpd`"],"why":"Use `dnf remove`. `stop` is a `systemctl` word and does not remove packages.","id":"rh.b.02.8"}
      ] },
      { id: "rh.b.03", level: "b", n: 3, title: "Services with systemctl", cards: [
        {"t":"learn","topic":"RedHat","title":"Services with systemctl","body":"A **service** is a program that runs in the background, like a web server. **systemd** manages services and you control them with `systemctl`. `start` runs it now, `enable` makes it start at boot, and `enable --now` does both.","code":"sudo systemctl start httpd\nsudo systemctl enable --now httpd\nsystemctl status httpd","lang":"shell","id":"rh.b.03.1"},
        {"t":"choice","topic":"RedHat","q":"Asha runs `sudo systemctl enable httpd`. What happens?","a":"It will start at boot, but it is not started now","w":["It starts now and never at boot","It is removed from the system","It opens port 80 in the firewall"],"why":"`enable` only sets the start at boot. Add `--now` to also start it right away.","id":"rh.b.03.2"},
        {"t":"choice","topic":"RedHat","q":"Which command starts a service now AND makes it start at every boot?","a":"`sudo systemctl enable --now httpd`","w":["`sudo systemctl boot httpd`","`sudo systemctl start-all httpd`","`sudo systemctl run --boot httpd`"],"why":"`enable --now` does both steps in one command.","id":"rh.b.03.3"},
        {"t":"bug","topic":"RedHat","q":"Ravi wants to see if `httpd` is running, but one line gives 'Unknown command verb'. Which line?","code":["sudo systemctl restart httpd","systemctl stats httpd","sudo systemctl stop httpd"],"lang":"shell","bad":1,"why":"The verb is `status`, not `stats`. A small spelling mistake is a common reason for this error.","id":"rh.b.03.4"},
        {"t":"order","topic":"RedHat","q":"Put the steps in order to run a web server on a fresh RHEL.","items":["`sudo dnf install httpd`","`sudo systemctl enable --now httpd`","`systemctl status httpd`"],"why":"Install the package first, then start and enable it, then check the status.","id":"rh.b.03.5"},
        {"t":"choice","topic":"RedHat","q":"After changing the config file of `httpd`, Lakshmi wants to stop and start it again. Which command does this?","a":"`sudo systemctl restart httpd`","w":["`sudo systemctl reload-all httpd`","`sudo systemctl enable httpd`","`sudo systemctl status httpd`"],"why":"`restart` stops and starts the service. `enable` only changes the boot setting.","id":"rh.b.03.6"},
        {"t":"check","topic":"RedHat","q":"What does `systemctl disable httpd` do?","a":"Stops it from starting at boot","w":["Stops it right now","Removes the package","Blocks it in the firewall"],"why":"`disable` removes the start at boot. A running service keeps running until you `stop` it.","id":"rh.b.03.7"},
        {"t":"check","topic":"RedHat","q":"Which tool manages services on RHEL?","a":"systemd, controlled with `systemctl`","w":["`dnf`","`firewall-cmd`","`restorecon`"],"why":"`systemctl` talks to systemd. `dnf` handles packages and `firewall-cmd` handles the firewall.","id":"rh.b.03.8"}
      ] },
      { id: "rh.b.04", level: "b", n: 4, title: "Firewall with firewalld", cards: [
        {"t":"learn","topic":"RedHat","title":"Firewall with firewalld","body":"**firewalld** is the firewall on RHEL. Rules are grouped in **zones**, and you control it with `firewall-cmd`. Rules added without `--permanent` are lost after a reload or reboot, so use `--permanent` and then `--reload`.","code":"sudo firewall-cmd --state\nsudo firewall-cmd --permanent --add-service=http\nsudo firewall-cmd --reload\nsudo firewall-cmd --list-all","lang":"shell","id":"rh.b.04.1"},
        {"t":"choice","topic":"RedHat","q":"Which command checks that firewalld is running?","a":"`sudo firewall-cmd --state`","w":["`sudo firewall-cmd --running`","`systemctl firewall`","`sudo firewall-cmd --alive`"],"why":"`--state` prints `running` when firewalld is active.","id":"rh.b.04.2"},
        {"t":"choice","topic":"RedHat","q":"Ravi's web server needs HTTP traffic on port 80. Which option allows it by service name?","a":"`--add-service=http`","w":["`--open-web`","`--add-zone=http`","`--allow=80`"],"why":"A service name like `http` is easier than remembering the port number.","id":"rh.b.04.3"},
        {"t":"bug","topic":"RedHat","q":"Asha's app listens on port 8080 but the command is rejected as invalid. Which line is wrong?","code":["sudo firewall-cmd --permanent --add-port=8080","sudo firewall-cmd --reload"],"lang":"shell","bad":0,"why":"A port needs the protocol, like `8080/tcp`. Without it the command is rejected.","id":"rh.b.04.4"},
        {"t":"order","topic":"RedHat","q":"Put the steps in order to open port 8080/tcp permanently and check it.","items":["`sudo firewall-cmd --permanent --add-port=8080/tcp`","`sudo firewall-cmd --reload`","`sudo firewall-cmd --list-all`"],"why":"A permanent rule is saved first. The reload applies it, then `--list-all` shows it.","id":"rh.b.04.5"},
        {"t":"pipe","topic":"RedHat","q":"Build the flow to allow HTTP so it stays after a reboot.","steps":[["ADD","add-service=http --permanent","src"],["RLD","--reload"],["LST","--list-all"]],"extra":[["DNF","dnf remove httpd"]],"why":"Add the rule as permanent, reload to apply it, then list to confirm. Removing the package does not open the port.","id":"rh.b.04.6"},
        {"t":"check","topic":"RedHat","q":"Lakshmi adds a rule WITHOUT `--permanent`. What happens after `firewall-cmd --reload`?","a":"The rule is lost","w":["The rule becomes permanent","The firewall turns off","The rule moves to another zone"],"why":"Rules without `--permanent` are only in the running setup, and a reload removes them.","id":"rh.b.04.7"},
        {"t":"check","topic":"RedHat","q":"Which command shows the rules of the default zone?","a":"`sudo firewall-cmd --list-all`","w":["`sudo firewall-cmd --show-zone-only`","`sudo firewall-cmd --reload`","`sudo firewall-cmd --state`"],"why":"`--list-all` lists services, ports and more. `--state` only says whether firewalld runs.","id":"rh.b.04.8"}
      ] },
      { id: "rh.b.05", level: "b", n: 5, title: "SELinux basics", cards: [
        {"t":"learn","topic":"RedHat","title":"SELinux basics","body":"**SELinux** adds extra security rules on RHEL. It has modes: **Enforcing** (blocks), **Permissive** (only logs) and **Disabled**. Files and processes have labels, which you can see with `ls -Z`.","code":"getenforce\nls -Z /var/www/html","lang":"shell","id":"rh.b.05.1"},
        {"t":"choice","topic":"RedHat","q":"Which SELinux mode blocks actions that break the rules?","a":"Enforcing","w":["Permissive","Disabled","Passive"],"why":"Enforcing blocks. Permissive only writes a log message. There is no mode called Passive.","id":"rh.b.05.2"},
        {"t":"output","topic":"RedHat","q":"SELinux is in Enforcing mode. What does the last command print?","code":"sudo setenforce 0\ngetenforce","lang":"shell","a":"Permissive","w":["Enforcing","Disabled","Off"],"why":"`setenforce 0` switches to Permissive until the next reboot.","id":"rh.b.05.3"},
        {"t":"choice","topic":"RedHat","q":"Ravi runs `sudo setenforce 0`. How long does it last?","a":"Until the next reboot","w":["Forever","For one command only","For one hour"],"why":"`setenforce` changes the running mode only. A permanent change needs the file `/etc/selinux/config`.","id":"rh.b.05.4"},
        {"t":"choice","topic":"RedHat","q":"A web page is blocked by SELinux. What should Asha do first?","a":"Check the file labels and fix them","w":["Disable SELinux for good","Delete the web page","Remove the firewall"],"why":"Disabling SELinux removes protection. Look at labels with `ls -Z` and fix them.","id":"rh.b.05.5"},
        {"t":"choice","topic":"RedHat","q":"Which command resets a file to its default SELinux label?","a":"`restorecon -v /var/www/html/index.html`","w":["`getenforce /var/www/html/index.html`","`setenforce /var/www/html/index.html`","`chmod -Z /var/www/html/index.html`"],"why":"`restorecon` restores the default label. `-v` shows what changed.","id":"rh.b.05.6"},
        {"t":"bug","topic":"RedHat","q":"Lakshmi wants to see SELinux labels of files, but one command shows no labels. Which line?","code":["ls -Z /var/www/html","ls -l /var/www/html","ls -Z /etc/hosts"],"lang":"shell","bad":1,"why":"The `-Z` option shows the SELinux label. `-l` only shows the long list.","id":"rh.b.05.7"},
        {"t":"check","topic":"RedHat","q":"Which command shows the current SELinux mode?","a":"`getenforce`","w":["`setenforce`","`selinux --mode`","`restorecon`"],"why":"`getenforce` prints Enforcing, Permissive or Disabled.","id":"rh.b.05.8"},
        {"t":"check","topic":"RedHat","q":"Where is the SELinux mode set permanently, so it stays after a reboot?","a":"`/etc/selinux/config`","w":["`/etc/hosts`","`/etc/passwd`","`/var/log/messages`"],"why":"`setenforce` changes only the running mode. The file `/etc/selinux/config` holds the mode used at boot.","id":"rh.b.05.9"}
      ] },
      { id: "rh.b.06", level: "b", n: 6, title: "OpenShift: what it is", cards: [
        {"t":"learn","topic":"RedHat","title":"OpenShift: what it is","body":"**OpenShift** is Red Hat's platform built on **Kubernetes** to run apps in containers. You work in a **project** (a Kubernetes namespace with extra features). Use the `oc` command or the web console, and a **Route** gives the app a URL.","code":"oc login\noc new-project demo\noc get pods","lang":"shell","id":"rh.b.06.1"},
        {"t":"choice","topic":"RedHat","q":"What is OpenShift?","a":"Red Hat's Kubernetes platform for running container apps","w":["A Linux text editor","A type of SELinux mode","A package manager like dnf"],"why":"OpenShift is built on Kubernetes and adds tools for developers and operators.","id":"rh.b.06.2"},
        {"t":"choice","topic":"RedHat","q":"Which command creates a new project named `shop`?","a":"`oc new-project shop`","w":["`oc create-project shop`","`oc make shop`","`oc project-new shop`"],"why":"The command is `oc new-project`. `oc project shop` only switches to an existing project.","id":"rh.b.06.3"},
        {"t":"bug","topic":"RedHat","q":"Ravi wants to create a project and list pods, but one line is not an `oc` command. Which line?","code":["oc login","oc create-project demo","oc get pods"],"lang":"shell","bad":1,"why":"The right command is `oc new-project`.","id":"rh.b.06.4"},
        {"t":"indent","topic":"RedHat","step":2,"q":"Fix the indentation of this Route, which sends traffic to the service `web`.","lang":"k8s","lines":[[0,"apiVersion: route.openshift.io/v1"],[0,"kind: Route"],[0,"metadata:"],[2,"name: web"],[0,"spec:"],[2,"to:"],[4,"kind: Service"],[4,"name: web"]],"why":"Child keys go two spaces deeper than their parent: `name` under `metadata`, and `kind` and `name` under `to`.","id":"rh.b.06.5"},
        {"t":"choice","topic":"RedHat","q":"Asha's app runs inside the cluster. What exposes it to users outside with a URL?","a":"A Route","w":["A SELinux label","A dnf repository","A systemd unit"],"why":"A Route gives the service a host name that users can open from outside.","id":"rh.b.06.6"},
        {"t":"order","topic":"RedHat","q":"Put the steps in order to see your app's pods.","items":["`oc login`","`oc new-project demo`","`oc get pods`"],"why":"Log in first, then create or choose a project, then list its pods.","id":"rh.b.06.7"},
        {"t":"check","topic":"RedHat","q":"Which command lists the pods in your current project?","a":"`oc get pods`","w":["`oc list pods`","`oc show containers`","`oc pods get`"],"why":"The pattern is `oc get <resource>`.","id":"rh.b.06.8"},
        {"t":"check","topic":"RedHat","q":"What is a project in OpenShift?","a":"A Kubernetes namespace with extra features","w":["A Linux user account","A firewall zone","A dnf package"],"why":"Projects group your apps and resources, and control who can see them.","id":"rh.b.06.9"}
      ] },
      { id: "rh.i.07", level: "i", n: 7, title: "Users, groups and sudo", cards: [
        {"t":"learn","topic":"RedHat","title":"Users, groups and sudo","body":"Create a user with `useradd`, then set a password with `passwd`. On RHEL, members of the **wheel** group can use `sudo`, so add admins with `usermod -aG wheel`. Do daily work as a normal user, not as root.","code":"sudo useradd asha\nsudo passwd asha\nsudo usermod -aG wheel asha\nid asha","lang":"shell","id":"rh.i.07.1"},
        {"t":"choice","topic":"RedHat","q":"Ravi must get sudo access and keep his other groups. Which command does this?","a":"`sudo usermod -aG wheel ravi`","w":["`sudo usermod -G wheel ravi`","`sudo groupadd wheel ravi`","`sudo passwd wheel ravi`"],"why":"`-aG` appends the group. Without `-a`, `-G` replaces his other groups, a common mistake.","id":"rh.i.07.2"},
        {"t":"choice","topic":"RedHat","q":"Where are the hashed (scrambled) passwords of Linux users stored?","a":"`/etc/shadow`","w":["`/etc/passwd`","`/etc/hosts`","`/etc/sudoers`"],"why":"`/etc/passwd` can be read by everyone and lists accounts. Password hashes are in `/etc/shadow`, readable only by root.","id":"rh.i.07.3"},
        {"t":"bug","topic":"RedHat","q":"Lakshmi's account exists, but the last line fails with an error. Which line is wrong?","code":["sudo useradd lakshmi","sudo passwd lakshmi","sudo useradd -aG wheel lakshmi"],"lang":"shell","bad":2,"why":"`useradd` has no `-a` option and it cannot change an existing user. Use `usermod` to add a group.","fix":"sudo usermod -aG wheel lakshmi","id":"rh.i.07.4"},
        {"t":"order","topic":"RedHat","q":"Put the steps in order to make Asha an admin user and check it.","items":["`sudo useradd asha`","`sudo usermod -aG wheel asha`","`id asha`"],"why":"Create the user, add the wheel group, then check with `id` that `wheel` is listed. Also set her password with `passwd` before she logs in.","id":"rh.i.07.5"},
        {"t":"choice","topic":"RedHat","q":"Which command is the safe way to edit the sudo rules file?","a":"`sudo visudo`","w":["`sudo nano /etc/sudoers`","`sudo cat /etc/sudoers`","`sudo useradd sudoers`"],"why":"`visudo` checks the syntax before saving. A typo saved by hand can lock you out of sudo.","id":"rh.i.07.6"},
        {"t":"choice","topic":"RedHat","q":"Which command shows a user's UID and groups?","a":"`id ravi`","w":["`who ravi`","`groupadd ravi`","`ls -Z ravi`"],"why":"`id` prints the user ID, group ID and all groups. `who` only lists logged-in users.","id":"rh.i.07.7"},
        {"t":"check","topic":"RedHat","q":"Which group gives its members sudo access on RHEL by default?","a":"`wheel`","w":["`admin`","`root`","`users`"],"why":"The `%wheel` line in `/etc/sudoers` lets this group use sudo. Other Linux systems may use a different group name.","id":"rh.i.07.8"},
        {"t":"check","topic":"RedHat","q":"Why avoid logging in as root for daily work?","a":"One wrong command can damage the whole system","w":["Root cannot run any commands","Root has no password at all","Root can only use the web console"],"why":"Root can change or delete anything. Use a normal user and `sudo` only when needed.","id":"rh.i.07.9"}
      ] },
      { id: "rh.i.08", level: "i", n: 8, title: "Storage: LVM and file systems", cards: [
        {"t":"learn","topic":"RedHat","title":"Storage with LVM and XFS","body":"**LVM** stacks storage in layers: a disk becomes a **physical volume** (`pvcreate`), physical volumes join a **volume group** (`vgcreate`), and you carve **logical volumes** (`lvcreate`). Put a file system on it with `mkfs.xfs`, since **XFS** is the RHEL default.","code":"sudo pvcreate /dev/sdb\nsudo vgcreate data_vg /dev/sdb\nsudo lvcreate -L 5G -n data_lv data_vg\nsudo mkfs.xfs /dev/data_vg/data_lv","lang":"shell","id":"rh.i.08.1"},
        {"t":"pipe","topic":"RedHat","q":"Build the storage from a new disk, step by step. Drop the block that does not belong.","steps":[["DISK","/dev/sdb","src"],["PV","pvcreate"],["VG","vgcreate"],["LV","lvcreate"],["XFS","mkfs.xfs"]],"extra":[["RM","lvremove"]],"why":"The order is disk, physical volume, volume group, logical volume, file system. `lvremove` deletes a logical volume.","id":"rh.i.08.2"},
        {"t":"choice","topic":"RedHat","q":"Asha needs 2 GB more on `/dev/data_vg/data_lv`, including its file system. Which command does it?","a":"`sudo lvextend -r -L +2G /dev/data_vg/data_lv`","w":["`sudo lvextend -L +2G /dev/data_vg/data_lv`","`sudo lvcreate -L +2G /dev/data_vg/data_lv`","`sudo pvcreate -L +2G /dev/data_vg/data_lv`"],"why":"`-r` also resizes the file system. Without it, the volume grows but the file system stays the old size.","id":"rh.i.08.3"},
        {"t":"choice","topic":"RedHat","q":"Which is the safest way to name a disk in `/etc/fstab`?","a":"By UUID","w":["By /dev/sdb only","By the size of the disk","By the user name"],"why":"Names like `/dev/sdb` can change after a reboot. The UUID of a file system stays the same. See it with `lsblk --fs`.","id":"rh.i.08.4"},
        {"t":"bug","topic":"RedHat","q":"Ravi's volume group command fails because the arguments are in the wrong order. Which line is wrong?","code":["sudo pvcreate /dev/sdb","sudo vgcreate /dev/sdb data_vg","sudo lvcreate -L 5G -n data_lv data_vg"],"lang":"shell","bad":1,"why":"`vgcreate` takes the new group name first, then the physical volumes.","fix":"sudo vgcreate data_vg /dev/sdb","id":"rh.i.08.5"},
        {"t":"choice","topic":"RedHat","q":"Which command lists disks and partitions as a tree?","a":"`lsblk`","w":["`lvcreate`","`df -h`","`mkfs.xfs`"],"why":"`lsblk` shows block devices. `df -h` shows used and free space of mounted file systems.","id":"rh.i.08.6"},
        {"t":"choice","topic":"RedHat","q":"Ravi wants to make an XFS file system 2 GB smaller. What is true?","a":"XFS cannot shrink. Back up, make a smaller one, then restore","w":["`sudo lvreduce -r -L -2G` shrinks it safely","Shrink the logical volume first and XFS adjusts by itself","`xfs_growfs` with a minus size shrinks it"],"why":"XFS can only grow (`lvextend -r` or `xfs_growfs`). Shrinking the volume under it destroys data, so plan sizes carefully.","id":"rh.i.08.7"},
        {"t":"check","topic":"RedHat","q":"What is the correct order of LVM layers, from disk upwards?","a":"Physical volume, volume group, logical volume","w":["Logical volume, volume group, physical volume","Volume group, physical volume, logical volume","Physical volume, logical volume, volume group"],"why":"Disks become physical volumes, they join a volume group, and logical volumes are cut from the group.","id":"rh.i.08.8"},
        {"t":"check","topic":"RedHat","q":"Which file system is the default on RHEL?","a":"XFS","w":["NTFS","FAT32","ISO9660"],"why":"RHEL uses XFS by default. You create it with `mkfs.xfs`.","id":"rh.i.08.9"}
      ] },
      { id: "rh.i.09", level: "i", n: 9, title: "Logs with journalctl", cards: [
        {"t":"learn","topic":"RedHat","title":"Logs with journalctl","body":"**systemd** keeps logs in the **journal**, and `journalctl` reads it. Filter by service with `-u`, follow live with `-f`, and show only errors with `-p err`. Use `-b` for the current boot.","code":"journalctl -u sshd\njournalctl -f\njournalctl -p err -b","lang":"shell","id":"rh.i.09.1"},
        {"t":"choice","topic":"RedHat","q":"Asha wants only the log lines of the SSH service. Which command?","a":"`journalctl -u sshd`","w":["`journalctl -s sshd`","`journalctl -f sshd`","`journalctl sshd -p`"],"why":"`-u` means unit. `-f` follows new lines and does not select a service.","id":"rh.i.09.2"},
        {"t":"choice","topic":"RedHat","q":"Which option keeps printing new log lines as they arrive?","a":"`-f`","w":["`-b`","`-p`","`-u`"],"why":"`-f` follows the journal like `tail -f`. Press Ctrl+C to stop.","id":"rh.i.09.3"},
        {"t":"choice","topic":"RedHat","q":"Ravi wants only error messages (and worse) from this boot. Which command?","a":"`journalctl -p err -b`","w":["`journalctl -u err -b`","`journalctl -f err`","`journalctl --boot=error`"],"why":"`-p err` filters by priority and `-b` limits to the current boot.","id":"rh.i.09.4"},
        {"t":"bug","topic":"RedHat","q":"This command fails because the time text has spaces. Which line is wrong?","code":["journalctl -u sshd","journalctl -p err -b","journalctl --since 1 hour ago"],"lang":"shell","bad":2,"why":"A value with spaces needs quotes so the shell treats it as one argument.","fix":"journalctl --since \"1 hour ago\"","id":"rh.i.09.5"},
        {"t":"choice","topic":"RedHat","q":"By default the journal may be lost at reboot. Which directory makes it persistent?","a":"`/var/log/journal`","w":["`/etc/journal`","`/tmp/journal`","`/home/journal`"],"why":"If `/var/log/journal` exists, systemd-journald keeps logs on disk across reboots.","id":"rh.i.09.6"},
        {"t":"choice","topic":"RedHat","q":"Which classic log file is also written by rsyslog on RHEL?","a":"`/var/log/messages`","w":["`/var/log/syslog`","`/etc/messages`","`/usr/log/journal`"],"why":"rsyslog writes text logs such as `/var/log/messages` next to the journal. `/var/log/syslog` is the name on Ubuntu, not RHEL.","id":"rh.i.09.7"},
        {"t":"check","topic":"RedHat","q":"Which command shows logs of the `httpd` service from the current boot?","a":"`journalctl -u httpd -b`","w":["`journalctl -p httpd -b`","`journalctl -f -u`","`journalctl httpd --boot-u`"],"why":"`-u` picks the unit and `-b` limits to this boot.","id":"rh.i.09.8"},
        {"t":"check","topic":"RedHat","q":"Which tool reads the systemd journal?","a":"`journalctl`","w":["`systemctl`","`firewall-cmd`","`restorecon`"],"why":"`systemctl` controls services. `journalctl` reads their logs.","id":"rh.i.09.9"}
      ] },
      { id: "rh.i.10", level: "i", n: 10, title: "Podman containers", cards: [
        {"t":"learn","topic":"RedHat","title":"Containers with Podman","body":"**Podman** runs containers without a background daemon, and it works for normal users (**rootless**). Its commands are like Docker's. Red Hat's **UBI** images come from `registry.access.redhat.com`.","code":"podman pull registry.access.redhat.com/ubi9/httpd-24\npodman run -d --name web -p 8080:8080 registry.access.redhat.com/ubi9/httpd-24\npodman ps","lang":"shell","id":"rh.i.10.1"},
        {"t":"pipe","topic":"RedHat","q":"Run a web container and read its log. Drop the block that does not belong.","steps":[["PULL","podman pull"],["RUN","podman run -d"],["PS","podman ps"],["LOGS","podman logs"]],"extra":[["RMI","podman rmi"]],"why":"Pull the image, run it, check it is running, then read logs. `podman rmi` removes an image.","id":"rh.i.10.2"},
        {"t":"choice","topic":"RedHat","q":"In `-p 8080:80`, what does 8080 mean?","a":"The port on the host machine","w":["The port inside the container","The number of containers","The process ID"],"why":"The format is `host_port:container_port`. Visit the host on 8080 to reach port 80 in the container.","id":"rh.i.10.3"},
        {"t":"choice","topic":"RedHat","q":"What does \"daemonless\" mean for Podman?","a":"No always-running background service is needed to run containers","w":["Containers cannot run in the background","It cannot use images","Only root can use it"],"why":"Podman starts containers as normal processes. The `-d` flag still runs a container in the background.","id":"rh.i.10.4"},
        {"t":"bug","topic":"RedHat","q":"Ravi's container is named `web`. The last line fails. Which line is wrong?","code":["podman pull registry.access.redhat.com/ubi9/httpd-24","podman run -d --name web -p 8080:8080 registry.access.redhat.com/ubi9/httpd-24","podman logs -f"],"lang":"shell","bad":2,"why":"`podman logs` needs the container name or ID.","fix":"podman logs web","id":"rh.i.10.5"},
        {"t":"choice","topic":"RedHat","q":"Which command lists the images stored on this machine?","a":"`podman images`","w":["`podman ps`","`podman pull`","`podman stop`"],"why":"`podman ps` lists containers, not images. Add `-a` to include stopped containers.","id":"rh.i.10.6"},
        {"t":"choice","topic":"RedHat","q":"Where does a rootless user put a Quadlet `.container` file to run a container as a systemd service?","a":"`~/.config/containers/systemd/`","w":["`/etc/systemd/system/`","`~/.local/share/containers/storage/`","`/var/lib/containers/`"],"why":"Quadlet reads `.container` files from that folder. After `systemctl --user daemon-reload` it creates a normal systemd service.","id":"rh.i.10.7"},
        {"t":"check","topic":"RedHat","q":"Which command stops a container named `web`?","a":"`podman stop web`","w":["`podman ps web`","`podman rmi web`","`podman pull web`"],"why":"`rmi` removes images and `ps` only lists containers. Stop unused containers to save resources.","id":"rh.i.10.8"},
        {"t":"check","topic":"RedHat","q":"Docker users move to Podman. What is true?","a":"Most commands, like `run` and `ps`, work the same way","w":["No command is the same","Podman needs a paid license to run","Podman cannot run Linux containers"],"why":"Podman is Docker-compatible for common commands, so `podman run` replaces `docker run`.","id":"rh.i.10.9"}
      ] },
      { id: "rh.i.11", level: "i", n: 11, title: "OpenShift projects and the oc CLI", cards: [
        {"t":"learn","topic":"RedHat","title":"OpenShift projects and oc","body":"You know `oc login`, `oc new-project` and `oc get pods`. Now: `oc whoami` shows your user, `oc project` switches project, `oc logs` shows a pod's output, and `oc describe` shows its details and events. The **web console** shows the same things. `oc` does every `kubectl` command and adds OpenShift ones.","code":"oc whoami\noc project shop\noc get pods\noc logs pod-name\noc describe pod pod-name","lang":"shell","id":"rh.i.11.1"},
        {"t":"choice","topic":"RedHat","q":"Lakshmi is not sure which user she is logged in as. Which command tells her?","a":"`oc whoami`","w":["`oc project`","`oc login --show`","`oc describe me`"],"why":"`oc whoami` prints your user name. `oc project` shows the current project, not the user.","id":"rh.i.11.2"},
        {"t":"choice","topic":"RedHat","q":"Which command switches to the project `shop`?","a":"`oc project shop`","w":["`oc login shop`","`oc logs shop`","`oc describe shop`"],"why":"`oc project` changes the current project. Without a name it shows the current one.","id":"rh.i.11.3"},
        {"t":"choice","topic":"RedHat","q":"Ravi's pod keeps failing. Which command shows its output?","a":"`oc logs pod-name`","w":["`oc get logs`","`oc new-app pod-name`","`oc project pod-name`"],"why":"`oc logs` prints container output. `oc describe pod` shows events and settings.","id":"rh.i.11.4"},
        {"t":"bug","topic":"RedHat","q":"Lakshmi wants to list pods, but the last line fails. Which line is wrong?","code":["oc login -u developer https://api.example.com:6443","oc new-project demo","oc get pods -n"],"lang":"shell","bad":2,"why":"`-n` needs a project name after it. Since `demo` is the current project, plain `oc get pods` works.","fix":"oc get pods","id":"rh.i.11.5"},
        {"t":"order","topic":"RedHat","q":"Ravi's pod in project `shop` is failing. Put the steps in order to read its output.","items":["`oc login`","`oc project shop`","`oc get pods`","`oc logs pod-name`"],"why":"Log in, switch to the project, find the pod name, then read its logs.","id":"rh.i.11.6"},
        {"t":"choice","topic":"RedHat","q":"What is the main difference between `oc` and `kubectl`?","a":"`oc` has extra OpenShift commands such as `new-project` and `new-app`","w":["`oc` cannot list pods","`oc` cannot apply YAML files","`kubectl` cannot connect to OpenShift at all"],"why":"`oc` includes the Kubernetes commands plus OpenShift ones like `login` and `new-app`. `kubectl` also works on OpenShift, without the extras.","id":"rh.i.11.7"},
        {"t":"check","topic":"RedHat","q":"Which is true about the OpenShift web console?","a":"It shows the same projects, pods and logs that `oc` shows","w":["Only the cluster admin can open it","It cannot show pod logs","You can use it without logging in"],"why":"The web console and `oc` talk to the same cluster. Use whichever is easier, but you always log in first.","id":"rh.i.11.8"},
        {"t":"check","topic":"RedHat","q":"Which command shows details and events of one pod?","a":"`oc describe pod pod-name`","w":["`oc project pod-name`","`oc login pod-name`","`oc new-project pod-name`"],"why":"`describe` shows settings and events, handy when a pod will not start.","id":"rh.i.11.9"}
      ] },
      { id: "rh.i.12", level: "i", n: 12, title: "Deploying an app on OpenShift", cards: [
        {"t":"learn","topic":"RedHat","title":"Deploy an app on OpenShift","body":"`oc new-app` deploys an app from an image, or builds one from Git source code with **Source-to-Image (S2I)**. It creates a Deployment and a Service (plus a build for Git source). Then `oc expose service/web` creates a **Route** so people can reach it.","code":"oc new-app --name=web https://github.com/sclorg/cakephp-ex\noc expose service/web\noc get route\noc scale deployment/web --replicas=3","lang":"shell","id":"rh.i.12.1"},
        {"t":"choice","topic":"RedHat","q":"Which command makes the service `web` reachable from outside with a Route?","a":"`oc expose service/web`","w":["`oc scale service/web`","`oc login service/web`","`oc logs service/web`"],"why":"`oc expose service` creates a Route that gives the app a public host name.","id":"rh.i.12.2"},
        {"t":"choice","topic":"RedHat","q":"Asha wants 3 copies of the app. Which command?","a":"`oc scale deployment/web --replicas=3`","w":["`oc expose deployment/web --replicas=3`","`oc get deployment/web --copies=3`","`oc new-app web --scale 3`"],"why":"`oc scale` changes the number of replicas. More pods share the traffic.","id":"rh.i.12.3"},
        {"t":"bug","topic":"RedHat","q":"The app is deployed, but the last line fails. Which line is wrong?","code":["oc new-app --name=web https://github.com/sclorg/cakephp-ex","oc get pods","oc expose web"],"lang":"shell","bad":2,"why":"`oc expose` needs the resource type and name.","fix":"oc expose service/web","id":"rh.i.12.4"},
        {"t":"indent","topic":"RedHat","step":2,"q":"Fix the indentation of the top part of this Deployment.","lang":"k8s","lines":[[0,"apiVersion: apps/v1"],[0,"kind: Deployment"],[0,"metadata:"],[2,"name: web"],[0,"spec:"],[2,"replicas: 2"],[2,"selector:"],[4,"matchLabels:"],[6,"app: web"]],"why":"Child keys are indented two spaces more than their parent. `app: web` sits under `matchLabels`.","id":"rh.i.12.5"},
        {"t":"choice","topic":"RedHat","q":"Containers on OpenShift usually start as which kind of user?","a":"A non-root user ID that OpenShift picks for the project","w":["Always root","Always the person who logged in","No user at all"],"why":"The default `restricted-v2` SCC (security context constraint) runs pods with a user ID from the project's range, never root. So images must not need root.","id":"rh.i.12.6"},
        {"t":"order","topic":"RedHat","q":"Put these steps in order to publish an app.","items":["`oc new-project demo`","`oc new-app --name=web <git-url>`","`oc expose service/web`","`oc get route`"],"why":"Make the project, deploy the app, expose it, then read the Route host name.","id":"rh.i.12.7"},
        {"t":"check","topic":"RedHat","q":"`oc expose service/web` creates a Route. Where does the Route send traffic?","a":"To the Service `web`, which sends it to the pods","w":["Straight to the container image","To the BuildConfig","To the project's quota"],"why":"A Route maps a host name to a Service. The Service then spreads traffic across the matching pods.","id":"rh.i.12.8"},
        {"t":"check","topic":"RedHat","q":"What does Source-to-Image (S2I) do?","a":"Builds a container image from your source code","w":["Turns an image into a text file","Opens the firewall","Creates Linux users"],"why":"S2I combines your code with a builder image to produce a ready container image.","id":"rh.i.12.9"}
      ] },
      { id: "rh.a.13", level: "a", n: 13, title: "Ansible for RHEL", cards: [] },
      { id: "rh.a.14", level: "a", n: 14, title: "OpenShift routes and security", cards: [] },
      { id: "rh.a.15", level: "a", n: 15, title: "RHCSA-style practice", cards: [] }
    ] },
    { id: "sql", name: "SQL", topic: "SQL", blurb: "Ask questions of data: filter, sort, join and summarise.", units: [
      { id: "sql.b.01", level: "b", n: 1, title: "SELECT and FROM", cards: [
        {"t":"learn","topic":"SQL","title":"SELECT and FROM","body":"`SELECT` says **which columns** you want. `FROM` says **which table** to read. Use `SELECT *` to get **all columns**. `DISTINCT` removes repeated rows, and `AS` gives a column a new name in the result.","code":"SELECT name, city\nFROM students;\n\nSELECT DISTINCT city\nFROM students;","lang":"sql","id":"sql.b.01.1"},
        {"t":"output","topic":"SQL","code":"-- students: (Asha, Guntur), (Ravi, Pune), (Lakshmi, Guntur)\nSELECT name\nFROM students;","lang":"sql","a":"Asha, Ravi, Lakshmi","w":["Guntur, Pune, Guntur","Asha","name, name, name"],"why":"SELECT name returns the name column for every row. It does not filter any rows.","id":"sql.b.01.2"},
        {"t":"output","topic":"SQL","code":"-- students: (Asha, Guntur), (Ravi, Pune), (Lakshmi, Guntur)\nSELECT DISTINCT city\nFROM students;","lang":"sql","a":"Guntur, Pune","w":["Guntur, Pune, Guntur","Guntur","Asha, Ravi, Lakshmi"],"why":"DISTINCT shows each different value only once. Guntur is in two rows but appears once.","id":"sql.b.01.3"},
        {"t":"choice","topic":"SQL","q":"What does SELECT * mean?","a":"Show all columns of the table","w":["Show only the first column","Show only the first row","Count all the rows"],"why":"The star * means every column. It does not limit the rows.","id":"sql.b.01.4"},
        {"t":"bug","topic":"SQL","q":"Asha wants the name and the city columns. Which line is wrong?","code":["SELECT name city","FROM students;"],"bad":0,"why":"Columns must be separated by a comma: `SELECT name, city`. Without the comma, city is read as a new name for the name column.","id":"sql.b.01.5"},
        {"t":"choice","topic":"SQL","q":"What does AS do in `SELECT amount AS price FROM orders`?","a":"Gives the amount column a new name, price, in the result","w":["Changes the column name inside the table forever","Keeps only rows where amount is a price","Adds a new column called AS"],"why":"AS makes an alias. It changes the name only in the result, not in the table.","id":"sql.b.01.6"},
        {"t":"check","topic":"SQL","q":"A table has one row: Asha, Pune. What does this query return?","code":"-- students has columns name, city\n-- one row: (Asha, Pune)\nSELECT *\nFROM students;","lang":"sql","a":"Asha | Pune","w":["Asha","Pune","name | city"],"why":"The star * returns all columns, so you see both values in the row.","id":"sql.b.01.7"},
        {"t":"check","topic":"SQL","q":"Which query shows each city only once from the table students?","a":"SELECT DISTINCT city FROM students;","w":["SELECT city DISTINCT FROM students;","SELECT city FROM DISTINCT students;","SELECT ONLY city FROM students;"],"why":"DISTINCT goes right after SELECT, before the column name.","id":"sql.b.01.8"}
      ] },
      { id: "sql.b.02", level: "b", n: 2, title: "WHERE", cards: [
        {"t":"learn","topic":"SQL","title":"Filtering rows","body":"`WHERE` keeps only the rows that match a condition. Combine conditions with `AND` and `OR`.","code":"SELECT name, amount\nFROM orders\nWHERE amount > 500 AND city = 'Guntur';","lang":"sql","id":"sql.b.02.1"},
        {"t":"choice","topic":"SQL","q":"Which SQL keyword keeps only the rows that match a condition?","a":"WHERE","w":["ORDER BY","SELECT","GROUP BY"],"why":"`WHERE` filters rows, e.g. `SELECT * FROM orders WHERE amount > 500`. `ORDER BY` sorts, `GROUP BY` makes groups.","id":"sql.b.02.2"},
        {"t":"output","topic":"SQL","code":"-- students: (Asha, Guntur, 80), (Ravi, Pune, 55), (Lakshmi, Guntur, 92)\nSELECT name\nFROM students\nWHERE marks > 60;","lang":"sql","a":"Asha, Lakshmi","w":["Ravi","Asha, Ravi, Lakshmi","80, 92"],"why":"Only Asha (80) and Lakshmi (92) have marks above 60. Ravi has 55, so his row is dropped.","id":"sql.b.02.3"},
        {"t":"output","topic":"SQL","code":"-- students: (Asha, Guntur, 80), (Ravi, Pune, 55), (Lakshmi, Guntur, 92)\nSELECT name\nFROM students\nWHERE city = 'Guntur'\n  AND marks > 85;","lang":"sql","a":"Lakshmi","w":["Asha, Lakshmi","Asha","Ravi"],"why":"AND needs both conditions true. Asha is in Guntur but has 80, so only Lakshmi passes.","id":"sql.b.02.4"},
        {"t":"output","topic":"SQL","code":"-- students: (Asha, Guntur, 80), (Ravi, Pune, 55), (Lakshmi, Guntur, 92)\nSELECT name\nFROM students\nWHERE city = 'Pune'\n   OR marks > 90;","lang":"sql","a":"Ravi, Lakshmi","w":["Ravi","Lakshmi","Asha, Ravi, Lakshmi"],"why":"OR needs only one condition true. Ravi is in Pune, and Lakshmi has marks above 90.","id":"sql.b.02.5"},
        {"t":"bug","topic":"SQL","q":"This query should show students from Guntur. Which line is wrong?","code":["SELECT name","FROM students","WHERE city = Guntur;"],"bad":2,"why":"Text values need single quotes: `WHERE city = 'Guntur'`. Without quotes, SQL looks for a column called Guntur.","id":"sql.b.02.6"},
        {"t":"check","topic":"SQL","q":"Which order ids does this query return?","code":"-- orders: (1, 500), (2, 1200), (3, 800)\nSELECT id\nFROM orders\nWHERE amount > 700\n  AND amount < 1000;","lang":"sql","a":"3","w":["2","2, 3","1, 3"],"why":"AND needs amount above 700 and below 1000. Only 800 fits; 500 is too small and 1200 is too big.","id":"sql.b.02.7"},
        {"t":"check","topic":"SQL","q":"Which WHERE line keeps only the students from Pune?","a":"WHERE city = 'Pune'","w":["WHERE city = Pune","WHERE city := 'Pune'","IF city = 'Pune'"],"why":"Use WHERE with a single = sign and the text in single quotes.","id":"sql.b.02.8"}
      ] },
      { id: "sql.b.03", level: "b", n: 3, title: "ORDER BY", cards: [
        {"t":"learn","topic":"SQL","title":"Sorting results","body":"`ORDER BY amount DESC` sorts big to small; `ASC` (the default) sorts small to big. The written order is always `SELECT`, `FROM`, `WHERE`, `ORDER BY`.","id":"sql.b.03.1"},
        {"t":"choice","topic":"SQL","q":"Which line sorts orders from the highest amount to the lowest?","a":"ORDER BY amount DESC","w":["ORDER BY amount ASC","SORT amount DOWN","GROUP BY amount"],"why":"`ORDER BY` sorts. `DESC` means descending (big to small); `ASC`, the default, is small to big.","id":"sql.b.03.2"},
        {"t":"order","topic":"SQL","q":"Order the parts of this SQL query.","items":["SELECT name, amount","FROM orders","WHERE amount > 500","ORDER BY amount DESC"],"why":"The written order is always `SELECT` → `FROM` → `WHERE` → `ORDER BY`. (`GROUP BY` would go between `WHERE` and `ORDER BY`.)","id":"sql.b.03.3"},
        {"t":"output","topic":"SQL","code":"-- students: (Asha, Guntur, 80), (Ravi, Pune, 55), (Lakshmi, Guntur, 92)\nSELECT name\nFROM students\nORDER BY marks DESC;","lang":"sql","a":"Lakshmi, Asha, Ravi","w":["Ravi, Asha, Lakshmi","Asha, Ravi, Lakshmi","Lakshmi, Ravi, Asha"],"why":"DESC sorts from the biggest marks to the smallest: 92, 80, 55.","id":"sql.b.03.4"},
        {"t":"output","topic":"SQL","code":"-- students: (Ravi, Pune), (Lakshmi, Guntur), (Asha, Guntur)\nSELECT name\nFROM students\nORDER BY name;","lang":"sql","a":"Asha, Lakshmi, Ravi","w":["Ravi, Lakshmi, Asha","Ravi, Asha, Lakshmi","Lakshmi, Asha, Ravi"],"why":"Without ASC or DESC, the sort is ascending. Text is sorted A to Z.","id":"sql.b.03.5"},
        {"t":"pipe","topic":"SQL","q":"Inside, SQL runs a query in a logical order. Build that flow.","steps":[["FROM","Pick the table","src"],["WHER","WHERE keeps matching rows"],["SEL","SELECT picks the columns"],["SORT","ORDER BY sorts the result"]],"extra":[["DROP","Delete the table"]],"why":"SQL first finds the table (FROM), then filters rows (WHERE), then picks columns (SELECT), and sorts last (ORDER BY). This is different from the order you write it.","id":"sql.b.03.6"},
        {"t":"check","topic":"SQL","q":"Which names does this query return, and in which order?","code":"-- students: (Asha, Guntur, 80), (Ravi, Pune, 55), (Lakshmi, Guntur, 92)\nSELECT name\nFROM students\nWHERE marks > 60\nORDER BY marks;","lang":"sql","a":"Asha, Lakshmi","w":["Lakshmi, Asha","Ravi, Asha, Lakshmi","Asha, Ravi, Lakshmi"],"why":"WHERE removes Ravi (55). Then ORDER BY marks sorts small to big: Asha (80), Lakshmi (92).","id":"sql.b.03.7"},
        {"t":"check","topic":"SQL","q":"You write `ORDER BY marks` with no ASC or DESC. How is the result sorted?","a":"Ascending, from the smallest marks to the biggest","w":["Descending, from the biggest marks to the smallest","Not sorted at all","In the order the rows were added"],"why":"ASC is the default sort order. Add DESC if you want the biggest first.","id":"sql.b.03.8"}
      ] },
      { id: "sql.b.04", level: "b", n: 4, title: "COUNT, SUM, AVG", cards: [
        {"t":"learn","topic":"SQL","title":"Summaries","body":"`COUNT(*)` counts rows, `SUM(col)` adds up, `AVG(col)` averages, and `MIN` / `MAX` find the smallest and biggest.","code":"SELECT COUNT(*), SUM(amount), AVG(amount)\nFROM orders;","lang":"sql","id":"sql.b.04.1"},
        {"t":"choice","topic":"SQL","q":"Which query counts the rows in a table called orders?","a":"SELECT COUNT(*) FROM orders;","w":["SELECT SUM(orders);","COUNT orders;","SELECT * FROM COUNT(orders);"],"why":"`COUNT(*)` counts rows. The pattern is always `SELECT <what> FROM <table>`.","id":"sql.b.04.2"},
        {"t":"output","topic":"SQL","code":"-- orders: (Guntur, 500), (Guntur, 1200), (Pune, 800), (Pune, 1500)\nSELECT SUM(amount)\nFROM orders;","lang":"sql","a":"4000","w":["4","1000","1500"],"why":"SUM adds all the amounts: 500 + 1200 + 800 + 1500 = 4000.","id":"sql.b.04.3"},
        {"t":"output","topic":"SQL","code":"-- orders: (Guntur, 500), (Guntur, 1200), (Pune, 800), (Pune, 1500)\nSELECT AVG(amount)\nFROM orders;","lang":"sql","a":"1000","w":["4000","800","1500"],"why":"AVG is the sum divided by the number of rows: 4000 / 4 = 1000.","id":"sql.b.04.4"},
        {"t":"output","topic":"SQL","code":"-- orders: (Guntur, 500), (Guntur, 1200), (Pune, 800), (Pune, 1500)\nSELECT COUNT(*)\nFROM orders\nWHERE city = 'Pune';","lang":"sql","a":"2","w":["4","2300","1"],"why":"WHERE keeps the 2 Pune rows first. Then COUNT(*) counts them.","id":"sql.b.04.5"},
        {"t":"output","topic":"SQL","code":"-- orders: (Guntur, 500), (Guntur, 1200), (Pune, 800), (Pune, 1500)\nSELECT MAX(amount)\nFROM orders;","lang":"sql","a":"1500","w":["500","4000","4"],"why":"MAX returns the biggest value in the column. MIN would return 500.","id":"sql.b.04.6"},
        {"t":"check","topic":"SQL","q":"What does this query return?","code":"-- orders: (Guntur, 500), (Guntur, 1200), (Pune, 800), (Pune, 1500)\nSELECT MIN(amount)\nFROM orders\nWHERE city = 'Guntur';","lang":"sql","a":"500","w":["800","1200","1700"],"why":"WHERE keeps the Guntur rows (500 and 1200). MIN picks the smaller one, 500.","id":"sql.b.04.7"},
        {"t":"check","topic":"SQL","q":"How many orders are 800 or more?","code":"-- orders: (Guntur, 500), (Guntur, 1200), (Pune, 800), (Pune, 1500)\nSELECT COUNT(*)\nFROM orders\nWHERE amount >= 800;","lang":"sql","a":"3","w":["2","4","3500"],"why":"The amounts 1200, 800 and 1500 are all 800 or more. COUNT(*) counts these 3 rows.","id":"sql.b.04.8"}
      ] },
      { id: "sql.i.05", level: "i", n: 5, title: "GROUP BY and HAVING", cards: [
        {"t":"learn","topic":"SQL","title":"GROUP BY and HAVING","body":"**GROUP BY** puts rows with the same value into one group, so you can count or add each group. **HAVING** filters the groups after they are made. **WHERE** filters single rows before grouping.","code":"SELECT city, SUM(amount)\nFROM sales\nGROUP BY city\nHAVING SUM(amount) > 500;","lang":"sql","id":"sql.i.05.1"},
        {"t":"output","topic":"SQL","code":"-- sales: (Guntur,500) (Pune,300) (Guntur,200)\n--        (Pune,400) (Pune,100) (Delhi,150)\nSELECT city, COUNT(*)\nFROM sales\nGROUP BY city\nORDER BY city;","lang":"sql","a":"Delhi | 1\nGuntur | 2\nPune | 3","w":["Delhi | 150\nGuntur | 700\nPune | 800","Guntur | 2\nPune | 3","6"],"why":"`COUNT(*)` counts the rows in each group: Delhi 1, Guntur 2, Pune 3.","id":"sql.i.05.2"},
        {"t":"output","topic":"SQL","code":"-- sales: (Guntur,500) (Pune,300) (Guntur,200)\n--        (Pune,400) (Pune,100) (Delhi,150)\nSELECT city, SUM(amount)\nFROM sales\nGROUP BY city\nORDER BY city;","lang":"sql","a":"Delhi | 150\nGuntur | 700\nPune | 800","w":["Delhi | 1\nGuntur | 2\nPune | 3","Guntur | 700\nPune | 800","Delhi | 150\nGuntur | 500\nPune | 400"],"why":"`SUM(amount)` adds the amounts inside each city: Guntur 500 + 200 = 700, Pune 300 + 400 + 100 = 800.","id":"sql.i.05.3"},
        {"t":"output","topic":"SQL","code":"-- sales: (Guntur,500) (Pune,300) (Guntur,200)\n--        (Pune,400) (Pune,100) (Delhi,150)\nSELECT city, SUM(amount)\nFROM sales\nGROUP BY city\nHAVING SUM(amount) > 500\nORDER BY city;","lang":"sql","a":"Guntur | 700\nPune | 800","w":["Delhi | 150\nGuntur | 700\nPune | 800","Pune | 800","Guntur | 500\nPune | 400"],"why":"`HAVING` keeps only the groups whose total is above 500. Delhi has only 150, so it is removed.","id":"sql.i.05.4"},
        {"t":"choice","topic":"SQL","q":"What is the difference between `WHERE` and `HAVING`?","a":"`WHERE` filters rows before grouping; `HAVING` filters groups after grouping","w":["`WHERE` filters groups; `HAVING` filters rows","They are the same; you can use either","`HAVING` sorts the result; `WHERE` does not"],"why":"Use `WHERE` for single rows. Use `HAVING` when the filter needs `SUM`, `COUNT` or another total.","id":"sql.i.05.5"},
        {"t":"bug","topic":"SQL","q":"Asha wants the cities that have more than 1 sale. The query gives an error. Which line is wrong?","code":["SELECT city, COUNT(*)","FROM sales","WHERE COUNT(*) > 1","GROUP BY city;"],"bad":2,"why":"`WHERE` runs before grouping, so it cannot use `COUNT(*)`. Use `HAVING COUNT(*) > 1` after `GROUP BY`.","id":"sql.i.05.6"},
        {"t":"order","topic":"SQL","q":"Order the clauses in a grouped query.","items":["SELECT","FROM","GROUP BY","HAVING","ORDER BY"],"why":"SQL needs the clauses in this order: `SELECT`, `FROM`, `GROUP BY`, `HAVING`, `ORDER BY`.","id":"sql.i.05.7"},
        {"t":"check","topic":"SQL","q":"What does this query return?","code":"-- sales: (Guntur,500) (Pune,300) (Guntur,200)\n--        (Pune,400) (Pune,100) (Delhi,150)\nSELECT city\nFROM sales\nGROUP BY city\nHAVING COUNT(*) >= 2\nORDER BY city;","lang":"sql","a":"Guntur and Pune","w":["Delhi, Guntur and Pune","Pune only","Delhi only"],"why":"Guntur has 2 sales and Pune has 3. Delhi has only 1, so `HAVING` removes it.","id":"sql.i.05.8"},
        {"t":"check","topic":"SQL","q":"How many rows does this query return?","code":"-- sales has 6 rows in 3 cities\nSELECT city, SUM(amount)\nFROM sales\nGROUP BY city;","lang":"sql","a":"3","w":["6","1","2"],"why":"`GROUP BY` gives one row for each different city. There are 3 cities.","id":"sql.i.05.9"}
      ] },
      { id: "sql.i.06", level: "i", n: 6, title: "JOINs", cards: [
        {"t":"learn","topic":"SQL","title":"JOIN two tables","body":"A **JOIN** puts rows from two tables side by side, using a matching column in `ON`. `INNER JOIN` keeps only the rows that match in both tables. `LEFT JOIN` keeps every row of the left table, and fills the missing right side with `NULL`.","code":"SELECT c.name, o.amount\nFROM customers c\nLEFT JOIN orders o\n  ON c.id = o.customer_id;","lang":"sql","id":"sql.i.06.1"},
        {"t":"output","topic":"SQL","code":"-- customers: (1,Asha) (2,Ravi) (3,Lakshmi)\n-- orders(id, customer_id, amount):\n--   (101,1,500) (102,1,200) (103,2,300)\nSELECT c.name, o.amount\nFROM customers c\nINNER JOIN orders o ON c.id = o.customer_id\nORDER BY o.id;","lang":"sql","a":"Asha | 500\nAsha | 200\nRavi | 300","w":["Asha | 500\nAsha | 200\nRavi | 300\nLakshmi | NULL","Asha | 700\nRavi | 300","Asha | 500\nRavi | 300\nLakshmi | 0"],"why":"`INNER JOIN` keeps only customers who have orders. Lakshmi has none, so she is not in the result.","id":"sql.i.06.2"},
        {"t":"output","topic":"SQL","code":"-- customers: (1,Asha) (2,Ravi) (3,Lakshmi)\n-- orders(id, customer_id, amount):\n--   (101,1,500) (102,1,200) (103,2,300)\nSELECT c.name, o.amount\nFROM customers c\nLEFT JOIN orders o ON c.id = o.customer_id\nORDER BY c.id, o.id;","lang":"sql","a":"Asha | 500\nAsha | 200\nRavi | 300\nLakshmi | NULL","w":["Asha | 500\nAsha | 200\nRavi | 300","Asha | 500\nRavi | 300\nLakshmi | NULL","Asha | 700\nRavi | 300\nLakshmi | 0"],"why":"`LEFT JOIN` keeps every customer. Lakshmi has no order, so `amount` is `NULL`.","id":"sql.i.06.3"},
        {"t":"choice","topic":"SQL","q":"What does the `ON` part of a JOIN do?","a":"It says which column of one table matches which column of the other","w":["It sorts the joined rows","It chooses which table to delete","It counts the matching rows"],"why":"`ON c.id = o.customer_id` tells SQL how to pair each customer with their orders.","id":"sql.i.06.4"},
        {"t":"choice","topic":"SQL","q":"Which join keeps all rows from the left table, even when there is no match?","a":"LEFT JOIN","w":["INNER JOIN","CROSS JOIN","SELF JOIN"],"why":"`LEFT JOIN` never drops a left row. `INNER JOIN` drops rows without a match.","id":"sql.i.06.5"},
        {"t":"bug","topic":"SQL","q":"Ravi wants each customer with their order amounts. One line joins the wrong columns. Which one?","code":["SELECT c.name, o.amount","FROM customers c","JOIN orders o ON c.id = o.id;"],"bad":2,"why":"`o.id` is the order number. The customer is stored in `o.customer_id`, so use `ON c.id = o.customer_id`.","id":"sql.i.06.6"},
        {"t":"output","topic":"SQL","code":"-- customers: (1,Asha) (2,Ravi) (3,Lakshmi)\n-- orders(id, customer_id, amount):\n--   (101,1,500) (102,1,200) (103,2,300)\nSELECT c.name, SUM(o.amount)\nFROM customers c\nJOIN orders o ON c.id = o.customer_id\nGROUP BY c.name\nORDER BY c.name;","lang":"sql","a":"Asha | 700\nRavi | 300","w":["Asha | 500\nRavi | 300","Asha | 700\nLakshmi | 0\nRavi | 300","Asha | 1000"],"why":"The join pairs orders with customers, then `GROUP BY` adds each customer's amounts: Asha 500 + 200 = 700.","id":"sql.i.06.7"},
        {"t":"check","topic":"SQL","q":"What does this query return?","code":"-- customers: (1,Asha) (2,Ravi) (3,Lakshmi)\n-- orders(id, customer_id, amount):\n--   (101,1,500) (102,1,200) (103,2,300)\nSELECT c.name\nFROM customers c\nLEFT JOIN orders o ON c.id = o.customer_id\nWHERE o.id IS NULL;","lang":"sql","a":"Lakshmi","w":["Asha","Ravi","Asha, Ravi and Lakshmi"],"why":"After the `LEFT JOIN`, a customer with no order has `NULL` in the order columns. Only Lakshmi is left.","id":"sql.i.06.8"},
        {"t":"check","topic":"SQL","q":"How many rows does this query return?","code":"-- customers has 3 rows, orders has 3 rows\n-- Lakshmi has no orders\nSELECT COUNT(*)\nFROM customers c\nINNER JOIN orders o ON c.id = o.customer_id;","lang":"sql","a":"3","w":["2","4","6"],"why":"Each order matches one customer: 3 orders give 3 joined rows. Lakshmi has no order, so she adds none.","id":"sql.i.06.9"}
      ] },
      { id: "sql.i.07", level: "i", n: 7, title: "Subqueries", cards: [
        {"t":"learn","topic":"SQL","title":"Subqueries","body":"A **subquery** is a query inside another query, written in brackets. A subquery that returns one value can be used in `WHERE` like a number. A subquery that returns a list can be used with `IN`.","code":"SELECT name\nFROM products\nWHERE price > (\n  SELECT AVG(price) FROM products\n);","lang":"sql","id":"sql.i.07.1"},
        {"t":"output","topic":"SQL","code":"-- products: (1,Pen,10) (2,Book,150)\n--           (3,Bag,500) (4,Lamp,300)\nSELECT name\nFROM products\nWHERE price > (SELECT AVG(price) FROM products)\nORDER BY name;","lang":"sql","a":"Bag\nLamp","w":["Book\nPen","Bag","Bag\nBook\nLamp\nPen"],"why":"The average price is (10 + 150 + 500 + 300) / 4 = 240. Only Bag (500) and Lamp (300) are above it.","id":"sql.i.07.2"},
        {"t":"output","topic":"SQL","code":"-- products: (1,Pen,10) (2,Book,150)\n--           (3,Bag,500) (4,Lamp,300)\n-- order_items(product_id): 2, 2, 3\nSELECT name\nFROM products\nWHERE id IN (SELECT product_id FROM order_items)\nORDER BY name;","lang":"sql","a":"Bag\nBook","w":["Book\nBook\nBag","Lamp\nPen","Bag\nBook\nLamp\nPen"],"why":"The subquery gives the list 2, 2, 3. `IN` keeps the products with id 2 or 3: Book and Bag, each once.","id":"sql.i.07.3"},
        {"t":"output","topic":"SQL","code":"-- products: (1,Pen,10) (2,Book,150)\n--           (3,Bag,500) (4,Lamp,300)\n-- order_items(product_id): 2, 2, 3\nSELECT name\nFROM products\nWHERE id NOT IN (SELECT product_id FROM order_items)\nORDER BY name;","lang":"sql","a":"Lamp\nPen","w":["Bag\nBook","Lamp","Book\nBook\nBag"],"why":"`NOT IN` keeps the products that are not in the list 2, 2, 3. Those are Pen (1) and Lamp (4).","id":"sql.i.07.4"},
        {"t":"choice","topic":"SQL","q":"What does a scalar subquery return?","a":"A single value","w":["A whole table","A list of column names","Nothing; it only changes data"],"why":"\"Scalar\" means one value, like the result of `AVG(price)`. You can compare it with `>` or `=`.","id":"sql.i.07.5"},
        {"t":"bug","topic":"SQL","q":"This query fails with a syntax error. Which line is wrong?","code":["SELECT name FROM products","WHERE price > (SELECT AVG(price) FROM products","ORDER BY name;"],"bad":1,"why":"The subquery is missing its closing bracket `)`. Every `(` needs a matching `)`.","id":"sql.i.07.6"},
        {"t":"output","topic":"SQL","code":"-- products: (1,Pen,10) (2,Book,150)\n--           (3,Bag,500) (4,Lamp,300)\nSELECT name\nFROM products\nWHERE price = (SELECT MAX(price) FROM products);","lang":"sql","a":"Bag","w":["Pen","Lamp","Bag\nLamp"],"why":"The subquery finds the highest price, 500. The outer query returns the product with that price: Bag.","id":"sql.i.07.7"},
        {"t":"check","topic":"SQL","q":"How many products does this query return?","code":"-- products: (1,Pen,10) (2,Book,150)\n--           (3,Bag,500) (4,Lamp,300)\nSELECT COUNT(*)\nFROM products\nWHERE price < (SELECT AVG(price) FROM products);","lang":"sql","a":"2","w":["1","3","4"],"why":"The average is 240. Pen (10) and Book (150) are below it, so the count is 2.","id":"sql.i.07.8"},
        {"t":"check","topic":"SQL","q":"What is a subquery?","a":"A query written inside another query, in brackets","w":["A query that runs on a second computer","A query that only deletes rows","A table saved inside another table"],"why":"The inner query runs first, and the outer query uses its result.","id":"sql.i.07.9"}
      ] },
      { id: "sql.i.08", level: "i", n: 8, title: "INSERT, UPDATE, DELETE", cards: [
        {"t":"learn","topic":"SQL","title":"INSERT, UPDATE and DELETE","body":"`INSERT` adds a new row, `UPDATE` changes values in existing rows, and `DELETE` removes rows. Always add a **WHERE** to `UPDATE` and `DELETE`. Without `WHERE`, they change or remove **every** row in the table.","code":"INSERT INTO students VALUES (4, 'Meena', 'Pune');\nUPDATE students SET city = 'Pune' WHERE id = 1;\nDELETE FROM students WHERE id = 2;","lang":"sql","id":"sql.i.08.1"},
        {"t":"output","topic":"SQL","code":"-- students: (1,Asha,Guntur) (2,Ravi,Pune)\n--           (3,Lakshmi,Guntur)\nINSERT INTO students VALUES (4, 'Meena', 'Pune');\nSELECT COUNT(*) FROM students;","lang":"sql","a":"4","w":["3","1","5"],"why":"The table had 3 rows. `INSERT` adds one more row, so there are 4.","id":"sql.i.08.2"},
        {"t":"output","topic":"SQL","code":"-- students: (1,Asha,Guntur) (2,Ravi,Pune)\n--           (3,Lakshmi,Guntur)\nUPDATE students SET city = 'Pune' WHERE id = 1;\nSELECT name FROM students\nWHERE city = 'Pune'\nORDER BY name;","lang":"sql","a":"Asha\nRavi","w":["Ravi","Asha","Asha\nLakshmi\nRavi"],"why":"Only the row with id 1 changes: Asha moves to Pune. Now Asha and Ravi live in Pune.","id":"sql.i.08.3"},
        {"t":"output","topic":"SQL","code":"-- students: (1,Asha,Guntur) (2,Ravi,Pune)\n--           (3,Lakshmi,Guntur)\nDELETE FROM students WHERE city = 'Guntur';\nSELECT name FROM students;","lang":"sql","a":"Ravi","w":["Asha\nLakshmi","Asha\nRavi\nLakshmi","No rows"],"why":"`DELETE ... WHERE city = 'Guntur'` removes Asha and Lakshmi. Only Ravi is left.","id":"sql.i.08.4"},
        {"t":"output","topic":"SQL","code":"-- students: (1,Asha,Guntur) (2,Ravi,Pune)\n--           (3,Lakshmi,Guntur)\nUPDATE students SET city = 'Delhi';\nSELECT DISTINCT city FROM students;","lang":"sql","a":"Delhi","w":["Guntur\nPune","Delhi\nGuntur\nPune","No rows"],"why":"There is no `WHERE`, so `UPDATE` changes the city of every row to Delhi.","id":"sql.i.08.5"},
        {"t":"bug","topic":"SQL","q":"Which line would delete every student, not just Ravi (id 2)?","code":["DELETE FROM students WHERE id = 2;","DELETE FROM students WHERE name = 'Ravi';","DELETE FROM students;"],"bad":2,"why":"A `DELETE` without `WHERE` removes all rows from the table.","id":"sql.i.08.6"},
        {"t":"check","topic":"SQL","q":"What does this query return?","code":"-- students: (1,Asha,Guntur) (2,Ravi,Pune)\n--           (3,Lakshmi,Guntur)\nDELETE FROM students WHERE id > 1;\nSELECT COUNT(*) FROM students;","lang":"sql","a":"1","w":["0","2","3"],"why":"`id > 1` matches Ravi (2) and Lakshmi (3). They are deleted, and only Asha is left.","id":"sql.i.08.7"},
        {"t":"check","topic":"SQL","q":"What is a safe habit before running an `UPDATE` or `DELETE`?","a":"Run a `SELECT` with the same `WHERE` first, to see which rows will change","w":["Remove the `WHERE`, so it runs faster","Run it twice, to be sure","Close the database first"],"why":"A `SELECT` with the same `WHERE` shows exactly the rows you are about to change.","id":"sql.i.08.8"}
      ] },
      { id: "sql.a.09", level: "a", n: 9, title: "Window functions", cards: [
        {"t":"learn","topic":"SQL","title":"Window functions","body":"A **window function** calculates over a set of rows but **keeps every row**, unlike `GROUP BY`. `OVER (...)` defines the window: `PARTITION BY` splits rows into groups and `ORDER BY` sets the order inside each group. Common ones are `ROW_NUMBER`, `RANK`, `SUM() OVER` and `LAG`.","code":"SELECT student, score,\n  RANK() OVER (ORDER BY score DESC) AS pos\nFROM marks;","lang":"sql","id":"sql.a.09.1"},
        {"t":"output","topic":"SQL","q":"Five students, two with the same score. What does this query return?","code":"-- marks: (Asha,A,90) (Ravi,A,80) (Lakshmi,A,80)\n--        (Kiran,B,70) (Sita,B,85)\nSELECT student,\n  RANK() OVER (ORDER BY score DESC) AS r\nFROM marks\nORDER BY r, student;","lang":"sql","a":"Asha | 1\nSita | 2\nLakshmi | 3\nRavi | 3\nKiran | 5","w":["Asha | 1\nSita | 2\nLakshmi | 3\nRavi | 3\nKiran | 4","Asha | 1\nSita | 2\nLakshmi | 3\nRavi | 4\nKiran | 5","Asha | 1\nSita | 2\nLakshmi | 3\nRavi | 3\nKiran | 3"],"why":"`RANK()` gives tied rows the same rank and then **skips** numbers: two students at 3, so the next rank is 5. `DENSE_RANK()` would not skip.","id":"sql.a.09.2"},
        {"t":"output","topic":"SQL","q":"Now with `DENSE_RANK()`. Which row is returned?","code":"-- marks: (Asha,A,90) (Ravi,A,80) (Lakshmi,A,80)\n--        (Kiran,B,70) (Sita,B,85)\nSELECT student,\n  DENSE_RANK() OVER (ORDER BY score DESC) AS r\nFROM marks\nORDER BY r DESC LIMIT 1;","lang":"sql","a":"Kiran | 4","w":["Kiran | 5","Kiran | 3","Kiran | 1"],"why":"`DENSE_RANK()` never skips numbers: 90, 85, 80 (two students), 70 are ranks 1, 2, 3 and 4. `RANK()` would give Kiran 5.","id":"sql.a.09.3"},
        {"t":"output","topic":"SQL","q":"A running total of daily sales. What does the query return?","code":"-- daily: (1,100) (2,50) (3,200)\nSELECT day,\n  SUM(amt) OVER (ORDER BY day) AS run\nFROM daily ORDER BY day;","lang":"sql","a":"1 | 100\n2 | 150\n3 | 350","w":["1 | 100\n2 | 50\n3 | 200","1 | 350\n2 | 350\n3 | 350","1 | 100\n2 | 150\n3 | 200"],"why":"`SUM(...) OVER (ORDER BY day)` adds up all rows from the first day up to the current day: 100, 100 + 50, 150 + 200. Without the `ORDER BY` inside `OVER (...)`, every row would show 350.","id":"sql.a.09.4"},
        {"t":"output","topic":"SQL","q":"Ravi wants the change from the previous day. What does `LAG` give?","code":"-- daily: (1,100) (2,50) (3,200)\nSELECT day,\n  amt - LAG(amt) OVER (ORDER BY day) AS chg\nFROM daily ORDER BY day;","lang":"sql","a":"1 | NULL\n2 | -50\n3 | 150","w":["1 | 0\n2 | -50\n3 | 150","1 | 100\n2 | -50\n3 | 150","1 | -50\n2 | 150\n3 | NULL"],"why":"`LAG(amt)` reads the value from the **previous** row. Day 1 has no previous row, so it is `NULL`, and `100 - NULL` stays `NULL`. `LEAD` looks at the next row instead.","id":"sql.a.09.5"},
        {"t":"bug","topic":"SQL","q":"Asha wants a number for each student inside their class, but SQLite gives a syntax error. Which line is wrong?","code":["SELECT student, class, score,","  ROW_NUMBER() OVER (GROUP BY class ORDER BY score DESC) AS rn","FROM marks;"],"lang":"sql","bad":1,"why":"Inside `OVER (...)` the split is written with `PARTITION BY`, not `GROUP BY`.","fix":"  ROW_NUMBER() OVER (PARTITION BY class ORDER BY score DESC) AS rn","id":"sql.a.09.6"},
        {"t":"order","topic":"SQL","q":"Lakshmi needs the top scorer of each class. Put the steps in order.","items":["Number each class's students with `ROW_NUMBER() OVER (PARTITION BY class ORDER BY score DESC)`","Wrap that query in a subquery or CTE","Filter the outer query with `WHERE rn = 1`"],"why":"`WHERE` runs before window functions are calculated, so you cannot use `rn` there. Number the rows, wrap the query, then keep `rn = 1` (Snowflake and BigQuery also offer a shortcut, `QUALIFY`).","id":"sql.a.09.7"},
        {"t":"check","topic":"SQL","q":"How is a window function different from `GROUP BY`?","a":"It keeps every row and adds a calculated column","w":["It removes duplicate rows","It returns one row per group","It can only be used in `WHERE`"],"why":"`GROUP BY` collapses each group into one row. A window function keeps all rows and adds a value such as a rank or a total.","id":"sql.a.09.8"},
        {"t":"check","topic":"SQL","q":"What does this query return?","code":"-- marks: (Asha,A,90) (Ravi,A,80) (Lakshmi,A,80)\n--        (Kiran,B,70) (Sita,B,85)\nSELECT student,\n  COUNT(*) OVER (PARTITION BY class) AS n\nFROM marks\nORDER BY student LIMIT 2;","lang":"sql","a":"Asha | 3\nKiran | 2","w":["Asha | 5\nKiran | 5","Asha | 1\nKiran | 1","Asha | 3\nKiran | 3"],"why":"`PARTITION BY class` counts the rows inside each class: A has 3 students and B has 2. Every row stays in the result.","id":"sql.a.09.9"}
      ] },
      { id: "sql.a.10", level: "a", n: 10, title: "CTEs", cards: [
        {"t":"learn","topic":"SQL","title":"CTEs: give a step a name","body":"A **CTE** (common table expression) names a step with `WITH name AS (...)`. The main query then reads from that name, so the query reads top to bottom instead of inside-out. It lives only for that one query.","code":"WITH t AS (SELECT city, SUM(amount) AS total\n           FROM orders GROUP BY city)\nSELECT city FROM t WHERE total > 300;","lang":"sql","id":"sql.a.10.1"},
        {"t":"output","topic":"SQL","q":"Which rows does this query return?","code":"-- orders: (1,Guntur,300) (2,Pune,200) (3,Guntur,100)\n--         (4,Pune,300) (5,Delhi,100)\nWITH t AS (SELECT city, SUM(amount) AS total\n           FROM orders GROUP BY city)\nSELECT city, total FROM t\nWHERE total > 300 ORDER BY city;","lang":"sql","a":"Guntur | 400\nPune | 500","w":["Delhi | 100\nGuntur | 400\nPune | 500","Pune | 500","Delhi | 100"],"why":"The CTE first totals each city: Delhi 100, Guntur 400, Pune 500. The main query then keeps only the totals above 300.","id":"sql.a.10.2"},
        {"t":"output","topic":"SQL","q":"Two CTEs, separated by a comma. What is each city's share of all sales?","code":"-- orders: (1,Guntur,300) (2,Pune,200) (3,Guntur,100)\n--         (4,Pune,300) (5,Delhi,100)\nWITH t AS (SELECT SUM(amount) AS tot FROM orders),\n  c AS (SELECT city, SUM(amount) AS s\n        FROM orders GROUP BY city)\nSELECT city, s * 100 / tot AS pct\nFROM c, t ORDER BY city;","lang":"sql","a":"Delhi | 10\nGuntur | 40\nPune | 50","w":["Delhi | 100\nGuntur | 400\nPune | 500","Delhi | 10\nGuntur | 50\nPune | 40","Delhi | 0.1\nGuntur | 0.4\nPune | 0.5"],"why":"`t` has one row (`tot` = 1000), so `FROM c, t` puts it beside every city: 100, 400 and 500 give 10, 40 and 50. In SQLite, PostgreSQL and SQL Server integer / integer gives a whole number; MySQL and Snowflake give decimals.","id":"sql.a.10.3"},
        {"t":"bug","topic":"SQL","q":"Ravi defines two CTEs, but SQLite stops with a syntax error. Which line is wrong?","code":["WITH big AS (SELECT city FROM orders WHERE amount > 200),","WITH small AS (SELECT city FROM orders WHERE amount <= 200)","SELECT 'big', COUNT(*) FROM big","UNION ALL SELECT 'small', COUNT(*) FROM small;"],"lang":"sql","bad":1,"why":"`WITH` is written only once. Further CTEs follow after a comma, with no second `WITH`.","fix":"small AS (SELECT city FROM orders WHERE amount <= 200)","id":"sql.a.10.4"},
        {"t":"order","topic":"SQL","q":"Put the steps in order to write a query with a CTE.","items":["Write `WITH totals AS (`","Write the SELECT you want to name, then close the bracket `)`","Write the main SELECT that reads `FROM totals`"],"why":"A CTE comes **before** the main query. You name and define the step first, then use the name.","id":"sql.a.10.5"},
        {"t":"output","topic":"SQL","q":"A recursive CTE builds the numbers 1 to 5. What does the query return?","code":"WITH RECURSIVE n(x) AS (\n  SELECT 1 UNION ALL\n  SELECT x + 1 FROM n WHERE x < 5\n)\nSELECT SUM(x) FROM n;","lang":"sql","a":"15","w":["5","10","120"],"why":"`WITH RECURSIVE` starts at 1 and keeps adding `x + 1` while `x < 5`, giving 1, 2, 3, 4, 5. Their sum is 15. The `WHERE` stops the loop.","id":"sql.a.10.6"},
        {"t":"choice","topic":"SQL","q":"Why do many people prefer a CTE to a deeply nested subquery?","a":"Each step gets a name, so the query is easier to read","w":["It always runs faster","It saves the result in a table for later","It lets you change the rows in the table"],"why":"A CTE is mainly about readability. Speed is usually the same, and the result is not saved: it exists only for that query.","id":"sql.a.10.7"},
        {"t":"check","topic":"SQL","q":"A CTE named `t` is defined in a `WITH` clause. Where can you use `t`?","a":"Only in that one query","w":["In every later query in the session","Anywhere, like a saved table","Only inside its own brackets"],"why":"A CTE exists only while its query runs. For something you reuse later, create a view or a table.","id":"sql.a.10.8"},
        {"t":"check","topic":"SQL","q":"The CTE `t` is used twice. What does this query return?","code":"-- orders: (1,Guntur,300) (2,Pune,200) (3,Guntur,100)\n--         (4,Pune,300) (5,Delhi,100)\nWITH t AS (SELECT city, SUM(amount) AS s\n           FROM orders GROUP BY city)\nSELECT COUNT(*) FROM t\nWHERE s > (SELECT AVG(s) FROM t);","lang":"sql","a":"2","w":["1","3","5"],"why":"The city totals are 100, 400 and 500, and their average is about 333. Two cities (Guntur and Pune) are above it.","id":"sql.a.10.9"}
      ] },
      { id: "sql.a.11", level: "a", n: 11, title: "Indexes and performance", cards: [
        {"t":"learn","topic":"SQL","title":"Indexes: find rows fast","body":"An **index** works like the index at the back of a book: the database jumps to the matching rows instead of reading the whole table. It makes searches faster, but every insert and update must also update the index, and it takes extra space.","code":"CREATE INDEX idx_orders_city\n  ON orders(city);","lang":"sql","id":"sql.a.11.1"},
        {"t":"choice","topic":"SQL","q":"What is the cost of adding an index to a table?","a":"Inserts and updates get slower, and it uses extra space","w":["Searches get slower","The table's rows change permanently","Query results change"],"why":"The database must keep the index up to date on every write. That is why you do not index everything.","id":"sql.a.11.2"},
        {"t":"choice","topic":"SQL","q":"Ravi creates `CREATE INDEX idx ON orders(city, amount)`. Which filter can use this index for a fast lookup?","a":"`WHERE city = 'Pune'`","w":["`WHERE amount = 300` alone","Neither, it needs both columns in the `WHERE`","Only a query with `ORDER BY`"],"why":"A multi-column index is sorted by its **leftmost** column first, like a phone book sorted by surname and then first name. A filter on `city` can use it; a filter on `amount` alone normally cannot (SQLite's rare skip-scan is an exception).","id":"sql.a.11.3"},
        {"t":"bug","topic":"SQL","q":"Asha indexed `name`, but this query is still slow. Which line stops the index from being used?","code":["CREATE INDEX idx_name ON students(name);","SELECT id, name FROM students","WHERE UPPER(name) = 'ASHA';"],"lang":"sql","bad":2,"why":"The index stores `name`, not `UPPER(name)`, so a function on the column hides it from the index. Compare the plain column, or, if you need a case-insensitive search, create an index on `UPPER(name)` itself (SQLite and PostgreSQL allow this).","fix":"WHERE name = 'Asha';","id":"sql.a.11.4"},
        {"t":"order","topic":"SQL","q":"A query is slow. Put the steps in a sensible order.","items":["Find the slow query and the column in its `WHERE`","Look at its plan with `EXPLAIN` (in SQLite: `EXPLAIN QUERY PLAN`)","Create an index on that column","Run the query again and check that it is faster"],"why":"Look first, then change one thing, then measure. Keep the index only if it really helps.","id":"sql.a.11.5"},
        {"t":"output","topic":"SQL","q":"The index `idx_city` exists on `orders(city)`. What does the query return?","code":"-- orders: (1,Guntur,300) (2,Pune,200) (3,Guntur,100)\n-- index idx_city on orders(city) exists\nSELECT COUNT(*) FROM orders\nWHERE city = 'Guntur';","lang":"sql","a":"2","w":["1","3","0"],"why":"An index changes **speed**, never the result. Two rows have the city Guntur, with or without the index.","id":"sql.a.11.6"},
        {"t":"choice","topic":"SQL","q":"Lakshmi's report needs only `city` and `amount` from a table with 40 columns. What is the better query?","a":"`SELECT city, amount FROM orders`","w":["`SELECT * FROM orders`, in case she needs more","`SELECT DISTINCT * FROM orders`","`SELECT COUNT(*) FROM orders`"],"why":"Ask only for the columns you need. `SELECT *` reads and sends more data than the report uses.","id":"sql.a.11.7"},
        {"t":"check","topic":"SQL","q":"Which statement about indexes is true?","a":"Index the columns you often search or join on, not every column","w":["More indexes always mean faster queries","An index makes `INSERT` faster","An index never needs updating after it is created"],"why":"Each index costs space and must be updated on every write. Add one when a big table is often searched or joined on that column.","id":"sql.a.11.8"},
        {"t":"check","topic":"SQL","q":"Which column is the best one to index?","a":"`customer_id`, used in `WHERE` and `JOIN` on a table with millions of rows","w":["`is_active`, a yes/no column in a table of 50 rows","`notes`, a long text column that is only shown in reports","A column that is updated all day and never searched"],"why":"An index pays off when a big table is searched often by that column. Otherwise it only adds cost.","id":"sql.a.11.9"}
      ] },
      { id: "sql.a.12", level: "a", n: 12, title: "Star schema modelling", cards: [
        {"t":"learn","topic":"SQL","title":"Star schema","body":"Reporting models use a **fact** table of measurements (sale amount, quantity) linked to **dimension** tables that describe them (customer, product, date). Drawn out, it looks like a star.","id":"sql.a.12.1"},
        {"t":"choice","topic":"SQL","q":"In a star schema, which table holds the numbers, like sale amount and quantity?","a":"The fact table","w":["A dimension table","The staging folder","A lookup CSV"],"why":"The **fact** table holds measurements (amount, quantity). **Dimension** tables describe them (customer, product, date) and sit around it like a star.","id":"sql.a.12.2"},
        {"t":"choice","topic":"SQL","q":"Lakshmi's `fact_sales` has the columns `bill_no`, `line_no`, `product_key`, `qty`, `amount`. What is its **grain** (what does one row mean)?","a":"One bill line: one product on one bill","w":["One bill","One product","One day"],"why":"`bill_no` + `line_no` together point to one row, so each row is one product line on a bill. Decide the grain first: every measure and dimension must fit it.","id":"sql.a.12.3"},
        {"t":"output","topic":"SQL","q":"Join the fact table to a dimension. What does the report show?","code":"-- fact_sales (prod_key,date_key,amount): (1,20260101,300)\n--   (2,20260101,200) (1,20260102,100) (3,20260102,400)\n-- dim_product (prod_key,category): (1,Snacks) (2,Drinks) (3,Drinks)\nSELECT p.category, SUM(f.amount)\nFROM fact_sales f\nJOIN dim_product p ON p.prod_key = f.prod_key\nGROUP BY p.category\nORDER BY p.category;","lang":"sql","a":"Drinks | 600\nSnacks | 400","w":["Drinks | 400\nSnacks | 600","Drinks | 2\nSnacks | 2","Drinks | 600\nSnacks | 300"],"why":"The fact rows hold the amounts and the dimension gives the category. Drinks is 200 + 400, Snacks is 300 + 100.","id":"sql.a.12.4"},
        {"t":"pipe","topic":"SQL","q":"Shop sales must reach a Power BI report every night. Build the star-schema flow.","steps":[["APP","Billing app database","src"],["ETL","Nightly load and clean"],["STAR","Fact + dimension tables"],["BI","Power BI report"]],"extra":[["STRM","Live event stream"]],"why":"The billing data is loaded and cleaned into a **fact table plus dimensions**, and the report reads those. A live stream is not needed for a nightly report.","id":"sql.a.12.5"},
        {"t":"bug","topic":"SQL","q":"In SQLite, Ravi's report shows no rows. The join links the fact table to the dimension on the wrong columns. Which line is wrong?","code":["SELECT p.category, SUM(f.amount)","FROM fact_sales f","JOIN dim_product p ON p.category = f.prod_key","GROUP BY p.category;"],"lang":"sql","bad":2,"why":"A fact row links to a dimension through its **key**: join `prod_key` to `prod_key`, not a name column to a key. (PostgreSQL would stop with a type error instead of returning no rows.)","fix":"JOIN dim_product p ON p.prod_key = f.prod_key","id":"sql.a.12.6"},
        {"t":"choice","topic":"SQL","q":"How is a **snowflake schema** different from a star schema?","a":"Its dimensions are split into more linked tables, such as product and then category","w":["It has no fact table","It keeps all data in one flat table","It stores the data in the Snowflake cloud"],"why":"A star keeps each dimension in one table. A snowflake normalises dimensions into extra tables. It saves a little space but needs more joins.","id":"sql.a.12.7"},
        {"t":"choice","topic":"SQL","q":"Why does `dim_product` use its own `prod_key` (1, 2, 3...) instead of the shop's product code as the key?","a":"It stays the same even if the source code changes, and lets one product have several history rows","w":["A primary key column cannot hold text","It makes the table smaller than having no key","The source product code must be deleted"],"why":"A **surrogate key** is a simple number made by the warehouse. It does not depend on the source system, and SCD Type 2 history needs it because one product can then have several rows. (Text keys are allowed in SQL; that is not the reason.)","id":"sql.a.12.8"},
        {"t":"check","topic":"SQL","q":"In a sales fact table, which column is a **measure** (a number you add up)?","a":"`amount`","w":["`customer_city`","`product_category`","`month_name`"],"why":"Measures are numbers you sum or average, like `amount` or `quantity`. City, category and month name describe the sale, so they are attributes in dimensions.","id":"sql.a.12.9"},
        {"t":"check","topic":"SQL","q":"Why keep a separate **date dimension** instead of only a date column in the fact table?","a":"It holds month, quarter, weekday and holiday flags once, so reports can group by them","w":["The fact table cannot store dates","It replaces the need for a fact table","It makes every query return fewer rows"],"why":"A date dimension has one row per day with ready-made attributes. Reports group by month or quarter without repeating those values in every fact row.","id":"sql.a.12.10"}
      ] }
    ] },
    { id: "pbi", name: "Power BI", topic: "PowerBI", blurb: "Turn data into reports and dashboards people actually use.", units: [
      { id: "pbi.b.01", level: "b", n: 1, title: "What Power BI is", cards: [
        {"t":"learn","topic":"PowerBI","title":"What Power BI is","body":"**Power BI** turns data into reports and charts. **Power BI Desktop** is a free app for Windows where you build reports. The **Power BI service** (app.powerbi.com) is the website where you share them, and there are **mobile apps** to view them on a phone.","id":"pbi.b.01.1"},
        {"t":"choice","topic":"PowerBI","q":"What is Power BI used for?","a":"Turning data into reports and charts","w":["Writing Word letters","Editing videos","Sending emails"],"why":"Power BI connects to data and shows it as visuals, so people can understand it quickly.","id":"pbi.b.01.2"},
        {"t":"choice","topic":"PowerBI","q":"Which free Windows app do you use to build Power BI reports?","a":"Power BI Desktop","w":["Power BI service","Power BI mobile app","Microsoft Word"],"why":"Power BI Desktop is the free Windows app for building reports. The service is a website for sharing.","id":"pbi.b.01.3"},
        {"t":"choice","topic":"PowerBI","q":"Which part of Power BI is the website at app.powerbi.com?","a":"Power BI service","w":["Power BI Desktop","Power Query","Power BI mobile app"],"why":"The Power BI service is the online part, where you publish, view and share reports.","id":"pbi.b.01.4"},
        {"t":"choice","topic":"PowerBI","q":"What is a Power BI report?","a":"One or more pages of visuals built from your data","w":["A single page of tiles pinned from other reports","A free Windows app","A list of users"],"why":"A report can have many pages. A dashboard is the single page of pinned tiles.","id":"pbi.b.01.5"},
        {"t":"choice","topic":"PowerBI","q":"A manager wants to look at sales numbers on a phone. What can they use?","a":"The Power BI mobile app","w":["Power BI Desktop only","Power Query","A slicer"],"why":"Power BI has mobile apps for viewing reports and dashboards on phones and tablets.","id":"pbi.b.01.6"},
        {"t":"check","topic":"PowerBI","q":"Where do you usually build a new report first?","a":"In Power BI Desktop","w":["In the Power BI mobile app","In a gateway","In a slicer"],"why":"You build the report in Power BI Desktop, then publish it to the service.","id":"pbi.b.01.7"},
        {"t":"check","topic":"PowerBI","q":"Which Power BI item is one page that shows tiles pinned from reports?","a":"A dashboard","w":["A report","A slicer","A gateway"],"why":"A dashboard is a single page of tiles. A report can have many pages of visuals.","id":"pbi.b.01.8"}
      ] },
      { id: "pbi.b.02", level: "b", n: 2, title: "Getting data", cards: [
        {"t":"learn","topic":"PowerBI","title":"Getting data","body":"In Power BI Desktop, click **Get data** on the Home tab to connect to a source such as Excel, CSV, SQL Server, SharePoint or a web page. **Import** copies the data into Power BI. **DirectQuery** keeps data in the source and asks it when needed. With Import, use **Refresh** to get new data.","id":"pbi.b.02.1"},
        {"t":"choice","topic":"PowerBI","q":"Which button on the Home tab starts connecting to your data?","a":"Get data","w":["Publish","New measure","Format"],"why":"Get data opens the list of sources you can connect to.","id":"pbi.b.02.2"},
        {"t":"choice","topic":"PowerBI","q":"Which sources can Power BI Desktop get data from?","a":"Excel, CSV, SQL Server and web pages, and many more","w":["Only Excel files","Only the Power BI service","Only pictures"],"why":"Power BI connects to many sources, including files, databases, online services and web pages.","id":"pbi.b.02.3"},
        {"t":"choice","topic":"PowerBI","q":"In Import mode, where is a copy of the data kept?","a":"Inside the Power BI model","w":["Only in the source, never copied","On the reader's phone only","In a Word file"],"why":"Import loads a copy of the data into Power BI. You refresh it to get new data.","id":"pbi.b.02.4"},
        {"t":"choice","topic":"PowerBI","q":"In DirectQuery mode, what happens when you open a visual?","a":"Power BI sends queries to the data source to get the data","w":["Power BI shows an old copy only","Power BI deletes the source data","Power BI asks for a new Excel file"],"why":"DirectQuery does not copy the data. It asks the source each time it needs data.","id":"pbi.b.02.5"},
        {"t":"choice","topic":"PowerBI","q":"Your Excel file changed. Your report uses Import. What should you do to see the new numbers?","a":"Refresh the data","w":["Delete the report","Change the colour of the visual","Switch to a dashboard"],"why":"An Import model holds a copy, so it must be refreshed to pick up the changes.","id":"pbi.b.02.6"},
        {"t":"check","topic":"PowerBI","q":"Which mode copies the data into Power BI?","a":"Import","w":["DirectQuery","Dashboard","Slicer"],"why":"Import loads the data into Power BI. DirectQuery leaves it in the source.","id":"pbi.b.02.7"},
        {"t":"check","topic":"PowerBI","q":"How do you begin adding a CSV file to your report?","a":"Click Get data, then choose Text/CSV","w":["Click Publish, then choose CSV","Add a slicer, then choose CSV","Open a gateway, then choose CSV"],"why":"Every data source starts with Get data. Then you pick the kind of source.","id":"pbi.b.02.8"}
      ] },
      { id: "pbi.b.03", level: "b", n: 3, title: "Power Query basics", cards: [
        {"t":"learn","topic":"PowerBI","title":"Power Query basics","body":"**Power Query Editor** is where you clean and shape data before you load it. Each change you make is saved as a step in the **Applied Steps** list. Common steps are remove columns, **change type** and filter rows. **Merge** joins columns from two queries, **Append** stacks rows. The steps are written in the **M** language.","id":"pbi.b.03.1"},
        {"t":"choice","topic":"PowerBI","q":"What is Power Query Editor used for?","a":"Cleaning and shaping data before loading it","w":["Sharing reports with other people","Drawing the final charts","Printing the report"],"why":"In Power Query you prepare the data. You build charts later in the report view.","id":"pbi.b.03.2"},
        {"t":"choice","topic":"PowerBI","q":"Where does Power Query list each change you make?","a":"The Applied Steps list","w":["The Fields pane","The Filters pane","The Format pane"],"why":"Applied Steps records every transformation, in order. You can click a step to see it or delete it.","id":"pbi.b.03.3"},
        {"t":"choice","topic":"PowerBI","q":"You have two tables with the same columns, January and February sales. How do you stack them into one table?","a":"Append queries","w":["Merge queries","Remove columns","Change type"],"why":"Append adds the rows of one query below another. Merge adds columns by matching a key.","id":"pbi.b.03.4"},
        {"t":"choice","topic":"PowerBI","q":"Which action joins two tables by matching a key column, like a lookup?","a":"Merge queries","w":["Append queries","Remove rows","Add a slicer"],"why":"Merge brings columns from a second table by matching values in a column you choose.","id":"pbi.b.03.5"},
        {"t":"choice","topic":"PowerBI","q":"Which language is behind the steps in Power Query?","a":"M (the Power Query formula language)","w":["DAX","Python only","HTML"],"why":"Power Query steps are written in M. DAX is used later, in the data model, for calculations.","id":"pbi.b.03.6"},
        {"t":"check","topic":"PowerBI","q":"A Price column was loaded as text, so you cannot add it up. What step fixes it?","a":"Change type to a number","w":["Append queries","Publish the report","Add a slicer"],"why":"Change type tells Power BI that the column holds numbers, so it can add and average them.","id":"pbi.b.03.7"},
        {"t":"check","topic":"PowerBI","q":"Which things can you do in Power Query Editor?","a":"Remove columns, change types and filter rows","w":["Only publish reports","Only draw charts","Only send emails"],"why":"Power Query is for preparing data: removing, changing, filtering and combining it.","id":"pbi.b.03.8"}
      ] },
      { id: "pbi.b.04", level: "b", n: 4, title: "Visuals and charts", cards: [
        {"t":"learn","topic":"PowerBI","title":"Visuals and charts","body":"A **visual** shows data. Use a **line chart** for change over time, a **bar chart** to compare groups, a **card** for one big number, and a **table** or **matrix** for detail. A **map** shows places. Drag fields into the **wells** in the Visualizations pane, and use the **Format** options to change the look.","id":"pbi.b.04.1"},
        {"t":"choice","topic":"PowerBI","q":"You want to show sales for each month of a year. Which visual fits best?","a":"Line chart","w":["Card","Map","Slicer"],"why":"A line chart shows how a value changes over time.","id":"pbi.b.04.2"},
        {"t":"choice","topic":"PowerBI","q":"You want to compare sales of five products. Which visual fits best?","a":"Bar chart","w":["Card","Slicer","Gauge"],"why":"A bar (or column) chart makes it easy to compare the size of different groups.","id":"pbi.b.04.3"},
        {"t":"choice","topic":"PowerBI","q":"Which visual shows one big number, such as total sales?","a":"Card","w":["Line chart","Matrix","Map"],"why":"A card shows a single value, so people see the key number at once.","id":"pbi.b.04.4"},
        {"t":"choice","topic":"PowerBI","q":"Where do you drag fields to choose the axis and values of a visual?","a":"The field wells in the Visualizations pane","w":["The Applied Steps list","The Home ribbon only","The Filters pane"],"why":"Wells such as Axis and Values tell the visual which fields to use.","id":"pbi.b.04.5"},
        {"t":"choice","topic":"PowerBI","q":"Which visual works like a pivot table, with rows and columns that group the data?","a":"Matrix","w":["Card","Pie chart","Table"],"why":"A matrix can group data by rows and by columns. A table shows a flat list.","id":"pbi.b.04.6"},
        {"t":"check","topic":"PowerBI","q":"Where do you change the colour or title of a visual?","a":"The Format options in the Visualizations pane","w":["The Applied Steps list","The Get data button","The workspace settings"],"why":"Format options control the look of the selected visual.","id":"pbi.b.04.7"},
        {"t":"check","topic":"PowerBI","q":"Which visual shows your data on a geographic location?","a":"Map","w":["Card","Matrix","Line chart"],"why":"A map visual plots values by place, such as city or state.","id":"pbi.b.04.8"}
      ] },
      { id: "pbi.b.05", level: "b", n: 5, title: "Filters and slicers", cards: [
        {"t":"learn","topic":"PowerBI","title":"Filters and slicers","body":"A **slicer** is a visual that lets readers click to filter the page, for example by year. The **Filters pane** has three levels: **visual** (one chart), **page** (one page) and **report** (all pages). Clicking a bar in one visual also changes the other visuals on the page. This is **cross-filtering** (or cross-highlighting).","id":"pbi.b.05.1"},
        {"t":"choice","topic":"PowerBI","q":"Which visual lets readers click a value, like a year, to filter the page?","a":"Slicer","w":["Card","Matrix","Gauge"],"why":"A slicer is built for readers to filter the other visuals.","id":"pbi.b.05.2"},
        {"t":"choice","topic":"PowerBI","q":"Which filter applies to all pages of the report?","a":"Report-level filter","w":["Visual-level filter","Page-level filter","Applied step"],"why":"Report-level filters apply everywhere. Page-level only one page. Visual-level only one visual.","id":"pbi.b.05.3"},
        {"t":"choice","topic":"PowerBI","q":"Which filter changes only one chart?","a":"Visual-level filter","w":["Report-level filter","Page-level filter","Merge query"],"why":"A visual-level filter applies only to the visual you selected.","id":"pbi.b.05.4"},
        {"t":"choice","topic":"PowerBI","q":"You click the Guntur bar in a bar chart, and the other visuals on the page change to match Guntur. What is this called?","a":"Cross-filtering between visuals","w":["Publishing","Merging queries","Refreshing"],"why":"Selecting data in one visual affects the other visuals on the page.","id":"pbi.b.05.5"},
        {"t":"choice","topic":"PowerBI","q":"Where do you set visual, page and report filters?","a":"The Filters pane","w":["The Fields pane","The Get data button","The Publish button"],"why":"The Filters pane lists the filters at each level.","id":"pbi.b.05.6"},
        {"t":"check","topic":"PowerBI","q":"You want to hide a test region from every page of the report. Which level do you use?","a":"Report-level filter","w":["Visual-level filter","Page-level filter","A card"],"why":"A report-level filter works on all pages, so the test region disappears everywhere.","id":"pbi.b.05.7"},
        {"t":"check","topic":"PowerBI","q":"What does a slicer do?","a":"Lets readers choose values to filter the visuals on the page","w":["Loads data from a file","Merges two tables","Publishes the report"],"why":"A slicer is a filter that readers can click, right on the report page.","id":"pbi.b.05.8"}
      ] },
      { id: "pbi.b.06", level: "b", n: 6, title: "Publish and share", cards: [
        {"t":"learn","topic":"PowerBI","title":"Publish and share","body":"Click **Publish** in Power BI Desktop to send the report to a **workspace** in the Power BI service. Share it with people there, or bundle reports into an **app**. **Scheduled refresh** updates the data automatically. Data on your own network (on-premises) needs a **gateway**. To share, you need a Power BI **Pro** or **Premium Per User** licence, unless the content is in a Premium capacity.","id":"pbi.b.06.1"},
        {"t":"choice","topic":"PowerBI","q":"What is a workspace in the Power BI service?","a":"A place where reports are stored and shared with a team","w":["A Windows folder on your laptop","A type of chart","A step in Power Query"],"why":"Workspaces hold reports and other items for people who work together.","id":"pbi.b.06.2"},
        {"t":"choice","topic":"PowerBI","q":"What does scheduled refresh do?","a":"Updates the data in the service automatically at set times","w":["Changes the colours of visuals","Deletes old reports","Merges two tables"],"why":"Scheduled refresh keeps published reports up to date without you clicking Refresh each time.","id":"pbi.b.06.3"},
        {"t":"choice","topic":"PowerBI","q":"Where is data that needs a gateway?","a":"On a computer or server inside your own network (on-premises)","w":["On the public web only","Inside a slicer","In the Power BI mobile app"],"why":"The gateway lets the online service reach on-premises data safely.","id":"pbi.b.06.4"},
        {"t":"choice","topic":"PowerBI","q":"What is a Power BI app in the service?","a":"A bundle of reports and dashboards for a group of people","w":["A free Windows program","A step in Power Query","A type of slicer"],"why":"An app packages related content so many people can open it in one place.","id":"pbi.b.06.5"},
        {"t":"pipe","topic":"PowerBI","q":"Build the usual Power BI flow, from data to sharing.","steps":[["GET","Get data","src"],["PQ","Transform in Power Query"],["MOD","Model the data"],["VIS","Build visuals"],["PUB","Publish"]],"extra":[["DEL","Delete the data source"]],"why":"You get data, clean it in Power Query, model it, build visuals, then publish to the service.","id":"pbi.b.06.6"},
        {"t":"check","topic":"PowerBI","q":"Which button sends your finished report from Desktop to the Power BI service?","a":"Publish","w":["Get data","Refresh","Format"],"why":"Publish uploads the report to a workspace in the Power BI service.","id":"pbi.b.06.7"},
        {"t":"check","topic":"PowerBI","q":"Your data is on an on-premises SQL Server. What lets the service refresh it on a schedule?","a":"A gateway","w":["A slicer","A dashboard tile","The mobile app"],"why":"A gateway is the bridge between the service and data inside your network.","id":"pbi.b.06.8"}
      ] },
      { id: "pbi.i.07", level: "i", n: 7, title: "Data modelling and relationships", cards: [
        {"t":"learn","topic":"PowerBI","title":"Star schema and relationships","body":"A good model has a **fact table** in the middle (numbers like sales) and **dimension tables** around it (like Product, Customer, Date). This is a **star schema**. Relationships are usually **one-to-many**: one product, many sales. See them in **Model view**.","id":"pbi.i.07.1"},
        {"t":"choice","topic":"PowerBI","q":"A Sales table lists each sale with amount. Product, Customer and Date tables describe the details. Which is the fact table?","a":"Sales","w":["Product","Customer","Date"],"why":"A fact table holds events or numbers to measure. Dimension tables describe the who, what and when.","id":"pbi.i.07.2"},
        {"t":"choice","topic":"PowerBI","q":"One product in Product appears in many rows of Sales. Which relationship type is this?","a":"One-to-many","w":["Many-to-one only from Sales to nothing","One-to-one","No relationship"],"why":"The Product side has unique keys (one) and Sales has repeated keys (many).","id":"pbi.i.07.3"},
        {"t":"choice","topic":"PowerBI","q":"In a relationship, what does cross-filter direction decide?","a":"Which way filters can flow between the two tables","w":["The colour of the report","How many rows are imported","Which user can sign in"],"why":"Single direction usually filters from the dimension to the fact. Both lets filters flow either way, so use it carefully.","id":"pbi.i.07.4"},
        {"t":"choice","topic":"PowerBI","q":"Where in Power BI Desktop do you view and edit table relationships?","a":"Model view","w":["Report view only","Data gateway page","Deployment pipeline page"],"why":"Model view shows tables as boxes with lines for relationships.","id":"pbi.i.07.5"},
        {"t":"order","topic":"PowerBI","q":"Order the steps to build a simple model in Power BI Desktop.","items":["Load the Sales and Product tables","Open Model view","Create a one-to-many relationship on the key column","Build a visual that uses both tables"],"why":"First get the tables, then relate them in Model view, then use them together in a visual.","id":"pbi.i.07.6"},
        {"t":"check","topic":"PowerBI","q":"Why is a star schema recommended for Power BI?","a":"It is simple, fast and easy to filter by dimensions","w":["It avoids using relationships","It puts every column in one table","It stops reports from filtering"],"why":"One fact table with dimensions around it gives simple, clear filtering and good performance.","id":"pbi.i.07.7"},
        {"t":"check","topic":"PowerBI","q":"Which table would be a dimension?","a":"Customer, with CustomerID, Name and City","w":["Sales, with one row per sale and amounts","Orders, with quantity and price per line","Payments, with amounts per date"],"why":"Dimensions describe things (customers, products, dates). Facts record events and numbers.","id":"pbi.i.07.8"}
      ] },
      { id: "pbi.i.08", level: "i", n: 8, title: "DAX basics: measures and columns", cards: [
        {"t":"learn","topic":"PowerBI","title":"DAX: measures and columns","body":"**DAX** is the formula language of Power BI. A **calculated column** is worked out for each row when data is loaded and is stored in the model. A **measure** is worked out when a visual needs it, using the current filters. Example measure: `Total Sales = SUM(Sales[Amount])`.","id":"pbi.i.08.1"},
        {"t":"choice","topic":"PowerBI","q":"Which is calculated when the visual needs it, using the current filters?","a":"A measure","w":["A calculated column","A raw source file","A relationship"],"why":"Measures are evaluated at query time in the current filter context. Calculated columns are computed during refresh, row by row.","id":"pbi.i.08.2"},
        {"t":"choice","topic":"PowerBI","q":"Ravi needs total sales that changes with each slicer selection. Which formula fits as a measure?","a":"`Total Sales = SUM(Sales[Amount])`","w":["`Sales[Amount] = 100`","`Total Sales = INSERT(Sales[Amount])`","`Total Sales = SUM(Amount)` with no table"],"why":"SUM adds the values in a column. A measure then reacts to slicers and filters.","id":"pbi.i.08.3"},
        {"t":"choice","topic":"PowerBI","q":"Which DAX function counts the rows in a table?","a":"`COUNTROWS`","w":["`SUMROWS`","`ROWCOUNTER`","`LISTROWS`"],"why":"`COUNTROWS(Sales)` returns the number of rows in the Sales table.","id":"pbi.i.08.4"},
        {"t":"choice","topic":"PowerBI","q":"Why use `DIVIDE(a, b)` instead of `a / b`?","a":"It handles divide by zero safely and returns blank or a value you choose","w":["It rounds to whole numbers","It is the only way to add numbers","It deletes zero rows"],"why":"DIVIDE avoids errors when b is zero. You can give an alternate result as the third argument.","id":"pbi.i.08.5"},
        {"t":"choice","topic":"PowerBI","q":"Which formula is a correct measure to count the rows in the Sales table?","a":"`Order Count = COUNTROWS(Sales)`","w":["`Order Count = ROWS(Sales[Amount])`","`Order Count = SUM(Sales)`","`Order Count = COUNT(Sales) + TABLE`"],"why":"`COUNTROWS` takes a table and returns its number of rows. SUM needs a numeric column.","id":"pbi.i.08.6"},
        {"t":"check","topic":"PowerBI","q":"Margin % should be shown correctly at each level (by city and in total). What should you use?","a":"A measure with DIVIDE of total profit by total sales","w":["A calculated column that stores a percent per row, then add them","A calculated column averaged by hand","A static number typed into a card"],"why":"Percentages must be calculated from totals at each level. A measure does this; adding row percentages is wrong.","id":"pbi.i.08.7"},
        {"t":"check","topic":"PowerBI","q":"When is a calculated column computed?","a":"During data refresh, for each row, and then stored","w":["Only when someone hovers over a chart","Never, it is only a name","Every time a slicer changes"],"why":"Calculated columns are stored in the model and take memory. Measures run when queried.","id":"pbi.i.08.8"}
      ] },
      { id: "pbi.i.09", level: "i", n: 9, title: "CALCULATE and filter context", cards: [
        {"t":"learn","topic":"PowerBI","title":"Filter context and CALCULATE","body":"**Filter context** is the set of filters on a calculation, from slicers, rows and columns of a visual. **Row context** means working one row at a time, like in a calculated column. `CALCULATE` changes the filter context: `Sales South = CALCULATE([Total Sales], Sales[Region] = \"South\")`.","id":"pbi.i.09.1"},
        {"t":"choice","topic":"PowerBI","q":"A matrix shows sales by city. For the Guntur row, what is the filter context for `[Total Sales]`?","a":"City = Guntur","w":["No filters at all","Every city at the same time","Only the first row of the table"],"why":"Each visual cell filters the data by its row and column headings, plus slicers.","id":"pbi.i.09.2"},
        {"t":"choice","topic":"PowerBI","q":"Which function changes the filter context of a measure?","a":"`CALCULATE`","w":["`COUNTROWS`","`SUM` only","`DIVIDE` only"],"why":"`CALCULATE(expression, filters...)` evaluates the expression under the new filters.","id":"pbi.i.09.3"},
        {"t":"choice","topic":"PowerBI","q":"What does `CALCULATE([Total Sales], ALL(Sales[Region]))` do?","a":"Removes any filter on Region, so it returns sales for all regions","w":["Returns only the biggest region","Deletes the Region column","Returns the number of regions"],"why":"`ALL` removes filters from the given column or table, which is useful for totals and percent-of-total measures.","id":"pbi.i.09.4"},
        {"t":"choice","topic":"PowerBI","q":"Which is an example of row context?","a":"A calculated column that works out `Price * Qty` for each row","w":["A slicer set to Pune","A page-level filter","A matrix heading"],"why":"A calculated column is evaluated one row at a time. Filters from visuals make filter context.","id":"pbi.i.09.5"},
        {"t":"choice","topic":"PowerBI","q":"Which formula gives sales only for Pune, whatever city the user picks in a slicer?","a":"`CALCULATE([Total Sales], Sales[City] = \"Pune\")`","w":["`SUM(Sales[City]) = \"Pune\"`","`COUNTROWS([Total Sales], \"Pune\")`","`DIVIDE([Total Sales], \"Pune\")`"],"why":"`CALCULATE` takes a measure and a filter, and evaluates the measure under that filter.","id":"pbi.i.09.6"},
        {"t":"check","topic":"PowerBI","q":"A card shows `CALCULATE([Total Sales], Sales[Region] = \"South\")`. A slicer on the same column, Sales[Region], picks North. What does the card show?","a":"South sales, since CALCULATE replaces the filter on Region","w":["North sales only","Blank always","An error always"],"why":"CALCULATE **replaces** the existing filter on the same column, Sales[Region], with South. (If the slicer used a different column, both filters would apply together.)","id":"pbi.i.09.7"},
        {"t":"check","topic":"PowerBI","q":"To show each city's share of total sales you need the total for all cities in the same cell. Which idea helps?","a":"`CALCULATE` with `ALL` to remove the city filter for the denominator","w":["A calculated column of ones","Turning off all slicers in Desktop","Using only the `COUNTROWS` function"],"why":"Divide the filtered sales by sales with `ALL(City)` applied so the denominator ignores the city filter.","id":"pbi.i.09.8"}
      ] },
      { id: "pbi.i.10", level: "i", n: 10, title: "Time intelligence", cards: [
        {"t":"learn","topic":"PowerBI","title":"Time intelligence","body":"Time intelligence DAX needs a **date table** with one row per day and no gaps. In Power BI, use **Mark as date table** and relate it to your fact table. Then `TOTALYTD`, `SAMEPERIODLASTYEAR` and `DATEADD` work on the Date column.","id":"pbi.i.10.1"},
        {"t":"choice","topic":"PowerBI","q":"What must a date table contain for time intelligence to work well?","a":"A continuous list of dates, with one row per day","w":["Only the dates that have sales","Only month names as text","Only the current year"],"why":"Time functions need every date with no gaps, even days with no sales.","id":"pbi.i.10.2"},
        {"t":"choice","topic":"PowerBI","q":"What is the purpose of Mark as date table?","a":"It tells Power BI which table and column are the official dates","w":["It deletes older dates","It hides the table","It makes the table a measure"],"why":"Marking the table lets time intelligence functions use it correctly.","id":"pbi.i.10.3"},
        {"t":"choice","topic":"PowerBI","q":"Which function totals sales from 1 January up to the current date in the filter?","a":"`TOTALYTD`","w":["`COUNTROWS`","`DIVIDE`","`ALL`"],"why":"`TOTALYTD([Total Sales], 'Date'[Date])` gives the year-to-date total.","id":"pbi.i.10.4"},
        {"t":"choice","topic":"PowerBI","q":"Which function brings the same period from the previous year for a comparison?","a":"`SAMEPERIODLASTYEAR`","w":["`TOTALYTD`","`SUM`","`RELATED`"],"why":"`SAMEPERIODLASTYEAR('Date'[Date])` shifts the dates back by one year.","id":"pbi.i.10.5"},
        {"t":"order","topic":"PowerBI","q":"Order the steps to set up time intelligence for Sales.","items":["Create a date table with one row per day","Mark it as the date table","Relate its Date column to the Sales date","Write a measure with TOTALYTD"],"why":"The date table must exist and be marked and related before time functions give correct results.","id":"pbi.i.10.6"},
        {"t":"check","topic":"PowerBI","q":"`CALCULATE([Total Sales], DATEADD('Date'[Date], -1, MONTH))` returns what?","a":"Total sales for the previous month","w":["Total sales for next month","A count of months","Sales for all time"],"why":"`DATEADD` shifts the dates by the number and unit you give. Minus one month means one month earlier.","id":"pbi.i.10.7"},
        {"t":"check","topic":"PowerBI","q":"Your date table is missing some weekend dates. What can go wrong?","a":"Time intelligence results can be wrong or give errors","w":["Nothing, gaps are fine","Power BI adds the dates by itself in every case","Only colours change"],"why":"Time intelligence expects a continuous date table.","id":"pbi.i.10.8"}
      ] },
      { id: "pbi.i.11", level: "i", n: 11, title: "Row-level security", cards: [
        {"t":"learn","topic":"PowerBI","title":"Row-level security","body":"**Row-level security (RLS)** shows each person only the rows they are allowed to see. In Power BI Desktop you create a **role** with a DAX filter such as `[Region] = \"South\"`. Test with **View as**. After publishing, add **members** to the role in the Power BI service.","id":"pbi.i.11.1"},
        {"t":"choice","topic":"PowerBI","q":"Asha manages the South region. She must see only South rows. What do you create?","a":"A role with the DAX filter `[Region] = \"South\"`","w":["A new measure called South","A bigger gateway","A second date table"],"why":"A role holds table filters written in DAX. Rows that do not match are hidden.","id":"pbi.i.11.2"},
        {"t":"choice","topic":"PowerBI","q":"Where do you test what a role can see in Power BI Desktop?","a":"View as","w":["Mark as date table","Model refresh","Get data"],"why":"View as lets you see the report as a chosen role would see it.","id":"pbi.i.11.3"},
        {"t":"choice","topic":"PowerBI","q":"After publishing, where do you add people to a role?","a":"In the Power BI service, in the dataset (semantic model) security","w":["In the DAX formula bar","In Windows Explorer","In the Date table"],"why":"Roles are created in Desktop, but members are assigned in the service.","id":"pbi.i.11.4"},
        {"t":"choice","topic":"PowerBI","q":"What does RLS control?","a":"Which rows of data a person can see","w":["How fast refresh runs","Which colour theme loads","How many pages a report has"],"why":"RLS filters data rows for each role. It is not for visual style or speed.","id":"pbi.i.11.5"},
        {"t":"order","topic":"PowerBI","q":"Order the steps to set up row-level security.","items":["Create a role with a DAX filter in Desktop","Test it with View as","Publish to the Power BI service","Add members to the role in the service"],"why":"Build and test the role first, publish, then assign people in the service.","id":"pbi.i.11.6"},
        {"t":"check","topic":"PowerBI","q":"Why must you test a role before sharing?","a":"To check that people see only the rows they should","w":["To make the report load faster","To change the data source","To create a new date table"],"why":"A wrong filter can show too much or too little data. View as helps you find mistakes.","id":"pbi.i.11.7"},
        {"t":"check","topic":"PowerBI","q":"The report has RLS roles. Ravi is a workspace Viewer and is not a member of any role. What does he see?","a":"No data, because RLS only shows rows to members of a role","w":["All the data","Only the South rows","An error that deletes the data"],"why":"With RLS on, a Viewer who is in **no role** typically sees no data. RLS does not apply to workspace Admins, Members or Contributors.","id":"pbi.i.11.8"}
      ] },
      { id: "pbi.i.12", level: "i", n: 12, title: "Refresh and gateways", cards: [
        {"t":"learn","topic":"PowerBI","title":"Refresh and gateways","body":"In **Import** mode, Power BI keeps a copy of the data, so it must be **refreshed**. You can set a **scheduled refresh** in the service. If the data is on your own network, an **on-premises data gateway** connects the service to it. **DirectQuery** reads the source live and needs no import refresh.","id":"pbi.i.12.1"},
        {"t":"choice","topic":"PowerBI","q":"A Power BI dataset in Import mode shows old numbers. What keeps it up to date?","a":"Scheduled refresh","w":["Mark as date table","View as","Cross-filter direction"],"why":"Import mode keeps a copy, so refresh brings in new data.","id":"pbi.i.12.2"},
        {"t":"choice","topic":"PowerBI","q":"The data is in a SQL Server inside your office network. What connects the Power BI service to it?","a":"An on-premises data gateway","w":["A Key Vault","A star schema","A calculated column"],"why":"The gateway is software on a machine that can reach your data. The service uses it for refresh.","id":"pbi.i.12.3"},
        {"t":"choice","topic":"PowerBI","q":"What is incremental refresh?","a":"Refreshing only recent or changed data instead of everything","w":["Refreshing only the colours","Deleting old reports","Adding new slicers"],"why":"Incremental refresh keeps older data and refreshes only a recent range, which saves time on large tables.","id":"pbi.i.12.4"},
        {"t":"pipe","topic":"PowerBI","q":"Office SQL Server data must refresh in the Power BI service every night.","steps":[["SQL","On-premises SQL Server","src"],["GW","On-premises data gateway"],["DS","Dataset in the service"],["RPT","Report"]],"extra":[["PRT","Print the report"]],"why":"The gateway lets the service reach the office data. The dataset refreshes and the report shows the new data.","id":"pbi.i.12.5"},
        {"t":"choice","topic":"PowerBI","q":"In which mode does Power BI keep a copy of the data that needs refreshing?","a":"Import","w":["DirectQuery","View as","Mark as date table"],"why":"Import stores a copy in the model, so it must be refreshed. DirectQuery reads the source live.","id":"pbi.i.12.6"},
        {"t":"check","topic":"PowerBI","q":"A report uses DirectQuery. Does it need a scheduled import refresh?","a":"No, it queries the source each time it needs data","w":["Yes, every minute only","Yes, it stores a full copy","No, because it has no data source"],"why":"DirectQuery does not import a copy. Visuals send queries to the source.","id":"pbi.i.12.7"},
        {"t":"check","topic":"PowerBI","q":"When is an on-premises data gateway usually needed?","a":"When the Power BI service must reach data in your own network","w":["For every Excel file in OneDrive","To create a measure","To mark a date table"],"why":"Cloud sources usually connect directly. On-premises sources need a gateway.","id":"pbi.i.12.8"}
      ] },
      { id: "pbi.a.13", level: "a", n: 13, title: "Performance tuning", cards: [] },
      { id: "pbi.a.14", level: "a", n: 14, title: "Composite models and aggregations", cards: [] },
      { id: "pbi.a.15", level: "a", n: 15, title: "Deployment pipelines and Git", cards: [] },
      { id: "pbi.a.16", level: "a", n: 16, title: "Power BI in Microsoft Fabric", cards: [] },
      { id: "pbi.a.17", level: "a", n: 17, title: "Paginated reports", cards: [] },
      { id: "pbi.a.18", level: "a", n: 18, title: "Governance and sensitivity labels", cards: [] }
    ] },
    { id: "dk", name: "Docker", topic: "Docker", blurb: "Package any app so it runs the same everywhere.", units: [
      { id: "dk.b.01", level: "b", n: 1, title: "Images vs containers", cards: [
        {"t":"learn","topic":"Docker","title":"Images and containers","body":"An **image** is a packaged template: your app plus everything it needs to run. A **container** is a running copy of an image. One image can start many containers.","id":"dk.b.01.1"},
        {"t":"choice","topic":"Docker","q":"What is a Docker image?","a":"A packaged template with the app and everything it needs to run","w":["A running server","A screenshot of the app","A backup of a database"],"why":"An **image** is the package (code, libraries, settings). A **container** is a running copy of that image.","id":"dk.b.01.2"},
        {"t":"choice","topic":"Docker","q":"Ravi starts three containers from the same nginx image. What happens to the image?","a":"It stays the same; each container is a separate running copy","w":["It is used up, so he needs three images","It changes into a container","It is deleted after the first container starts"],"why":"An image is a read-only template. You can start many containers from one image.","id":"dk.b.01.3"},
        {"t":"choice","topic":"Docker","q":"Which command lists the images stored on your computer?","a":"`docker images`","w":["`docker ps`","`docker list`","`docker run`"],"why":"`docker images` (also `docker image ls`) lists images. `docker ps` lists containers.","id":"dk.b.01.4"},
        {"t":"bug","topic":"Docker","q":"Ravi wants the newest nginx image. One command has a typo in the tag, so Docker cannot find it. Which line?","code":["docker pull nginx","docker pull nginx:latest","docker pull nginx:lastest"],"lang":"shell","bad":2,"why":"The tag is `latest`, not `lastest`. If you give no tag, Docker uses `latest`.","id":"dk.b.01.5"},
        {"t":"order","topic":"Docker","q":"Order the steps to get an image and run it as a container.","items":["`docker pull nginx`","`docker images`","`docker run -d nginx`","`docker ps`"],"why":"First download the image, check it is there, start a container from it, then check the container is running.","id":"dk.b.01.6"},
        {"t":"choice","topic":"Docker","q":"In the name `nginx:latest`, what is `latest`?","a":"The tag, which picks a version of the image","w":["The name of the container","The name of the registry","A command that updates the image"],"why":"The format is `name:tag`. If you do not write a tag, Docker uses `latest`.","id":"dk.b.01.7"},
        {"t":"check","topic":"Docker","q":"Which command downloads an image from a registry such as Docker Hub?","a":"`docker pull`","w":["`docker get`","`docker start`","`docker install`"],"why":"`docker pull` downloads an image. `docker run` also pulls it if it is missing, but pull is the direct command.","id":"dk.b.01.8"},
        {"t":"check","topic":"Docker","q":"You run `docker pull nginx` with no tag. Which image do you get?","a":"`nginx:latest`","w":["The oldest nginx image","`nginx:stable` only","Nothing, Docker asks for a tag"],"why":"When no tag is given, Docker uses the `latest` tag by default.","id":"dk.b.01.9"}
      ] },
      { id: "dk.b.02", level: "b", n: 2, title: "run, ps, stop", cards: [
        {"t":"learn","topic":"Docker","title":"run, ps, stop","body":"`docker run` makes a new container from an image and starts it. Add `-d` to run it in the **background** and `-p 8080:80` to open a port. Use `docker ps` to see running containers and `docker stop` to stop one.","code":"docker run -d -p 8080:80 --name web nginx\ndocker ps\ndocker stop web","lang":"shell","id":"dk.b.02.1"},
        {"t":"choice","topic":"Docker","q":"What does the `-d` option do in `docker run -d nginx`?","a":"Runs the container in the background (detached)","w":["Deletes the container when it stops","Downloads the image again","Shows debug messages"],"why":"`-d` means detached. Your terminal is free and the container keeps running.","id":"dk.b.02.2"},
        {"t":"choice","topic":"Docker","q":"Asha runs `docker run -d -p 8080:80 nginx`. Which port does she open in her browser?","a":"8080, on her own computer","w":["80, on her own computer","8080, inside the container","Both 80 and 8080"],"why":"`-p host:container`. The first number is the port on your computer, the second is inside the container.","id":"dk.b.02.3"},
        {"t":"choice","topic":"Docker","q":"Your container stopped. Which command still shows it in the list?","a":"`docker ps -a`","w":["`docker ps`","`docker images`","`docker list`"],"why":"`docker ps` shows only running containers. `docker ps -a` shows all containers, also stopped ones.","id":"dk.b.02.4"},
        {"t":"bug","topic":"Docker","q":"Asha starts a container named web, stops it, then removes it. One line fails. Which one?","code":["docker run -d --name web nginx","docker stop web","docker rm nginx"],"lang":"shell","bad":2,"why":"`docker rm` needs the container name or ID, not the image name. Here the container is called `web`.","id":"dk.b.02.5"},
        {"t":"order","topic":"Docker","q":"Order the steps to run, check and clean up a container.","items":["`docker run -d --name web nginx`","`docker logs web`","`docker stop web`","`docker rm web`"],"why":"Start it, read its logs, stop it, then remove it. A running container must be stopped before a normal `docker rm`.","id":"dk.b.02.6"},
        {"t":"choice","topic":"Docker","q":"Which command shows the messages a container has printed?","a":"`docker logs web`","w":["`docker print web`","`docker ps web`","`docker output web`"],"why":"`docker logs` fetches the logs of a container. Use it first when an app does not work.","id":"dk.b.02.7"},
        {"t":"check","topic":"Docker","q":"What does `--name web` do in `docker run`?","a":"Gives the container the name web","w":["Names the image web","Sets the web server port","Names the folder inside the container"],"why":"`--name` sets the container name, so you can use `docker stop web` instead of a long ID.","id":"dk.b.02.8"},
        {"t":"check","topic":"Docker","q":"Which command stops a running container named web?","a":"`docker stop web`","w":["`docker rm web`","`docker pause image web`","`docker kill nginx`"],"why":"`docker stop` stops the container. `docker rm` removes a container that is already stopped.","id":"dk.b.02.9"}
      ] },
      { id: "dk.b.03", level: "b", n: 3, title: "Dockerfile", cards: [
        {"t":"learn","topic":"Docker","title":"The Dockerfile","body":"A **Dockerfile** is the recipe. `FROM` picks a base image, `COPY` adds your files and `CMD` says what to start. `docker build -t myapp .` makes the image; `docker run myapp` starts it.","code":"FROM python:3.12-slim\nCOPY app.py .\nCMD [\"python\", \"app.py\"]","lang":"docker","id":"dk.b.03.1"},
        {"t":"order","topic":"Docker","q":"Order the steps to run your app in a Docker container.","items":["Write a Dockerfile","docker build -t myapp .","docker run -p 8080:80 myapp","Open the app in the browser"],"why":"The **Dockerfile** is the recipe, `docker build` makes an image from it, and `docker run` starts a container from that image.","id":"dk.b.03.2"},
        {"t":"choice","topic":"Docker","q":"What does the FROM line at the top of a Dockerfile do?","a":"Picks the base image to start from","w":["Copies your files","Opens a network port","Deletes old containers"],"why":"`FROM python:3.12-slim` starts from an image that already has Python. Then `COPY`, `RUN` and `CMD` add your app on top.","id":"dk.b.03.3"},
        {"t":"choice","topic":"Docker","q":"In a Dockerfile, what is the difference between `RUN` and `CMD`?","a":"`RUN` runs while building the image; `CMD` runs when the container starts","w":["`RUN` runs when the container starts; `CMD` runs while building","They are the same","`CMD` installs packages; `RUN` copies files"],"why":"Use `RUN` for setup steps such as installing packages. Use `CMD` for the command that starts your app.","id":"dk.b.03.4"},
        {"t":"pipe","topic":"Docker","q":"Build the flow from your own Dockerfile to a running app.","steps":[["DKF","Dockerfile","src"],["BLD","docker build"],["IMG","Image"],["CTR","Container"]],"extra":[["PULL","docker pull"]],"why":"A Dockerfile is built into an image, and a container runs from that image. `docker pull` downloads an image someone else made.","id":"dk.b.03.5"},
        {"t":"bug","topic":"Docker","q":"Lakshmi's app is a web server. It should start when the container starts, but one line runs it during the build instead, so the build gets stuck. Which line?","code":["FROM python","WORKDIR /app","COPY . .","RUN python app.py"],"lang":"docker","bad":3,"why":"`RUN` runs at build time. The start command belongs in `CMD`.","id":"dk.b.03.6"},
        {"t":"choice","topic":"Docker","q":"Why do we `COPY requirements.txt` and run `pip install` before `COPY . .`?","a":"Docker can reuse the cached install layer when only your code changes","w":["pip only works before other files exist","It makes the image smaller by half","Docker needs requirements.txt to be the first file"],"why":"Docker caches each layer. If requirements.txt did not change, the slow install step is reused.","id":"dk.b.03.7"},
        {"t":"check","topic":"Docker","q":"Which command builds an image named myapp from the Dockerfile in the current folder?","a":"`docker build -t myapp .`","w":["`docker run myapp .`","`docker image myapp`","`docker build myapp`"],"why":"`-t` gives the image a name (tag) and the dot `.` is the folder to build from.","id":"dk.b.03.8"},
        {"t":"check","topic":"Docker","q":"What does `EXPOSE 80` in a Dockerfile do?","a":"It documents that the app uses port 80; it does not publish it","w":["It opens port 80 on your computer","It makes the app visible on the internet","It starts the app on port 80"],"why":"`EXPOSE` is only a note. To reach the app you still need `-p` in `docker run`.","id":"dk.b.03.9"}
      ] },
      { id: "dk.i.04", level: "i", n: 4, title: "Volumes", cards: [
        {"t":"learn","topic":"Docker","title":"Volumes keep your data","body":"Files a container writes are lost when the container is **removed**. A **volume** is storage managed by Docker that lives outside the container. Mount it with `-v name:/path` and the data survives.","code":"docker volume create appdata\ndocker run -d --name db -v appdata:/data alpine sleep 3600\ndocker volume ls","lang":"shell","id":"dk.i.04.1"},
        {"t":"choice","topic":"Docker","q":"Asha's app writes files inside a container with no volume. She runs `docker rm` on that container. What happens to those files?","a":"They are lost with the container","w":["They move to the image","They are copied to her laptop","They stay in Docker Hub"],"why":"Data written inside a container lives only as long as that container. Use a volume to keep it.","id":"dk.i.04.2"},
        {"t":"bug","topic":"Docker","q":"Asha wants to mount the volume `site-data` into nginx. One line has the mount written the wrong way round. Which line?","code":["docker volume create site-data","docker run -d --name web -v /usr/share/nginx/html:site-data nginx","docker volume ls"],"lang":"shell","bad":1,"why":"With `-v`, the volume name comes first and the path inside the container comes second: `name:/path`.","id":"dk.i.04.3"},
        {"t":"order","topic":"Docker","q":"Order the steps to prove that a named volume keeps data after the container is removed.","items":["`docker volume create appdata`","`docker run --name app1 -v appdata:/data alpine sh -c \"echo hi > /data/a.txt\"`","`docker rm app1`","`docker run --rm -v appdata:/data alpine cat /data/a.txt`"],"why":"Create the volume, write a file through the first container, remove it, then a new container using the same volume can still read the file.","id":"dk.i.04.4"},
        {"t":"choice","topic":"Docker","q":"Ravi edits code on his laptop and wants the container to see every change at once. What should he use?","a":"A bind mount, such as `-v $(pwd):/app`","w":["A new image for every edit","The `-p` option","`docker volume rm`"],"why":"A bind mount shares a folder from your computer with the container, so edits show up straight away.","id":"dk.i.04.5"},
        {"t":"choice","topic":"Docker","q":"What is the main difference between a named volume and a bind mount?","a":"Docker manages a named volume; a bind mount points to a folder you choose on the host","w":["A named volume is deleted when the container stops; a bind mount is not","A bind mount works only for databases","A named volume can be used by only one image ever"],"why":"Named volumes are created and managed by Docker. A bind mount uses an exact path on your own machine.","id":"dk.i.04.6"},
        {"t":"check","topic":"Docker","q":"Which command lists the volumes on your computer?","a":"`docker volume ls`","w":["`docker ps -v`","`docker images volume`","`docker storage ls`"],"why":"`docker volume ls` lists volumes. `docker ps` lists containers.","id":"dk.i.04.7"},
        {"t":"check","topic":"Docker","q":"Lakshmi runs a database in Docker and must not lose its data when the container is replaced. What should she do?","a":"Store the data in a named volume","w":["Write the data only inside the container","Use `-p` to publish the port","Add `EXPOSE` to the Dockerfile"],"why":"A named volume lives outside the container, so a new container can mount it and find the data.","id":"dk.i.04.8"}
      ] },
      { id: "dk.i.05", level: "i", n: 5, title: "Networks", cards: [
        {"t":"learn","topic":"Docker","title":"Docker networks","body":"Containers on the same **user-defined bridge network** can reach each other by **container name**. Create one with `docker network create` and join it with `--network`. Use `-p` only when you need to reach a container from your own computer.","code":"docker network create shop-net\ndocker run -d --name cache --network shop-net redis","lang":"shell","id":"dk.i.05.1"},
        {"t":"choice","topic":"Docker","q":"Two containers are on the same user-defined network. The first is named `cache`. How can the second one reach it?","a":"Use the name `cache` as the host name","w":["Only by `localhost`","Only with `-p` on both containers","It cannot; containers are always separate"],"why":"User-defined bridge networks have built-in DNS, so a container name works as a host name.","id":"dk.i.05.2"},
        {"t":"bug","topic":"Docker","q":"Asha's `web` container cannot find `cache` by name. Which line is the cause?","code":["docker network create shop-net","docker run -d --name cache redis","docker run -d --name web --network shop-net nginx"],"lang":"shell","bad":1,"why":"Without `--network`, `cache` joins the default bridge network. Both containers must join `shop-net`.","id":"dk.i.05.3"},
        {"t":"order","topic":"Docker","q":"Order the steps to start a container on your own network and look at the network.","items":["`docker network create shop-net`","`docker run -d --name cache --network shop-net redis`","`docker network inspect shop-net`"],"why":"The network must exist before a container can join it. Then you can inspect it to see the connected containers.","id":"dk.i.05.4"},
        {"t":"choice","topic":"Docker","q":"Ravi wants to open a web app running in a container from his browser on the same computer. Which option does that?","a":"Publish a port with `-p 8080:80`","w":["Create a network with `docker network create`","Use `docker volume create`","Add `--name web`"],"why":"`-p host:container` publishes a container port on the host. Networks are for container-to-container traffic.","id":"dk.i.05.5"},
        {"t":"choice","topic":"Docker","q":"Which command lists the Docker networks on your computer?","a":"`docker network ls`","w":["`docker ps --network`","`docker net list`","`docker network show`"],"why":"`docker network ls` lists networks. You will see the default `bridge` network there.","id":"dk.i.05.6"},
        {"t":"choice","topic":"Docker","q":"What is true about container names on the default bridge network?","a":"Containers there cannot find each other by name; a user-defined network can","w":["They find each other by name automatically","They cannot talk to each other in any way","They are reached by name only with `-p`"],"why":"Automatic name resolution is a feature of user-defined bridge networks, not of the default bridge.","id":"dk.i.05.7"},
        {"t":"check","topic":"Docker","q":"Which command makes a new network named `shop-net`?","a":"`docker network create shop-net`","w":["`docker create network shop-net`","`docker run --network shop-net`","`docker network add shop-net`"],"why":"`docker network create NAME` creates a network. `--network` only joins a container to one that already exists.","id":"dk.i.05.8"},
        {"t":"check","topic":"Docker","q":"The container `api` and the container `db` are on the same user-defined network. In the app config for `api`, what should the database host be?","a":"`db`","w":["`localhost`","`127.0.0.1`","`docker`"],"why":"`localhost` inside `api` means `api` itself. Use the other container's name, `db`.","id":"dk.i.05.9"}
      ] },
      { id: "dk.i.06", level: "i", n: 6, title: "Docker Compose", cards: [
        {"t":"learn","topic":"Docker","title":"Docker Compose","body":"A **Compose file** (`compose.yaml`) describes many containers in one place. Each entry under `services` is one container. Run all of them with `docker compose up -d`.","code":"services:\n  web:\n    image: nginx\n    ports:\n      - \"8080:80\"","lang":"compose","id":"dk.i.06.1"},
        {"t":"indent","topic":"Docker","step":2,"q":"Fix the indentation of this Compose file.","lang":"compose","lines":[[0,"services:"],[2,"web:"],[4,"image: nginx"],[4,"ports:"],[6,"- \"8080:80\""],[2,"cache:"],[4,"image: redis"]],"why":"Services sit under `services`, their settings sit under the service name, and list items sit under `ports`.","id":"dk.i.06.2"},
        {"t":"bug","topic":"Docker","q":"Asha's `web` service must connect to the Redis service. One line uses the wrong host name. Which line?","code":["services:","  web:","    build: .","    environment:","      REDIS_HOST: localhost","  cache:","    image: redis"],"lang":"compose","bad":4,"why":"Compose services reach each other by service name. `localhost` inside `web` is the `web` container itself.","id":"dk.i.06.3"},
        {"t":"order","topic":"Docker","q":"Order the Compose steps, from writing the file to cleaning up.","items":["Write `compose.yaml`","`docker compose up -d`","`docker compose ps`","`docker compose logs`","`docker compose down`"],"why":"Write the file, start the services, check they run, read their logs, then stop and remove them.","id":"dk.i.06.4"},
        {"t":"choice","topic":"Docker","q":"What does `depends_on: [db]` on the `web` service do by default?","a":"Starts `db` before `web`, but does not wait until `db` is ready","w":["Waits until `db` accepts connections","Copies the `db` data into `web`","Makes `web` and `db` share one container"],"why":"`depends_on` controls start order only. A service may need a health check if the other must be ready.","id":"dk.i.06.5"},
        {"t":"choice","topic":"Docker","q":"Does `docker compose down` delete named volumes by default?","a":"No, they are kept unless you add the `-v` option","w":["Yes, always","Yes, but only the unused ones","No, because `down` removes nothing"],"why":"`down` removes the containers and networks it created. Named volumes stay, so your data is safe.","id":"dk.i.06.6"},
        {"t":"choice","topic":"Docker","q":"Which command starts every service in the Compose file in the background?","a":"`docker compose up -d`","w":["`docker compose run -d`","`docker compose start --all`","`docker compose build -d`"],"why":"`docker compose up` creates and starts the services; `-d` runs them detached in the background.","id":"dk.i.06.7"},
        {"t":"check","topic":"Docker","q":"In a Compose file there are two services, `web` and `db`. How does `web` reach `db`?","a":"By the service name `db`","w":["By `localhost`","By the image name","By the name of the folder"],"why":"Compose puts the services on one network, and each service name works as a host name.","id":"dk.i.06.8"},
        {"t":"check","topic":"Docker","q":"Which command shows the state of the services in your Compose project?","a":"`docker compose ps`","w":["`docker compose images`","`docker compose top-level`","`docker ps --compose-file`"],"why":"`docker compose ps` lists the containers of the project and their state.","id":"dk.i.06.9"}
      ] },
      { id: "dk.i.07", level: "i", n: 7, title: "Registries", cards: [
        {"t":"learn","topic":"Docker","title":"Registries","body":"A **registry** stores images so servers can download them, for example Docker Hub or **Azure Container Registry**. `docker push` uploads an image and `docker pull` downloads it.","id":"dk.i.07.1"},
        {"t":"pipe","topic":"Docker","q":"Ship an app with Docker and Azure: from recipe to running app.","steps":[["DF","Dockerfile","src"],["IMG","docker build: image"],["ACR","Push to Container Registry"],["RUN","Run as a container"]],"extra":[["VM","Install Python by hand"]],"why":"The **image** is built from the Dockerfile, stored in a registry (Azure Container Registry), and run anywhere as a container. No hand installs.","id":"dk.i.07.2"},
        {"t":"choice","topic":"Docker","q":"In the image name `myregistry.azurecr.io/myapp:1.0`, what is `myapp`?","a":"The repository","w":["The registry","The tag","The container name"],"why":"The format is `registry/repository:tag`. Here the registry is `myregistry.azurecr.io` and the tag is `1.0`.","id":"dk.i.07.3"},
        {"t":"bug","topic":"Docker","q":"Ravi wants to push his image to his Azure registry, but one line does not send it there. Which line?","code":["docker build -t myapp:1.0 .","docker tag myapp:1.0 myregistry.azurecr.io/myapp:1.0","docker push myapp:1.0"],"lang":"shell","bad":2,"why":"The image name tells Docker which registry to push to. Without the registry name, Docker pushes to Docker Hub.","id":"dk.i.07.4"},
        {"t":"order","topic":"Docker","q":"Order the steps to share an image through a registry.","items":["`docker build -t myapp:1.0 .`","`docker tag myapp:1.0 myregistry.azurecr.io/myapp:1.0`","`docker push myregistry.azurecr.io/myapp:1.0`","`docker pull myregistry.azurecr.io/myapp:1.0`"],"why":"Build the image, tag it with the registry name, push it, and then any other machine can pull it.","id":"dk.i.07.5"},
        {"t":"choice","topic":"Docker","q":"Which command signs you in to a registry such as Docker Hub?","a":"`docker login`","w":["`docker auth`","`docker signin`","`docker connect`"],"why":"`docker login` signs in to a registry. You do this before pushing to a private registry.","id":"dk.i.07.6"},
        {"t":"choice","topic":"Docker","q":"Lakshmi's app needs a database password. Where should it NOT go?","a":"Inside the image, in the Dockerfile","w":["In a secret store","In an environment value given at run time","In a secret passed during build, not saved in the image"],"why":"Anyone who can pull the image can read what is inside it. Pass secrets at run time or use build secrets.","id":"dk.i.07.7"},
        {"t":"check","topic":"Docker","q":"What does `docker tag myapp:1.0 myregistry.azurecr.io/myapp:1.0` do?","a":"Gives the same image a second name that includes the registry","w":["Uploads the image to the registry","Builds a new image","Deletes the old name"],"why":"`docker tag` only adds a new name to an image. `docker push` is the command that uploads.","id":"dk.i.07.8"},
        {"t":"check","topic":"Docker","q":"What happens when you run `docker pull myapp:1.0` with no registry name?","a":"Docker looks for it on Docker Hub","w":["Docker looks on the Azure registry","Docker looks only on your computer","Docker builds it from a Dockerfile"],"why":"Docker Hub is the default registry when the name has no registry part.","id":"dk.i.07.9"}
      ] },
      { id: "dk.a.08", level: "a", n: 8, title: "Multi-stage builds", cards: [
        {"t":"learn","topic":"Docker","title":"Multi-stage builds","body":"A multi-stage Dockerfile has several `FROM` lines. Each `FROM` starts a new **stage**. Build your app in one stage, then use `COPY --from=build` to take only the result into the last stage, so the final image has no compilers or source code.","code":"FROM node:22 AS build\nCOPY . /app\nRUN cd /app && npm ci && npm run build\nFROM nginx:alpine\nCOPY --from=build /app/dist /usr/share/nginx/html","lang":"docker","id":"dk.a.08.1"},
        {"t":"choice","topic":"Docker","q":"What does `COPY --from=build /app/out /srv` do in a multi-stage Dockerfile?","a":"Copies /app/out from the earlier stage named `build` into this stage","w":["Downloads /app/out from a website called build","Copies a folder named build from your laptop","Copies files out of a running container named build"],"why":"`--from` points to an earlier stage (by name or number) or to another image. It is not a URL or a running container.","id":"dk.a.08.2"},
        {"t":"choice","topic":"Docker","q":"Ravi wants to build only the stage named `build` and stop there. Which command does that?","a":"`docker build --target build .`","w":["`docker build --tag build .`","`docker build --file build .`","`docker build --no-cache .`"],"why":"`--target` builds up to the named stage and stops. `--tag` only names the image, `--file` picks a different Dockerfile, and `--no-cache` just ignores the cache.","id":"dk.a.08.3"},
        {"t":"bug","topic":"Docker","q":"The build tries to pull an image called `builder` and fails. Which line is wrong?","code":["FROM node:22 AS build","WORKDIR /app","COPY . .","RUN npm ci && npm run build","FROM nginx:alpine","COPY --from=builder /app/dist /usr/share/nginx/html"],"lang":"docker","bad":5,"why":"The first stage is named `build`, not `builder`. When the name matches no stage, Docker thinks it is an image name and tries to pull it.","fix":"COPY --from=build /app/dist /usr/share/nginx/html","id":"dk.a.08.4"},
        {"t":"order","topic":"Docker","q":"Put these Dockerfile lines in the best order so the slow `npm ci` layer stays cached when only your source code changes.","items":["COPY package*.json ./","RUN npm ci","COPY . .","RUN npm run build"],"why":"A layer is reused only if everything before it is unchanged. Copy the dependency files first, install, then copy the code that changes often.","id":"dk.a.08.5"},
        {"t":"pipe","topic":"Docker","q":"Build a multi-stage Dockerfile flow. Drop the block that does not belong.","steps":[["BASE","Start the build stage","src"],["BLD","Build the app"],["SLIM","Start a small final stage"],["CPY","COPY --from=build the result"]],"extra":[["GCC","Install a compiler in the final stage"]],"why":"Compilers belong only in the build stage. The final stage just copies the finished app, so the image stays small and has less to attack.","id":"dk.a.08.6"},
        {"t":"choice","topic":"Docker","q":"Why is the final image of a multi-stage build usually smaller and safer?","a":"It contains only the files you copied into it, not the build tools or source code","w":["Docker deletes the last layer of every image automatically","Multi-stage images always run as a non-root user","The final stage is not allowed to use `RUN` commands"],"why":"Earlier stages are used for building and are not part of the final image. Nothing is removed by magic, you choose what to copy.","id":"dk.a.08.7"},
        {"t":"check","topic":"Docker","q":"Lakshmi needs a compiled file from the stage named `build` in her final stage. Which line does it?","a":"`COPY --from=build /app/server /server`","w":["`COPY --chown=build /app/server /server`","`COPY build:/app/server /server`","`RUN cp build/app/server /server`"],"why":"`--from` selects the stage. `--chown` only sets the file owner. The other two are not how Dockerfiles reach another stage.","id":"dk.a.08.8"},
        {"t":"check","topic":"Docker","q":"A Dockerfile copies `package.json`, runs `npm ci`, then runs `COPY . .`. You edit one source file and build again. What happens to the `npm ci` layer?","a":"It is reused from the cache because package.json did not change","w":["It always runs again, because any file change breaks all layers","It is deleted from the image","It is skipped, so the packages are missing from the image"],"why":"Docker reuses a layer when its instruction and the files it uses are unchanged. Only layers after the first changed one are rebuilt.","id":"dk.a.08.9"}
      ] },
      { id: "dk.a.09", level: "a", n: 9, title: "Image size and security", cards: [
        {"t":"learn","topic":"Docker","title":"Smaller and safer images","body":"Small images download fast and have less software to attack. Use a **slim** or **distroless** base, add a `.dockerignore`, and run as a **non-root** user with `USER`. Never put passwords inside an image.","code":"FROM python:3-slim\nRUN useradd --create-home app\nUSER app\nCMD [\"python\", \"app.py\"]","lang":"docker","id":"dk.a.09.1"},
        {"t":"choice","topic":"Docker","q":"What does a `.dockerignore` file do?","a":"Lists files and folders that are left out of the build context sent to the builder","w":["Stops running containers from reading those files","Hides the listed containers from `docker ps`","Removes the listed files from the base image"],"why":"It works at build time. Leaving out `.git`, `node_modules` or secret files makes builds faster and keeps them out of `COPY . .`.","id":"dk.a.09.2"},
        {"t":"bug","topic":"Docker","q":"Asha's build needs a token to download data. Anyone with the image can read the token with `docker history`. Which line is the problem?","code":["FROM python:3-slim","WORKDIR /app","COPY . .","RUN API_TOKEN=abc123 ./download-data.sh","CMD [\"python\", \"app.py\"]"],"lang":"docker","bad":3,"why":"The full `RUN` command, token included, is saved in the image history. Use a build secret with `RUN --mount=type=secret` and `docker build --secret id=api_token,env=API_TOKEN .`, so the token is never stored in a layer.","fix":"RUN --mount=type=secret,id=api_token,env=API_TOKEN ./download-data.sh","id":"dk.a.09.3"},
        {"t":"choice","topic":"Docker","q":"Why is it better to run `apt-get install` and the cache clean-up in the same `RUN` line?","a":"A file deleted in a later layer still stays in the earlier layer, so cleaning in the same layer is what shrinks the image","w":["Docker allows only one `apt-get` command in a Dockerfile","A second `RUN` line runs before the first one","It makes the container run as root"],"why":"Layers only add up. Cleaning in a separate `RUN` hides the files but the earlier layer still carries their size.","id":"dk.a.09.4"},
        {"t":"choice","topic":"Docker","q":"What is the strongest way to always get the exact same base image?","a":"Pin the image by its digest, like `python@sha256:...`","w":["Use the `latest` tag","Add the image folder to `.dockerignore`","Build with `--no-cache` every time"],"why":"Tags can move to a new image, but a digest points to one exact image. `latest` is just a tag and changes over time.","id":"dk.a.09.5"},
        {"t":"indent","topic":"Docker","step":2,"q":"Fix the indentation of this compose file. The service runs as a normal user, with a read-only file system and a health check.","lang":"compose","lines":[[0,"services:"],[2,"web:"],[4,"image: myapp:1.0"],[4,"user: \"1000:1000\""],[4,"read_only: true"],[4,"healthcheck:"],[6,"test: [\"CMD\", \"curl\", \"-f\", \"http://localhost:8080/health\"]"],[6,"interval: 30s"]],"why":"`user`, `read_only` and `healthcheck` belong to the service `web`. The `test` and `interval` keys belong inside `healthcheck`.","id":"dk.a.09.6"},
        {"t":"choice","topic":"Docker","q":"Ravi's final stage uses a distroless base image. Why does `docker exec -it web sh` fail?","why":"Less software means less to attack, but also no `sh` for debugging. Many teams debug with a normal image and ship the distroless one.","a":"Distroless images hold only the app and what it needs to run, with no shell or package manager","w":["Distroless images run only on Kubernetes","`docker exec` works only on stopped containers","Distroless images have no file system at all"],"id":"dk.a.09.7"},
        {"t":"check","topic":"Docker","q":"What does the `HEALTHCHECK` instruction do?","a":"Runs a command inside the container from time to time to test that the app still works","w":["Scans the image for known security problems","Checks the Dockerfile for spelling mistakes","Blocks all network traffic to the container"],"why":"Docker shows the container as healthy or unhealthy based on the command result. Scanning images is done by tools such as `docker scout`.","id":"dk.a.09.8"},
        {"t":"check","topic":"Docker","q":"Which Dockerfile line makes the main process run as a non-root user called `app`?","a":"`USER app`","w":["`WORKDIR /home/app`","`EXPOSE 8080`","`ENV USER=app`"],"why":"`USER` sets the user for the next instructions and for the running container. `WORKDIR` only changes the folder, `EXPOSE` documents a port, and `ENV` only sets a variable.","id":"dk.a.09.9"}
      ] }
    ] },
    { id: "k8s", name: "Kubernetes", topic: "Kubernetes", blurb: "Run containers at scale: pods, deployments and services.", units: [
      { id: "k8s.b.01", level: "b", n: 1, title: "Cluster, node, pod", cards: [
        {"t":"learn","topic":"Kubernetes","title":"Pods, nodes and clusters","body":"A **pod** runs one or more containers. Pods run on **nodes** (machines), and all the nodes together form the **cluster**. Kubernetes restarts and moves pods to keep your app running.","id":"k8s.b.01.1"},
        {"t":"choice","topic":"Kubernetes","q":"In Kubernetes, what is the smallest unit that runs your container?","a":"A Pod","w":["A Node","A Cluster","A Namespace"],"why":"A **Pod** wraps one (sometimes more) containers. Pods run on **nodes** (machines), and all nodes together make the **cluster**.","id":"k8s.b.01.2"},
        {"t":"choice","topic":"Kubernetes","q":"What does the kubelet do on a node?","a":"It makes sure the containers in pods are running as expected","w":["It stores the cluster's container images","It decides which cloud provider to use","It builds Docker images"],"why":"The kubelet is an agent on every node. It checks that the pods assigned to that node are running.","id":"k8s.b.01.3"},
        {"t":"bug","topic":"Kubernetes","q":"Asha writes a Pod manifest. One line has the wrong apiVersion for a Pod. Which line?","code":["apiVersion: apps/v1","kind: Pod","metadata:","  name: web","spec:","  containers:","    - name: web","      image: nginx"],"lang":"k8s","bad":0,"why":"A Pod uses `apiVersion: v1`. `apps/v1` is used for Deployments.","id":"k8s.b.01.4"},
        {"t":"order","topic":"Kubernetes","q":"Order these from the biggest to the smallest.","items":["Cluster","Node","Pod","Container"],"why":"A cluster has nodes, a node runs pods, and a pod holds one or more containers.","id":"k8s.b.01.5"},
        {"t":"choice","topic":"Kubernetes","q":"A node fails, and the pods on it are lost. A Deployment created those pods. What happens?","a":"The Deployment creates new pods on healthy nodes","w":["The old pods are repaired on the failed node","The whole cluster restarts","Nothing, you must recreate them by hand"],"why":"Pods are not repaired or moved. A controller such as a Deployment makes new replacement pods on nodes that work.","id":"k8s.b.01.6"},
        {"t":"choice","topic":"Kubernetes","q":"What is the job of the control plane?","a":"It manages the cluster and decides where pods run","w":["It runs your application containers only","It stores your source code","It is the screen where you see logs"],"why":"The control plane makes decisions for the cluster. Your apps run on worker nodes.","id":"k8s.b.01.7"},
        {"t":"check","topic":"Kubernetes","q":"What is a node in Kubernetes?","a":"A machine (physical or virtual) that runs pods","w":["A single container","A file that describes an app","A group of clusters"],"why":"A node is a worker machine in the cluster. Pods run on nodes.","id":"k8s.b.01.8"},
        {"t":"check","topic":"Kubernetes","q":"Two containers are in the same pod. How can they talk to each other?","a":"Using localhost, because they share the pod's network","w":["They cannot talk to each other","Only through the internet","Only if they run on different nodes"],"why":"Containers in a pod share the same network, so they can reach each other on `localhost`.","id":"k8s.b.01.9"}
      ] },
      { id: "k8s.b.02", level: "b", n: 2, title: "kubectl basics", cards: [
        {"t":"learn","topic":"Kubernetes","title":"kubectl basics","body":"`kubectl` is the command-line tool to talk to a Kubernetes cluster. Use `get` to list things, `describe` for details, and `logs` to read what a pod printed. Use `apply -f` to create or update things from a YAML file.","code":"kubectl get pods\nkubectl describe pod web\nkubectl logs web\nkubectl apply -f pod.yaml","lang":"shell","id":"k8s.b.02.1"},
        {"t":"choice","topic":"Kubernetes","q":"Which command lists the pods in the current namespace?","a":"`kubectl get pods`","w":["`kubectl list pods`","`kubectl show pods`","`kubectl ps`"],"why":"`kubectl get` lists resources. `get pods` lists pods.","id":"k8s.b.02.2"},
        {"t":"bug","topic":"Kubernetes","q":"Ravi wants to create a pod from the file pod.yaml. One command is wrong. Which line?","code":["kubectl get nodes","kubectl apply pod.yaml","kubectl get pods"],"lang":"shell","bad":1,"why":"You must use `-f` to tell kubectl that the next word is a file.","id":"k8s.b.02.3"},
        {"t":"order","topic":"Kubernetes","q":"Order the steps to create a pod, check it and then remove it.","items":["`kubectl apply -f pod.yaml`","`kubectl get pods`","`kubectl logs web`","`kubectl delete pod web`"],"why":"Create it, see it is running, read its logs, then delete it.","id":"k8s.b.02.4"},
        {"t":"choice","topic":"Kubernetes","q":"Lakshmi's pods are in a namespace called dev. How does she list them?","a":"`kubectl get pods -n dev`","w":["`kubectl get pods dev`","`kubectl get dev pods`","`kubectl get pods --name dev`"],"why":"`-n` (or `--namespace`) picks the namespace. Without it, kubectl uses the current default.","id":"k8s.b.02.5"},
        {"t":"choice","topic":"Kubernetes","q":"A pod is stuck and not starting. Which command shows its details and recent events?","a":"`kubectl describe pod web`","w":["`kubectl get pod web`","`kubectl show pod web`","`kubectl nodes pod web`"],"why":"`describe` shows details and events, which often explain why a pod does not start.","id":"k8s.b.02.6"},
        {"t":"choice","topic":"Kubernetes","q":"What does adding `-o wide` to `kubectl get pods` do?","a":"Shows extra information, such as the node name","w":["Makes the pods larger","Shows the pods of all clusters","Prints the pod's YAML file"],"why":"`-o wide` adds more columns, for example the node where each pod runs.","id":"k8s.b.02.7"},
        {"t":"check","topic":"Kubernetes","q":"Which command creates or updates resources from a YAML file?","a":"`kubectl apply -f file.yaml`","w":["`kubectl get -f file.yaml`","`kubectl run file.yaml`","`kubectl logs file.yaml`"],"why":"`apply -f` reads the file and makes the cluster match it.","id":"k8s.b.02.8"},
        {"t":"check","topic":"Kubernetes","q":"What does `kubectl get nodes` show?","a":"The machines in the cluster","w":["The pods in the cluster","The files in the current folder","The images on your computer"],"why":"`get nodes` lists the nodes. Use `get pods` for pods.","id":"k8s.b.02.9"}
      ] },
      { id: "k8s.b.03", level: "b", n: 3, title: "Deployments", cards: [
        {"t":"learn","topic":"Kubernetes","title":"Deployments","body":"A **Deployment** keeps a set number of identical pods (`replicas`) running. If a pod dies, it creates a new one. When you change the image, it does a **rolling update**, replacing pods a few at a time.","code":"kubectl apply -f deploy.yaml\nkubectl scale deployment web --replicas=5\nkubectl rollout status deployment/web","lang":"shell","id":"k8s.b.03.1"},
        {"t":"indent","topic":"Kubernetes","step":2,"q":"Fix the indentation of this Deployment.","lang":"k8s","lines":[[0,"apiVersion: apps/v1"],[0,"kind: Deployment"],[0,"metadata:"],[2,"name: web"],[0,"spec:"],[2,"replicas: 3"],[2,"selector:"],[4,"matchLabels:"],[6,"app: web"],[2,"template:"],[4,"metadata:"],[6,"labels:"],[8,"app: web"],[4,"spec:"],[6,"containers:"],[8,"- name: web"],[10,"image: nginx"]],"why":"`replicas`, `selector` and `template` are inside `spec`. The containers list is inside `template.spec`.","id":"k8s.b.03.2"},
        {"t":"bug","topic":"Kubernetes","q":"This Deployment fails to apply. One line has the wrong apiVersion. Which line?","code":["apiVersion: v1","kind: Deployment","metadata:","  name: web","spec:","  replicas: 2","  selector:","    matchLabels:","      app: web","  template:","    metadata:","      labels:","        app: web","    spec:","      containers:","        - name: web","          image: nginx"],"lang":"k8s","bad":0,"why":"Deployments use `apps/v1`. Plain `v1` is for Pods and Services.","id":"k8s.b.03.3"},
        {"t":"choice","topic":"Kubernetes","q":"A Deployment has 3 replicas. Someone deletes one of the pods. What happens?","a":"The Deployment creates a new pod to get back to 3","w":["Nothing, it stays at 2 pods","All pods are deleted and recreated","The Deployment is deleted"],"why":"This is self-healing. The Deployment keeps the number of pods you asked for.","id":"k8s.b.03.4"},
        {"t":"choice","topic":"Kubernetes","q":"What is a rolling update?","a":"Pods with the new version replace the old pods a few at a time","w":["All old pods are stopped first, then new ones start","The cluster restarts one node at a time","The pods roll back to the old version"],"why":"Rolling updates let the app keep serving users while it is updated.","id":"k8s.b.03.5"},
        {"t":"choice","topic":"Kubernetes","q":"Which command changes the web Deployment to 5 pods?","a":"`kubectl scale deployment web --replicas=5`","w":["`kubectl replicas web 5`","`kubectl get deployment web --replicas=5`","`kubectl add pods web 5`"],"why":"`kubectl scale` sets the number of replicas.","id":"k8s.b.03.6"},
        {"t":"choice","topic":"Kubernetes","q":"What does `kubectl rollout status deployment/web` do?","a":"Shows whether the update of the Deployment has finished","w":["Rolls the Deployment back","Lists all Deployments","Deletes old pods"],"why":"It waits and reports on the progress of the rollout.","id":"k8s.b.03.7"},
        {"t":"check","topic":"Kubernetes","q":"In a Deployment, what must be true about `selector.matchLabels` and the pod template labels?","a":"They must match","w":["They must be different","Only the template needs labels","They must both be empty"],"why":"The selector tells the Deployment which pods it owns, so it must match the template labels.","id":"k8s.b.03.8"},
        {"t":"check","topic":"Kubernetes","q":"A new version of the app is broken. Which command goes back to the previous version?","a":"`kubectl rollout undo deployment/web`","w":["`kubectl rollout stop deployment/web`","`kubectl delete deployment/web --undo`","`kubectl scale deployment/web --back`"],"why":"`rollout undo` returns to the previous revision.","id":"k8s.b.03.9"}
      ] },
      { id: "k8s.i.04", level: "i", n: 4, title: "Services", cards: [
        {"t":"learn","topic":"Kubernetes","title":"Services","body":"Pod IP addresses change when pods are replaced. A **Service** gives a stable name and IP, and sends traffic to pods chosen by their **labels**. The default type is `ClusterIP`, reachable only inside the cluster.","code":"apiVersion: v1\nkind: Service\nmetadata:\n  name: web\nspec:\n  selector:\n    app: web","lang":"k8s","id":"k8s.i.04.1"},
        {"t":"choice","topic":"Kubernetes","q":"Why do we put a Service in front of pods?","a":"Pod IPs change, but the Service keeps a stable address","w":["Pods cannot run without a Service","A Service makes pods use less memory","A Service stores the pods' files"],"why":"Pods are replaced and get new IPs. The Service stays the same and finds the right pods using labels.","id":"k8s.i.04.2"},
        {"t":"indent","topic":"Kubernetes","step":2,"q":"Fix the indentation of this Service.","lang":"k8s","lines":[[0,"apiVersion: v1"],[0,"kind: Service"],[0,"metadata:"],[2,"name: web"],[0,"spec:"],[2,"selector:"],[4,"app: web"],[2,"ports:"],[4,"- port: 80"],[6,"targetPort: 8080"]],"why":"`selector` and `ports` sit under `spec`. The list item `- port` sits under `ports`, and `targetPort` lines up with `port`.","id":"k8s.i.04.3"},
        {"t":"bug","topic":"Kubernetes","q":"Asha's pods have the label `app: web`, but the Service sends no traffic to them. Which line is wrong?","code":["apiVersion: v1","kind: Service","metadata:","  name: web","spec:","  selector:","    app: shop","  ports:","    - port: 80","      targetPort: 8080"],"lang":"k8s","bad":6,"why":"The `selector` must match the pod labels. With `app: shop` it finds no pods.","id":"k8s.i.04.4"},
        {"t":"choice","topic":"Kubernetes","q":"Which Service type exposes the Service outside the cluster on a port of every node?","a":"`NodePort`","w":["`ClusterIP`","`Pod`","`Selector`"],"why":"`NodePort` opens a port on each node. `ClusterIP` is the default and is only for use inside the cluster.","id":"k8s.i.04.5"},
        {"t":"choice","topic":"Kubernetes","q":"In a Service, what is `targetPort`?","a":"The port on the pods that traffic is sent to","w":["The port on the node's disk","The port that `kubectl` uses","The number of pods to start"],"why":"`port` is the port of the Service. `targetPort` is the port the container listens on.","id":"k8s.i.04.6"},
        {"t":"choice","topic":"Kubernetes","q":"A pod in another namespace wants to call the Service `web` in the namespace `shop`. Which DNS name works?","a":"`web.shop`","w":["`shop.web`","`web/shop`","`shop-web`"],"why":"The DNS name is `<service>.<namespace>`. From the same namespace, `web` alone is enough.","id":"k8s.i.04.7"},
        {"t":"check","topic":"Kubernetes","q":"Which command lists the Services in the current namespace?","a":"`kubectl get svc`","w":["`kubectl get services -all`","`kubectl list svc`","`kubectl describe nodes`"],"why":"`svc` is the short name for `services`, so `kubectl get svc` lists them.","id":"k8s.i.04.8"},
        {"t":"check","topic":"Kubernetes","q":"A Service has the type `ClusterIP`. Who can reach it?","a":"Only things inside the cluster","w":["Anyone on the internet","Only the control plane","Only the pod with the same name"],"why":"`ClusterIP` is the default type. It gives an IP that works only inside the cluster.","id":"k8s.i.04.9"}
      ] },
      { id: "k8s.i.05", level: "i", n: 5, title: "ConfigMaps and Secrets", cards: [
        {"t":"learn","topic":"Kubernetes","title":"ConfigMaps and Secrets","body":"Keep settings out of the image. A **ConfigMap** holds normal settings; a **Secret** holds passwords and tokens. A pod reads them as environment variables or as mounted files.","code":"kubectl create configmap app-config --from-literal=mode=production","lang":"shell","id":"k8s.i.05.1"},
        {"t":"choice","topic":"Kubernetes","q":"Ravi must store a colour setting `blue` and a database password. Where does each go?","a":"The colour in a ConfigMap, the password in a Secret","w":["Both in a ConfigMap","The colour in a Secret, the password in a ConfigMap","Both inside the image"],"why":"ConfigMaps are for non-secret settings. Passwords and tokens belong in a Secret.","id":"k8s.i.05.2"},
        {"t":"choice","topic":"Kubernetes","q":"Secret values in a manifest are base64-encoded. What does that mean?","a":"They are only encoded, not encrypted, so anyone can decode them","w":["They are strongly encrypted","They can be read only by the pod owner","They are deleted after one use"],"why":"Base64 is an encoding, not protection. Control who can read Secrets and never put them in public files.","id":"k8s.i.05.3"},
        {"t":"indent","topic":"Kubernetes","step":2,"q":"Fix the indentation of this pod that loads all values of a ConfigMap as environment variables.","lang":"k8s","lines":[[0,"apiVersion: v1"],[0,"kind: Pod"],[0,"metadata:"],[2,"name: app"],[0,"spec:"],[2,"containers:"],[4,"- name: app"],[6,"image: nginx"],[6,"envFrom:"],[8,"- configMapRef:"],[12,"name: app-config"]],"why":"`envFrom` is a setting of the container. `configMapRef` is a list item under it, and its `name` sits under `configMapRef`.","id":"k8s.i.05.4"},
        {"t":"bug","topic":"Kubernetes","q":"The setting `mode` is stored in the ConfigMap `app-config`, but the pod asks for it from the wrong kind of object. Which line?","code":["apiVersion: v1","kind: Pod","metadata:","  name: app","spec:","  containers:","    - name: app","      image: nginx","      env:","        - name: APP_MODE","          valueFrom:","            secretKeyRef:","              name: app-config","              key: mode"],"lang":"k8s","bad":11,"why":"A value from a ConfigMap uses `configMapKeyRef`. `secretKeyRef` looks for a Secret.","id":"k8s.i.05.5"},
        {"t":"order","topic":"Kubernetes","q":"Order the steps so the pod can read its setting.","items":["`kubectl create configmap app-config --from-literal=mode=production`","`kubectl apply -f pod.yaml`","`kubectl get pods`"],"why":"Create the ConfigMap first, because the pod needs it. Then apply the pod and check its status.","id":"k8s.i.05.6"},
        {"t":"choice","topic":"Kubernetes","q":"Which command creates a Secret from a literal value?","a":"`kubectl create secret generic`","w":["`kubectl create password`","`kubectl secret new`","`kubectl apply secret`"],"why":"`kubectl create secret generic NAME --from-literal=key=value` makes a Secret. Do not type real passwords in shared places.","id":"k8s.i.05.7"},
        {"t":"check","topic":"Kubernetes","q":"Why keep configuration in a ConfigMap instead of inside the image?","a":"You can change the setting without rebuilding the image","w":["The image becomes smaller than 1 MB","The pod then never restarts","It makes the setting secret"],"why":"The same image can run in dev and prod with different ConfigMaps.","id":"k8s.i.05.8"},
        {"t":"check","topic":"Kubernetes","q":"Which is a safe way to protect a Secret?","a":"Limit who can read it and do not commit it to a public repository","w":["Rely on base64 to hide it","Put it in the ConfigMap too","Write it in the Dockerfile"],"why":"Base64 hides nothing. Access rules and a private place to keep the manifest are what protect it.","id":"k8s.i.05.9"}
      ] },
      { id: "k8s.i.06", level: "i", n: 6, title: "Scaling", cards: [
        {"t":"learn","topic":"Kubernetes","title":"Scaling pods","body":"Change `replicas` to run more or fewer pods. Use `kubectl scale` for a quick change. A **HorizontalPodAutoscaler** can add pods when CPU use is high.","code":"kubectl scale deployment web --replicas=3\nkubectl autoscale deployment web --min=2 --max=5 --cpu-percent=80","lang":"shell","id":"k8s.i.06.1"},
        {"t":"bug","topic":"Kubernetes","q":"Asha wants 3 copies of her app. One line uses the wrong kind of object. Which line?","code":["kubectl apply -f deployment.yaml","kubectl scale pod web --replicas=3","kubectl get deployment web"],"lang":"shell","bad":1,"why":"`kubectl scale` works on a Deployment, ReplicaSet or StatefulSet. A bare pod has no `replicas`.","id":"k8s.i.06.2"},
        {"t":"choice","topic":"Kubernetes","q":"In `kubectl autoscale deployment web --min=2 --max=5 --cpu-percent=80`, what does `--min=2` mean?","a":"There will always be at least 2 pods","w":["The pods use at least 2 CPUs","Scaling starts after 2 minutes","The Deployment has 2 nodes"],"why":"`--min` and `--max` set the lowest and highest number of pods. `--cpu-percent` is the CPU target.","id":"k8s.i.06.3"},
        {"t":"choice","topic":"Kubernetes","q":"What must be in place for a HorizontalPodAutoscaler to scale on CPU?","a":"A metrics server, and CPU requests set on the pods","w":["A Secret with the CPU limit","A second cluster","Two Services"],"why":"The autoscaler reads CPU numbers from the metrics server, and the percentage is worked out against the pods' CPU requests.","id":"k8s.i.06.4"},
        {"t":"choice","topic":"Kubernetes","q":"What is the difference between a resource request and a limit?","a":"A request is the amount reserved for scheduling; a limit is the most a container may use","w":["A request is the most a container may use; a limit is the amount reserved","They mean the same thing","A request is for memory only; a limit is for CPU only"],"why":"The scheduler places a pod using its requests. Limits stop a container from using more than its share.","id":"k8s.i.06.5"},
        {"t":"indent","topic":"Kubernetes","step":2,"q":"Fix the indentation of this HorizontalPodAutoscaler.","lang":"k8s","lines":[[0,"apiVersion: autoscaling/v2"],[0,"kind: HorizontalPodAutoscaler"],[0,"metadata:"],[2,"name: web"],[0,"spec:"],[2,"scaleTargetRef:"],[4,"apiVersion: apps/v1"],[4,"kind: Deployment"],[4,"name: web"],[2,"minReplicas: 2"],[2,"maxReplicas: 5"],[2,"metrics:"],[4,"- type: Resource"],[6,"resource:"],[8,"name: cpu"],[8,"target:"],[10,"type: Utilization"],[10,"averageUtilization: 80"]],"why":"`scaleTargetRef`, the replica limits and `metrics` sit under `spec`. The CPU target is nested under `resource`.","id":"k8s.i.06.6"},
        {"t":"choice","topic":"Kubernetes","q":"Ravi runs `kubectl scale deployment web --replicas=5`. What does Kubernetes do?","a":"It works to keep 5 pods of `web` running","w":["It adds 5 more pods to the existing ones","It adds 5 nodes","It restarts the Deployment once"],"why":"`replicas` is the wanted total. Kubernetes starts or stops pods until the count matches.","id":"k8s.i.06.7"},
        {"t":"check","topic":"Kubernetes","q":"Which command lists the autoscalers in a namespace?","a":"`kubectl get hpa`","w":["`kubectl get scale`","`kubectl get autoscale pods`","`kubectl describe replicas`"],"why":"`hpa` is the short name for HorizontalPodAutoscaler.","id":"k8s.i.06.8"},
        {"t":"check","topic":"Kubernetes","q":"Lakshmi's app gets busy at night. She wants the pod count to rise and fall by itself. What should she use?","a":"A HorizontalPodAutoscaler","w":["A ConfigMap","A NodePort Service","A larger image"],"why":"A HorizontalPodAutoscaler changes the number of pods based on a metric such as CPU use.","id":"k8s.i.06.9"}
      ] },
      { id: "k8s.a.07", level: "a", n: 7, title: "Ingress", cards: [
        {"t":"learn","topic":"Kubernetes","title":"Ingress: one door for many apps","body":"An **Ingress** sends HTTP and HTTPS traffic to different Services by **host** and **path**, so many apps can share one entry point. It only works if an **Ingress controller** (for example Traefik or Contour) is installed, and `ingressClassName` says which controller handles it. The Ingress API is frozen, and Kubernetes now recommends the newer **Gateway API** for new work.","code":"spec:\n  ingressClassName: traefik\n  rules:\n  - host: shop.example.com","lang":"k8s","id":"k8s.a.07.1"},
        {"t":"choice","topic":"Kubernetes","q":"Ravi creates an Ingress, but nothing happens. What must be installed in the cluster for any Ingress to work?","a":"An Ingress controller, such as Traefik or Contour","w":["A NetworkPolicy that allows all traffic","A PersistentVolume for the routing table","A ConfigMap named ingress-rules"],"why":"The Ingress object is only a set of rules. A controller reads the rules and does the real routing, so creating the Ingress alone has no effect.","id":"k8s.a.07.2"},
        {"t":"indent","topic":"Kubernetes","step":2,"q":"Fix the indentation of this Ingress `paths` block so /api goes to the api Service on port 8080.","lang":"k8s","lines":[[0,"paths:"],[0,"- path: /api"],[2,"pathType: Prefix"],[2,"backend:"],[4,"service:"],[6,"name: api"],[6,"port:"],[8,"number: 8080"]],"why":"`pathType` and `backend` belong to the same path item, so they line up with `path`. `service` holds `name` and `port`, and `number` sits under `port`.","id":"k8s.a.07.3"},
        {"t":"bug","topic":"Kubernetes","q":"Asha's `networking.k8s.io/v1` Ingress is rejected. One line uses a field from the old beta Ingress API, which Kubernetes has removed. Which line is wrong?","code":["backend:","  service:","    serviceName: web","    port:","      number: 80"],"lang":"k8s","bad":2,"fix":"    name: web","why":"In `networking.k8s.io/v1` the backend is `service.name` plus `service.port.number`. `serviceName` belonged to the removed beta API, so do not copy it from old blog posts.","id":"k8s.a.07.4"},
        {"t":"choice","topic":"Kubernetes","q":"An Ingress rule has `path: /shop` with `pathType: Prefix`. Which request path matches it?","a":"/shop/cart","w":["/shopping","/cart/shop","/Shop/cart"],"why":"Prefix matching works on whole path elements split by `/`, and it is case sensitive. So `/shop/cart` matches, but `/shopping` and `/Shop/cart` do not.","id":"k8s.a.07.5"},
        {"t":"pipe","topic":"Kubernetes","q":"Build the path of a web request when your app is exposed with an Ingress. Remove the block that does not belong.","steps":[["USER","Browser sends request","src"],["ING","Ingress controller applies the rules"],["SVC","Service picks a Pod"],["POD","Pod answers"]],"extra":[["PVC","PersistentVolumeClaim"]],"why":"A request reaches the Ingress controller first, then the Service, then one of its Pods. A PersistentVolumeClaim is for storage, not for routing traffic.","id":"k8s.a.07.6"},
        {"t":"check","topic":"Kubernetes","q":"The newer Kubernetes Gateway API describes HTTP routing rules in which resource?","a":"HTTPRoute","w":["IngressRoute","RouteRule","VirtualService"],"why":"Kubernetes now recommends Gateway API, which uses `Gateway` for the entry point and `HTTPRoute` for HTTP routing rules. `VirtualService` belongs to Istio and `IngressRoute` to Traefik.","id":"k8s.a.07.7"},
        {"t":"check","topic":"Kubernetes","q":"Lakshmi wants HTTPS for `shop.example.com` on an Ingress. Where does the Ingress get its certificate and private key?","a":"From a Secret whose name is given in `spec.tls`","w":["From a ConfigMap listed under `data`","From the `targetPort` of the Service","From the value of `ingressClassName`"],"why":"The `tls` section lists the hosts and a `secretName`. That Secret holds the certificate and key. A ConfigMap is not for private keys.","id":"k8s.a.07.8"}
      ] },
      { id: "k8s.a.08", level: "a", n: 8, title: "Helm", cards: [
        {"t":"learn","topic":"Kubernetes","title":"Helm: a package manager for Kubernetes","body":"A **chart** is a package of templates plus a `values.yaml` file with default settings. Installing a chart creates a **release**, one named copy in your cluster that you can upgrade, roll back or uninstall by its name. You change settings with `-f my-values.yaml` or `--set key=value`, and these override the chart's defaults.","code":"helm repo add myrepo https://example.com/charts\nhelm install myrel myrepo/mychart\nhelm upgrade myrel myrepo/mychart -f my-values.yaml\nhelm list","lang":"shell","id":"k8s.a.08.1"},
        {"t":"choice","topic":"Kubernetes","q":"In Helm, what is a **release**?","a":"One installed instance of a chart in a cluster, with its own name","w":["The folder of templates before you install it","A Kubernetes namespace that Helm creates","The version number of the helm program"],"why":"A chart is the package. A release is one running installation of it. You can install the same chart many times, each with a different release name.","id":"k8s.a.08.2"},
        {"t":"choice","topic":"Kubernetes","q":"The chart's `values.yaml` has `replicaCount: 1`, and `prod.yaml` has `replicaCount: 2`. Ravi runs `helm install myrel ./mychart -f prod.yaml --set replicaCount=3`. Which value is used?","a":"3","w":["1","2","Helm shows an error because the value is set twice"],"why":"`--set` wins over a values file given with `-f`, and a `-f` file wins over the chart's own defaults.","id":"k8s.a.08.3"},
        {"t":"bug","topic":"Kubernetes","q":"Asha runs these commands, but the install fails because Helm has no release name. Which line is wrong?","code":["helm repo add myrepo https://example.com/charts","helm repo update","helm install myrepo/mychart"],"lang":"shell","bad":2,"fix":"helm install myrel myrepo/mychart","why":"The form is `helm install RELEASE_NAME CHART`. Without a name, Helm can only continue if you add `--generate-name`.","id":"k8s.a.08.4"},
        {"t":"indent","topic":"Kubernetes","step":2,"q":"Fix the indentation of this chart's `values.yaml`.","lang":"k8s","lines":[[0,"replicaCount: 2"],[0,"image:"],[2,"repository: nginx"],[2,"pullPolicy: IfNotPresent"],[0,"service:"],[2,"type: ClusterIP"],[2,"port: 80"]],"why":"Top-level keys start at the left edge. The settings inside `image` and `service` are indented by 2 spaces. Templates read them as `.Values.image.repository`.","id":"k8s.a.08.5"},
        {"t":"order","topic":"Kubernetes","q":"Put these Helm commands in a sensible order: install, upgrade, look at the history, then go back to the first version.","items":["helm install myrel ./mychart","helm upgrade myrel ./mychart --set replicaCount=3","helm history myrel","helm rollback myrel 1"],"why":"Each install or upgrade creates a numbered revision. `helm history` shows the numbers, and `helm rollback myrel 1` goes back to revision 1.","id":"k8s.a.08.6"},
        {"t":"choice","topic":"Kubernetes","q":"What does `helm uninstall myrel` do?","a":"Removes the release and the Kubernetes resources it created","w":["Deletes the chart from the chart repository","Stops the Pods but keeps the Deployment","Removes the helm program from your computer"],"why":"Uninstall works on a release, not on the chart or the helm program. Resources created by that release are removed.","id":"k8s.a.08.7"},
        {"t":"check","topic":"Kubernetes","q":"Which file in a chart holds the default values that users can override?","a":"values.yaml","w":["Chart.yaml","templates/NOTES.txt",".helmignore"],"why":"`values.yaml` has the defaults. `Chart.yaml` has chart details such as the name and version. `.helmignore` lists files to leave out of the package.","id":"k8s.a.08.8"},
        {"t":"check","topic":"Kubernetes","q":"Which command lists the releases installed in your current namespace?","a":"`helm list`","w":["`helm repo list`","`helm search repo`","`helm show values`"],"why":"`helm repo list` shows added repositories, `helm search repo` finds charts, and `helm show values` prints a chart's default values. Only `helm list` shows releases.","id":"k8s.a.08.9"}
      ] },
      { id: "k8s.a.09", level: "a", n: 9, title: "RBAC", cards: [
        {"t":"learn","topic":"Kubernetes","title":"RBAC: who can do what","body":"**RBAC** decides which users, groups and ServiceAccounts can do which actions. A **Role** lists permissions in one namespace, and a **RoleBinding** gives that Role to someone. Each rule names `apiGroups`, `resources` and `verbs`, and permissions only add up, so give the least needed.","code":"rules:\n- apiGroups: [\"\"]\n  resources: [\"pods\"]\n  verbs: [\"get\", \"list\", \"watch\"]","lang":"k8s","id":"k8s.a.09.1"},
        {"t":"choice","topic":"Kubernetes","q":"Nodes are not inside any namespace. Which object can grant permissions on them?","a":"A ClusterRole","w":["A Role","A RoleBinding that points to a Role","A Namespace"],"why":"A Role works only inside one namespace. Cluster-scoped resources such as nodes need a ClusterRole.","id":"k8s.a.09.2"},
        {"t":"indent","topic":"Kubernetes","step":2,"q":"Fix the indentation of this RoleBinding part. It gives the Role `pod-reader` to the ServiceAccount `asha-app`.","lang":"k8s","lines":[[0,"subjects:"],[0,"- kind: ServiceAccount"],[2,"name: asha-app"],[2,"namespace: dev"],[0,"roleRef:"],[2,"kind: Role"],[2,"name: pod-reader"],[2,"apiGroup: rbac.authorization.k8s.io"]],"why":"`subjects` is a list of who gets the access, and `roleRef` says which Role they get. The fields of each are indented 2 spaces under their parent.","id":"k8s.a.09.3"},
        {"t":"bug","topic":"Kubernetes","q":"This Role should let people list Pods, but it gives no access to Pods. Which line is wrong?","code":["rules:","- apiGroups: [\"apps\"]","  resources: [\"pods\"]","  verbs: [\"get\", \"list\"]"],"lang":"k8s","bad":1,"fix":"- apiGroups: [\"\"]","why":"Pods are in the **core** API group, which is written as an empty string. The `apps` group has Deployments and similar objects.","id":"k8s.a.09.4"},
        {"t":"choice","topic":"Kubernetes","q":"Which command checks whether you are allowed to delete Pods in the current namespace?","a":"`kubectl auth can-i delete pods`","w":["`kubectl get roles`","`kubectl config view`","`kubectl describe pods`"],"why":"`kubectl auth can-i VERB RESOURCE` asks the API server and answers yes or no. Listing Roles does not show what you personally can do.","id":"k8s.a.09.5"},
        {"t":"order","topic":"Kubernetes","q":"Put the steps of an API request in the order Kubernetes handles them.","items":["Authentication: who are you?","Authorization: are you allowed (RBAC)?","Admission control","The object is stored"],"why":"The API server first finds out who you are, then checks permissions, then runs admission control. Only then is the object saved.","id":"k8s.a.09.6"},
        {"t":"choice","topic":"Kubernetes","q":"A Pod does not set `serviceAccountName`. Which ServiceAccount does it use?","a":"The `default` ServiceAccount of its own namespace","w":["The `admin` ServiceAccount of kube-system","None, because a Pod has no identity","A ServiceAccount bound to `cluster-admin`"],"why":"Every namespace has a `default` ServiceAccount, and Pods use it unless you choose another. Give each app its own ServiceAccount with only the access it needs.","id":"k8s.a.09.7"},
        {"t":"check","topic":"Kubernetes","q":"A RoleBinding in the namespace `dev` points to a ClusterRole that allows reading Pods. Where can the subject read Pods?","a":"Only in the namespace `dev`","w":["In all namespaces","Nowhere, because a RoleBinding can only point to a Role","Only on nodes"],"why":"A RoleBinding can use a ClusterRole as a shared set of rules, but the access stays inside the RoleBinding's namespace. Use a ClusterRoleBinding for cluster-wide access.","id":"k8s.a.09.8"},
        {"t":"check","topic":"Kubernetes","q":"Asha has one RoleBinding that allows `get` on Pods and another that allows `delete` on Pods. What can she do?","a":"Both get and delete, because permissions add up","w":["Only get, because the first binding wins","Only delete, because the last binding wins","Nothing, because the two bindings cancel each other"],"why":"RBAC is additive. It only grants permissions and does not have deny rules, so all matching bindings are combined.","id":"k8s.a.09.9"}
      ] },
      { id: "k8s.a.10", level: "a", n: 10, title: "AKS on Azure", cards: [
        {"t":"learn","topic":"Kubernetes","title":"AKS: Kubernetes on Azure","body":"**Azure Kubernetes Service (AKS)** is Kubernetes where Azure runs the **control plane** for you, and your apps run on the **node pools** you create. You create a cluster with `az aks create`, then `az aks get-credentials` sets up `kubectl` to talk to it. Practice clusters keep costing money, so stop or delete them when you finish.","code":"az group create --name rg-demo --location centralindia\naz aks create -g rg-demo -n demo-aks --node-count 2 --generate-ssh-keys\naz aks get-credentials -g rg-demo -n demo-aks\nkubectl get nodes","lang":"shell","id":"k8s.a.10.1"},
        {"t":"choice","topic":"Kubernetes","q":"In AKS, who runs and manages the Kubernetes control plane?","a":"Azure does","w":["You, by logging in to the control plane servers","Your Azure Container Registry","Each node pool, by itself"],"why":"AKS creates and manages the control plane for you. You work with the nodes your apps run on and with `kubectl`.","id":"k8s.a.10.2"},
        {"t":"choice","topic":"Kubernetes","q":"What does `az aks get-credentials` do?","a":"Adds the cluster's connection details to your kubeconfig so `kubectl` can use it","w":["Creates a new AKS cluster","Downloads the container images to your laptop","Gives your Azure user the Owner role on the cluster"],"why":"By default the credentials are merged into your kubeconfig file. After that, `kubectl` commands go to the AKS cluster.","id":"k8s.a.10.3"},
        {"t":"bug","topic":"Kubernetes","q":"Ravi wants `kubectl get nodes` to reach his new AKS cluster, but `kubectl` does not know the cluster. Which line is wrong?","code":["az group create --name rg-demo --location centralindia","az aks create -g rg-demo -n demo-aks --generate-ssh-keys","az aks show -g rg-demo -n demo-aks","kubectl get nodes"],"lang":"shell","bad":2,"fix":"az aks get-credentials -g rg-demo -n demo-aks","why":"`az aks show` only prints details of the cluster. `az aks get-credentials` is the command that sets up `kubectl`.","id":"k8s.a.10.4"},
        {"t":"pipe","topic":"Kubernetes","q":"Build the path of your app image from your laptop to a running Pod on AKS. Remove the block that does not belong.","steps":[["DEV","Build the image","src"],["ACR","Push to Azure Container Registry"],["AKS","AKS pulls the image"],["POD","Pods run the app"]],"extra":[["SQL","Run a SQL query"]],"why":"The image is built, stored in a registry such as Azure Container Registry, pulled by the cluster, and run as Pods. A SQL query is not a step in shipping an image.","id":"k8s.a.10.5"},
        {"t":"choice","topic":"Kubernetes","q":"Which AKS node pool mode is mainly meant for critical system Pods such as CoreDNS and metrics-server?","a":"System","w":["User","Spot","Windows"],"why":"System node pools mainly host the system Pods. User node pools are where you put your app Pods.","id":"k8s.a.10.6"},
        {"t":"choice","topic":"Kubernetes","q":"Some Pods stay Pending because no node has enough room. Which AKS feature can add nodes automatically?","a":"The cluster autoscaler","w":["The Horizontal Pod Autoscaler","An Ingress controller","The `az aks stop` command"],"why":"The cluster autoscaler adds or removes **nodes**. The Horizontal Pod Autoscaler changes the number of **Pods**, so it cannot fix a full cluster by itself.","id":"k8s.a.10.7"},
        {"t":"choice","topic":"Kubernetes","q":"Lakshmi finished practice for today and wants to stop the cluster but start it again later. Which command does that?","a":"`az aks stop -g rg-demo -n demo-aks`","w":["`az aks show -g rg-demo -n demo-aks`","`kubectl delete pods --all`","`az aks get-credentials -g rg-demo -n demo-aks`"],"why":"`az aks stop` stops the cluster, and `az aks start` brings it back. Deleting Pods does not stop the nodes.","id":"k8s.a.10.8"},
        {"t":"check","topic":"Kubernetes","q":"Which `az aks create` option lets the cluster pull images from your Azure Container Registry?","a":"`--attach-acr`","w":["`--generate-ssh-keys`","`--node-count`","`--enable-cluster-autoscaler`"],"why":"`--attach-acr` gives the cluster's identity the AcrPull role on the named registry. The other options make SSH keys, set the starting node count, and turn on the cluster autoscaler.","id":"k8s.a.10.9"},
        {"t":"check","topic":"Kubernetes","q":"Practice is over and you will not need the cluster again. It is the only thing in the resource group `rg-demo`. What removes it for good?","a":"`az group delete --name rg-demo`","w":["`az aks stop -g rg-demo -n demo-aks`","`kubectl delete namespace default`","`az aks get-credentials -g rg-demo -n demo-aks`"],"why":"Deleting the resource group removes everything in it, so nothing keeps costing money. `az aks stop` only pauses the cluster and keeps its resources.","id":"k8s.a.10.10"}
      ] }
    ] },
    { id: "ans", name: "Ansible", topic: "Ansible", blurb: "Automate servers with simple YAML playbooks.", units: [
      { id: "ans.b.01", level: "b", n: 1, title: "Inventory", cards: [
        {"t":"learn","topic":"Ansible","title":"The inventory","body":"The **inventory** lists your servers in groups, like `[web]` with `web1` and `web2`. Playbooks then target a group with `hosts: web`. Ansible connects over SSH; no agent is needed on the servers.","code":"[web]\nweb1\nweb2","lang":"ini","id":"ans.b.01.1"},
        {"t":"choice","topic":"Ansible","q":"Where do you list the servers that Ansible manages?","a":"The inventory file","w":["The handlers section","The Dockerfile","A Jinja2 template"],"why":"The **inventory** lists hosts and groups, like `[web]` with `web1` and `web2`. A playbook then targets a group with `hosts: web`.","id":"ans.b.01.2"},
        {"t":"order","topic":"Ansible","q":"What happens when you run ansible-playbook? Order the steps.","items":["Read the inventory","Connect to each server over SSH","Run the tasks in order","Show the PLAY RECAP"],"why":"Ansible needs **no agent** on the servers: it reads the inventory, connects with SSH, runs each task top to bottom, then prints a summary.","id":"ans.b.01.3"},
        {"t":"choice","topic":"Ansible","q":"Which group name always exists in Ansible, and contains every host in your inventory?","a":"all","w":["servers","default","everyone"],"why":"Ansible always gives you the built-in group `all`. You do not need to write it in the file.","id":"ans.b.01.4"},
        {"t":"choice","topic":"Ansible","q":"In an inventory line `web1 ansible_host=10.0.0.5`, what does `ansible_host` do?","a":"It tells Ansible which IP address or DNS name to connect to","w":["It sets the name of the group","It installs software on the host","It sets the password of the host"],"why":"`web1` is just a short name. `ansible_host` is the real address Ansible connects to.","id":"ans.b.01.5"},
        {"t":"bug","topic":"Ansible","q":"Ravi wrote this inventory, but Ansible cannot read the host variable on one line. Which line is wrong?","code":["[web]","web1 ansible_host=10.0.0.10","web2 ansible_host: 10.0.0.11","[db]","db1 ansible_host=10.0.0.20"],"lang":"ini","bad":2,"why":"In an INI inventory, variables are written as `key=value` with no colon. The colon style is for YAML inventories.","id":"ans.b.01.6"},
        {"t":"choice","topic":"Ansible","q":"Asha keeps her servers in a file called `inventory.ini`. Which option tells Ansible to use it?","a":"`-i inventory.ini`","w":["`-f inventory.ini`","`--hosts inventory.ini`","`-p inventory.ini`"],"why":"`-i` stands for inventory. For example: `ansible all -i inventory.ini -m ping`.","id":"ans.b.01.7"},
        {"t":"check","topic":"Ansible","q":"You write `[web]` and then two server names under it. What have you created?","a":"A group called web with two hosts","w":["A playbook with two tasks","A module called web","Two separate inventories"],"why":"A name in square brackets starts a group. The lines under it are the hosts in that group.","id":"ans.b.01.8"},
        {"t":"check","topic":"Ansible","q":"Which command shows how Ansible sees your hosts and groups as a tree?","a":"`ansible-inventory -i inventory.ini --graph`","w":["`ansible-playbook --graph`","`ansible all -m graph`","`ansible-galaxy --graph`"],"why":"`ansible-inventory` reads your inventory. `--graph` shows groups and hosts as a tree, and `--list` shows JSON.","id":"ans.b.01.9"}
      ] },
      { id: "ans.b.02", level: "b", n: 2, title: "Ad-hoc commands", cards: [
        {"t":"learn","topic":"Ansible","title":"Ad-hoc commands","body":"An **ad-hoc command** is one quick Ansible command from the terminal, with no playbook. The pattern is `ansible <hosts> -m <module> -a \"<arguments>\"`. Use it for small jobs, like checking if servers respond.","code":"ansible all -i inventory.ini -m ping\nansible web -i inventory.ini -m command -a \"uptime\"","lang":"shell","id":"ans.b.02.1"},
        {"t":"choice","topic":"Ansible","q":"What does `ansible all -m ping` check?","a":"That Ansible can connect to the host and use Python there","w":["That the server answers an ICMP ping packet","That the server has internet access","That the server has enough disk space"],"why":"The Ansible `ping` module is not the network ping tool. It tests the connection and that Python is usable on the host.","id":"ans.b.02.2"},
        {"t":"choice","topic":"Ansible","q":"What does the `-b` option do in an ad-hoc command?","a":"It runs the task with higher rights (become, like sudo)","w":["It runs the task in the background","It backs up the file first","It builds a new inventory"],"why":"`-b` means become. Tasks like installing packages need it because they need root rights.","id":"ans.b.02.3"},
        {"t":"bug","topic":"Ansible","q":"Ravi logs in to the servers as a normal user, not root. One command fails with a permission error. Which one?","code":["ansible all -i inventory.ini -m ping","ansible web -i inventory.ini -m command -a \"uptime\"","ansible web -i inventory.ini -m ansible.builtin.apt -a \"name=git state=present\""],"lang":"shell","bad":2,"why":"Installing a package changes the system, so it needs `-b`. The other two only read information.","id":"ans.b.02.4"},
        {"t":"choice","topic":"Ansible","q":"In `ansible web -m ping`, what is `web`?","a":"The host pattern: which hosts or group to run on","w":["The module name","The name of the playbook","The user name to log in with"],"why":"The first word after `ansible` selects the hosts. Here it is the group `web` from your inventory.","id":"ans.b.02.5"},
        {"t":"choice","topic":"Ansible","q":"Lakshmi needs to run the same set of 10 steps on 50 servers every week. What is best?","a":"Write a playbook","w":["Type 10 ad-hoc commands by hand each week","Write the steps in a text file and read it","Run `ansible all -m ping` 10 times"],"why":"Ad-hoc commands are for quick one-off jobs. A playbook keeps repeatable steps in a file you can save and reuse.","id":"ans.b.02.6"},
        {"t":"choice","topic":"Ansible","q":"Which module can run a command that uses a pipe `|` or a redirect `>`?","a":"`shell`","w":["`command`","`ping`","`copy`"],"why":"The `command` module does not go through a shell, so `|`, `>` and `;` do not work. Use `shell` only when you need them; `command` is safer.","id":"ans.b.02.7"},
        {"t":"check","topic":"Ansible","q":"Which command runs `uptime` on every host in the group `web`?","a":"`ansible web -i inventory.ini -m command -a \"uptime\"`","w":["`ansible -m command web -a uptime -i`","`ansible-playbook web -m command uptime`","`ansible uptime -m web`"],"why":"The shape is: `ansible`, then the hosts, then `-m` with the module, then `-a` with the arguments.","id":"ans.b.02.8"},
        {"t":"check","topic":"Ansible","q":"You only need a quick answer, like \"are all servers reachable?\". What is the best tool?","a":"An ad-hoc command","w":["A full playbook with roles","A new inventory file","Logging in to each server by SSH"],"why":"Ad-hoc commands are fast for one-time checks. Use a playbook when the steps must be repeated.","id":"ans.b.02.9"}
      ] },
      { id: "ans.b.03", level: "b", n: 3, title: "Playbook structure", cards: [
        {"t":"learn","topic":"Ansible","title":"Playbooks","body":"A **playbook** is a YAML list of plays. Each play has `hosts` (which servers) and `tasks` (what to do, in order). YAML uses spaces, never tabs: 2 per level here.","code":"- name: Set up web\n  hosts: web\n  tasks:\n    - name: Install nginx\n      ansible.builtin.apt:\n        name: nginx","lang":"yaml","id":"ans.b.03.1"},
        {"t":"indent","topic":"Ansible","step":2,"q":"Fix this Ansible playbook so it installs git on every server.","lines":[[0,"- name: Set up developer tools"],[2,"hosts: all"],[2,"become: true"],[2,"tasks:"],[4,"- name: Install git"],[6,"ansible.builtin.apt:"],[8,"name: git"],[8,"state: present"]],"why":"Play settings (`hosts`, `become`, `tasks`) sit 2 spaces under the play. Each task is a list item under `tasks:`, and the module’s options go one level deeper.","id":"ans.b.03.2"},
        {"t":"choice","topic":"Ansible","q":"What does `ansible-playbook --check site.yml` do?","a":"Shows what would change, without changing the servers","w":["Checks the internet connection","Deletes old tasks from the file","Runs the playbook twice"],"why":"`--check` is a dry run. It predicts changes but does not apply them.","id":"ans.b.03.3"},
        {"t":"choice","topic":"Ansible","q":"What does `ansible-playbook --syntax-check site.yml` do?","a":"Checks the playbook for syntax errors without running it","w":["Runs only the first task","Installs missing modules","Checks if the servers are reachable"],"why":"It reads the file and reports errors in how it is written. It does not connect to any server.","id":"ans.b.03.4"},
        {"t":"bug","topic":"Ansible","q":"This playbook does not work, because one keyword is wrong. Which line is wrong?","code":["---","- name: Install git","  hosts: web","  become: true","  task:","    - name: Install git","      ansible.builtin.apt:","        name: git","        state: present"],"lang":"yaml","bad":4,"why":"The list of tasks must be under the keyword `tasks`. `task` is not a valid play keyword.","id":"ans.b.03.5"},
        {"t":"choice","topic":"Ansible","q":"In the PLAY RECAP, what does `changed=2` mean?","a":"Two tasks changed something on that server","w":["Two tasks failed","Two servers were skipped","Two tasks were already done"],"why":"`changed` counts tasks that made a change. Tasks that were already in the right state count under `ok`.","id":"ans.b.03.6"},
        {"t":"choice","topic":"Ansible","q":"In a playbook, what does `hosts: web` tell Ansible?","a":"Run this play on the group called web","w":["Create a new server called web","Install a package called web","Use the file called web"],"why":"`hosts` picks which inventory hosts or groups the play runs on.","id":"ans.b.03.7"},
        {"t":"check","topic":"Ansible","q":"Which command runs a playbook named `site.yml` with your inventory?","a":"`ansible-playbook -i inventory.ini site.yml`","w":["`ansible -i inventory.ini site.yml`","`ansible-inventory -i inventory.ini site.yml`","`ansible-run site.yml`"],"why":"Playbooks are run with `ansible-playbook`. The plain `ansible` command is for ad-hoc jobs.","id":"ans.b.03.8"},
        {"t":"check","topic":"Ansible","q":"A play has `name`, `hosts`, `become` and `tasks`. What is `become: true` for?","a":"To run the tasks with higher rights (like sudo)","w":["To make the tasks run faster","To skip tasks that fail","To run tasks on every server in the world"],"why":"`become` lets Ansible use root rights, which package installs and service changes need.","id":"ans.b.03.9"}
      ] },
      { id: "ans.b.04", level: "b", n: 4, title: "apt, copy, service", cards: [
        {"t":"learn","topic":"Ansible","title":"Everyday modules","body":"`ansible.builtin.apt` installs packages, `copy` sends files and `service` starts or restarts services. Modules are **idempotent**: run them twice and the second run changes nothing if the server is already right.","id":"ans.b.04.1"},
        {"t":"choice","topic":"Ansible","q":"Which Ansible module installs a package on an Ubuntu server?","a":"ansible.builtin.apt","w":["ansible.builtin.copy","ansible.builtin.service","ansible.builtin.template"],"why":"**apt** is Ubuntu’s package manager, so `ansible.builtin.apt` installs packages. `copy` sends files, `service` starts and stops services.","id":"ans.b.04.2"},
        {"t":"choice","topic":"Ansible","q":"You run the same playbook twice. The second run shows changed=0. What is this good property called?","a":"Idempotency","w":["Inventory","Templating","Encryption"],"why":"**Idempotent** means running it again changes nothing if the server is already right. That makes automation safe to repeat.","id":"ans.b.04.3"},
        {"t":"indent","topic":"Ansible","step":2,"q":"Fix this playbook so nginx starts on the web servers.","lines":[[0,"- name: Start web server"],[2,"hosts: web"],[2,"become: true"],[2,"tasks:"],[4,"- name: Start nginx"],[6,"ansible.builtin.service:"],[8,"name: nginx"],[8,"state: started"]],"why":"`name` and `state` are options of the service module, so they sit 2 spaces deeper than `ansible.builtin.service:`.","id":"ans.b.04.4"},
        {"t":"choice","topic":"Ansible","q":"Which `copy` options say where the file comes from and where it goes?","a":"`src` and `dest`","w":["`from` and `to`","`path` and `target`","`source` and `output`"],"why":"In `ansible.builtin.copy`, `src` is the file on your machine and `dest` is the path on the server.","id":"ans.b.04.5"},
        {"t":"bug","topic":"Ansible","q":"This task should start nginx, but one value is not allowed for the service module. Which line is wrong?","code":["- name: Start nginx","  ansible.builtin.service:","    name: nginx","    state: running","    enabled: true"],"lang":"yaml","bad":3,"why":"The valid states are `started`, `stopped`, `restarted` and `reloaded`. `running` is not one of them.","id":"ans.b.04.6"},
        {"t":"choice","topic":"Ansible","q":"What does `enabled: true` do in the `service` module?","a":"The service starts automatically when the server boots","w":["The service starts right now only","The service is installed","The service is deleted after use"],"why":"`state: started` starts it now. `enabled: true` makes it start on boot.","id":"ans.b.04.7"},
        {"t":"check","topic":"Ansible","q":"Which `apt` option installs a package only if it is missing, and does not upgrade it?","a":"`state: present`","w":["`state: latest`","`state: absent`","`state: started`"],"why":"`present` means \"make sure it is installed\". `latest` upgrades it, and `absent` removes it.","id":"ans.b.04.8"},
        {"t":"check","topic":"Ansible","q":"Ansible modules aim to be idempotent. What does that mean?","a":"Running them again does not change anything that is already correct","w":["They run only once and then delete themselves","They always reinstall everything","They ask you before each change"],"why":"Idempotent means the same result however many times you run it. The second run shows `changed=0`.","id":"ans.b.04.9"}
      ] },
      { id: "ans.i.05", level: "i", n: 5, title: "Variables", cards: [
        {"t":"learn","topic":"Ansible","title":"Variables","body":"Define values once under `vars:` and use them anywhere with `{{ name }}`. If a value starts with `{{`, put it in quotes.","id":"ans.i.05.1"},
        {"t":"indent","topic":"Ansible","step":2,"q":"Fix this playbook that uses a variable for the package name.","lines":[[0,"- name: Set up web"],[2,"hosts: web"],[2,"become: true"],[2,"vars:"],[4,"pkg: nginx"],[2,"tasks:"],[4,"- name: Install the package"],[6,"ansible.builtin.apt:"],[8,"name: \"{{ pkg }}\""]],"why":"The variable `pkg` lives under `vars:` (4 spaces). `{{ pkg }}` is replaced by `nginx` when the task runs.","id":"ans.i.05.2"},
        {"t":"choice","topic":"Ansible","q":"Ravi runs a playbook with `-e pkg=curl`, but the playbook also has `vars: pkg: nginx`. Which value does the task use?","a":"curl, because extra vars from `-e` have the highest precedence","w":["nginx, because `vars:` in the play always wins","Ansible stops with an error because the name is used twice","Both: it installs nginx first and then curl"],"why":"Extra vars given with `-e` override almost every other place a variable can be set. This lets you change a value for one run without editing files.","id":"ans.i.05.3"},
        {"t":"bug","topic":"Ansible","q":"Asha wrote this playbook, but Ansible says the YAML is invalid. Which line is wrong?","lang":"yaml","code":["- name: Say hello","  hosts: all","  vars:","    greeting: Namaste","  tasks:","    - name: Show message","      ansible.builtin.debug:","        msg: {{ greeting }} from Guntur"],"bad":7,"why":"A YAML value that starts with `{{` must be quoted, or YAML reads it as a dictionary. Write `msg: \"{{ greeting }} from Guntur\"`.","id":"ans.i.05.4"},
        {"t":"choice","topic":"Ansible","q":"Lakshmi wants every host in the group `web` to share the variable `http_port`. Where can she put it?","a":"In a file named `group_vars/web.yml` next to the playbook","w":["In a file named `web_vars/group.yml`","In the inventory as a task name","In a handler called `web`"],"why":"Ansible reads `group_vars/<group name>.yml` automatically for hosts in that group. Per-host values go in `host_vars/<host name>.yml`.","id":"ans.i.05.5"},
        {"t":"choice","topic":"Ansible","q":"Which task shows the value of the variable `pkg` while the playbook runs?","a":"`ansible.builtin.debug` with `var: pkg`","w":["`ansible.builtin.ping` with `var: pkg`","`ansible.builtin.copy` with `msg: pkg`","`ansible.builtin.setup` with `show: pkg`"],"why":"The `debug` module prints a message with `msg` or a variable with `var`. With `var` you write the plain name, without `{{ }}`.","id":"ans.i.05.6"},
        {"t":"order","topic":"Ansible","q":"Put the steps in order to use a variable in a playbook.","items":["Define `pkg: nginx` under `vars:`","Quote the value as `\"{{ pkg }}\"` in a task","Run `ansible-playbook` on the file","Ansible replaces `{{ pkg }}` with `nginx`"],"why":"You first define the variable, then use it in double braces, then run the play. Ansible fills in the value when the task runs.","id":"ans.i.05.7"},
        {"t":"check","topic":"Ansible","q":"Which place has the HIGHEST precedence for a variable value?","a":"Extra vars given with `-e` on the command line","w":["`vars:` inside the play","A file in `group_vars/`","The inventory file"],"why":"Extra vars always win over the other places, so use them for a quick change in one run.","id":"ans.i.05.8"},
        {"t":"check","topic":"Ansible","q":"Which line is valid YAML for a Jinja2 variable?","a":"`msg: \"{{ name }}\"`","w":["`msg: {{ name }}`","`msg: {name}`","`msg: ${name}`"],"why":"Quote a value that starts with `{{`. Without quotes YAML treats the braces as a dictionary.","id":"ans.i.05.9"}
      ] },
      { id: "ans.i.06", level: "i", n: 6, title: "Handlers", cards: [
        {"t":"learn","topic":"Ansible","title":"Handlers: run only when something changed","body":"A **handler** is a task that runs only when it is **notified**. A task uses `notify` when it reports `changed`. Handlers run at the **end of the play**, and only once, even if notified many times.","lang":"yaml","code":"- name: Copy nginx config\n  ansible.builtin.copy:\n    src: nginx.conf\n    dest: /etc/nginx/nginx.conf\n  notify: Restart nginx","id":"ans.i.06.1"},
        {"t":"choice","topic":"Ansible","q":"Asha copies a config file and the task notifies a handler. The file was already identical, so the task says `ok`. What happens to the handler?","a":"It does not run, because the task did not change anything","w":["It runs anyway, because it was named in `notify`","It runs first, before all other tasks","Ansible shows an error for the unused handler"],"why":"A task notifies its handlers only when it reports `changed`. No change means no restart, which is the point of handlers.","id":"ans.i.06.2"},
        {"t":"choice","topic":"Ansible","q":"Three tasks in one play all notify the handler `Restart nginx`. How many times does nginx restart?","a":"Once, at the end of the play","w":["Three times, once after each task","Twice: after the first and the last task","Never, because handlers need a `when` condition"],"why":"Even if many tasks notify the same handler, it runs just one time, after all normal tasks are done.","id":"ans.i.06.3"},
        {"t":"bug","topic":"Ansible","q":"The config task never restarts nginx. The handler is never found. Which line is wrong?","lang":"yaml","code":["- name: Web server setup","  hosts: web","  become: true","  tasks:","    - name: Copy nginx config","      ansible.builtin.copy:","        src: nginx.conf","        dest: /etc/nginx/nginx.conf","      notify: Reload web","  handlers:","    - name: Restart nginx","      ansible.builtin.service:","        name: nginx","        state: restarted"],"bad":8,"why":"The text in `notify` must match the handler `name` exactly. Here `notify` says `Reload web` but the handler is `Restart nginx`.","id":"ans.i.06.4"},
        {"t":"order","topic":"Ansible","q":"Put these events in the order they happen.","items":["The config task changes the file","The task notifies the handler","The other tasks in the play finish","The handler restarts nginx"],"why":"Notified handlers wait until the end of the play, then run once.","id":"ans.i.06.5"},
        {"t":"indent","topic":"Ansible","step":2,"q":"Fix the indentation of this playbook with a handler.","lang":"yaml","lines":[[0,"- name: Web server setup"],[2,"hosts: web"],[2,"become: true"],[2,"tasks:"],[4,"- name: Copy nginx config"],[6,"ansible.builtin.copy:"],[8,"src: nginx.conf"],[8,"dest: /etc/nginx/nginx.conf"],[6,"notify: Restart nginx"],[2,"handlers:"],[4,"- name: Restart nginx"],[6,"ansible.builtin.service:"],[8,"name: nginx"],[8,"state: restarted"]],"why":"`handlers:` sits at the same level as `tasks:`, inside the play. `notify` belongs to the task, at the same level as the module name.","id":"ans.i.06.6"},
        {"t":"choice","topic":"Ansible","q":"Ravi wants to restart nginx only after its config changes. Which is the best choice?","a":"A `notify` on the config task and a handler that restarts nginx","w":["A restart task at the top of the play, with no `notify`","A restart task at the end that always runs","A second playbook he runs by hand every time"],"why":"Handlers avoid needless restarts. nginx is restarted only when the config file really changed.","id":"ans.i.06.7"},
        {"t":"check","topic":"Ansible","q":"When does a handler run?","a":"At the end of the play, if a task notified it after reporting `changed`","w":["Right after every task in the play","Before the first task, to prepare the host","Only when you add `--check`"],"why":"Handlers are notified by changed tasks and run once, after the normal tasks.","id":"ans.i.06.8"},
        {"t":"check","topic":"Ansible","q":"A task has `notify: Restart nginx`. What must be true for it to work?","a":"A handler with the exact name `Restart nginx` exists in the play","w":["A variable called `Restart nginx` is defined","The task must use the `service` module","The handler must be listed before the tasks"],"why":"`notify` finds a handler by its name, so the two names must match.","id":"ans.i.06.9"}
      ] },
      { id: "ans.i.07", level: "i", n: 7, title: "Templates", cards: [
        {"t":"learn","topic":"Ansible","title":"Templates: files filled with variables","body":"A **template** is a text file with Jinja2 placeholders like `{{ port }}`. The `ansible.builtin.template` module fills them in and copies the result to the host. File names usually end with `.j2`, and each host can get different values.","lang":"yaml","code":"- name: Create app config\n  ansible.builtin.template:\n    src: app.conf.j2\n    dest: /etc/app.conf","id":"ans.i.07.1"},
        {"t":"choice","topic":"Ansible","q":"The file `app.conf.j2` has the line `listen {{ http_port }};`. Which module fills in the value of `http_port`?","a":"`ansible.builtin.template`","w":["`ansible.builtin.copy`","`ansible.builtin.fetch`","`ansible.builtin.stat`"],"why":"`template` processes Jinja2 and then writes the file. `copy` sends the file exactly as it is, so the braces would stay in the text.","id":"ans.i.07.2"},
        {"t":"bug","topic":"Ansible","q":"Lakshmi's file `app.conf.j2` still shows `{{ http_port }}` on the server. Which line is wrong?","lang":"yaml","code":["- name: Create app config","  ansible.builtin.copy:","    src: app.conf.j2","    dest: /etc/app.conf"],"bad":1,"why":"`copy` does not fill in variables. Use `ansible.builtin.template` so Jinja2 is processed.","id":"ans.i.07.3"},
        {"t":"indent","topic":"Ansible","step":2,"q":"Fix the indentation of this template task.","lang":"yaml","lines":[[0,"- name: Config for each host"],[2,"hosts: web"],[2,"tasks:"],[4,"- name: Create app config"],[6,"ansible.builtin.template:"],[8,"src: app.conf.j2"],[8,"dest: /etc/app.conf"],[8,"mode: \"0644\""]],"why":"The options `src`, `dest` and `mode` belong under the module name, indented two more spaces.","id":"ans.i.07.4"},
        {"t":"choice","topic":"Ansible","q":"Which Jinja2 block writes one line for each name in a list called `servers`?","a":"`{% for s in servers %}` ... `{% endfor %}`","w":["`{{ for s in servers }}` ... `{{ endfor }}`","`{% loop servers as s %}` ... `{% end %}`","`{# for s in servers #}` ... `{# endfor #}`"],"why":"Jinja2 uses `{% %}` for logic like `for` and `if`, and `{{ }}` only to print a value. A `for` block always closes with `{% endfor %}`.","id":"ans.i.07.5"},
        {"t":"order","topic":"Ansible","q":"Put the steps in order for a template task.","items":["Write `app.conf.j2` with `{{ http_port }}`","Set `http_port` for the host or group","Run the playbook with the `template` task","The host gets `app.conf` with the real number"],"why":"You prepare the template and the value first. Ansible then renders the file for each host.","id":"ans.i.07.6"},
        {"t":"choice","topic":"Ansible","q":"Two servers have `http_port: 80` and `http_port: 8080`. Both run the same template task. What do they get?","a":"Each server gets its own file, filled with its own value","w":["Both get the same value, taken from the first server","Ansible stops because the values are different","Both get the file with `{{ http_port }}` unchanged"],"why":"Ansible renders the template once for every host, using that host's variables.","id":"ans.i.07.7"},
        {"t":"check","topic":"Ansible","q":"What is the main difference between `template` and `copy`?","a":"`template` fills in Jinja2 placeholders; `copy` sends the file unchanged","w":["`template` works only on Windows hosts","`copy` fills in variables; `template` does not","`template` can only copy files smaller than 1 KB"],"why":"Use `copy` for fixed files and `template` when the content depends on variables.","id":"ans.i.07.8"},
        {"t":"check","topic":"Ansible","q":"Which tag prints the value of a variable inside a `.j2` file?","a":"`{{ name }}`","w":["`{% name %}`","`{# name #}`","`<name>`"],"why":"Double curly braces print a value. `{% %}` is for statements and `{# #}` is for comments.","id":"ans.i.07.9"}
      ] },
      { id: "ans.i.08", level: "i", n: 8, title: "Loops and conditions", cards: [
        {"t":"learn","topic":"Ansible","title":"Loops and conditions","body":"Use `loop:` to run one task for many values, and read the current one as `{{ item }}`. Use `when:` to run a task only if a condition is true. Inside `when` you write the expression directly, without `{{ }}`.","lang":"yaml","code":"- name: Install tools\n  ansible.builtin.apt:\n    name: \"{{ item }}\"\n    state: present\n  loop:\n    - git\n    - curl\n  when: ansible_facts['os_family'] == \"Debian\"","id":"ans.i.08.1"},
        {"t":"choice","topic":"Ansible","q":"In a task with `loop:`, how do you read the current value?","a":"`{{ item }}`","w":["`{{ loop }}`","`{{ value }}`","`{{ each }}`"],"why":"By default, the current element of a `loop` is called `item`.","id":"ans.i.08.2"},
        {"t":"bug","topic":"Ansible","q":"Ravi wants this task to run only on Debian-family hosts, but Ansible reports an error in the condition. Which line is wrong?","lang":"yaml","code":["- name: Install git","  ansible.builtin.apt:","    name: git","    state: present","  when: ansible_facts['os_family'] = \"Debian\""],"bad":4,"why":"To compare two values you need `==`. A single `=` is not a comparison in Jinja2 expressions.","id":"ans.i.08.3"},
        {"t":"indent","topic":"Ansible","step":2,"q":"Fix the indentation of this task that uses a loop.","lang":"yaml","lines":[[0,"- name: Install tools"],[2,"hosts: web"],[2,"become: true"],[2,"tasks:"],[4,"- name: Install packages"],[6,"ansible.builtin.apt:"],[8,"name: \"{{ item }}\""],[8,"state: present"],[6,"loop:"],[8,"- git"],[8,"- curl"]],"why":"`loop` is a task keyword, so it sits at the same level as the module name, not inside the module options.","id":"ans.i.08.4"},
        {"t":"choice","topic":"Ansible","q":"Which `when` line is written correctly?","a":"`when: result.rc == 0`","w":["`when: \"{{ result.rc }} == 0\"`","`when: {{ result.rc == 0 }}`","`when: result.rc is 0 then`"],"why":"`when` already treats its value as a Jinja2 expression, so you do not add `{{ }}`.","id":"ans.i.08.5"},
        {"t":"choice","topic":"Ansible","q":"Asha writes `register: conf` on a `stat` task. What does `register` do?","a":"It saves the task result in the variable `conf` so later tasks can use it","w":["It adds the host to the inventory","It makes the task run once on all hosts","It saves the playbook in a registry"],"why":"`register` stores the output of a task. A later task can test it, for example `when: not conf.stat.exists`.","id":"ans.i.08.6"},
        {"t":"order","topic":"Ansible","q":"Put these tasks in order to create a file only if it is missing.","items":["`stat` the file path and `register: conf`","Run the next task with `when: not conf.stat.exists`","The file is created if it does not exist"],"why":"You must register the check result before a later task can use it in `when`.","id":"ans.i.08.7"},
        {"t":"check","topic":"Ansible","q":"Which task keyword runs one task for each item in a list?","a":"`loop`","w":["`notify`","`register`","`become`"],"why":"`loop` repeats the task once for every value, giving it as `item`.","id":"ans.i.08.8"},
        {"t":"check","topic":"Ansible","q":"Which is correct for a condition on the operating system family?","a":"`when: ansible_facts['os_family'] == \"Debian\"`","w":["`when: \"{{ ansible_facts['os_family'] }}\" = Debian`","`if: ansible_facts['os_family'] == \"Debian\"`","`when: ansible_facts['os_family'] => \"Debian\"`"],"why":"Use `when` with `==` and no braces. Facts such as `os_family` come from `ansible_facts`.","id":"ans.i.08.9"}
      ] },
      { id: "ans.a.09", level: "a", n: 9, title: "Roles", cards: [
        {"t":"learn","topic":"Ansible","title":"Roles: reusable bundles","body":"A **role** is a folder with a fixed layout: `tasks/main.yml`, `handlers/`, `templates/`, `files/`, `vars/`, `defaults/` and `meta/`. Ansible loads each folder by name, so you can reuse the same role in many playbooks. Use it in a play with `roles:`.","code":"- hosts: web\n  roles:\n    - nginx","lang":"yaml","id":"ans.a.09.1"},
        {"t":"choice","topic":"Ansible","q":"Ravi wants a new role called `nginx` with the standard folders created for him. Which command does that?","a":"ansible-galaxy role init nginx","w":["ansible-galaxy role install nginx","ansible-galaxy role list nginx","ansible-galaxy role remove nginx"],"why":"`ansible-galaxy role init` creates the empty role skeleton. `install` downloads a role that someone else published.","id":"ans.a.09.2"},
        {"t":"choice","topic":"Ansible","q":"Which role folder holds variables with the LOWEST precedence, so users can easily override them?","a":"`defaults/main.yml`","w":["`vars/main.yml`","`tasks/main.yml`","`meta/main.yml`"],"why":"Role defaults are the easiest to override. Values in `vars/` win over them, so keep `vars/` for things users should not change.","id":"ans.a.09.3"},
        {"t":"indent","topic":"Ansible","step":2,"q":"Fix the indentation so this play runs two roles on the web servers.","lang":"yaml","lines":[[0,"- hosts: web"],[2,"become: true"],[2,"roles:"],[4,"- common"],[4,"- nginx"]],"why":"`roles:` is a key of the play, so it lines up with `hosts`. The role names are list items one level deeper.","id":"ans.a.09.4"},
        {"t":"order","topic":"Ansible","q":"Put the steps of creating and using a role in a sensible order.","items":["Run `ansible-galaxy role init web` to make the role folders","Write the tasks in `web/tasks/main.yml`","Run the playbook that lists `web` under `roles:`","Read the play recap to see what changed"],"why":"`role init` makes the folders, so `tasks/main.yml` exists only after it. Run the play once the tasks are written, and the recap prints at the end of the run.","id":"ans.a.09.5"},
        {"t":"bug","topic":"Ansible","q":"This play should use the `nginx` role as a task, but Ansible says it cannot find that module. Which line is wrong?","code":["- hosts: web","  tasks:","    - name: Use the nginx role","      ansible.builtin.import_roles:","        name: nginx"],"lang":"yaml","bad":3,"fix":"      ansible.builtin.import_role:","why":"The module name is `ansible.builtin.import_role`, singular. A small spelling change makes a module that does not exist.","id":"ans.a.09.6"},
        {"t":"choice","topic":"Ansible","q":"Where does a role keep its Jinja2 files (ending in `.j2`) that the `ansible.builtin.template` module fills in?","a":"`templates/`","w":["`files/`","`handlers/`","`defaults/`"],"why":"`templates/` is for Jinja2 templates. `files/` holds plain files that `ansible.builtin.copy` sends as they are.","id":"ans.a.09.7"},
        {"t":"check","topic":"Ansible","q":"A role sets `port: 80` in `defaults/main.yml` and `port: 8080` in `vars/main.yml`. Which value does the role use?","a":"8080, because `vars/` beats `defaults/`","w":["80, because `defaults/` is read first","Both values are merged into a list","The play stops with an error"],"why":"Role variables in `vars/` have higher precedence than role defaults. That is why defaults are the safe place for values users may override.","id":"ans.a.09.8"},
        {"t":"check","topic":"Ansible","q":"A play lists the role `common` under `roles:`. Which file holds the role's main list of tasks?","a":"`roles/common/tasks/main.yml`","w":["`roles/common/handlers/main.yml`","`roles/common/defaults/main.yml`","`roles/common/vars/main.yml`"],"why":"The entry point for a role's tasks is `tasks/main.yml`. Handlers run only when a task notifies them, and `defaults/` and `vars/` hold variables, not tasks.","id":"ans.a.09.9"}
      ] },
      { id: "ans.a.10", level: "a", n: 10, title: "Ansible Vault", cards: [
        {"t":"learn","topic":"Ansible","title":"Vault: keep secrets safe","body":"**Ansible Vault** encrypts a file so you can keep passwords in Git without showing them. Create one with `ansible-vault create`, then run the playbook with `--ask-vault-pass`. Keep the vault password itself out of the repository.","code":"ansible-vault create secrets.yml\nansible-playbook site.yml --ask-vault-pass","lang":"shell","id":"ans.a.10.1"},
        {"t":"choice","topic":"Ansible","q":"Asha wants to open her encrypted `secrets.yml` in an editor and change a value. Which command do you use?","a":"`ansible-vault edit secrets.yml`","w":["`ansible-vault view secrets.yml`","`ansible-vault rekey secrets.yml`","`ansible-vault encrypt_string secrets.yml`"],"why":"`edit` decrypts, opens your editor, then encrypts again. `view` only shows the content, and `rekey` changes the vault password.","id":"ans.a.10.2"},
        {"t":"choice","topic":"Ansible","q":"The playbook reads an encrypted file. Which option makes Ansible ask for the vault password when you run it?","a":"`--ask-vault-pass`","w":["`--ask-pass`","`--ask-become-pass`","`--check`"],"why":"`--ask-pass` asks for the SSH password and `--ask-become-pass` asks for the sudo password. Only `--ask-vault-pass` is for Vault.","id":"ans.a.10.3"},
        {"t":"bug","topic":"Ansible","q":"Ravi set `no_log: false` while testing. Now the API token shows in the output when he runs with `-vvv`. Which line is wrong?","code":["- name: Call the deploy API","  ansible.builtin.uri:","    url: https://api.example.com/deploy","    headers:","      Authorization: \"Bearer {{ vault_api_token }}\"","  no_log: false"],"lang":"yaml","bad":5,"fix":"  no_log: true","why":"`no_log: true` hides the task's arguments and results in output and logs. Vault only protects the file on disk: once Ansible uses the value, it is plain text.","id":"ans.a.10.4"},
        {"t":"pipe","topic":"Ansible","q":"Build the safe flow for using a secret in a playbook. One block does not belong.","steps":[["CRTE","ansible-vault create secrets.yml"],["VARS","Add `vars_files: secrets.yml` to the play"],["PASS","ansible-playbook site.yml --ask-vault-pass"],["USE","Tasks use the secret, with `no_log: true`"]],"extra":[["DCRY","ansible-vault decrypt secrets.yml first"]],"why":"Ansible decrypts the vault in memory while the play runs, so you never decrypt it on disk. A decrypted file can be committed to Git by mistake.","id":"ans.a.10.5"},
        {"t":"choice","topic":"Ansible","q":"What does `ansible-vault encrypt_string` do?","a":"Encrypts one value so you can paste it into a normal YAML file","w":["Encrypts a whole playbook folder","Shows the secret in plain text","Changes the vault password of every file"],"why":"It is good when only one value is secret. The rest of the file stays readable in code review.","id":"ans.a.10.6"},
        {"t":"choice","topic":"Ansible","q":"Lakshmi saves the vault password in a file so runs need no typing. Where should that file live?","a":"Outside the Git repository, or listed in `.gitignore`","w":["Next to `secrets.yml`, committed to Git","Inside the playbook as a comment","In `group_vars/all.yml` in the repository"],"why":"If the password sits beside the encrypted file in Git, the encryption protects nothing. Use `--vault-password-file` to point at it.","id":"ans.a.10.7"},
        {"t":"check","topic":"Ansible","q":"What does `ansible-vault view secrets.yml` do?","a":"Shows the decrypted content and leaves the file encrypted","w":["Permanently decrypts the file on disk","Changes the vault password","Encrypts the file a second time"],"why":"`view` is read-only. `decrypt` is the command that turns the file back into plain text on disk.","id":"ans.a.10.8"},
        {"t":"check","topic":"Ansible","q":"Which setting keeps a secret out of the console output of one task?","a":"`no_log: true`","w":["`become: true`","`ignore_errors: true`","`gather_facts: false`"],"why":"`no_log: true` hides that task's data. The other settings control privileges, errors and facts, not output.","code":"- name: Use token\n  ansible.builtin.command: /usr/local/bin/login\n  no_log: true","lang":"yaml","id":"ans.a.10.9"}
      ] },
      { id: "ans.a.11", level: "a", n: 11, title: "Galaxy", cards: [
        {"t":"learn","topic":"Ansible","title":"Galaxy and collections","body":"A **collection** bundles modules, roles and plugins under a name like `community.general` (`namespace.collection`). **Ansible Galaxy** is the hub where you find them. Install one with `ansible-galaxy collection install`, and call its modules by the full name (**FQCN**).","code":"ansible-galaxy collection install community.general","lang":"shell","id":"ans.a.11.1"},
        {"t":"choice","topic":"Ansible","q":"In the module name `ansible.posix.firewalld`, what is `ansible.posix`?","a":"The collection (namespace and name)","w":["The module name","The role name","The playbook name"],"why":"An FQCN is `namespace.collection.module`. Here `ansible.posix` is the collection and `firewalld` is the module.","id":"ans.a.11.2"},
        {"t":"choice","topic":"Ansible","q":"A file `requirements.yml` lists collections. Which command installs them?","a":"`ansible-galaxy collection install -r requirements.yml`","w":["`ansible-galaxy collection list`","`ansible-galaxy collection download -r requirements.yml`","`ansible-galaxy collection build`"],"why":"`install -r` reads the requirements file and installs everything in it. `list` only shows what is installed, `download` only saves the files for offline use, and `build` packs your own collection.","id":"ans.a.11.3"},
        {"t":"indent","topic":"Ansible","step":2,"q":"Fix the indentation of this `requirements.yml` with collections and one role.","lang":"yaml","lines":[[0,"collections:"],[2,"- name: community.general"],[2,"- name: ansible.posix"],[0,"roles:"],[2,"- name: geerlingguy.nginx"]],"why":"`collections:` and `roles:` are top-level keys. Each item under them is a list entry with a `name`.","id":"ans.a.11.4"},
        {"t":"bug","topic":"Ansible","q":"`ansible-galaxy install -r requirements.yml` stops with an error about the base keys of this file. Which line is wrong?","code":["collection:","  - name: community.general","  - name: ansible.posix"],"lang":"yaml","bad":0,"fix":"collections:","why":"The top-level key must be `collections`, plural. ansible-galaxy accepts only `roles` and `collections` as base keys, so a typo stops it with an error.","id":"ans.a.11.5"},
        {"t":"order","topic":"Ansible","q":"Put these steps in order to use a Galaxy collection in a playbook.","items":["List the collection in `requirements.yml`","Run `ansible-galaxy collection install -r requirements.yml`","Call its module by the FQCN in a task","Run the playbook"],"why":"Install before you use. A task that calls a module from a missing collection fails.","id":"ans.a.11.6"},
        {"t":"choice","topic":"Ansible","q":"Why does Ansible recommend writing `ansible.builtin.copy` instead of just `copy`?","a":"The full name shows exactly which collection the module comes from","w":["The short name is slower to run","The short name only works on Windows hosts","The full name makes the task run as root"],"why":"Two collections can have modules with the same short name. The FQCN removes any doubt about which one runs.","id":"ans.a.11.7"},
        {"t":"check","topic":"Ansible","q":"In `ansible.builtin.copy`, what is `copy`?","a":"The module name","w":["The collection name","The namespace","The role name"],"why":"The three parts are namespace (`ansible`), collection (`builtin`) and module (`copy`).","id":"ans.a.11.8"},
        {"t":"check","topic":"Ansible","q":"What does this command install?","a":"The `community.general` collection with its modules and plugins","w":["A single role called `community`","Only the `general` module","A copy of Ansible itself"],"why":"A collection is a package of many modules, roles and plugins. It is installed once and used by FQCN.","code":"ansible-galaxy collection install community.general","lang":"shell","id":"ans.a.11.9"}
      ] },
      { id: "ans.a.12", level: "a", n: 12, title: "Testing idempotency", cards: [
        {"t":"learn","topic":"Ansible","title":"Idempotency: safe to run twice","body":"An **idempotent** playbook changes nothing on the second run: the recap shows `changed=0`. Use modules that describe the end state, like `state: directory`. Test with `--check` (dry run) and `--diff` (show the changes).","code":"- name: Make the app folder\n  ansible.builtin.file:\n    path: /opt/app\n    state: directory","lang":"yaml","id":"ans.a.12.1"},
        {"t":"choice","topic":"Ansible","q":"Every task in Asha's playbook is idempotent and the servers are already correct. What does the play recap show on a second run?","a":"`changed=0`","w":["`changed=1`","`failed=1`","`unreachable=1`"],"why":"Idempotent means the second run finds nothing to change. A `changed` number above 0 means some task is not idempotent.","id":"ans.a.12.2"},
        {"t":"choice","topic":"Ansible","q":"Which option does a dry run, showing what would change without changing it?","a":"`--check`","w":["`--syntax-check`","`--list-tasks`","`--diff`"],"why":"`--syntax-check` only checks the playbook syntax, and `--list-tasks` only prints task names. `--diff` alone still makes the changes; use it with `--check` to see them without changing anything.","id":"ans.a.12.3"},
        {"t":"bug","topic":"Ansible","q":"This task only reads a file, but the recap shows a change on every run. Which line is wrong?","code":["- name: Read the OS release","  ansible.builtin.command: cat /etc/os-release","  register: os_info","  changed_when: true"],"lang":"yaml","bad":3,"fix":"  changed_when: false","why":"`command` reports changed every time unless you say otherwise. A read-only task should set `changed_when: false`.","id":"ans.a.12.4"},
        {"t":"choice","topic":"Ansible","q":"Ravi must make sure nginx is installed. Which task is the more idempotent choice?","a":"`ansible.builtin.dnf` with `name: nginx` and `state: present`","w":["`ansible.builtin.shell` running `dnf install -y nginx`","`ansible.builtin.command` running `dnf install nginx`","`ansible.builtin.raw` running `dnf install -y nginx`"],"why":"A module checks the current state and acts only if needed. `shell`, `command` and `raw` just run the text every time.","id":"ans.a.12.5"},
        {"t":"order","topic":"Ansible","q":"Put a safe testing routine for a playbook in order.","items":["Run `ansible-lint` on the playbook","Run with `--check --diff`","Run it for real","Run it again and look for `changed=0`"],"why":"Check cheaply first, then change, then prove idempotency with a second run.","id":"ans.a.12.6"},
        {"t":"choice","topic":"Ansible","q":"Which tool runs your role in a test setup, runs it again to check idempotency, then cleans up?","a":"Molecule","w":["ansible-lint","ansible-vault","ansible-galaxy"],"why":"Molecule runs real test scenarios, including an `idempotence` step. `ansible-lint` only reads your code for bad habits and runs nothing.","id":"ans.a.12.7"},
        {"t":"check","topic":"Ansible","q":"What does `creates:` do on `ansible.builtin.command`?","a":"Skips the command if the given path already exists","w":["Creates the given path after the command","Deletes the given path first","Sets the owner of the given path"],"why":"`creates` makes a command idempotent: when the file is already there, the command does not run again.","code":"- ansible.builtin.command:\n    cmd: /usr/bin/make-report\n    creates: /tmp/report.txt","lang":"yaml","id":"ans.a.12.8"},
        {"t":"check","topic":"Ansible","q":"Why is `ansible.builtin.shell` risky for idempotency?","a":"It reports changed on every run unless you add `creates` or `changed_when`","w":["It refuses to run a second time","It ignores `become`","It can only run on localhost"],"why":"Ansible cannot know what your shell text does, so it assumes a change. Prefer a proper module when one exists.","id":"ans.a.12.9"}
      ] }
    ] },
    { id: "ops", name: "DevOps and CI/CD", topic: "DevOps", blurb: "How modern teams build, test and ship safely.", units: [
      { id: "ops.b.01", level: "b", n: 1, title: "What DevOps is", cards: [
        {"t":"learn","topic":"DevOps","title":"What DevOps is","body":"**DevOps** joins building software and running it: small changes, automated tests and releases, fast feedback, and shared responsibility, including safe habits like never sharing passwords in chat.","id":"ops.b.01.1"},
        {"t":"order","topic":"DevOps","q":"A user reports a bug. Order the steps a professional follows.","items":["Reproduce the bug","Find the cause","Fix the code","Test again"],"why":"First make the bug happen yourself, then find **why** it happens, fix it, and test that it is really fixed (and nothing else broke).","id":"ops.b.01.2"},
        {"t":"choice","topic":"DevOps","q":"Someone messages you on WhatsApp asking for the server password “to fix an urgent bug”. What do you do?","a":"Refuse, and give access only through proper accounts","w":["Send it, because it is urgent","Send half of it","Post it in the team group"],"why":"Never share passwords in chats. Real teams give each person their own account with only the access they need, and keep secrets in a vault.","id":"ops.b.01.3"},
        {"t":"choice","topic":"DevOps","q":"Which sentence describes DevOps best?","a":"A way of working where developers and operations teams share the work and goals","w":["A tool you install on a server","A new programming language","A job title only for the person who manages servers"],"why":"DevOps is mostly culture. Tools help, but they are not DevOps by themselves.","id":"ops.b.01.4"},
        {"t":"choice","topic":"DevOps","q":"Why do DevOps teams release small changes often, instead of one big change every 6 months?","a":"Small changes are easier to test, and easier to fix when something breaks","w":["Big changes are not allowed","Small changes need no testing","It makes the code run faster"],"why":"A small change has fewer places for a bug to hide. Fixing or undoing it is quick.","id":"ops.b.01.5"},
        {"t":"pipe","topic":"DevOps","q":"Build the DevOps loop for a small change.","steps":[["PLAN","Plan"],["CODE","Code","src"],["TEST","Test"],["REL","Release"]],"extra":[["BLAM","Blame someone"]],"why":"DevOps is a loop: plan, code, test, release, then learn from feedback. Blaming people is not a step.","id":"ops.b.01.6"},
        {"t":"choice","topic":"DevOps","q":"Why do teams automate tests and releases?","a":"Machines repeat the steps the same way every time, so there are fewer mistakes","w":["Because people are not allowed to run commands","Because automation costs nothing","To avoid writing any code"],"why":"Manual steps are slow and easy to get wrong. Automation is fast and repeatable.","id":"ops.b.01.7"},
        {"t":"check","topic":"DevOps","q":"Ravi says: \"We bought a tool, so now we do DevOps.\" Is that right?","a":"Not fully. DevOps also needs teamwork, shared responsibility and feedback","w":["Yes, the tool is all DevOps needs","Yes, as long as it is a paid tool","No, DevOps cannot use any tools"],"why":"Tools help automate. But DevOps is mainly how people work together.","id":"ops.b.01.8"},
        {"t":"check","topic":"DevOps","q":"A release breaks the app. What does the DevOps way say?","a":"The whole team fixes it and learns how to prevent it next time","w":["Developers say it is an operations problem","Operations say it is a developer problem","Hide the problem and wait for users to complain"],"why":"Shared responsibility and fast feedback are core DevOps ideas.","id":"ops.b.01.9"}
      ] },
      { id: "ops.b.02", level: "b", n: 2, title: "Environments: dev, staging, prod", cards: [
        {"t":"learn","topic":"DevOps","title":"Dev, staging and prod","body":"Teams run the app in different **environments**. **Dev** is where developers try things. **Staging** is a copy of production for final tests, and **production (prod)** is the real app used by customers.","id":"ops.b.02.1"},
        {"t":"choice","topic":"DevOps","q":"What is the dev environment mainly for?","a":"Developers to build and try changes safely","w":["Real customers to place orders","The final approval by the CEO","Storing backups of production data"],"why":"Dev is a safe place to experiment. A mistake there does not affect real users.","id":"ops.b.02.2"},
        {"t":"choice","topic":"DevOps","q":"Why should staging be set up like production?","a":"So problems show up in staging before real users meet them","w":["So staging can serve real customers too","So developers can skip testing","Because staging is cheaper to run"],"why":"If staging is very different from production, a test that passes there may still fail in production.","id":"ops.b.02.3"},
        {"t":"order","topic":"DevOps","q":"Put the environments in the order a change moves through them.","items":["Dev","Staging","Production"],"why":"A change is tested in dev, checked in staging, and only then released to production.","id":"ops.b.02.4"},
        {"t":"choice","topic":"DevOps","q":"Lakshmi wants to test a new feature using real customer phone numbers. Where should she do it?","a":"Nowhere. Use fake test data in dev or staging","w":["In production, because the data is real","In staging with a copy of real customer data","On her personal laptop with the real data"],"why":"Real customer data must be protected. Test with fake data that looks like the real thing.","id":"ops.b.02.5"},
        {"t":"bug","topic":"DevOps","q":"This job deploys to staging. One line is unsafe. Which one?","code":["name: Deploy","on: workflow_dispatch","jobs:","  deploy:","    runs-on: ubuntu-latest","    steps:","      - uses: actions/checkout@v4","      - run: ./deploy.sh staging","        env:","          DB_PASSWORD: \"MyPass123\""],"lang":"ci","bad":9,"why":"A password written in the file is visible to everyone who can see the code. Keep it in the secret store and read it with `secrets`. Each environment has its own value.","id":"ops.b.02.6"},
        {"t":"check","topic":"DevOps","q":"Your app has a database password. How should it differ between dev and prod?","a":"Each environment has its own secret, stored in a secret store","w":["Both use the same password written in the code","Prod uses no password","Dev uses the prod password to be sure it works"],"why":"Different environments use different config and secrets, so a leak in dev cannot open production.","id":"ops.b.02.7"},
        {"t":"check","topic":"DevOps","q":"What does \"promote the same build\" through environments mean?","a":"The exact same build that passed in staging is released to prod","w":["Rebuild the code again for prod","Write new code for each environment","Skip staging when you are in a hurry"],"why":"If you rebuild, prod may get something different from what you tested. Same build, different config.","id":"ops.b.02.8"}
      ] },
      { id: "ops.b.03", level: "b", n: 3, title: "Continuous integration", cards: [
        {"t":"learn","topic":"DevOps","title":"Continuous integration","body":"With **CI**, every push is built and tested automatically, so problems are caught in minutes. **CD** then delivers the tested build to staging and production.","id":"ops.b.03.1"},
        {"t":"choice","topic":"DevOps","q":"In CI/CD, what does CI stand for?","a":"Continuous Integration","w":["Code Inspection","Cloud Infrastructure","Continuous Installation"],"why":"**Continuous Integration**: every code change is merged often and tested automatically. CD is Continuous Delivery or Deployment.","id":"ops.b.03.2"},
        {"t":"pipe","topic":"DevOps","q":"Build a CI/CD pipeline: from a code push to production.","steps":[["GIT","Code pushed","src"],["BLD","Build"],["TEST","Run tests"],["STG","Deploy to staging"],["PROD","Deploy to production"]],"extra":[["DEL","Delete the logs"]],"why":"Build first, then **test**, so broken code never ships. Staging is a safe copy of production to check it there before real users see it.","id":"ops.b.03.3"},
        {"t":"indent","topic":"DevOps","step":2,"q":"Fix the indentation of this tiny GitHub Actions workflow.","lang":"ci","lines":[[0,"name: CI"],[0,"on: push"],[0,"jobs:"],[2,"build:"],[4,"runs-on: ubuntu-latest"],[4,"steps:"],[6,"- uses: actions/checkout@v4"],[6,"- run: npm test"]],"why":"`jobs` holds jobs, and each job has `runs-on` and `steps`. Each step starts with a dash. Wrong spaces change the meaning in YAML.","id":"ops.b.03.4"},
        {"t":"choice","topic":"DevOps","q":"The CI build on `main` turns red (failed). What should the team do first?","a":"Fix the broken build before adding new features","w":["Ignore it and merge more code","Turn off the tests","Wait for the next release"],"why":"A broken build blocks everyone. Fixing it quickly is a core CI habit.","id":"ops.b.03.5"},
        {"t":"bug","topic":"DevOps","q":"This workflow is not valid. One key is spelled wrongly. Which line?","code":["name: CI","on: push","jobs:","  build:","    runs_on: ubuntu-latest","    steps:","      - uses: actions/checkout@v4","      - run: echo \"Hello\""],"lang":"ci","bad":4,"why":"The key is `runs-on` with a hyphen. YAML keys must be spelled exactly.","id":"ops.b.03.6"},
        {"t":"choice","topic":"DevOps","q":"Where do GitHub Actions workflow files live in a repository?","a":"In `.github/workflows/`","w":["In `.actions/`","In the `workflows.yml` file in the root","In `.git/hooks/`"],"why":"GitHub looks for YAML files in `.github/workflows/`.","id":"ops.b.03.7"},
        {"t":"check","topic":"DevOps","q":"What is the difference between continuous delivery and continuous deployment?","a":"Delivery keeps every build ready to release by a person; deployment releases it to production automatically","w":["They mean exactly the same thing","Delivery is only for dev, deployment only for staging","Deployment needs no tests at all"],"why":"With delivery a person presses the release button. With deployment, passing builds go live automatically.","id":"ops.b.03.8"},
        {"t":"check","topic":"DevOps","q":"What does `on: push` mean in a workflow?","a":"The workflow runs every time someone pushes code","w":["The workflow pushes code to production","The workflow runs once a day","The workflow runs only when you click a button"],"why":"`on` sets the event that starts the workflow. `push` is the event when commits are pushed.","id":"ops.b.03.9"}
      ] },
      { id: "ops.i.04", level: "i", n: 4, title: "Pipeline YAML", cards: [
        {"t":"learn","topic":"DevOps","title":"Pipeline YAML in GitHub Actions","body":"A workflow file lives in `.github/workflows/`. `on` says when it runs, `jobs` lists the work, `runs-on` picks the machine, and `steps` are the commands (`run`) or ready-made actions (`uses`). Azure Pipelines is similar: `trigger`, `pool`, `steps`.","lang":"ci","code":"name: CI\non:\n  push:\njobs:\n  test:\n    runs-on: ubuntu-latest\n    steps:\n      - uses: actions/checkout@v4\n      - run: npm test","id":"ops.i.04.1"},
        {"t":"indent","topic":"DevOps","step":2,"q":"Fix the indentation of this workflow with two jobs.","lang":"ci","lines":[[0,"jobs:"],[2,"build:"],[4,"runs-on: ubuntu-latest"],[4,"steps:"],[6,"- run: echo build"],[2,"deploy:"],[4,"needs: build"],[4,"runs-on: ubuntu-latest"],[4,"steps:"],[6,"- run: echo deploy"]],"why":"Each job name sits under `jobs:`. Keys like `needs`, `runs-on` and `steps` sit inside the job.","id":"ops.i.04.2"},
        {"t":"bug","topic":"DevOps","q":"Lakshmi's workflow does not start any job and GitHub reports an error. Which line is wrong?","lang":"ci","code":["name: CI","on:","  push:","jobs:","  test:","    run-on: ubuntu-latest","    steps:","      - uses: actions/checkout@v4","      - run: npm test"],"bad":5,"why":"The correct key is `runs-on`. A job needs it to know which machine to use.","id":"ops.i.04.3"},
        {"t":"choice","topic":"DevOps","q":"The `deploy` job must start only after the `build` job succeeds. Which key does this?","a":"`needs: build`","w":["`after: build`","`runs-on: build`","`depends_on: build`"],"why":"Jobs run in parallel by default. `needs` makes a job wait for the jobs it lists.","id":"ops.i.04.4"},
        {"t":"choice","topic":"DevOps","q":"In a step, what is the difference between `uses` and `run`?","a":"`uses` runs a ready-made action; `run` runs a command","w":["`uses` runs a command; `run` picks the machine","`uses` sets the trigger; `run` sets the job name","They are the same; you can write either one"],"why":"`uses: actions/checkout@v4` calls an action, while `run: npm test` runs a shell command.","id":"ops.i.04.5"},
        {"t":"choice","topic":"DevOps","q":"In Azure Pipelines, which part chooses the machine image, such as `ubuntu-latest`?","a":"`pool` with `vmImage`","w":["`trigger` with `branches`","`steps` with `script`","`on` with `runs-on`"],"why":"In Azure Pipelines, `pool: vmImage:` names the image; `trigger` says when to run and `steps` hold the `script` lines.","id":"ops.i.04.6"},
        {"t":"pipe","topic":"DevOps","q":"Build the pipeline that starts when Asha pushes code.","steps":[["GIT","Push code","src"],["TRIG","Workflow starts"],["BLD","Build"],["TEST","Run tests"]],"extra":[["EDIT","Edit the server by hand"]],"why":"A push triggers the workflow, which builds and tests. Editing the server by hand is not part of an automated pipeline.","id":"ops.i.04.7"},
        {"t":"check","topic":"DevOps","q":"Which `on:` setting lets you start a workflow by clicking a button in GitHub?","a":"`workflow_dispatch`","w":["`pull_request`","`push`","`schedule`"],"why":"`workflow_dispatch` adds a manual Run workflow button. `push` and `pull_request` start it from Git events.","id":"ops.i.04.8"},
        {"t":"check","topic":"DevOps","q":"In which folder must GitHub Actions workflow files be?","a":"`.github/workflows/`","w":["`.workflows/github/`","`actions/`","`.github/pipelines/`"],"why":"GitHub only reads workflow YAML files from `.github/workflows/` in the repository.","id":"ops.i.04.9"}
      ] },
      { id: "ops.i.05", level: "i", n: 5, title: "Test stages", cards: [
        {"t":"learn","topic":"DevOps","title":"Test stages: fast first","body":"Write **many unit tests** (fast, small), **fewer integration tests**, and **few end-to-end tests** (slow, whole app). This is the test pyramid. In a pipeline, run the quick checks first so a problem **fails fast**.","lang":"ci","code":"steps:\n  - run: npm run lint\n  - run: npm test","id":"ops.i.05.1"},
        {"t":"choice","topic":"DevOps","q":"In the test pyramid, which kind of test should you have the MOST of?","a":"Unit tests","w":["End-to-end tests","Manual tests on production","Integration tests"],"why":"Unit tests are small and fast, so you can have many. Slow end-to-end tests are fewer because they cost more time.","id":"ops.i.05.2"},
        {"t":"order","topic":"DevOps","q":"Put the stages in the best order, fast first.","items":["Lint","Unit tests","Integration tests","End-to-end tests"],"why":"Quick checks go first, so a mistake is found in seconds and slow tests are skipped when an earlier stage fails.","id":"ops.i.05.3"},
        {"t":"bug","topic":"DevOps","q":"Ravi's tests fail, but the job still shows green. Which line is the problem?","lang":"ci","code":["jobs:","  test:","    runs-on: ubuntu-latest","    steps:","      - uses: actions/checkout@v4","      - run: npm test","        continue-on-error: true"],"bad":6,"why":"`continue-on-error: true` lets the job pass even if the step fails. Remove it or set it to `false`.","id":"ops.i.05.4"},
        {"t":"choice","topic":"DevOps","q":"A step in a job fails. What happens to the later steps in that job by default?","a":"They are skipped and the job fails","w":["They still run and the job is marked passed","They run twice to make sure","Only the next step is skipped"],"why":"By default, a failed step stops the rest of the job. This is how a pipeline fails fast.","id":"ops.i.05.5"},
        {"t":"indent","topic":"DevOps","step":2,"q":"Fix the indentation of this workflow that tests every pull request.","lang":"ci","lines":[[0,"name: Tests"],[0,"on:"],[2,"pull_request:"],[0,"jobs:"],[2,"test:"],[4,"runs-on: ubuntu-latest"],[4,"steps:"],[6,"- uses: actions/checkout@v4"],[6,"- run: npm run lint"],[6,"- run: npm test"]],"why":"With `pull_request`, tests run before the code is merged. Steps are items of the `steps` list.","id":"ops.i.05.6"},
        {"t":"choice","topic":"DevOps","q":"A test passes on Monday and fails on Tuesday with the same code. What is this test called?","a":"A flaky test","w":["A unit test","A lint test","A staging test"],"why":"A flaky test gives different results without any code change. People then stop trusting the pipeline, so fix or remove flaky tests.","id":"ops.i.05.7"},
        {"t":"check","topic":"DevOps","q":"Why run lint and unit tests before end-to-end tests?","a":"They are fast, so mistakes are found early and time is saved","w":["They are more important than customers","End-to-end tests cannot run after them","They test the whole app better"],"why":"Fast checks first means a broken change fails in seconds instead of minutes.","id":"ops.i.05.8"},
        {"t":"check","topic":"DevOps","q":"Which `on:` setting runs the tests when someone opens a pull request?","a":"`pull_request`","w":["`workflow_dispatch`","`release`","`schedule`"],"why":"`pull_request` starts the workflow for pull request events, so tests run before merging.","id":"ops.i.05.9"}
      ] },
      { id: "ops.i.06", level: "i", n: 6, title: "Artifacts", cards: [
        {"t":"learn","topic":"DevOps","title":"Artifacts: build once, deploy many","body":"An **artifact** is the built output of a pipeline, like a package, a binary or a Docker image. It is not the source code. Build it **once**, save it, and deploy the **same artifact** to every environment.","lang":"ci","code":"- uses: actions/upload-artifact@v4\n  with:\n    name: site\n    path: dist","id":"ops.i.06.1"},
        {"t":"choice","topic":"DevOps","q":"Which is an artifact?","a":"A built package or Docker image made by the pipeline","w":["The Git repository history","A bug ticket in the tracker","The developer's laptop"],"why":"An artifact is the output of the build. The source code is the input.","id":"ops.i.06.2"},
        {"t":"choice","topic":"DevOps","q":"Lakshmi tested her app in staging. What is the safest way to deploy to production?","a":"Deploy the same artifact that passed the staging tests","w":["Build the code again for production","Copy the source files and build on the production server","Ask each developer to build it on their laptop"],"why":"A rebuild can differ from the tested build. Using the same artifact means production runs exactly what you tested.","id":"ops.i.06.3"},
        {"t":"bug","topic":"DevOps","q":"The deploy job cannot find the files that the build job saved. Which line is wrong?","lang":"ci","code":["jobs:","  build:","    runs-on: ubuntu-latest","    steps:","      - uses: actions/upload-artifact@v4","        with:","          name: site","          path: dist","  deploy:","    needs: build","    runs-on: ubuntu-latest","    steps:","      - uses: actions/download-artifact@v4","        with:","          name: website"],"bad":14,"why":"The download `name` must be the same as the upload `name`. The upload used `site`, but the download asked for `website`.","id":"ops.i.06.4"},
        {"t":"indent","topic":"DevOps","step":2,"q":"Fix the indentation of this build job that saves an artifact.","lang":"ci","lines":[[0,"jobs:"],[2,"build:"],[4,"runs-on: ubuntu-latest"],[4,"steps:"],[6,"- uses: actions/checkout@v4"],[6,"- run: npm run build"],[6,"- uses: actions/upload-artifact@v4"],[8,"with:"],[10,"name: site"],[10,"path: dist"]],"why":"`with` is part of the step, so it lines up with `uses`. Its inputs `name` and `path` go one level deeper.","id":"ops.i.06.5"},
        {"t":"pipe","topic":"DevOps","q":"Build once, deploy the same artifact everywhere.","steps":[["GIT","Source code","src"],["BLD","Build"],["ART","Artifact"],["STG","Staging"],["PRD","Production"]],"extra":[["RBLD","Rebuild for each environment"]],"why":"The artifact is made once and moved forward unchanged. Rebuilding for each environment breaks that rule.","id":"ops.i.06.6"},
        {"t":"choice","topic":"DevOps","q":"Why give each artifact a version or tag, like `app:1.4.2`?","a":"So you know exactly what is running and can go back to an older one","w":["So the artifact becomes smaller","So the pipeline runs without tests","So source code is no longer needed"],"why":"A version tells you which build is in each environment, and makes a rollback possible.","id":"ops.i.06.7"},
        {"t":"check","topic":"DevOps","q":"Which action saves files from a GitHub Actions job so another job can use them?","a":"`actions/upload-artifact`","w":["`actions/checkout`","`actions/cache-source`","`actions/save-code`"],"why":"`upload-artifact` stores the files, and `download-artifact` gets them in another job.","id":"ops.i.06.8"},
        {"t":"check","topic":"DevOps","q":"What is the best rule for artifacts across environments?","a":"Build once and deploy the same artifact to every environment","w":["Build a new artifact for every environment","Keep only the source code, never an artifact","Build on the production server"],"why":"The same artifact in every environment means what you tested is what you release.","id":"ops.i.06.9"}
      ] },
      { id: "ops.a.07", level: "a", n: 7, title: "Blue-green and canary releases", cards: [
        {"t":"learn","topic":"DevOps","title":"Blue-green and canary releases","body":"In a **blue-green** release you keep two identical environments. Users use blue, you deploy and test the new version on green, then switch traffic to green. In a **canary** release a small share of users get the new version first while you watch the metrics. Always keep a **rollback plan**.","id":"ops.a.07.1"},
        {"t":"choice","topic":"DevOps","q":"A blue-green release goes wrong on green. What is the quickest rollback?","a":"Switch the traffic back to the old (blue) environment","w":["Rebuild the old version from scratch","Delete the green environment and wait","Ask users to clear their browser cache"],"why":"Blue is still running the old version, so rollback is just a traffic switch. This is the main strength of blue-green.","id":"ops.a.07.2"},
        {"t":"choice","topic":"DevOps","q":"Asha sends a small share of users to the new version. What should she do next?","a":"Watch error rate and speed, then raise the share if all looks good","w":["Delete the old version right away","Wait a full month without looking","Switch all users to the new version at once"],"why":"The point of a canary is to learn from a small group. If metrics get worse, stop and roll back. If they stay good, increase step by step.","id":"ops.a.07.3"},
        {"t":"choice","topic":"DevOps","q":"Which update strategy does a Kubernetes Deployment use by default?","a":"`RollingUpdate`","w":["`Recreate`","Blue-green","Canary"],"why":"`RollingUpdate` replaces old pods with new ones a few at a time. `Recreate` is a real option, but you must ask for it.","id":"ops.a.07.4"},
        {"t":"order","topic":"DevOps","q":"Put the steps of a canary release in order.","items":["Deploy the new version next to the old one","Send a small share of users to it","Watch errors and speed","Raise the share until it reaches all users"],"why":"You only increase traffic after the metrics look healthy. If they do not, you send the users back to the old version instead.","id":"ops.a.07.5"},
        {"t":"pipe","topic":"DevOps","q":"Build a blue-green release. Drop the block that does not belong.","steps":[["BLUE","Blue serves users","src"],["GRN","Deploy and test on green"],["SWCH","Switch traffic to green"],["KEEP","Keep blue ready for rollback"]],"extra":[["DEL","Delete blue before the switch"]],"why":"If you delete blue first, you lose your instant rollback. Keep it until green has proved itself.","id":"ops.a.07.6"},
        {"t":"bug","topic":"DevOps","q":"Lakshmi wants a rolling update, but this Deployment stops all old pods before starting new ones. Which line is wrong?","code":["kind: Deployment","spec:","  replicas: 3","  strategy:","    type: Recreate"],"lang":"k8s","bad":4,"why":"`Recreate` kills all old pods first, so the app is down for a moment. `RollingUpdate` replaces pods gradually.","fix":"    type: RollingUpdate","id":"ops.a.07.7"},
        {"t":"choice","topic":"DevOps","q":"Ravi ships code for a new UPI screen but keeps it hidden until a setting is turned on. What is this?","a":"A feature flag","w":["A canary release","A blue-green switch","A rolling update"],"why":"A **feature flag** turns a feature on or off without a new deploy, so you can ship code early and switch it off fast.","id":"ops.a.07.8"},
        {"t":"check","topic":"DevOps","q":"Which release method needs two full, identical environments?","a":"Blue-green","w":["Canary release","Rolling update","`Recreate` update"],"why":"Blue-green keeps a full old and a full new environment and switches between them. Canary and rolling updates mix old and new versions inside one environment.","id":"ops.a.07.9"},
        {"t":"check","topic":"DevOps","q":"Why does a canary release start with only a small share of users?","a":"If the new version has a bug, only a few users are affected","w":["Small shares make the servers faster","It lets you skip testing","It needs less code than a normal release"],"why":"A canary limits the damage while you collect real data. It does not replace testing.","id":"ops.a.07.10"}
      ] },
      { id: "ops.a.08", level: "a", n: 8, title: "Infrastructure as code", cards: [
        {"t":"learn","topic":"DevOps","title":"Infrastructure as code","body":"**Infrastructure as code** means you describe servers and networks in files and keep them in Git. Terraform is **declarative**: you write the end result you want and it works out the steps. Review the plan, then apply it.","code":"resource \"aws_s3_bucket\" \"logs\" {\n  bucket = \"asha-demo-logs-2026\"\n}","lang":"shell","id":"ops.a.08.1"},
        {"t":"order","topic":"DevOps","q":"Put the Terraform commands in the usual order for a new project, ending with cleanup.","items":["`terraform init`","`terraform plan`","`terraform apply`","`terraform destroy`"],"why":"`init` downloads providers, `plan` previews changes, `apply` makes them and `destroy` removes what Terraform created. Destroy practice resources so they do not cost money.","id":"ops.a.08.2"},
        {"t":"choice","topic":"DevOps","q":"What does `terraform plan` do?","a":"Shows the changes Terraform would make, without changing anything","w":["Creates all the resources in your code","Deletes the state file","Formats your .tf files"],"why":"Plan is a safe preview. Formatting is done by `terraform fmt`, and resources are created only by `apply`.","id":"ops.a.08.3"},
        {"t":"choice","topic":"DevOps","q":"What is the Terraform state file used for?","a":"It records which real resources Terraform manages and links them to your code","w":["It stores the Terraform license key","It is a backup copy of the provider code","It is a log of who ran `terraform plan`"],"why":"Terraform compares your code, the state and the real world to decide what to change. Lose the state and Terraform forgets what it created.","id":"ops.a.08.4"},
        {"t":"choice","topic":"DevOps","q":"Where should a team keep the Terraform state file?","a":"In a remote backend with access control and locking, not in Git","w":["Committed to the Git repository","On one team member's laptop","Sent by email after every apply"],"why":"State can contain sensitive values, so it must never be committed. A remote backend lets the whole team share one safe copy.","id":"ops.a.08.5"},
        {"t":"pipe","topic":"DevOps","q":"Build a safe review flow for Terraform changes. Drop the block that does not belong.","steps":[["PR","Open a pull request","src"],["PLAN","CI runs terraform plan"],["REV","Team reviews the plan"],["APLY","Apply after the merge"]],"extra":[["EDIT","Edit resources by hand in the console"]],"why":"Reviewing the plan in a pull request catches mistakes before anything changes. Hand edits cause drift between your code and the real system.","id":"ops.a.08.6"},
        {"t":"indent","topic":"DevOps","step":2,"q":"Fix the indentation of this GitHub Actions workflow that runs a Terraform plan on every pull request.","lang":"ci","lines":[[0,"name: Terraform plan"],[0,"on: pull_request"],[0,"jobs:"],[2,"plan:"],[4,"runs-on: ubuntu-latest"],[4,"steps:"],[6,"- uses: actions/checkout@v6"],[6,"- uses: hashicorp/setup-terraform@v4"],[6,"- run: terraform init"],[6,"- run: terraform plan"]],"why":"Jobs sit under `jobs`, and `runs-on` and `steps` sit inside a job. Each step is a list item under `steps`.","id":"ops.a.08.7"},
        {"t":"check","topic":"DevOps","q":"Someone changes a server by hand in the cloud console. Your Terraform code stays the same. What is this difference called?","a":"Drift","w":["A rollback","A state lock","An output"],"why":"Drift means the real system no longer matches the code. A new `terraform plan` shows it so you can fix the code or the system.","id":"ops.a.08.8"},
        {"t":"check","topic":"DevOps","q":"What does \"declarative\" mean in Terraform?","a":"You describe the end result you want, and Terraform works out the steps","w":["You write every step in the exact order to run","The code can run only one time","You must click each setting in a web console"],"why":"With declarative code you say \"I want one bucket\". Terraform checks what exists and creates or changes only what is needed.","id":"ops.a.08.9"}
      ] },
      { id: "ops.a.09", level: "a", n: 9, title: "Monitoring and SRE basics", cards: [
        {"t":"learn","topic":"DevOps","title":"Monitoring and SRE basics","body":"Watch your app with **metrics** (numbers over time), **logs** (event messages) and **traces** (the path of one request). Google SRE teaches four **golden signals**: latency, traffic, errors and saturation. Alert on what users feel, not on every small spike.","id":"ops.a.09.1"},
        {"t":"choice","topic":"DevOps","q":"Which list shows the four golden signals?","a":"Latency, traffic, errors, saturation","w":["CPU, memory, disk, network","Uptime, downtime, bugs, cost","Logs, metrics, traces, alerts"],"why":"The golden signals describe how users experience a service. CPU and memory are resources, and saturation tells you how full they are.","id":"ops.a.09.2"},
        {"t":"choice","topic":"DevOps","q":"What is an SLO?","a":"A target for a measurement, for example \"99.9% of requests succeed\"","w":["The measurement itself, such as the success rate","A paid contract that gives money back when you fail","A special type of log file"],"why":"The measurement is the SLI. The target for it is the SLO. A contract with penalties is an SLA.","id":"ops.a.09.3"},
        {"t":"choice","topic":"DevOps","q":"Your SLO is 99.9% of requests succeed. What is your error budget?","a":"The remaining 0.1% of requests that are allowed to fail","w":["99.9% of requests","1% of requests","10% of requests"],"why":"The error budget is 100% minus the SLO. While you have budget left you can ship changes. When it is used up, focus on reliability.","id":"ops.a.09.4"},
        {"t":"choice","topic":"DevOps","q":"Which problem should wake the on-call engineer at night?","a":"Many users get errors when they pay at checkout","w":["One server's CPU was high for a short time","A disk is half full","A deployment finished successfully"],"why":"Page people for symptoms users feel. Causes such as a CPU spike can go to a dashboard or a ticket unless users are hurt.","id":"ops.a.09.5"},
        {"t":"order","topic":"DevOps","q":"Put the steps of handling an incident in a sensible order.","items":["An alert fires and the on-call engineer responds","Reduce the impact, for example by rolling back","Find the root cause","Write a blameless postmortem"],"why":"First stop the pain for users, then find the cause and learn from it. The postmortem comes after the service is stable.","id":"ops.a.09.6"},
        {"t":"pipe","topic":"DevOps","q":"Build a monitoring flow. Drop the block that does not belong.","steps":[["APP","App sends metrics and logs","src"],["COLL","A collector gathers them"],["DASH","Dashboard shows the signals"],["ALRT","Alert pages the on-call engineer"]],"extra":[["MUTE","Mute all alerts for ever"]],"why":"Data flows from the app to storage, then to dashboards and alerts. Muting every alert leaves you blind, so fix noisy alerts instead.","id":"ops.a.09.7"},
        {"t":"check","topic":"DevOps","q":"Which one follows a single request as it moves through many services?","a":"A trace","w":["An SLO","A runbook","An error budget"],"why":"Traces show where time is spent for one request. A runbook is a set of steps for the on-call engineer.","id":"ops.a.09.8"},
        {"t":"check","topic":"DevOps","q":"What is the goal of a blameless postmortem?","a":"To learn how the system failed and fix it, without blaming a person","w":["To find who should be punished","To hide the incident from other teams","To skip fixing the problem"],"why":"People make mistakes when systems allow it. Blameless reviews make it safe to share facts, so the real fixes get made.","id":"ops.a.09.9"}
      ] }
    ] }
  ]
};
